bench_claim() {
  case "$1" in
    8251) echo 'The keyboard'"'"'s uPD8251A is pinned against NP2kai io/serial.c + keystat.c, qemu-pc98 hw/input/pc98-kbd.c and the NEC uPD8251A datasheet, every check carrying its reference: status bits DSR/TXEMPTY/TXRDY forced (0x85) and SYNDET 0, RXRDY set by a delivery and cleared by the 0x41 read (including a read while RXRDY=0, which returns the last latch as both software references do), RxEN/RTS not gating reception, delivery winning a same-clk read without losing the new byte, TxEN gating the 0x41 command path, unknown commands answered 0xFC, 0x95/0x9C/0x9D/0x96/0x9F/0x99 answered as the references do, an unrecognized 0x9D LED parameter answered with NO second byte (NP2kai and QEMU both ignore it), 0x9C 0x70 inhibiting typematic repeat, the bit-3 keyboard reset flushing the receive side while KEEPING the held-key state so the held key'"'"'s break still reaches the guest, and the FIFO'"'"'s 16-entry overrun semantics. The known, recorded divergences (the 8251'"'"'s mode-after-IR phase, the die'"'"'s overwrite-old overrun, 0x99'"'"'s QEMU-vs-NP2kai answer, the 0x96 ID byte QEMU reports as 0x85, and TxRDY/TXEMPTY forced) are printed as WAIVED lines with both values instead of being asserted.' ;;
    adpcma_law) echo 'Every one of the ADPCM-A increment table'"'"'s 392 entries equals the die'"'"'s law inc = ((2*nibble+1) * rss_delta[step]) / 8 as the elaborated RTL reads it -- the top-of-table entries (46,7)=2403, (47,7)=2645, (48,6)=2522 and (48,7)=2910 included, above 2047 and legal because the die'"'"'s sample field is 12 bits and WRAPS -- and the decoder that consumes the table emits the law'"'"'s sample stream up to step 48, a table clamped to 2047 at those four entries failing both legs.' ;;
    artic_atomic) echo 'TB_PC9821_ARTIC_ATOMIC: a word read of the ARTIC counter at 0x5E (the firmware delay() helper'"'"'s `in ax,5Eh`, cnt[23:8]) returns ONE instant'"'"'s value even when a 307.2 kHz tick carries out of bit 15 between its two byte lanes: swept across every phase of the tick around cnt = 0x00FFFF, the word is always 0x00FF or 0x0100 - never the torn 0x01FF that reads 65,536 ticks (213 ms) ahead and lets a `now - start >= n` delay loop exit early - matching both references'"'"' single snapshot per access (NP2kai io/artic.c artic_r16: `cnt = artic_getcnt(); if (port & 2) cnt >>= 8; return((UINT16)cnt);`; MAME src/mame/nec/pc9801.cpp timestamp_r returns `total_cycles() >> (8 * offset)` for the whole 16-bit access).' ;;
    artic_frame) echo 'The crt31k probe'"'"'s own measurement, run against the core: with clk_sys at 80 MHz, the real pc9821_iobus (its ARTIC counter at 0x5C-0x5F and its device bus), pc9821_dot_ce and pc9821_crtc wired as pc9821_system wires them, a CPU that executes tools/hwprobe/crt31k/crt31k.s'"'"'s measure_runs access for access through the iobus CPU port - wait_vsync_rise polling the master GDC status (port 0x60) bit 5 with its ARTIC timeout reads, artic_read'"'"'s 0x5F / word 0x5C / 0x5F reads and its wrap rule, the 24-bit delta - reads ARTIC ticks per 60 frames in the 24 kHz mode (as found and after the probe'"'"'s restore sequence) and per 70 frames in the 31 kHz mode within 100 ppm of the Xe10'"'"'s docs/probes/CRT31K.TXT rows (326 700 / 326 699 and 306 930; the two machines'"'"' ARTIC and dot oscillators are separate crystals), and the 24 kHz-to-31 kHz frame-period ratio, which cancels the ARTIC, within 10 ppm of the capture'"'"'s. A 24 kHz frame ~610 ppm long (the dot enable 200 parts in 327 680 slow) fails both checks.' ;;
    artic_layout) echo 'The ARTIC timestamp ports 0x5C..0x5F are ONE 24-bit counter at 307200 Hz in which 0x5E MIRRORS 0x5D - both return cnt[15:8], as NP2kai artic_bind attaches 0x5D and 0x5E to the same handler - and 0x5F is cnt[23:16], so the value reconstructed from 0x5C/0x5D/0x5F advances by the elapsed ticks while 0x5E equals 0x5D or is one ahead, and the real-machine captures prove the mirror: six of fourteen read 0x5E EQUAL to 0x5D and seven read +1, an agreement adjacent independent bytes cannot produce.' ;;
    artic_watch) echo 'The ARTIC timestamp counter advances at 307200 Hz through the guest own byte port, and the 16 bits a word read at 0x5E returns - cnt[23:8], with 0x5E mirroring 0x5D in the low byte - are exactly the 16 bits the watchdog message U field reports, so a capture decoded from that field is evidence about the value the firmware delay() helper is spinning on.' ;;
    artic_word) echo 'The ARTIC timestamp counter read the way the FIRMWARE reads it - as a 16-bit word at port 0x5E - returns cnt[23:8] on the data lines the guest expects (0x5E mirror of 0x5D in the low byte and 0x5F cnt[23:16] in the high byte) and ADVANCES every 256 ticks, so the deadline the option ROM delay() helper spins on is reachable in 833 us per unit rather than the 213 ms the old four-independent-byte layout produced. Every other bench in this tree drives one byte enable at a time, so the word path the firmware actually uses had no test.' ;;
    aspect) echo 'TB_PC9821_ASPECT: the OSD "Aspect ratio" field reaches video_freak as the NEC monitor'"'"'s shape: with pc9821_aspect fed the picture video_freak receives, "Original" (option 0) gives ARX:ARY 16:10 for a 640x400 picture (the 24 kHz 848 x 440 raster and the 31 kHz 800 x 449 one), for a 200-line picture and for the analog doubler'"'"'s 640x800 copy of the 400-line one, and 4:3 for a 640x480 picture (NEC display sheets: PC-KD854 d077 640x400 表示面積 240*150; PC-KD1522 d059 400 lines 260*163, 480 lines 260*195; PC-KD871 d087 200/400 lines 240*150 - square dots in every mode), including a 480-line frame whose last line ends on the very pixel enable VS rises on; the shape moves only within two clocks of a VS rising edge, and one frame cut short (a relock flush) does not flip it; options 1-3 stay 3:0, 1:0 and 2:0 in both modes; and through the framework'"'"'s own video_freak (1920x1080 HDMI) Scale 0 passes 16:10 / 4:3 through, while V-Integer and Narrower HV-Integer give 1280x800 for 400 lines and 1280x960 for 480 lines, the exact 2x of square dots. FAIL-FIRST: with option 0 fixed at 4:3 (the previous wiring) the 400-line checks fail, and Narrower HV-Integer gives 640x800 instead of 1280x800.' ;;
    ata) echo 'The ATA task file as the machine has it: the measured 0x0430 connection report (0x41), the DRDY|DSC reset status, IDENTIFY raising DRQ and IRQ, a status read acknowledging that IRQ, the IDENTIFY words, PIO reads and writes through the block adapter, both data phases as the ROM'"'"'s own 0x0432 save/restore bracket leaves them, and rejected commands honouring nIEN. Its single-sector write is preceded by one STRAY read of the high data port mid-transfer, which the read serializer must ignore because DRQ is set on the write path too: gating that read on DRQ alone advances the byte index by a word and hands the sector over two words early, which the drain of the modelled block adapter sees.' ;;
    ata_abort) echo 'A read-ahead that ERRORs while the FSM waits in X_WAIT_BLOCK truly abandons the block transfer: `abort_command` clears `prefetch_busy`, so `block_in_flight` goes false and the very next SRST (the authentic ROM'"'"'s own `out 0x74C,0x06` pair) returns the drive to READY instead of parking it in X_DRAIN with BSY set forever. On the current RTL the abort leaves `prefetch_busy` set, `block_in_flight` stays true, and the SRST sets BSY and never clears it -- a dead drive from one adapter error.' ;;
    ata_advertised) echo 'The mechanical advertised=>executable cross-check the multiple-mode defect needed: every capability bit the ATA model'"'"'s IDENTIFY page sets is executed or is not set - LBA support (word 49 bit 9) runs an LBA-mode READ SECTORS, the PIO-mode words (word 53 bit 1 / word 64) run SET FEATURES, and word 82'"'"'s NOP / DEVICE RESET / WRITE CACHE bits run 0x00 / 0x08 / 0xE7. It pins that multiple mode is NOT advertised (word 47 = 0, because the model buffers one sector and the Xe10 ROM'"'"'s READ MULTIPLE does not check DRQ) and that no DMA capability is advertised anywhere (word 49 bit 8, words 62/63/88 and word 53 bit 2 clear) because no 0xC8-0xCF command exists.' ;;
    ata_bankwindow) echo 'The 0x0432 bank window covers the ATA task file'"'"'s SIDE EFFECTS and its data port, not just the bytes they answer with: a 0x064E status poll at a floating bank leaves a pending interrupt set (only the interface bank acknowledges, NP2kai ideio_i64e'"'"'s getidedrv() NULL arm), a 0x0642 ERROR read at a floating bank does not clear the drive'"'"'s latched STATUS.ERR (NP2kai ideio_i642), and a 16-bit data-port write at a floating bank neither enters the sector buffer nor moves the word pointer in either direction (NP2kai ideio_w16) - the sector then round-trips through the modelled block adapter byte-exact, so no stray word consumed a slot.' ;;
    ata_blockmap) echo 'The 0x0432 IDE bank select as the machine has it: a write with bit 7 set is a no-op (so the ROM'"'"'s own save-and-restore bracket leaves the interface connected), which channel is connected is decided by bit 0 rather than a byte comparison, the task file answers at the hard disk'"'"'s channel - bit 0 clear, bank 0'"'"'s master, the fixed layout (the stock Xe10'"'"'s and NP2kai'"'"'s unit 0) - and floats at the other (the values measured on the probed Xe10, whose empty bank was 0x00 and live bank 0x01: an empty bank leaves every task-file register at 0x0E with 0x74C/0x74E at 0x0F and their odd partners at 0xFF; the live bank answers 0x064E = 0x50 with 0x074C = 0x50 and 0x074E = 0x00), a task-file write follows the same window, and a command issued after the bank select reaches the drive.' ;;
    ata_blockwait) echo 'The drive must account for a block transfer it has already issued before it issues another: pc9821_block_io accepts a request only while it is idle, so a request pulse that arrives while it is busy is never seen and the drive waits in X_WAIT_BLOCK forever. The sequence tested is one a guest can produce - a soft reset abandoning a transfer mid-flight, then a reissued READ SECTORS - and the assertion is that the adapter sees BOTH requests.' ;;
    ata_cmd_race) echo 'A0x64E COMMAND write landing in the SAME clk as the block_done completion that retires the old transfer must not vanish against the stale transfer state: the dispatch guard reads pre-update BSY/xfer_state, so post-fix the dispatch takes over against the POST-retire device exactly as if the completion had been observed a cycle earlier (the serialized NP2kai order), while a command write in a NON-completing transfer cycle is still ignored. The retiring handler'"'"'s read-ahead re-issue is folded out of the takeover so the adapter serves exactly the requests the surviving transfers own.' ;;
    ata_conform) echo 'A conformance sweep of the PC-9821 ATA interface against NP2kai'"'"'s cbus/ideio.c: the port register file (0x0430/0x0432/0x0433/0x0435 and the 0x064x/0x074x block), a PIO sector read and write, the status/error semantics (a command abort, an ERROR-register read, READ VERIFY) and the reset paths (software reset, DEVICE RESET, EXECUTE DEVICE DIAGNOSTIC, NOP) are each compared word for word with the reference, while the IDENTIFY words are asserted against the machine'"'"'s capture with the PIO-only core'"'"'s documented capability substitutions (docs/probes/IDE.TXT) for the same capacity. The NP2kai divergences Round 3 fixed are asserted; ten documented divergences (the measured 0x0430/0x074E/0x064C, the 0x0644/0x0646 post-reset default, the still-waived 0x90 diagnostic error signature, and the withdrawn multiple-mode words 47/59) print a WAIVED line, while DEVICE RESET is asserted against the MACHINE (status 0x51 / ERROR 0x04, docs/probes/IDE.TXT:343) where NP2kai'"'"'s 0x01|0x80 disagrees, and so is the ERROR-register read, which keeps STATUS.ERR on the machine (IDE.TXT:343,349) where NP2kai'"'"'s ideio_i642 clears it.' ;;
    ata_ctx) echo 'The ATA'"'"'s transfer context reaches the stall record'"'"'s new ALB and ASE fields end to end: ALB is current_lba, the LBA the in-flight or last READ SECTORS started at, and it names the sector a transfer stopped on; ASE packs the task file'"'"'s {sector_count, error_reg} high half first, so it reads 0101 at reset (the error register'"'"'s 0x01 diagnostic value), 0400 while a 4-sector read of LBA 0x00123456 runs, and 0404 when the block engine fails that read - error_reg 0x04 ABRT (abort_command, NP2kai cmdabort'"'"'s 0x41 class) with abort reason 1 (block engine error) in the ATA word above - which is the pair a capture needs to tell '"'"'the guest never issued a command'"'"' from '"'"'our own block engine failed'"'"'.' ;;
    ata_datapath) echo 'The IDE sector data path end to end through the real block adapter: a full 512-byte PIO sector read and write are byte-exact against a synthetic disk image, the CHS and LBA28 forms translate to the reference'"'"'s LBA and the read-ahead presents each sector once, the 16-bit data port assembles the sector word little-endian across 0x640/0x641 while the DRQ/data-phase handshake and the task-file advance follow NP2kai, and the measured per-sector cost is reported in clk_sys and MB/s at the CURRENT class table (IO_MIN_HOLD = 4, a 16-bit data word = 6 clk_sys, so the data phase is 1,536 clk_sys = 16.7 MB/s and a fetch-bound sector is 4,937 = 5.2 MB/s).' ;;
    ata_dbg) echo 'The ATA'"'"'s two diagnostic bytes, which the watchdog prints as the telemetry'"'"'s A, K, X and Z fields and from which a MiSTer was diagnosed, are asserted for the first time: the state byte packs {device_present, mounted, channel_reg[0], xfer_state, BSY, DRQ} and reads 0xC0 with an image mounted and the bank at its power-up 0x00 - the disk'"'"'s bank 0 since the layout was fixed (an A=0x40 is the same byte with the disk on bank 1) - selecting bank 0x01 drops device_present (0x60) and re-selecting bank 0x00 raises it again and so bit 7, the command word packs {aborts, last_cmd, commands, reason} and is zero on a card that has issued nothing, an accepted command increments only the command count while a rejected one increments both and carries its reason, and the IDENTIFY state is visible in the packed xfer_state field with DRQ set and BSY clear.' ;;
    ata_drq_takeover) echo 'TB_PC9821_ATA_DRQ_TAKEOVER: a command written to 0x064E while a PIO data phase is pending with BSY clear (DRQ set, words left to transfer) is EXECUTED the way NP2kai'"'"'s ideio_o64e dispatches every command byte (cbus/ideio.c `drv->cmd = dat;` then `switch (dat)`): RECALIBRATE in the middle of a READ SECTORS sector ends with status 0x50 and the drive takes the next IDENTIFY; a second IDENTIFY in the middle of an IDENTIFY page restarts the page at word 0 (0x044A); a READ SECTORS in the middle of a WRITE SECTORS sector abandons the half-filled sector (no block write reaches the image) and reads the new LBA; a READ SECTORS issued while the old READ'"'"'s read-ahead is still at the block adapter reads BSY, sends NO request until that read-ahead completes, then fetches exactly the new LBA and presents it with status 0x58; an SRST during that wait cancels the deferred command (no request, READY after the reset); and NOP (NP2kai `case 0x00: break`, no state change) leaves the data phase standing. A command written while BSY is set is still ignored (tb_pc9821_ata_cmd_race S2).' ;;
    ata_eject_abort) echo 'A guest READ SECTORS always terminates in one of the two outcomes ATA semantics allow - the block (DRQ rises) or an abort (STATUS.ERR, NP2kai/qemu'"'"'s 0x41) - whatever cycle the slot'"'"'s img_mounted pulse lands on, INCLUDING the cycles the shared adapter'"'"'s hand-over owns. The real pc9821_ata, pc9821_block_share and pc9821_block_io are driven with a host model for one READ SECTORS while an eject (img_mounted with img_size < 512) is swept across every offset around the hand-over at pulse widths 1 (offsets 0..8) and 3 (offsets 0..6), plus the no-eject control: every one must end with the drive not BSY and ERR set (a visible abort), and *no* offset may leave DRQ risen - a read of the ejected slot must fail, not be served. A second sweep grows a mounted image (64 -> 1000 blocks) one cycle at a time across the same window and then reads an LBA that exists only in the grown image: it must be SERVED at every offset, which pins that the adapter'"'"'s own mounted/capacity latch never keeps the pre-mount value after the pulse that delivered the new one.' ;;
    ata_err_sticky) echo 'TB_PC9821_ATA_ERR_STICKY: STATUS.ERR is sticky on the hard disk the way the Xe10 shows it: after an aborted command (DEVICE RESET 0x08 on the ATA disk: status 0x51, ERROR 0x04) neither a read of the ERROR register (0x0642) nor a device/head write (0x064C = 0xA0) changes STATUS - both still read 0x51 - and the next command (IDENTIFY) then clears it to its own DRQ status 0x58, replaying the ideprobe DIAG-then-IRQ sequence whose capture reads '"'"'after 0x08 status = 51 ; after 0x08 ERROR(0642) = 04'"'"' followed by '"'"'pre/final status = 51 58'"'"'.' ;;
    ata_idcyl) echo 'The IDENTIFY geometry words across every capacity class that separates implementations: each capacity is set, IDENTIFY is issued, the whole 256-word block is consumed through the task file and word 1 (the factory-default 16-head/63-sector cylinder count floor(capacity/1008)) and word 54 (the current 8-head/17-sector count floor(capacity/136)) are compared against the bench'"'"'s own integer division, each clamped at 0xFFFF - the clamp boundary 136*65536 and its neighbours, a capacity whose quotient is exactly 0xFFFF, and capacities up to 2^32 whose quotient the 16-bit field cannot hold. The module computes both with a free-running two-pass 32-clock shift-and-subtract divider that publishes a changed capacity within 96 clocks and the bench waits 160 after each change; the bench passes against either arithmetic, so it pins the answer rather than the divider.' ;;
    ata_identify) echo 'The ATA IDENTIFY DEVICE page against the machine'"'"'s own capture (docs/probes/IDE.TXT IDW rows): at the captured drive'"'"'s 7,880,544-sector capacity and its post-BIOS 8-head/17-sector geometry every one of the 256 words is compared with the capture, the model-specific identity words (serial, firmware, model) carry the core'"'"'s own documented Quantum identity, the vendor words 7/8 stay 0 (single-sample convention), and the capability words that would advertise a feature the PIO-only core cannot execute (DMA/UDMA in words 49/53/62/63/65-68/88, the security/CFA words, and word 82'"'"'s extra command bits) stay at the core'"'"'s honest value with the multiple-mode words 47/59 withdrawn; word 255 is recomputed as the ATA integrity word (0xA5 signature plus the two'"'"'s-complement checksum over the core'"'"'s own 255 words) instead of copying the capture'"'"'s E9; then a non-default INITIALIZE DEVICE PARAMETERS to 16 heads/63 sectors is issued and words 54/55/56/57/58 are asserted against the bench'"'"'s own integer division floor(capacity/(heads*sectors)) and current capacity equal to that times heads times sectors.' ;;
    ata_integrity) echo 'The disk-read data path is byte-exact all the way from the host'"'"'s 16-bit sector word to the guest'"'"'s 0x640/0x641 PIO lanes: a known image is read back through the real pc9821_ata + pc9821_block_io pair with a host model that carries the full hps_io multi-block handshake (sd_blk_cnt+1 sectors in one ack session), so a long sequential run, an unaligned run, an odd-length/short run, a far random read and a re-read served out of the read-ahead window are each compared word-for-word against the image, the low lane is shown to be the even byte and the high lane the odd byte with the word pointer advancing only on 0x641, and the host is shown to have fetched every sector the guest consumed (none skipped, none served stale, no re-fetch loop) with the window re-read costing no host handshake at all.' ;;
    ata_iowait) echo 'The CPU-side cost of an IDE PIO sector read, measured through the real pc9821_iobus instead of a bench-inserted gap: one 16-bit data-port word (0x0640) is ONE bus access that walks two lanes and stalls the CPU for 8 clk_sys, because EVERY lane the access FETCHES pays its own class (the rule the capture supports, docs/probes/IO.TXT pricing 0x0640 and 0x0641 as accesses in their own right, rows IDED0/IDED1, and the machine'"'"'s per-access values being that capture'"'"'s IDE.TXT rows IDEDAT0/IDEDAT1) and the min-profile class table puts both IDE data lanes at its FSM floor (IO_MIN_HOLD = 4), so a 512-byte sector'"'"'s data phase is 256 x 9 = 2304 clk_sys (11.1 MB/s at 50 MHz) and 2048 of those cycles are device-wait; the task file charges the same floor, the fabric holds device_rd_wait/device_rd_hold low as pc9821_system'"'"'s decode does for every ATA port, so the measured cost is the class and nothing else, and the 256 words are byte-exact against a synthetic disk image with the CPU'"'"'s own view of the port.' ;;
    ata_irq_hold) echo 'The IRQ line is level-until-ack exactly as NP2kai'"'"'s ideio.c holds it: setintr raises and only ideio_i64e'"'"'s pic_resetirq on a 0x064E status read lowers, so a deferred ISR'"'"'s 0x0433 source-identification read must still see bank 0'"'"'s pending bit (0x01, the Xe10'"'"'s 0x0433, docs/probes/KIT1-XE10-IDE1.TXT IRQ433 row) after a sector'"'"'s last word parks the FSM in the read-ahead wait arm - dropping the level there loses the interrupt source and deadlocks READ MULTIPLE at the next block boundary. Pinned for the READ MULTIPLE (M=2) wait arm, the WRITE SECTORS per-sector commit, and command issue (NP2kai'"'"'s command dispatch never lowers it): the level must never fall between a raise and its 0x064E ack.' ;;
    ata_linux_probe) echo 'The Linux/98 IDE driver'"'"'s probe (pc98_ide.c, pc98ide_init + pc98ide_identify), which boots on the real Xe10, replayed port for port against the REAL pc9821_ata -> pc9821_block_share -> pc9821_block_io stack with the one disk image mounted at bank 0 (the core'"'"'s fixed position, bank 0'"'"'s master, so pc98ide_init'"'"'s own `outb(0, PC98IDE_BANK)` reaches it unchanged): the bank select, SRST through 0x74C, then for unit 0 the 0xE0 select, four alternate-status reads, STATUS neither 0x00 nor 0xFF, IDENTIFY with STATUS non-zero, DRQ and words 60/61 = the image'"'"'s capacity; and for unit 1 the 0xF0 select, which must read STATUS = ALTSTATUS = 0x00 (ATA-3 r6 s.9.5.1, an absent device 1), so the driver gives the unit up - and an IDENTIFY written anyway must leave STATUS 0x00 with no DRQ and no IRQ (a command for the absent device is not executed), EXECUTE DEVICE DIAGNOSTIC written with device 1 selected is still executed by device 0 (IRQ, ERROR 0x01), and re-selecting 0xE0 must show the master'"'"'s 0x50 and serve a READ SECTORS of the image'"'"'s own data. FAIL-FIRST: on ffd1ae9d the absent slave answered 0x50 and IDENTIFY returned the master'"'"'s page, which is the phantom hdb Linux saw on the MiSTer.' ;;
    ata_multiple) echo 'ATA multiple-block mode, which NP2kai'"'"'s IDENTIFY page advertises (word 47 = 0x8080) and the xe10 IDE option ROM drives: SET MULTIPLE MODE accepts exactly 2/4/8/16/32/64/128 and ABRTs any other count, READ MULTIPLE serves its sectors byte-exact while raising the data interrupt once per negotiated BLOCK, and WRITE MULTIPLE (0xC5) drains its sectors byte-exact with the reference'"'"'s block-end cadence (four sectors in a block of 2 raise two interrupts, because the final sector always interrupts). It also pins the model'"'"'s streaming limit and the reason IDENTIFY word 47 withdraws the multiple advertisement: a host that reads a block without checking DRQ, as the Xe10 ROM does, sees 0x00 while the fetch stalls.' ;;
    ata_range) echo 'A data command (READ SECTORS 0x20) is accepted exactly when the image holds every sector it names, which is ATA'"'"'s own rule and the bench'"'"'s own 64-bit arithmetic, never a value read out of the RTL: the sector count (0 meaning 256) fits between the start LBA and the capacity (start + count <= capacity, so capacity 0 accepts nothing), in the LBA28 form for starts at 0, capacity-2, capacity-1, capacity, capacity+1 and 2^28-1 and counts 1, 2, 255 and 256 over capacities from 0 to 2^32-1 (the 2^28 and 2^31 classes included, whose starts the LBA28 field cannot name past 2^28-1), and in the CHS form of the default 8-head/17-sector geometry for sectors 0, 1, 17 and 18 and heads 7 and 8 around a capacity boundary, an invalid sector or head being refused whatever the capacity; an accepted command issues its block request at the LBA the task file named and a refused one answers STATUS 0x41 with ERROR 0x04 and issues none.' ;;
    ata_readahead) echo 'Sector read-ahead across a modelled HPS round trip: with a block adapter that takes a realistic fetch latency before its 256-word stream, a multi-sector READ SECTORS overlaps sector N+1'"'"'s fetch with sector N'"'"'s PIO drain, so the per-sector cost is the longer of the two terms rather than their sum -- 4856 cycles (5.3 MB/s) with the measured 4600-cycle fetch against the current class table'"'"'s 1536-cycle drain, where the serialized FSM paid 6392 (4.0 MB/s) -- while raising exactly one IRQ per sector and presenting each sector'"'"'s data with DRQ.' ;;
    ata_regs) echo 'Every ATA task-file register read back through its own port (0x0640..0x064E and the alternate status 0x074C), so a swapped pair or a lane permutation inside the ATA module is caught. The historical 16-bit data-read shift lived in pc9821_iobus.sv, which this bench does not build: that seam is guarded only by the integrated user-disk bench.' ;;
    ata_retire_race) echo 'The read-ahead block_done completion landing in the SAME clk the guest reads the last word of the current sector is consumed by exactly ONE handler: the last-word handler owns the collision and the prefetch-latch arm stands down, so the old source-order outcome (two consumers, the latch surviving only because its NBA lost) becomes a folded decision. The completion'"'"'s error record is written once per retire: after the collision prefetch_error equals block_error even when the completion errored (pre-fix abort_command'"'"'s later clear erased the record), and the clean collision swaps exactly one sector (no phantom prefetch_valid, no double buffer flip).' ;;
    ata_srst_race) echo 'An SRST write to 0x074C landing in the same clk_sys cycle as the one-shot block_done completion pulse must not wedge the drive: the drain/prefetch/X_WAIT_BLOCK/X_WAIT_WRITE handlers run before the task-file write handler and retire the pulse, so the SRST arm'"'"'s block_in_flight test sees the pre-consumption state and parks in X_DRAIN to wait for a completion that never recurs. The collision is driven in every consuming state - X_DRAIN, X_WAIT_WRITE, X_PIO_READ with a prefetch in flight, X_WAIT_BLOCK with sectors_left 1 and > 1, and against the SRST deassert write - and after the pair'"'"'s deassert the drive must reach X_IDLE with BSY clear and the post-diagnostic signature within a bounded number of cycles, then still accept the next command.' ;;
    ata_takeover_idle) echo 'TB_PC9821_ATA_TAKEOVER_IDLE: a NON-DATA command (RECALIBRATE 0x10, SET FEATURES 0xEF) written to 0x064E in the same clk as a block_done completion that would enter a DRQ phase - a READ'"'"'s sector fetch (X_WAIT_BLOCK), its read-ahead while the guest drains (X_PIO_READ), and a non-final WRITE sector (X_WAIT_WRITE) - takes over the drive the way NP2kai'"'"'s serialized model does (cbus/ideio.c ideio_o64e dispatches the command against the drive whatever transfer it was in, and RECALIBRATE/SET FEATURES end with `drv->status = IDESTAT_DRDY | IDESTAT_DSC` and no data phase): status reads 0x50 with DRQ clear AND the drive accepts the next command (an IDENTIFY presents word 0 = 0x044A), instead of the retiring transfer'"'"'s DRQ state standing under a non-DRQ status so every later command is ignored until SRST.' ;;
    ata_th5load) echo 'TH5'"'"'s text-box access pattern - one 128x128 dialog face = 21 sectors of CDG image in three file_read-shaped reads plus a scattered directory single and a .TX2 script slice (th05/main/dialog/dialog.cpp:293 + th03/formats/cdg_load.cpp:27, faces 128x128 = 10,240 B/image, th04/sprites/main_cdg.h:24), at both BIOS command granularities (count=1 per sector, count=N per file read) - is served byte-exact with one IRQ per sector and zero aborts by BOTH opcode streams the machine'"'"'s own ROM generates for it: 0x20/0x30 and the without-retry variants 0x21/0x31, whose command byte the ROM computes as (flags & 0xF7)|table[class] with flags bit 0 from the INT 1Bh caller'"'"'s fn bit 5 (BANK3 file 0x03B0-0x03B8), and which NP2kai serves exactly as 0x20/0x30 (cbus/ideio.c:902-923). The two streams must cost the same cycles - an abort routes the firmware into its multi-second recovery (SRST + RECALIBRATE + the BANK3 file 0x01F3 settle of 121*(0x28-[es:0x2060]) ARTIC units = up to 4.03 s) - and the run prices the whole scene per text box and per command in clk_sys and ns at 50 MHz for the machine-target comparison.' ;;
    ata_throughput) echo 'The steady-state DOS file-read pipeline priced per cycle through the real pc9821_ata and pc9821_block_io with a parameterised HPS host model, for 32-sector reads at an insw cadence (one 16-bit word per 6 clk_sys + loop/INT overhead) under three cadences (one READ SECTORS, one READ MULTIPLE, a per-sector-command loop) x latency {0, 50, 200, 4600} x stream cost {1, 8} clk/word: every cycle is sorted into the per-word PIO handshake, the guest'"'"'s inter-sector gap, the command'"'"'s block-fetch stall or the mid-stream read-ahead stall, and the run reports clk/sector and KB/s at 50 MHz. It pins that a streaming read is DRAIN-bound even at the measured 4600-clk HPS latency (under 3000 clk/sector where a handshake-per-512-byte adapter is fetch-bound at 4856 (4600 + the sector'"'"'s 256-word handshake, the bench'"'"'s own arithmetic)), and that the interrupt cadence stays NP2kai'"'"'s (one per sector for READ SECTORS, one per block for READ MULTIPLE) with every word byte-exact.' ;;
    atapi) echo 'TB_PC9821_ATAPI: The CD-ROM answers as bank 1'"'"'s ATAPI MASTER with the REAL pc9821_ata'"'"'s one disk on bank 0, every expected value taken from NP2kai (cbus/ideio.c, cbus/atapicmd.c), from the Xe10'"'"'s own IDE ROM or from the image the host model serves, never from the RTL: the IDE ROM'"'"'s probe of bank 1'"'"'s master (BANK3@0x0A0A) passes step by step - the pre-reset presence test (STATUS 0x00, the 0xEB/0x14 signature marking the unit ATAPI, ERROR 0x01), IDENTIFY DEVICE ending ERR+ABRT, the ready poll with its SRST-and-retry, and a PACKET INQUIRY whose byte 0 is 5 - so the ROM records a CD-ROM; device 0 on bank 1 reads the signature 0x01/0x01/0x14/0xEB with STATUS 0x00 after DEVICE RESET (ERROR 0x01, an interrupt) and 0x51/ERROR 0x01 after an SRST pulse, device 1 on bank 1 is an absent slave (STATUS and ALTERNATE STATUS 0x00, a command for it not executed), and bank 0'"'"'s master is the hard disk; IDENTIFY DEVICE aborts with the signature and IDENTIFY PACKET DEVICE returns word 0 = 0x8580 and the model "NEC CD-ROM DRIVE:98"; a PACKET command shows the CoD phase (0x644 = 0x01, DRQ, no interrupt), INQUIRY returns NP2kai'"'"'s 36 bytes ("NEC     CD-ROM DRIVE:98 1.0 ", type 5, removable) in a data phase whose 0x644/0x648/0x64A say IO and the byte count, then completes with 0x50 and 0x644 = 0x03; TEST UNIT READY with no medium and the first one after a mount answer CHECK with NOT READY / 0x3A in REQUEST SENSE (0x28 once NEC mode is on via MODE SENSE page 0x0F), the next one GOOD; READ CAPACITY returns the sector count and 2048; READ(10) streams each 2048-byte sector of an ISO and of a PCD (from the raw sector'"'"'s MODE1 user data) through the slot'"'"'s block handshake with one interrupt and byte count 2048 per sector, and a read past the end answers ILLEGAL REQUEST / 0x21; READ TOC format 0 returns the descriptors (LBA, MSF and NEC BCD forms) of a one-track ISO and of a three-track PCD header, lead-out 0xAA; MODE SENSE page 0x2A returns NP2kai'"'"'s page with medium type 0x03 for a data+audio disc; PLAY AUDIO MSF streams the audio sectors'"'"' {R,L} samples into the CD-DA port and READ SUB-CHANNEL reports playing/paused/completed with the current position, which wanders by NP2kai'"'"'s rand() & 7 frames while paused or completed; and PATACD.SYS'"'"'s unit probe (bank 0/1, master/slave, IDENTIFY PACKET with the 0xEC fallback, word 0 bits 15:14 = 10, INQUIRY type 5, polled with nIEN, NOP after the packet command) finds the CD at bank 1'"'"'s master, reads an all-zero identify page for the absent slave beside the disk, and finds nothing at the other three units; and the OSD'"'"'s Eject CD-ROM (pc9821_mount_eject'"'"'s size-0 event on slot 4, status[11] as the --cdrom top wires it) leaves no medium - TEST UNIT READY answers NOT READY / 0x3A every time and MODE SENSE reports medium type 0x70 - and a re-mount answers NOT READY / 0x28 once (NEC mode), then GOOD, and reads again; and a READ(10) whose block request waits while hps_io streams ANOTHER slot'"'"'s words takes none of them; and a PLAY AUDIO MSF issued while the previous play'"'"'s audio sector is still being fetched streams its own first sector whole and completes at its own end (NP2kai'"'"'s synchronous playaudio: the superseded fetch'"'"'s completion does not advance the new play); and with nIEN set a drive RELEASES INTRQ (ATA-3 Device Control: high impedance) so an unacknowledged CD request cannot hide the disk'"'"'s next IRQ9 edge on the ORed line (MAME pc9801.cpp ideirq INPUT_MERGER_ANY_HIGH), while the request itself follows NP2kai'"'"'s ideio.c - a completion under nIEN neither raises nor clears it and a status read under nIEN does not acknowledge it. 0x0433 reads 00 at bank 1 with nothing pending (the Xe10'"'"'s `433=00`, not NP2kai ideio_i433'"'"'s connected-device 0x02), and the CD'"'"'s pending interrupt is its bit 1 at either bank selection until the status read (qemu-pc98 irq_levels). The MSF converter is pinned at its 4500-frame boundary: a mount of N sectors makes the converter'"'"'s frame count N + 150 (atapicmd.c'"'"'s 150-frame lead-in), so media of 4349/4350/4351 and 4424/4425/4426 sectors put the minute compare one frame below, exactly on and one frame above its edge and the seconds step'"'"'s 74/75/76-frame tail there, and READ TOC MSF/BCD must answer 00:59:74, 01:00:00, 01:00:01, 01:00:74, 01:01:00 and 01:01:01. The CD-DA player reads ahead: behind a host that takes 20 ms per slot-4 request (longer than the 13.3 ms one sector lasts) an 800 ms play reaches the real src/soc/cdda.v ring'"'"'s output whole, every sample in order and equal to the disc with no silent sample inside it; READ SUB-CHANNEL reports NP2kai'"'"'s dacurpos, the next sector its mixer loads - one past the sector being heard, exactly lba0 + ceil(k / 588) after k samples have reached the speakers (ideio.c playdevaudio increments dacurpos as it loads a sector, before mixing it) - and reports completed only once every sample has left the ring; PAUSE and STOP silence the output at once, a paused drive reports that same position plus NP2kai'"'"'s rand() & 7, and RESUME continues from the start of the sector that was being heard with no gap. A sector finishing in the very cycle of a PAUSE is merged into the held position (one past it) and one finishing in a STOP'"'"'s cycle is discarded with the play (position 0). Another disc mounted over a playing one stops the play at once as NP2kai'"'"'s mixer does when the medium goes away (ideio.c playdevaudio: daflag 0x14): no sample of the old disc reaches the speakers after the mount, no sector of the new disc is fetched, READ SUB-CHANNEL answers 0x14 at NP2kai'"'"'s position, and the TEST UNIT READY that reports the new disc stops the player (0x13, position 0). A core reset in the middle of a play stops it as NP2kai'"'"'s ideio_reset does on every machine reset (cbus/cbuscore.c resetfn[]; devinit daflag 0x15): the speakers go silent at once, no audio sector is fetched, the disc stays mounted and READ SUB-CHANNEL answers 0x15 with no position. A play'"'"'s first sector completing in the very cycle of a PAUSE, or of another disc'"'"'s mount over the playing one, counts as loaded, as NP2kai'"'"'s dacurpos counts a sector the moment its mixer reads it (ideio.c playdevaudio): READ SUB-CHANNEL then reports one past it - 0x12 at lba0 + 1 + rand() & 7 with the lowest of 32 reports exactly lba0 + 1, and 0x14 at exactly lba0 + 1 - never lba0. A PAUSED play survives an eject and a different disc as it does in NP2kai (ideio_notify touches only the media flags; atapi_cmd_pauseresume tests only daflag 0x12): READ SUB-CHANNEL still answers 0x12 at the same position after the remount, a RESUME before any TEST UNIT READY answers GOOD and 0x11 and streams the NEW disc'"'"'s samples from the paused sector, and the TEST UNIT READY that reports the new disc then stops the player (0x13, position 0). With NO disc in the drive (an eject clears NP2kai'"'"'s READY flag) a RESUME of that pause, or a PLAY, answers GOOD with NO SENSE (atapi_cmd_pauseresume / playaudio_sub cmddone) and the play stops with 0x14, not 0x13, as NP2kai'"'"'s playdevaudio stops it when the device is not READY: READ SUB-CHANNEL answers CHECK with the sense untouched while no disc is in, no sample is streamed, and after a remount it answers 0x14 at exactly the paused dacurpos (or the PLAY'"'"'s start) with no rand(), a further RESUME changes nothing, and the TEST UNIT READY that reports the disc stops the player (0x13, position 0). A single-track image of raw 2352-byte sectors with no PCD header - the ECMA-130 sync at its start and a size that is a whole number of sectors, NP2kai'"'"'s openiso/issec2352 image - mounts as one data track of size / 2352 sectors: READ CAPACITY answers that count and 2048, READ TOC one data track (0x14) with the lead-out at it, and READ(10) returns each sector'"'"'s user bytes 16 bytes in (sec2352_read), or 24 in for MODE2 form 1 (after the CD-ROM XA subheader); the expected bytes are the image'"'"'s own user data by construction of the ECMA-130 layout; a file that starts with the sync but is not a whole number of 2352-byte sectors reads as an ISO (size / 2048 sectors). A disc change in the middle of a READ(10) fails it as NP2kai'"'"'s synchronous read does once the old disc is ejected (atapicmd.c atapi_dataread: sxsi_read fails, `drv->asc = 0x21; senderror(drv);`): a change during a data phase lets the buffered sector of the old disc drain whole and then answers CHECK, ILLEGAL REQUEST / 0x21 with no sector of the new disc, and a change while a sector'"'"'s fetch is at the host presents no data phase and answers the same.' ;;
    atapi_equiv) echo 'The CD-ROM drive src/pc9821/storage/pc9821_atapi.sv answers every packet command with exactly the values the drive of 284a2442 answered (tests/pc9821/pc9821_atapi_ref.sv, a byte-for-byte copy of it under another name, a golden model in the way pc9821_egc_ref.sv is): the same pseudo-random stream of ATA-level and packet commands is run through both drives, each behind its own hps_io slot model serving the same ISO, PCD or no medium, and after EVERY command the two traces must be equal - the packet-phase status, the interrupt-reason and byte-count registers of every data phase, every data byte, the completion STATUS, ERROR, sector count and cylinder registers. The stream covers TEST UNIT READY, REQUEST SENSE, INQUIRY, READ CAPACITY, READ(10) in and out of range, MODE SENSE(10) of every page code, 0x3F and an unknown one in both the default and the changeable form at allocation lengths from 0 to 255 (page 0x0F switching the drive to BCD MSF), READ TOC in formats 0 and 1 in LBA and MSF with every start track, READ SUB-CHANNEL, GET CONFIGURATION, START/STOP, SEEK, PLAY AUDIO(10) and MSF, PAUSE/RESUME, MODE SELECT, unknown opcodes, DEVICE RESET, EXECUTE DEVICE DIAGNOSTIC, IDENTIFY PACKET DEVICE and SRST, with the player held still (CDDA_REQ low) so its position is a function of the commands alone, plus plays of a few sectors run to completion with CDDA_REQ high whose streamed {R, L} samples must also be equal; the medium changes between the ISO, the PCD and an eject.' ;;
    audio_clip) echo 'The sound path under a dense worst case -- six full-level drums overlapping with six FM channels at maximum, the SSG at maximum and the 86-PCM voice playing at full scale, with the 0xA46C write-FIFO and the OPNA register queue (WRQ) flooded at the I/O floor cadence -- with three detections running throughout: (a) no dropped write or sample: every host write presented through the real pc9821_iobus master is accepted by the buffer and lands (WRQ entries drained to the chip, 0xA46C bytes stored in SDRAM byte-exact) now that both buffers back-pressure through io_wr_wait instead of silently dropping on full (the pre-fix WRQ lost 94/160 at this cadence and the 0xA46C FIFO dropped past 4 deep under SDRAM contention), with the stall itself counted so the no-drop result cannot be vacuous; (b) the die'"'"'s drum bus: the six-drum sum is recomputed from jt08_adpcm_acc'"'"'s per-drum slot inputs as the die'"'"'s 16-bit two'"'"'s-complement WRAP (YM2608-LLE fmopna_impl.c:5370) and compared with the DUT accumulator every slot; (c) no clipped-vs-expected sample: jt08_adpcm_acc'"'"'s pcm_out must equal that wrapped round sum, never a clamped one (the pre-fix 16-bit clamp there flat-topped dense overlaps before the FM mix -- audible as drums clipping) and the output chain must equal the single-clamp saturating model of the wide FM+ADPCM junction (the ymfm clamp16 / NP2kai _saturation_s16 point) followed by the AC-coupled (zero-mean) SSG and a WIDE join with NO intermediate saturation (datapaths.md F1: the pre-fix tree flat-topped there even when the FM+SSG+PCM86 sum fit in 16 bits; the single clamp is at the final DAC), the over-16 stimulus kept non-vacuous. Phase 1 pins the 0 dB single-drum output to the same model, so the default path stays bit-identical.' ;;
    audio_level_cdc) echo 'TB_PC9821_AUDIO_LEVEL_CDC: the OSD Audio page'"'"'s three 5-bit gain codes (PC-9801-86, Beeper, and CD audio on both channels) cross from clk_sys (90 MHz, base) to CLK_AUDIO (24.576 MHz) as one word through the held cdc_vector_handshake_continuous inside the real pc9821_audio_mix (level_cdc), so with every bit of the clk_sys source codes given its own routing delay (0.3 to 8.3 ns, inside one clk_sys period, the most a same-domain path may take) and each field stepped through every neighbouring pair of its eight entries (31/30/29/28/27/26/25/0, both directions, the 7-to-0 wrap included) hundreds of times at an incommensurate rate, alone and all three at once, the CLK_AUDIO side never holds a code that was not driven in any field - no 28-to-27 step ever shows 31 (0 dB) or 24, no 25-to-0 step a stray gain - a field that did not step never moves, the CD'"'"'s L and R codes never differ, every driven code arrives, every CLK_AUDIO load of the handshake finds its clk_sys hold word stable for longer than that skew, the latency is bounded and printed, and from power-up until the first code crosses the -86 and beeper codes read 31 (0 dB, the level those sources had before the page) and the CD code 0 (Off, as its own crossing powered up).' ;;
    audio_levels) echo 'TB_PC9821_AUDIO_LEVELS: the OSD Audio page'"'"'s three levels reach the mix through the real pc9821_osd decode and the real pc9821_audio_mix (its clk_sys-to-CLK_AUDIO level crossing, the ten-channel volume stage and the one clamp), on clk_sys 90 MHz and CLK_AUDIO 24.576 MHz, and each acts on its own source only: with the PC-9801-86 board, the beeper and CD audio all sounding at once, every one of the eight positions of each field (0 dB to -12 dB in 2 dB steps, Off) scales that source by round(65536 * 10^(-dB/20)) / 65536 (floored, as the Q16 stage takes it) while the other two fields sit at other positions and their sources come out at exactly their own levels, on both channels; Off removes exactly that source (the sum is exactly the other two); the -86 level is in series with pc9821_system'"'"'s vol_voice gain (vol_voice -6 dB and a -N dB level give -(6+N) dB), the guest'"'"'s master gain still scales the whole sum before the clamp, and all 32 gain codes of each source'"'"'s level, driven directly, give that source alone the law'"'"'s gain (the beeper'"'"'s ladder beep_vol * 1024 times it, at each of the three ladder steps).' ;;
    audio_mix) echo 'The PC-9801-86'"'"'s sample reaches AUDIO_L, and the volume stage obeys the law at its three broken points: with the mixer wired exactly as pc9821_audio_mix wires it at the OSD Audio page'"'"'s 0 dB - the same ten-channel sb_volume concat and the same volumes pc9821_system.sv drives, INCLUDING both vol_cd ends - 5'"'"'d31 (the OSD CD audio volume field'"'"'s 0 dB step) and 5'"'"'d0 (its Off step, and the constant without CD audio, pc9821_system.sv CDROM_AUDIO_EN; F6 asked for the muted arm) - a sample on the -86 slot comes out bit-identical at unity through the real pc9821_audio_volume, volume code 0 is TRUE MUTE (a full-scale CD-DA sample at vol_cd 0 contributes EXACTLY zero through the module and at mix_dry, both polarities - NP2kai volume-0 is exact 0, sound/beepg.c:161-163), and MASTER SCALING LANDS BEFORE THE FINAL SATURATION (NP2kai scales every source by vol_master pre-sum, sound/fmboard.c:103-133): at master code 28 (-6 dB) the over-rail sum +40000 comes out +20047 = floor(40000*32846/2^16) UNSATURATED and -40000 comes out -20048, where the old post-clamp master emitted +16422/-16423 of clipped audio turned down. It also pins the final DAC'"'"'s arithmetic (datapaths.md F1/F2): spk + the -86 stream + CD sum WIDE (20-bit, no wrap) and saturate ONCE at mix_dry on the master-scaled sum - a positive overflow clamps to 0x7FFF (the pre-fix 17-bit mix_tmp wrapped and the saturator emitted 0x8000: wrong rail AND polarity), the sum swept through 65530..65545 stays on the +rail across the wrap boundary, a large negative clamps to 0x8000, and a wide (-86-scale) sample_86 crosses pc9821_audio_volume unclipped at unity to be clipped exactly once at the DAC.' ;;
    audio_mix_equiv) echo 'TB_PC9821_AUDIO_MIX_EQUIV: with the OSD Audio page'"'"'s PC-9801-86 and Beeper levels at 0 dB (gain code 31, position 0, what a card with no config file and a config saved before the page both select), pc9821_audio_mix is clock-for-clock and bit-for-bit the CLK_AUDIO mix the top level carried before the page existed (pc9821_audio_mix_ref.sv, z486_mister.sv'"'"'s vol_cd_cdc, speaker block, audio_volume_inst and mix_tmp/mix_dry block as of 105f3263), from the first CLK_AUDIO edge after power-up: on clk_sys 90 MHz and CLK_AUDIO 24.576 MHz, with random 20-bit -86 samples, random CD-DA samples, a random speaker tone and 0x871E ladder code on every CLK_AUDIO clock, random vol_voice/vol_master/vol_midi codes, and the CD audio code stepped at random clk_sys times through every code, the valid strobe agrees on every clock and both clamped outputs agree on every clock; so the page adds no latency and changes no sample at its defaults.' ;;
    audio_out_alias) echo 'A 45 kHz tone carried on the mixer'"'"'s 2.4576 MHz grid - above the 48 kHz presentation'"'"'s 24 kHz Nyquist, so it MUST be removed and not folded - comes out of the pre-fix output stage (a bare register loaded on audio_volume_valid, the stage it replaced) folded onto 3 kHz at the tone'"'"'s own level, and comes out of pc9821_audio_out at <= -60 dB relative: the bare-latch arm measures the fold at about 0 dB (the fold is real and the stimulus is non-vacuous) while the filtered arm'"'"'s 5/32 polyphase prototype holds a 28 kHz stopband at -100 dB by design, so the aliasing that a bare strobe cannot avoid is removed instead of folded - and an in-band 3 kHz tone at the same drive on the right channel passes at 0 dB +-0.5 dB, so the stopband cannot be met by a stage that attenuates everything.' ;;
    audio_out_equiv) echo 'The 48 kHz output stage'"'"'s area rewrite (the FIR MAC engine accumulating straight from the multiplier into a 34-bit DSP accumulator instead of through a 34-bit product register and a 42-bit logic adder) is cycle-for-cycle identical to the pre-rewrite stage (pc9821_audio_out_ref.sv, the three modules as of 3b134fe4 with the clamp'"'"'s rail taken from the true sum'"'"'s sign, as in the engine): over random, full-scale, impulse, step, tone and silent mixer streams with the right channel changing off the valid edge, AUDIO_L/R, the CIC'"'"'s output strobe and samples, and the stage-B and stage-C start and done strobes match on every CLK_AUDIO cycle.' ;;
    audio_out_rate) echo 'The 48 kHz output stage presents one new AUDIO_L/R sample per 512 CLK_AUDIO cycles - the framework'"'"'s own DAC rate (24 576 000/48 000 = 512) with a bare 512-cycle cadence and no 511-cycle drift (sys/audio_out.sv'"'"'s own sample_ce runs at 24 576 000/511, which is the framework'"'"'s, not this core'"'"'s), and it holds that cadence for a worst-case changing input: the register changes on every presentation, never 10 cycles apart as the pre-fix code did (measured 10 before the fix and re-measured here), with the value stable in between for the framework'"'"'s two-consecutive-edges-agree latch (sys/audio_out.sv:170 (cl2 == cl1)).' ;;
    audio_out_sat) echo 'The 48 kHz output stage'"'"'s FIR clamp saturates toward the sign of the TRUE sum (the meaning of saturation: ymfm'"'"'s clamp16, ymfm_opn.cpp:1410-1414, and NP2kai'"'"'s _saturation_s16 junction): on every stage-B (19-tap) and stage-C (247-tap polyphase) run of pc9821_audio_out, the 16-bit output equals floor(acc / 2^17) clamped to [-32768, +32767], so an accumulator above the positive range gives exactly +32767 and one below the negative range exactly -32768 (the pre-fix engine picked the rail from bit 32, which on an overflow is the inverted sign, and turned a +32767 overshoot into -32768), over a full-scale square, a half-to-full-scale square and the Policenauts CD track-3 plateau (54 disc samples around track-3 sample 3668679, L = 32767, at 44.1 kHz on the 2.4576 MHz grid, three 44.1/48 kHz phases) with the right channel the negation of the left; and an input held inside [+16384, +32767] (or its negation) never yields an output of the opposite sign at stage B, stage C or AUDIO_L/R (the shipped tables bound the output above +4485 for such an input).' ;;
    audio_out_timeline) echo 'Two source staircases at their real rates - the OPNA'"'"'s 55 467 Hz DAC rate (7.9872 MHz/144) on the left and 44 100 Hz (PCM86 and CD-DA) on the right - come out of pc9821_audio_out at the SAME frequency they went in (measured within 0.5%), so the output stage'"'"'s 256/5 decimation is a resampling of real time and not a playback-rate change: the pre-fix stage'"'"'s 2.4576 MHz register write rate is 51.2x the framework'"'"'s 48 kHz DAC rate, and reading that ratio as '"'"'FM is 16% fast, PCM is 8% slow'"'"' is what this bench refutes - the measured pitch is 1.0000x for both sources, and a stage that played either source at the wrong rate (a 51.2x displacement, or the 511/512 drift sys/audio_out.sv'"'"'s own sample_ce carries) fails here by name.' ;;
    audio_volume_equiv) echo 'The ten-channel volume stage'"'"'s area rewrite (each consumed channel'"'"'s product captured once per epoch into its own field instead of shifted through a ten-deep output chain, and the sample and volume selects built over the consumed channels only) delivers, on every valid strobe, the same samples_out fields as the pre-rewrite stage (pc9821_audio_volume_ref.sv, the module as of 3b134fe4) for every consumed channel, and the same valid strobe on every clock: over random 20-bit samples and random volume codes that change on EVERY clock (so the instants each channel is sampled at are pinned, not only the values), for the all-channels-consumed configuration (every field) and for the configuration z486_mister.sv instantiates (master, -86 and CD fields).' ;;
    audio_volume_table) echo 'The 32-entry volume gain table, read from its 32x17 M10K ROM (the true-zero table moved from a LUT-selected packed vector into a synchronous ROM), produces the exact documented 17-bit gain for every one of the 32 volume codes 0..31 - code 0 = 17'"'"'h00000 (true mute), code 31 = 17'"'"'h10000 (unity), the -60 dB..-2 dB ladder bit-identical to the packed vector it replaced - and the guarded wire drives the DSP multiply: at unity a 0x4000 sample passes through unchanged and at code 0 it contributes exactly zero.' ;;
    audio_volume_x) echo 'The 10-channel master mix'"'"'s channel sequencer never X-locks: `ch`, `gain`, `sample`, `samples_out` and `valid` are seeded, so in 4-state simulation the self-fed counter resolves instead of poisoning the whole audio path with X forever (the same self-fed shape as crtc'"'"'s stat_dr, hidden by Verilator'"'"'s 2-state zero-init; run under iverilog for the fail-first X-lock), valid pulses exactly once per NUM_CH clks, and NUM_CH unity-gain constant channels assemble into samples_out with channel 0 at bits [15:0] in channel order.' ;;
    beeper) echo 'TB_PC9821_BEEPER: The machine'"'"'s own power-on beep is audible, and each half of it is the machine'"'"'s: the TONE is the PIT'"'"'s CHANNEL-1 mode-3 square wave, programmed through the 0x3FDF/0x3FDB port alias exactly as the firmware'"'"'s own beep routine programs it (BANK4@0x4B2B: control 0x76 = channel 1, LSB then MSB, mode 3, then divisor 0x04CD = 1229 low and high) and measuring 2.4576 MHz divided by the divisor = 2000 Hz, and the GATE is system port C bit 3 used as a MUTE, so the 8255 bit-set/reset form on 0x37 with 0x06 makes it audible and 0x07 silences it, a whole-register write on 0x35 does the same, and the sound stops the moment the bit is set even though the counter is still running; and channel 2 is shown NOT to be the beeper, so the routing that put the beep on the RS-232C channel'"'"'s divisor is caught. And the 0x871E software-DIP BEEPER VOLUME (bits [3:2]) actually scales the speaker amplitude: writing each code through the real I/O path yields the 0/1024/2048/3072 ladder through pc9821_audio_mix'"'"'s own amplitude stage at the OSD Beeper level'"'"'s 0 dB - linear in the code with 0 = EXACT mute (NP2kai io/dipsw.c:44-57 sets BEEP_VOL from (value>>2)&3, sound/beepg.c:113-114 scales `samp *= vol`, sound/beepg.c:161-163 goes silent at 0), the unit pinned so code 2 - NP2kai'"'"'s default '"'"'Mid'"'"' (pccore.c:847) - is the historical 2,048, and the exported beep_vol port carries exactly the written bits.' ;;
    bios_model) echo 'The Machine field reaches port 0xF0'"'"'s four-bit machine-ID strap LUT and nowhere else: set 0 (the config-less default) reads the MEASURED PC-9821Xe10 strap 0xA9, set 1 (PC-9821Xe) reads 0x89 (the same byte with bit 5 clear, the branch the Xe ITF needs to shadow the option ROM), and the reserved sets 2..15 read the measured Xe10 0xA9, while the neighbouring 0xF1 and 0xF2 straps stay put for every set (0xF1 open bus 0xFF; 0xF2 is the A20 read and answers 0xFF, its masked power-on state, tracking the latch per NP2kai cpuio_if2 as tb_pc9821_iobus pins). The set field is O[31:28], NOT the status[5]/status[6] pair the DDR framebuffer'"'"'s fb_fmt assignment read until that descriptor was deleted - the reason the field moved; the OSD contract gate'"'"'s field-disjointness check lives in tools/check_osd_contract.py. Without a non-Xe10 answer the authentic Xe set never runs its IDE option ROM (ATA cmds=0) and the guest runs away.' ;;
    bios_select) echo 'The Machine field applies ONLY at machine reset / reload: after a reset latches the Xe10 set, port 0xF0 reads the measured 0xA9, and a mid-run OSD flip to the Xe set (or any reserved set) leaves the strap at 0xA9 on every later read and across the warm CPU-reset class (out 0xF0, 0x534, a z486 shutdown); only the next machine reset / reload re-samples the field and moves the strap to the Xe byte 0x89, and the reset after that restores 0xA9 with the OSD back on the config-less default. The strap must be stable for the whole boot because the two ITFs read its bit 5 differently (the Xe10 masks bits 5 and 6 together at BANK4@0xF8207, the Xe tests bit 5 alone at BANK4@0xF81DF). Fail-first: on the old live-apply wiring the mid-run flip reads 0x89 immediately and this bench fails at step (b). And the OSD GDC Clock field (port 0x31 bit 7 - the machine'"'"'s own GDC 2.5/5 MHz strap, 0xF3 with bit 7 set at 2.5 MHz, 0x73 at 5 MHz, the BIOS sampling it once at POST at BIOS.ROM@0xFD93C and deriving the games'"'"' work-area 0x54D bit 2 from it) follows the identical contract on its own byte: entry 0 answers the measured 0xF3, a mid-run flip and the warm CPU-reset class move it not at all, and only the next machine reset applies 0x73 - each step FAIL-FIRST against a live-apply wiring, which answers 0x73 immediately in step (d2). And the ITF'"'"'s own software-DIP-switch verify is transcribed whole (BANK4@0xFC6BA-0xFC726: the direct odd-parity pass over bank A'"'"'s 0x84/0x86-0x88/0x89^0x8A/0x8B-0x8F, the port-0x31 MERGE for 0x851E, and bank B'"'"'s ten direct bytes) and passes at BOTH straps, because index 1'"'"'s bit 4 is the DIP-2 register'"'"'s parity bit and the machine-reset walk SEEDS it from the reset-qualified strap latch over the measured 0x02 byte; FAIL-FIRST, because with bit 4 stored the 5 MHz merge (0x73 & 0xEF) | (0x02 & 0x10) = 0x63 is EVEN, the ITF'"'"'s repair path cannot own the bit, and its SET THE SOFTWARE DIP-SWITCH printer (BANK4@0xFC8DE-0xFC903) writes 0xF0 = 0x00 - a reboot loop on this core. Step (e3) pins the SHAPE of that fix at both straps: after a machine reset - the store'"'"'s restore walk, now the ONLY restore, the OSD `Reset DIPs` action this step used to name having been removed - the store holds the MEASURED 0x02 data - the machine'"'"'s own bank A image, docs/probes/PORTS.TXT:258 - with bit 4 seeded, so 0x851E reads 0x12 with 5 MHz selected and 0x02 at the machine'"'"'s own 2.5 MHz, a recovery route that does not work on hardware. And port 0xF0 bit 5 is IDE unit 0'"'"'s drive-present line: the ITF builds its IDE drive bitmap and the 0x457 byte from bits 5/6 (BANK4@0x19DF-0x19F2, BANK4@0x1B6C-0x1B8F), so with a drive on bank 0 (the hard disk, bank 0'"'"'s master in the core'"'"'s fixed layout) the measured 0xA9 reads 0x89 and returns to 0xA9 without it (step f).' ;;
    block_hostcost) echo 'The host-facing cost of the disk path through the REAL stack the top level wires (pc9821_ata -> pc9821_block_share -> the shared pc9821_block_io engine) counted in the terms the ARM actually pays: HANDSHAKES per sector, 256-word transfers per sector and read-ahead window flushes, for seven realistic guest patterns (a 64-sector READ SECTORS, the DOS per-sector-command read loop, a 64-sector WRITE SECTORS, the per-sector-command write loop, an 8-page EMM386 swap pattern in both command shapes, and the per-sector read loop with a floppy read interleaved every four sectors), with a parameterised host model that charges the measured request-to-ack round trip, the ARM'"'"'s per-word stream cost and -- for a write -- the FileSeek/FileWriteAdv the ARM performs AFTER its word loop (O_SYNC on the OSD'"'"'s writable image, user_io.cpp:2151). It prints clk/sector, stall/sector, handshakes/sector and words/sector for every pattern, proves each sector'"'"'s data is byte-exact in both directions, and pins that a sequential read stays at ~0.2 handshakes/sector, that the per-sector-command read loop keeps its read-ahead run across the EMM386 swap pattern'"'"'s page writes (the bench FAILS at 24 read handshakes for 64 read sectors before that change and PASSES at 10), that a floppy read interleaved between every four disk sectors costs the disk NEITHER its read-ahead window NOR its handshake amortisation (the bench FAILS at 24 disk handshakes and 15 window losses before the window carried its image'"'"'s tag, and PASSES at 6 with none), that a floppy-only sequential read is one handshake per block (the bench FAILS at 3 handshakes, 1 of them multi-block), and that the write direction is one host handshake per sector by construction - the drive'"'"'s buffer holds one sector - with the ARM'"'"'s own FileSeek/FileWriteAdv of the 512 bytes sitting between one handshake and the next, which the bench sweeps (wr_tail 0 / 1500 / 20000 clk) because that is the only term fewer, larger handshakes could remove. It also runs a BURST-WIDTH SWEEP: pc9821_block_io instances at BURST_SECTORS 1/2/4/8 execute the same 64-sector stream and a mid-tail pattern break under the same host model at word rates 1 and 18 clk/word (18 = 360 ns/word, the rate the 2.9 ms burst-tail claim rests on) and at a no-ARM-cache stress latency, pinning the handshake law 2 + ceil(62/width), the word law 256 x (2 + width x ceil(62/width)) and the pattern-break stall (width-1) x 256 words of tail at the word rate - the laws the width-16/32 [ESTIMATED] figures extrapolate from, because the engine width check refuses to build those.' ;;
    block_io) echo 'The MiSTer image metadata and SD-block handshake: an unmounted request fails immediately, metadata is latched, the request reaches hps_io, the buffer streams through, sd_rd clears on acknowledge, completion pulses with status, and a short block is rejected; words hps_io streams for ANOTHER slot'"'"'s transfer while this request waits for its own acknowledge are ignored. And a remount empties the read-ahead window: after a third sequential read bursts 8 sectors into it, a window sector is replayed with no handshake and carries the image'"'"'s words, and after the slot is remounted the same LBA goes back to hps_io and returns the new image'"'"'s words.' ;;
    block_share) echo 'The hard disk and the floppy share ONE pc9821_block_io engine instead of owning one each, and this bench pins what that changes: a request presented while the engine is serving the other device is QUEUED and completed rather than dropped (the disk asks with a one-cycle pulse, the floppy with a level held until b_busy takes it, both modelled from their own sources), each device receives exactly its own image'"'"'s 256 words per block in order and nothing else or extra (also while hps_io streams ANOTHER slot'"'"'s transfer as the request waits for its own acknowledge), only one slot'"'"'s request ever reaches the host, and neither device can hold the engine for more than a few consecutive transfers. Both images deliberately overlap in LBA space (both devices read blocks 0..7 contended) and the window phase makes each device read blocks the other just cached into the read-ahead window, so a wrong-slot fetch or a stale window hit is a byte mismatch named by block and word - while the DISK'"'"'s own window must SURVIVE the floppy'"'"'s hand-over, so a disk block inside the burst it just fetched is replayed from the window with no host handshake at all (the bench FAILS at one handshake there while a hand-over still flushed the window). FAIL-FIRST: with the queue removed (the named noqueue variant) the request presented while the other device owns the engine is dropped and the phase reports sent/received counts.' ;;
    boot0_load) echo 'TB_PC9821_BOOT0_LOAD: the firmware pack sent as games/PC9821/boot0.rom at core start lands with Main_MiSTer'"'"'s own sequence (release 20261010, commit 738cb7a): status[0] set before the transfer (user_io.cpp:1613) and held across the whole ioctl download (index 0, user_io.cpp:1684), released only after it (user_io.cpp:1774). Through the real pc9821_system (its pc9821_memory_subsystem, pc9821_rom_loader and pc9821_cpu_reset_gate; the CPU is tests/pc9821/z486_stub.sv) with the top level'"'"'s reset synchroniser and the SDRAM backend'"'"'s reset as z486_mister.sv wires them, and a backend that is busy for its initialisation after every reset, every word of the pack reaches SDRAM, the pack is accepted (rom_ready high, load_error low) before status[0] falls, the CPU stays in reset until status[0] falls and leaves it after, and the backend is reset exactly once (at status[0]'"'"'s rise, before the download) so its initialisation is not re-run when status[0] falls. Regressions: a later OSD Reset (a status[0] pulse with no download) resets the backend once, keeps the pack (rom_ready stays high, no re-load needed) and releases the CPU after the controller'"'"'s initialisation; an OSD load of a second pack (status[0] low, menu.cpp:2752) takes the CPU into reset, rewrites the pack without resetting the backend and releases the CPU at its end. FAIL-FIRST: on the tree before the change (bench built with -DPRE_BOOT0_LOAD, the backend on the plain platform reset) the loader is held in the platform reset for the whole boot-ROM download, no word lands and rom_ready never rises.' ;;
    cache_flush) echo 'Every flush trigger through the real pc9821_iobus write path collapses to exactly ONE whole-L1 cache_flush request to the core - a one-cycle cache_flush_req pulse, released before the next launch - and the write completes only after the core answers cache_flush_done: the 0x043F=0xA0 guest flush, a bank or shadow change, an ems_state change (the 0x043F 0x2x writes AND the silent ems_state <= 0 in the other 0x043F writes), a 0x043B sys16m remap, a 0x0461 window-0 change, a 0x053D gate change and a 0x063C select that changes the 0xD8000 window'"'"'s bank each pulse exactly once, while a redundant no-change write pulses nothing and no request is ever issued while the core'"'"'s walk is busy.' ;;
    calendar) echo 'Both uPD4990A/4993A command forms. The serial form is the one the authentic BIOS uses: `send_serial` replays the exact 0x20 byte stream of its send routine (BANK7@0x5DA2, field held at 7, four opcode bits LSB-first on DATA with a CLK each, then an STB pulse) and the clock is read back through the firmware'"'"'s own 0x33/shr/rcr loop. Also the parallel form'"'"'s LSB-first bit order (BANK7@0x5D34), the reset and measured register values, and the tick-clear behaviour. The 4993A four-bit prefix vs suffix is an open question and is not settled here; the read uses the module'"'"'s documented zero prefix.' ;;
    calendar_protocol) echo 'The uPD4990A/4993A serial protocol against NP2kai io/upd4990.c and the machine'"'"'s own captured stream (docs/probes/TRS.TXT, /TIMEREF and /TIMESET): the 48 payload bits come out first and are followed by the 0x01 the reference loads into reg[1] (the machine'"'"'s 52-bit captures end 1,0,0,0), a register-hold (opcode 0) DISARMS the output walk, and the serial opcode is decoded as the full four bits (NP2kai io/upd4990.c:26), so 0xA the 30-second interrupt command does not alias TIMESET. All three pass on the fixed RTL.' ;;
    calendar_rollover) echo 'The calendar'"'"'s own counters carry like a real date, read back through the ITF'"'"'s own reader: the ITF clocks exactly 48 bits (BANK7@0x5D22, for each of 6 bytes 8 x in al,0x33 / shr al,1 / rcr ah,1) and skips nothing, so the seconds byte is the first byte out. The bench sets a boundary time through the framework load, advances exactly one second across the calendar'"'"'s own 1 Hz divider, and reads the six fields back, asserting the second, minute, hour, day, month and year carries plus BCD validity - including 2024-02-28 becoming 02-29 while 2026-02-28 becomes 03-01, which a fixed 28-day February satisfies only by being wrong about a quarter of all years. It also reaches the three months past September, where the month|weekday byte'"'"'s high nibble must carry the month'"'"'s DECIMAL value: October, November and December emit 0xA/0xB/0xC, which slicing the packed-BCD month'"'"'s low nibble turned into 0/1/2. It deliberately runs with the mode latch'"'"'s bit 5 CLEAR, where this module emits the payload starting at bit 0, because that is the alignment the machine'"'"'s own capture shows (docs/probes/TRS.TXT reports the seconds-field alignment as payload at bit 0, no prefix); the EXTENDED mode'"'"'s alignment is a separate defect and is OUT OF SCOPE here - this file neither encodes nor prints it.' ;;
    calendar_timeset) echo 'The uPD4990A TIMESET command (opcode 2) loads the counters from the 48-bit input stream the machine'"'"'s own writer sends - bios/xe10/BANK7.BIN@0x5D62 (and docs/probes/TRS.TXT running it), opcode 1 SHIFT, 48 data bits, opcode 2 - and the time reads back through the ITF'"'"'s own 48-clock reader unchanged. The payload bits are presented the way that writer presents them: the 3-bit field is held at 7, the serial command channel, with only bit 5 (DATA) changing (0x07/0x27), so an input accumulator gated on the field drops every payload bit and the time never loads. It pins the two field decodes the old code got wrong: the month lives in the HIGH nibble of the month|weekday byte at [39:36] (tested against its own 1..12 range, not the year'"'"'s low BCD digit at [43:40]), so a write is no longer dropped when the year ends in 0; and the month is stored and re-emitted as its DECIMAL value (NP2kai'"'"'s `(month << 4) | week`), so October, November and December round-trip as 0xA/0xB/0xC where the old low-nibble slice gave 0/1/2. A time set in October, November or December then CARRIES like a framework-loaded date (2026-10-31 23:59:59 rolls to November 1, 2026-11-30 to December 1, 2026-12-31 to January 1 of 2027): the stored month is internal packed BCD (0x10/0x12), which the round-trip reads cannot see, so a month stored as its binary value 0xA/0xC re-emits correctly yet breaks the day and year carry.' ;;
    calendar_xe10) echo 'TB_PC9821_CALENDAR_XE10: the calendar'"'"'s interval interrupt (0x128, IRQ15) answers the real Xe10'"'"'s XE10T /AUDIT CAL rows through the real pc9821_calendar: with bit 7 set it raises ONE request per tick whether or not the handler reads 0x128 (0x81 = 32 Hz, 0x80 = 64 Hz, 0x83 = 16 Hz: 16, 32 and 8 rising edges in half a second, read or not), 0x82 stops the tick, bit 7 clear (0x00, 0x01) raises nothing while the tick still runs, and the read-back is the written enable and rate with bit 4 the tick flag a read clears (0x91/0x90/0x93 after unread ticks, 0x81/0x80/0x83 after each tick was read, 0x82 stopped, 0x10/0x11 with the interrupt disabled). And the line'"'"'s shape answers kit 2'"'"'s /AUDIT2 CALIRR rows (docs/probes/KIT2-XE10-AUDIT2.TXT:27-41) on a 1 MHz instance: after 0x128 <- 81, 80 or 83 and a 0x128 read the line is already high (`first=1`), and over 100 ms it stays high but for one low pulse of at most 7 us per tick (the machine'"'"'s 1-2 ARTIC ticks), with as many rises as falls, whether nothing reads 0x128 (N) or a read follows every rise (R): the read leaves the line high.' ;;
    cdda_div_reset) echo 'The CDDA block'"'"'s CLK_AUDIO domain resolves out of reset: the 44.1 kHz divider accumulator, the clk_44100 toggle, the resample flops and the ring pointers are seeded, so the divider does not X-lock in 4-state simulation (run under iverilog for the fail-first X-lock), AUDIO_CE pulses at the divided rate, AUDIO_L/R are never X, and one word written through CDDA_WR comes out of AUDIO_L/R at the full-volume setting.' ;;
    cdda_equiv) echo 'The CD-DA block'"'"'s divider rewrite (the CLK_AUDIO accumulator held in units of the common factor of its 88200 step and its CLK_AUDIO_RATE threshold - 16 bits at 24.576 MHz instead of 32) is clock-for-clock identical to the pre-change block (cdda_ref.v, the module as of 3b134fe4): over a real 80 MHz and 24.576 MHz clock pair with random bursts that never fill the reference'"'"'s 2048-sample ring and a fast-rate configuration, AUDIO_CE, AUDIO_L and AUDIO_R match at every sampling instant (CDDA_REQ is not compared: the ring is deliberately larger since the read-ahead, whose requests tb_pc9821_atapi C20-C22 pin), and the divider'"'"'s toggle is the exact 44.1 kHz ratio.' ;;
    cg_gaiji_record) echo 'A user-defined character the guest writes into the CG store cells that also hold the configuration image (code 0x2056 - store 0x810..0x81D, the software-DIP record - and code 0x1256 row 7, store 0x0497) is what the text plane DRAWS for a cell carrying that code: with the configuration view ENABLED (cfg_seed_en = 1, as pc9821_system ties it), the machine'"'"'s own INT 18h AH=1Ah sequence (A3=0x56, A1=DL, A5=0x00 then 0x20, 32 bytes through the 0xA4000 window - F000:EBD7 in the Xe10 BIOS) loading Flame Zapper Kotsujin'"'"'s glyph 0 (CO2JIN.FNT, all zero - the game'"'"'s SPACE, code 0x7620) must render a blank 16x16 pair, and a patterned glyph loaded at 0x7612 must render exactly the bytes written, row for row, in both halves. The CPU-side configuration view is unchanged: after the ITF'"'"'s re-POST (cfg_seed_reload) the record still reads its static image through port 0xA9 (row 5 = 0xFF, row 8 = 0xAA). FAIL-FIRST: with the write gate suppressing guest stores to those twenty cells, the scanout drew the record bytes (04 C1 8C 81 CD FF 00 00 AA 55 67 86 8A FF) as the right half of every Kotsujin space - the red/yellow/white/pink garbage glyphs a GAME OVER screenshot showed.' ;;
    cg_np2) echo 'The character generator'"'"'s class table and page mapping are swept against NP2kai'"'"'s own decoder -- io/cgrom.c cgwindowset (:11-53) for the 0xA4000 window and cgrom_ia9 (:189-226) for port 0xA9 -- over every one-byte code and a two-byte set that crosses each class boundary, using a font image that TAGS each byte with the class and row it came from, so the byte a read returns names the class and page the RTL chose; every agreement is asserted and every divergence is printed with its exact code, page and the NP2kai rule it breaks; The 20 configuration cells (the software-DIP record at store 0x810..0x81D and the six CPU/size cells) are a SANCTIONED divergence from NP2kai'"'"'s writable-fontrom model (a deliberate requirement): NP2kai'"'"'s cgwindowset/cgrom_ia9 answer a tagged store there, while the machine'"'"'s battery-backed cells survive writes - the Xa7C9W runtime capture holds the record intact after its boot'"'"'s stage-1A KANJI wipe (qemu-pc98 roms/pc98bios/memsw.inc), a machine capture that outranks the software reference - and the core models that as a static read-only view with a transient write-through read echo. A sweep read of one of those cells must answer the configuration byte captured after the tagged load (the transient read echo, cleared at the `out 0xF0` re-POST), and that check replaces the class check there; the cell VALUES are pinned against the dumps in tb_pc9821_cg_read_path. the window'"'"'s only gate is the 0xA4000 address decode (port 0x53E is a read-only strap and the window-0 bank cannot move a 0xA4000 access), and the 16x16 JIS fetch is pinned to its FONT.ROM block (0x7C20 for code 0x4109, 0xCC20 for 0x2110).' ;;
    cg_read_path) echo 'The character generator'"'"'s read path: the four code classes NP2kai'"'"'s cgrom_ia9 decides, driven through port 0xA9 and through the memory-mapped 0xA4000 window, with every byte the classes return coming from the machine'"'"'s own FONT.ROM rows loaded into the read path'"'"'s two sources here -- the ANK class from the pack'"'"'s 8x16 slice, the JIS classes from the pack'"'"'s FONT.ROM copy at pack_base + 0x80000 -- so the assertion is the dump'"'"'s bytes (FONT.ROM[0x800+0x41*16], FONT.ROM[0x7C20], FONT.ROM[0xCC20]) and not a model of them. It does NOT drive the scanout (a text-plane cell holding a two-byte code still draws nothing) and it does not test the pack layout itself: it supplies the font region through the SDRAM'"'"'s second port exactly as a real pack would. Plus the 20 configuration cells - the 14 software-DIP record bytes at CG store 0x810..0x81D and the six CPU/size cells - are a STATIC READ-ONLY VIEW of the OSD settings with a WRITE-THROUGH READ ECHO: a guest store through port 0xA9 or the 0xA4000 window commits NOTHING to the stored configuration, but the read echoes the byte the guest just wrote until the ITF'"'"'s out 0xF0 re-POST (cfg_seed_reload) clears the echo, because the machine'"'"'s own battery-backed record cells survive the ITF'"'"'s stage-1A KANJI CG-RAM wipe (the Xa7C9W runtime capture holds the record intact at code 0x2056 rows 0..0xD, qemu-pc98 roms/pc98bios/memsw.inc) and the EMM386 fix'"'"'s read fallback is structural. The record IMAGE is the OSD Machine field'"'"'s own model table - Xe10 = the ITF'"'"'s factory table BANK4@0x4924, Xe = bios/xe/BANK4.BIN@0x48E8 - with row 4 bit 1 SET (SETUP'"'"'s Sound item stored 1 = "not used", the owner'"'"'s Xe10 as found: docs/bios-setup.md "The Xe10 as found", 0x881E 0x7C in docs/probes/XE10CFG-XE10-CFG0.TXT; the ITF'"'"'s sound POST reads that bit at BANK4@0x2967), so row 4 is 0xCF where the factory tables carry 0xCD, and its SUM-CHECK byte is COMPUTED as row 0x81A = -(sum of rows 0x811..0x819) mod 256, so the ITF'"'"'s marker/sum check (BANK4@0xFC677-0xFC6B5) passes for either model: at this bench'"'"'s OSD configuration (GDC 2.5 MHz, 1M) 0xE5 for the Xe10 and 0xE6 for the Xe, the dumps'"'"' own 0x67/0x68 less the two row 4 adds and plus the 0x80 that row 2 drops at 2.5 MHz (row 2 bit 7 is SETUP'"'"'s GDC clock, stored inverted: 0x0C where the factory 5 MHz row is 0x8C). FAIL-FIRST: on the static-no-echo design the guest'"'"'s write read back as the seed (the KANJI CG RAM ERROR defect), and on a constant-image design the Xe selection still answers the Xe10 rows.' ;;
    cg_ten_low) echo 'TB_PC9821_CG_TEN_LOW: a CPU read of a two-byte CG code whose column byte (0xA1) is 0x01-0x1F answers the glyph of column 0x20 in the same row, as the real Xe10 does (docs/probes/KIT1-XE10-CG0-USERCG.TXT:533-724, the CGX table: for 0xA3 = 0x01, 0x10 and 0x22 every 0xA1 from 0x01 to 0x20 reads 16 zero bytes in both halves, CRC 6A0A, and for 0xA3 = 0x5C every 0xA1 from 0x01 to 0x20 reads 16 0xFF bytes, CRC 6A4B, which is FONT.ROM'"'"'s column-0x20 slot of each row: all 188 two-byte rows of that table match the column-0x20 address and 119 do not match the wrapped address the core used, docs/probes/core/KIT1-CORE-rc-e6db0f05-s3-CG0.TXT), through port 0xA9 (both halves by 0xA5 bit 5, all 16 rows) and through the 0xA4000 window (the odd bytes, and for the plain JIS rows the even bytes too), for 0xA1 = 0x01, 0x10, 0x1A, 0x1B and 0x1F in rows 0xA3 = 0x01, 0x10, 0x22 and 0x5C, against a font whose every byte is tagged by its own offset; column 0x21 keeps its own slot and the user-CG class 0x56 at 0xA1 = 0x10 (the record code 0x1056) keeps reading the CG RAM, not the font. The window'"'"'s EVEN page is keyed on the class: it reads 0xFF for a one-byte code (docs/probes/KIT1-XE10-CG0-USERCG.TXT:533 `CGX 0001 P20=A97D P00=A97D W20E=6A4B`; codes 0x0001, 0x0010, 0x0022 and 0x005C, every row, both 0xA5 halves) and for a row 0x5C code (:710 `CGX 215C P20=AB09 P00=4386 W20E=6A4B`), and stays 0x8F for the user-CG code 0x1056 (:518 `CGW 1056 00 8FFF8F02`); and kit 2'"'"'s rows (docs/probes/KIT2-XE10-CG2-USERCG.TXT, 0xA1 = 0x01, 0x21 and 0x2F, both 0xA5 halves): the even page reads 0xFF in row 0x09 (:758 `CGX 2109 ... W20E=6A4B ... W00O=6A0A`) and row 0x0C (:806 `CGX 210C ... W20E=6A4B`) and 0x8F in row 0x58 (:822 `CGX 0158 ... W20E=A0DD`), not row 0x5C'"'"'s 0xFF, and row 0x09'"'"'s odd page with bit 5 clear reads 0x00. FAIL-FIRST: before the kit-2 change rows 0x09 and 0x0C read 0x8F on the even page, row 0x58 0xFF and row 0x09'"'"'s odd page with bit 5 clear 0x8F (336 mismatching bytes).' ;;
    cg_win_seed) echo 'The CG window read path'"'"'s one-cycle mux/seed flops come up at 0: win_rd_d, cfg_seed_hit_q and cfg_seed_read_q are seeded, so cg_rd_win and the shared cg_ram/ANK port-A address are real addresses from the first cycle and a window read issued as the very first transaction after reset answers the same byte as the settled path instead of X (run under iverilog for the fail-first X-lock).' ;;
    cg_window) echo 'The memory-mapped CG window at 0xA4000: a 32-byte repeating view of the glyph the CG registers select, the user CG RAM write/read path, the code registers packing as A1 high / A3 low, the decode boundary at 0xA5000, a ready-then-response phase, and the ITF configuration record present on a fresh power-up store (the marker at window offsets 0x11/0x13 and the ten bytes the ITF sums, read before anything writes them). It does NOT cover the ROM glyph classes: the ANK page is answered from the pack slice by tb_pc9821_cg_read_path. The even/low page is ASSERTED at the dummy byte the machine itself measures, 0x8F (docs/probes/VRAM.TXT, exactly this class; NP2kai has one dummy page shared by every class, io/cgrom.c:18): a store there vanishes and a read answers 0x8F.' ;;
    cpu_alias_coherency) echo 'The z486 L1 data cache holds no line for the PC-98 device-memory ALIASES - the 0xFA0000-0xFFFFFF low-memory mirror of 0xA0000-0xFFFFF, the PEGC windows at 0xF00000 and 0xFFF00000, and fw_high at 0xFFFF8000-0xFFFFFFFF - so a canonical uncached store (or a ROM bank flip, or the sys16m_en remap of 0xF00000-0xFFFFFF) is visible through the alias on the very next read with no flush in between, while RAM around the windows stays cached so the decode cannot pass by refusing to cache anything; and the A20-wrap aliases of 0xA0000-0xBFFFF classify as VGA device memory exactly as the wrapped physical address they resolve to. The platform'"'"'s alias arithmetic (pc9821_memory_subsystem.sv'"'"'s sys16m_mirror/peg_high bit splices, pc9821_memory_map.sv'"'"'s fw_high) is modelled before the backend storage, so the pair really is one byte with two names. FAIL-FIRST: every alias probe and round trip fails on the pre-fix tree, which cached the aliases and served the stale line.' ;;
    cpu_clk) echo 'TB_PC9821_CPU_CLK: the gated CPU clock gives the CPU exactly the edges the platform'"'"'s cpu_ce names - at a 90 MHz clk_sys, for Full, 33, 25, 16 and 8 MHz, the CPU'"'"'s clock rises at the end of a clk_sys cycle if and only if the gate'"'"'s cpu_ce was high in that cycle, the edge count is the selected rate (within one edge) and Full is every clk_sys edge - and this holds with the enable'"'"'s route into the clock-control block (modelled as the falling-edge register the Cyclone V block is, after a route delay) anywhere from 0 to 10.5 ns, i.e. beyond HALF a clk_sys period: the enable path into the block is a full-period path, so the placement of the throttle'"'"'s register cannot move the CPU'"'"'s edges off the bridge'"'"'s cycles (FAIL-FIRST: the unstaged enable, the throttle'"'"'s rising-edge register straight into the block, puts the CPU'"'"'s edges one cycle late at a 6.0 ns route).' ;;
    cpu_line_burst) echo 'What the CPU'"'"'s cache-line fill ACTUALLY issues: the real z486 L1 data cache (src/z486/l1_cache.sv), through the real SDRAM bridge (the controller on clk_sys) and the real src/memory/sdram.v controller against the chip model, fills its 16-byte line as ONE 4-dword BURST -- a single request carrying burst_cnt=4, accepted as ONE SDRAM transaction (one BankActivate, one auto-precharged row), returning all four dwords correctly -- where the same 16 bytes read as four single-word requests is FOUR accepted transactions. The fill is therefore already the authentic 486 burst and already the minimum number of SDRAM slots, so the earlier '"'"'already batched, nothing left'"'"' is pinned here rather than asserted, and the premise -- that the fill might be several shorter requests -- is refuted by measurement.' ;;
    cpu_reset_gate) echo 'The CPU reset release waits for the SDRAM controller to leave its initialization sequence as well as for the platform hold, so an OSD reset - which keeps rom_ready and therefore cpu_run - holds the CPU for the whole re-run JEDEC initialization instead of releasing it 15 cycles later, and the release is synchronous: cpu_reset_n is still low on the clock the last condition clears and rises exactly two clocks after it, which is the difference between a machine that boots every time and one that boots sometimes.' ;;
    cpu_vram_coherency) echo 'The z486 L1 data cache holds no line for the PC-98 device aperture, so a CPU store to a VRAM-window address reads back byte-exact and a second read still reaches the backend; every aperture address from 0xC0000 to 0xFFFFF, the 0xE0000 intensity window included, is served directly while the RAM addresses below 0xC0000 and the A0000-BFFFF internal VGA aperture are classified the way the machine classifies them; an external writer (the EGC/GRCG, played by a direct write to the backend storage) changing VRAM behind the CPU'"'"'s back is visible on the CPU'"'"'s next read, and the platform'"'"'s whole-L1 cache_flush clears the cached line so the flushed value is what the CPU sees on an address where the L1 really does hold one, with the self-modifying store/scanout-read/re-read round trip byte-exact at every step. The RAM above 32 MiB stays CACHEABLE and NOT device-classified (the platform'"'"'s device-MMIO window is OFF, its old 32-64 MiB base having been the pack base for a 64 MB module while a 128 MB module lets the OSD'"'"'s guest size reach 96 MiB, so 32 MiB of real guest RAM sat inside it L1-bypassed, and the runtime no-allocate bound above the guest RAM top already gives the memory-size probe the behaviour the window existed for), pinned by cacheability at 32, 48, just-below-64 and just-past-64 MiB with the dcache_req_is_device_mmio classifier asserted at both ends of the retired range.' ;;
    crt31k) echo 'Port 0x09A8 bit 0 switches the raster to the PC-9821'"'"'s separate 31 kHz dot clock, and the guest sees the Xe10'"'"'s own rates both ways: with clk_sys at 80 MHz, the real pc9821_iobus (0x09A8'"'"'s latch, written and read through its CPU port), pc9821_dot_ce and pc9821_crtc wired as pc9821_system wires them, and the master GDC programmed with the exact sequence tools/hwprobe/crt31k sends (HSB.EXE IGDC:31'"'"'s: RESET, VSYNC master, SYNC 10 4E 47 0C 07 0D 90 89, PITCH, ZOOM, SCROLL), the VSYNC status bit (the probe'"'"'s own method) rises at 70.0669 Hz and the hsync at 31.460 kHz - the capture'"'"'s frame and line rates, docs/probes/CRT31K.TXT `RUN frames=70 ticks=306930` with the ARTIC calibrated by the same file'"'"'s 24 kHz rows, within 3 ppm - with 0x09A8 reading back 01; writing 0x09A8 = 00 and the BIOS 24 kHz SYNC table brings back the capture'"'"'s 24 kHz rows (56.42 Hz, 24.82 kHz, the `24K (restored)` row within 3 ppm) and 0x09A8 reads 00; and an 784-dot line (x = 98 characters) runs unclamped both while 0x09A8 bit 0 is set (NP2kai io/gdc.c gdcclk[2] minx 92, which clamps an x = 91 line to 736 dots) and while it is clear (the real Xe10 runs x = 90 at 24 kHz, docs/probes/KIT2-XE10-TXTMODE.TXT:36, below gdcclk[1]'"'"'s minx 100) and the BIOS 640 x 480 block (NP2kai bios/bios18.c gdcmastersync[3]) runs VGA'"'"'s 800 x 525 at the same clock (59.92 Hz). FAIL-FIRST: before the 31 kHz dot clock 0x09A8 is a read-back latch only, so the 31 kHz block runs at the 21.05 MHz dot clock (58.61 Hz, 26.32 kHz) and the 784-dot line is clamped to 800 in both states.' ;;
    crt31k_video) echo 'When 0x09A8 bit 0 switches the raster to the 31 kHz dot clock, the video PLL follows it and the analog picture leaves at the Xe10'"'"'s own 31 kHz rates with the crossing FIFO locked: with clk_sys at 80 MHz, the core'"'"'s dot enable (pc9821_dot_ce) driving the BIOS 31 kHz raster (800 x 449, 640 x 400 active: NP2kai bios/bios18.c gdcmastersync[2], the block HSB.EXE IGDC:31 and the crt31k probe send) into pc9821_video_out, and the real pc9821_vpll_ctl reprogramming a PLL model whose output is the frequency its N/M/K/C0 words decode to (Altera'"'"'s Cyclone V reconfiguration map), the CLK_VIDEO after the switch runs at 2 x the capture'"'"'s 25 168 031 Hz within 3 ppm (docs/probes/CRT31K.TXT, calibrated), and over the whole run after the relock every CE_PIXEL period is 2 clocks, every line 1600 clocks (31.460 kHz, the capture'"'"'s line rate within 3 ppm) and every frame 449 lines with a 2-line VSYNC (70.067 Hz, the capture'"'"'s frame within 3 ppm), with the OSD'"'"'s Doubled setting asking all the while: the doubler stands aside for a 31 kHz raster, which is already VGA-rate; there is no FIFO re-centre or underflow over 40 frames after the relock (the two sides'"'"' dot rates are one ratio of the 50 MHz reference); and switching back to 24 kHz relocks the PLL to 2 x 400/19 MHz and brings the doubled 24 kHz picture back (848 clocks a line, 880 lines a frame) with no FIFO event over 20 frames. FAIL-FIRST: before the 31 kHz clock pc9821_video_out has no 31 kHz input and pc9821_vpll_ctl no 0x09A8 one, so the bench cannot build; and with the dot enable'"'"'s 31 kHz rate one fraction step (1/4096 of M) away from the PLL'"'"'s, the FIFO slips within a frame.' ;;
    crtc_geom_equiv) echo 'The CRTC'"'"'s registered raster geometry (V1-a: the clamped totals registered from the SYNC block as it arrives on the frame-boundary edge the copy of the block uses, and every derived compare operand - HACTIVE, the HSYNC/VSYNC bounds, the frame-end/walk-slot bounds - registered every clk_sys from them, one clk_sys behind, so every raster compare reads a register) is cycle-for-cycle identical to the pre-change module (pc9821_crtc_ref.sv, the module as of ad9ed84c): over tens of frames of random, default-perturbed and clamp-extreme SYNC blocks (every field at zero and at its maximum, cr past the line, lf of 0/1/1024, an all-zero blanking), with the block'"'"'s last byte landing one cycle before, ON and one cycle after the frame-boundary edge, random command/MODE/CRTC/palette/text-RAM traffic, irregular pixel_ce, and machine resets mid-frame, right after a SYNC write and on the frame-boundary edge, every one of the module'"'"'s 57 outputs (hs/vs/de, o_htotal/o_vtotal/o_vbp, the frame and snapshot strobes, the vsync IRQ and status bits, the text fetch and render ports, the palette ports, io_dout) matches on every clk_sys cycle - with the text-modes lane'"'"'s three stated exceptions: the stimulus keeps the CRTC render registers, CSRFORM'"'"'s LR and the PRAM partition lengths at their power-on values (the domain where the text line sequencer IS the old 16-line grid; beyond it tb_pc9821_text_modes holds NP2kai'"'"'s maketext), the kanji row cache'"'"'s bank NAME (jc_rd_bank) is not compared, and the text fetch/render ports are compared on active lines below 511 (a taller frame now fetches rows 32-35 instead of truncating them); and gfx_dig is compared against the reference'"'"'s live digital palette registers rather than its frame-boundary copy (the digital palette now reaches the screen at once, as on the real Xe10); and the Xe10 kit-2 exceptions (the TXTMODE /D captures): a block whose raw x is below 100 has its C/R byte raised to x = 100 (the shipped module'"'"'s 24 kHz x floor is the machine'"'"'s 90, docs/probes/KIT2-XE10-TXTMODE.TXT:36, where the reference clamps at 100), every block is followed by PITCH = its own C/R byte (a SYNC block'"'"'s C/R byte now loads the pitch) and the pitch family (o_v_pitch, dbg_pitch, o_v_row0_stale, the text fetch/render ports) is compared only from two lines after the two modules'"'"' pitch registers and copies agree again, and the text fetch/render ports only while the shipped module'"'"'s last cell is the reference'"'"'s fixed 79/39 (a block at least 80 cells wide) and from two lines after.' ;;
    crtc_norom_raster) echo 'With the system reset half asserted (rst_n_sys = 0, the state while cpu_run is low because no firmware pack has loaded) and only the raster position counter'"'"'s own reset released (rst_n_vid = !reset), the CRTC still emits the machine'"'"'s power-on defsyncm24 raster - hs_out pulses once per line for 64 dots and vs_out once per frame for 8 lines, 848 dots and 440 lines to the frame (NP2kai io/gdc.c:42) - so the framework measures a nonzero mode and the analog OSD, which is drawn in the core'"'"'s own timing, can be placed to load a ROM with no pack, while the GDC reset status the ITF polls is unchanged (o_stat_blank stays 1 and o_stat_vsync stays 0, the status flops remaining on rst_n_sys).' ;;
    crtc_stat_blank) echo 'GDC status bit 6 (port 0x60, mirrored by the graphics GDC'"'"'s 0xA0) follows the HORIZONTAL blank of every line, vertical-blank lines included: over two whole default frames it is high for 208 of every 848 dots (24.5 %, the machine'"'"'s capture reads 23.4 % against the 208/848 horizontal blank and not the ~31 % a frame-blank signal gives) and it rises once per line, 440 times a frame, where the previous model held it high for the whole 40-line vertical blank and low across every active line'"'"'s active dots only.' ;;
    crtc_stat_seed) echo 'The GDC status byte'"'"'s resample family comes up at its reset state: the first two port-0x60 status reads after a reset return the reset raster'"'"'s BLANK (bit 6) / NO-VSYNC (bit 5) values with bit 2 set and bit 0 a RESOLVED free-running toggle - stat_dr <= ~stat_dr is the same self-fed X-lock shape as audio_volume'"'"'s channel counter and was X forever in 4-state sim - and the steady-state byte never goes X (run under iverilog for the fail-first X-lock).' ;;
    dbg_cpu) echo 'The CPU debug instrument (pc9821_dbg_cpu) puts out the record format tools/decode_dbglog.py reads, every record whole: the sampler'"'"'s per-window counters sum to exactly the events driven (the event in the snapshot cycle included) with its flags, PIC and CS:EIP fields and a sequence number that gaps on a skipped sample; the memory snoop reports every CPU write and every read-response beat inside its window and none outside it (bursts, overlapping reads, a write and a beat in one cycle), and with the UART stalled and the queue overrun the delivered events plus the 0x32 LOST counts equal the events driven; the frozen trace fires on the PC, WRITE and RELOAD (zero-then-nonzero) triggers and on the second-stage window only after the first, honours the ARM_S delay and a POST count above 63, and dumps the last 2^TRACE_AW issues oldest first through several ring wraps followed by the trigger record; and a ring too small for the offered load drops whole records, never a part of one.' ;;
    dbg_mux) echo 'The debug-UART mux (pc9821_dbg_mux) puts three record sources onto one byte stream without ever interleaving two records: every output record is one source'"'"'s next record, byte for byte, in that source'"'"'s order, whatever the mix of CD-log, display-log and CPU-instrument record types (including payload bytes equal to the 0xA5 sync), with sources that pause between bytes and an output that stalls at random; with all sources always ready the grant rotates round-robin (no source starves); and a stray non-sync byte passes through as a record of its own without taking the grant.' ;;
    dbg_record) echo 'The 351+256+100-byte debug report is emitted in order and survives an accept handshake that withholds a byte for as long as the FIFO behind it cannot take it: a byte presented while the FIFO is full is HELD - its strobe stays high and the same byte stays presented - and it is emitted exactly once when the handshake returns, so the whole 707-byte report arrives with no byte lost and none duplicated.' ;;
    dbg_ring) echo 'The debug-UART byte ring (pc9821_dbg_ring) carries every pushed byte to the UART handshake exactly once and in order through many wraps, at a 32-byte, a 2 KiB and an 8 KiB depth, with the UART stalled until the ring is as full as its room flag allows and then drained at random pace: a pointer or drain index narrower than the ring (the pn-stall instrument'"'"'s 8 KiB ring with an 11-bit drain index) reads the wrong half after the first wrap and fails on a byte POSITION; room goes low exactly when fewer than MAX_REC bytes are free, so a producer that starts records only on room never overruns the ring.' ;;
    dbg_system) echo 'The debug-UART framework is wired into pc9821_system the way the build options say: with every DBG_* parameter at its default the debug arm (dbg_uart_we) never rises - the instruments do not exist - and with the display log, the CPU sampler and the snoop built the mux puts whole records on the arm under a randomly stalling accept: the display log'"'"'s ALIVE record (0x1F) at power-up, CPU samples (0x20/0x21) at the sampler'"'"'s period, a display-port write the bench forces onto the device bus (OUT 0xA4 = 0x01) as a 0x16 record carrying that value, and with the floppy log built its ALIVE record (0x5F) and an FDC data-port write the bench forces onto the iobus'"'"'s last-access latch (OUT 0x92 = 0x03, one strobe) as exactly one 0x50 record carrying that byte, and with the I/O trigger built (io:92:03, POST 0) and the trace depth threaded through (DBG_TRACE_AW 6, build_profile.py --dbg-trace-aw) that same latch strobe freezes the instruction trace: its dump of 2^6 = 64 entries followed by exactly one trigger record tagged I/O naming port 0x92, byte 0x03 and match 1, every record intact and in the table'"'"'s length.' ;;
    dbg_trace_deep) echo 'The CPU debug instrument'"'"'s PC trigger (pc9821_dbg_cpu TRIG_KIND 1, build_profile.py --dbg-trig pc:CS:EIP) matches a virtual-8086 code address by its segment value (pc:FD80:1A82 fires on the issue at FD80:00001A82 and not on 0028:00001A82, FD81:00001A82, 7D80:00001A82, FD80:00001A83 or FD80:00011A82), and a 2^12-entry ring (TRACE_AW 12, build_profile.py --dbg-trace-aw 12) in the last-occurrence mode with POST 4095 dumps the capture of the LAST match: exactly 4096 entries, indices 0..4095, the matching issue first and the 4095 issues after it in order, then one trigger record marked last naming FD80:1A82.' ;;
    dbg_trig_io) echo 'The CPU debug instrument'"'"'s I/O trigger (pc9821_dbg_cpu TRIG_KIND 4, build_profile.py --dbg-trig io:PORT[:VAL]) freezes the instruction trace on the first OUT of VAL to PORT seen on the iobus'"'"'s last-access latch after ARM_S - not on a match before ARM_S, an OUT of another byte, an OUT to another port, an IN that reads VAL from PORT, or a latch that holds the matching access without a new strobe - and dumps the last 2^TRACE_AW issues ending POST issues after it, followed by ONE trigger record tagged I/O that carries the port, the byte and a match count of 1; the any-byte form (io:PORT) fires on the first OUT of any byte; and the last-occurrence mode (LAST_MS, --dbg-last-ms) re-arms on every match, keeps its capture while the next one records into the other bank (a match inside the POST issues of the previous one restarts the count), dumps nothing while matches come closer together than LAST_MS, and once LAST_MS ms pass without a match dumps the capture of the LAST match - only the issues recorded since that bank was started, oldest first, through a ring wrap when there were more - with a trigger record marked last that counts every match since arming.' ;;
    device_select) echo 'Every one of the 65,536 port addresses gets the device-bus select vector the decode it replaced produced, plus the PC-9801-118 game port: the whole 10-bit vector is compared against a fixture of the addresses that assert.' ;;
    disk_change_reset) echo 'The image slot a mount lands in decides whether the core asks for a platform reset: a mount on hps_io slot 0 (the ATA/HDD image) while the firmware pack is loaded raises reset_req for exactly ONE cycle, a mount on slot 1 (the FDC0 floppy, the OSD'"'"'s S1,D88FDDHDMFDI,Mount FDD1 entry) raises nothing at all however long it is held, and a mount on either slot before the pack is loaded raises nothing either (the boot mount is excluded by ordering, since the MGL lists the image before the pack) - so changing a floppy cannot re-POST the machine, which is the rule and a FAIL-FIRST scoping proof against the reset wire that ORed both slots; a held mount level raises nothing on every new clock (the request is the rising edge) and a second separate mount pulses again; a mount on slot 3 (the second hard disk) resets exactly as slot 0'"'"'s does (one cycle with the pack loaded, none before it), and so does every change of the OSD'"'"'s IDE bank 1 field, in either direction (one cycle each with the pack loaded, none before it).' ;;
    disk_led) echo 'The access lamp (pc9821_disk_led'"'"'s led_disk, MiSTer LED_DISK, the lamp the I/O board shows) lights on the drives'"'"' DATA TRANSFERS with their images and on nothing else, as ao486 drives its lamps from the HPS block requests alone (ao486.sv `led hdd_led(clk_sys, |mgmt_req[5:0], LED_DISK[0]);`): with the REAL pc9821_ata (a mounted disk behind a block-engine model), the REAL pc9821_atapi (a mounted ISO behind an hps_io slot model) and the REAL pc9821_fdc (unit 0 with media, a DMA model), both lamps stay dark through a status-poll storm, IDENTIFY DEVICE and CHECK POWER MODE on the disk, TEST UNIT READY, GET EVENT STATUS NOTIFICATION, INQUIRY and REQUEST SENSE (the last two with a data phase) on the CD drive and SENSE DRIVE STATUS and SENSE INTERRUPT STATUS on the floppy controller; led_disk is lit in every cycle a two-sector READ SECTORS has a block request out at the engine, a CD READ(10) has its sector request out at the host, and a floppy READ DATA runs from its last parameter byte to its result interrupt, and goes out 50 ms of clk after the last of these ends (a second READ SECTORS inside the hold restarts it); the floppy lamp (led_fdd, MiSTer LED_USER) lights for the floppy READ DATA alone, with the same hold.' ;;
    disp_lines) echo 'The displayed picture height, the text rows and the planar walk of the 31 kHz BIOS blocks follow the GDC SYNC blocks as NP2kai derives them (vram/dispsync.c dispsync_renewalvertical: textymax from the master'"'"'s lf, grphymax from the slave'"'"'s lf, `scrnymax = MAX(grphymax, textymax)`), driven through the real pc9821_crtc and pc9821_gdc2 by the guest'"'"'s own 0x62/0x60 and 0xA2/0xA0 transactions with the bytes of NP2kai bios/bios18.c: (1) the 640x480 25-row block (master 10 4E 4B 0C 03 0B DB 95 = 800 x 525 with lf 475, slave 02 4E 4B 0C 83 06 E0 95 with lf 480) displays 480 lines with the slave SYNCed and STARTed (the taller of the two windows), its VSYNC pulse rising on the master'"'"'s line 486; (2) the 30-row 640x480 block (master gdcmastersync[5] 10 4E 4B 0C 03 06 E0 95, lf 480, 800 x 525, CSRFORM 0F 00 7B as bios0x18_30 writes it from crtdata[6], the CRTC at its 16-line row defaults) displays 480 lines in 30 text rows of 16 lines, line y being row y>>4 font line y&15, VSYNC rising on line 486; (3) the planar 640x400 31 kHz block (master 10 4E 47 0C 07 0D 90 89 = 800 x 449, slave 02 4E 47 0C 87 0D 90 89) displays 400 lines in a 449-line frame with the walk presenting the address y*80 for every decode line y, including the first lines of the odd-length frame after its wrap, VSYNC rising on line 413. All expected values are computed in the bench from the block bytes by a transcription of NP2kai gdc_updateclock and dispsync_renewalvertical and maketext.c, never read from the DUT. FAIL-FIRST: with the displayed lines limited to the master'"'"'s window case 1 shows 475 lines where NP2kai shows 480; with the planar walker stepping on the line parity alone, the 449-line frame shows the lines below the wrap one raster line late; with the text row index counted from a 25-row constant the 30-row block stops at row 24.' ;;
    dma_accept_steal) echo 'TB_PC9821_DMA_ACCEPT_STEAL: a DMA request raised 1-3 clk_sys after a CPU request has been launched at the backend cannot steal that request'"'"'s accept - the presenting owner is tagged from presentation to the one-cycle ack, so the CPU'"'"'s byte is committed exactly once with its own payload and its read response is still returned, and the DMA'"'"'s own byte is committed exactly once on its own accept; with the unlocked mux the CPU'"'"'s ack is consumed as dma_ready, the in-flight payload moves under the bridge'"'"'s bare crossing and the floppy byte is dropped. A DMA memory READ (the uPD71071'"'"'s memory-to-port direction, a floppy WRITE DATA'"'"'s bytes) is answered to the DMA with the backend'"'"'s own word, alone and with a CPU read in flight beside it, each response reaching only the requester whose read it is (fdd-write: the subsystem had no DMA read response at all, so a floppy write hung on hardware).' ;;
    dma_flush_coherency) echo 'TB_PC9821_DMA_FLUSH_COHERENCY: a floppy sector the real FDC and uPD71071 DMA write into a buffer the CPU has already cached is visible on the CPU'"'"'s next read - every bus-master byte the backend ACCEPTS snoops its line in both L1s (the 486'"'"'s EADS#, one invalidate per accepted write), and the re-fetch returns the sector'"'"'s own bytes where an unsnooped read returns the stale cached dword, with NO whole-L1 cache_flush walk raised on the DMA path; a differential replays a scenario suite (partial-line, multi-set/way, interleaved, wrap-around, back-to-back, wait-state) and six seeded fuzz sequences against the pre-T24 whole-L1 walk and requires every CPU read of a DMA-written address byte-identical, checking every read against a media-seeded oracle, and the tied-off, one-cycle-delayed, wrong-address and request-gated snoop mutants are each killed. A real 486 snoops EADS# per external write and software does not flush after a floppy DMA, so without the snoop the disk buffer is stale.' ;;
    dma_gvram) echo 'TB_PC9821_DMA_GVRAM: a uPD71071 bus-master byte addressed to a graphics plane window (0xA8000-0xBFFFF, 0xE0000-0xE7FFF) is stored in that GVRAM plane, so a CPU read of the window returns the transferred bytes, and a memory-to-port DMA read of the window answers the plane'"'"'s own bytes - the DMA is a bus master on the CPU'"'"'s memory map (NP2kai mem/dmax86.c MEMP_WRITE8 -> i386c/cpumem.c memfn0.wr8; MAME pc9801.cpp dma_write_byte -> program space); a floppy IPL that reads its directory into 0xA8000 by DMA then finds it there. The backend sees no write into the SDRAM hole under the windows, a DMA byte to guest RAM still commits to the backend, and with an EMS window active 0xB0000 stays the backend'"'"'s. A MODE WRITE LANDING WHILE A DMA BYTE IS IN FLIGHT does not move it: with port 0x6A'"'"'s 256-colour bit or the 0x043F EMS state written between the byte'"'"'s presentation and its accept, the byte is accepted exactly once, by the acceptor its class named when it was presented (a plane byte lands in its plane and never in the backend; an EMS-window byte, store or read, is the backend'"'"'s alone and plane R stays untouched) - the class is frozen for the request'"'"'s life, so the backend arm'"'"'s request cannot fall before its ack and the share'"'"'s granted visit cannot lose its request (FAIL-FIRST against the live mode decode, which re-routes the held byte).' ;;
    dma_regs) echo 'The uPD71071'"'"'s eight address/count ports are four 16-bit PAIRS behind ONE shared byte flip-flop, not eight independent byte latches - a single write then two reads returns the written byte then the untouched high half, two different writes then two reads return both bytes in order, a write to a neighbouring channel does not leak into 0x01, and the flip-flop is genuinely shared so a write to 0x01 moves which half a later write to 0x03 lands in - while the ITF'"'"'s own doubled self-test (BANK4@0x3E9) still passes, which eight latches satisfied by accident. Plus 0x19, the 8237'"'"'s clear-byte-pointer: writing it resets the shared flip-flop to LOW (NP2kai dmac_o19, io/dmac.c:196-200) so the next byte write lands the low half (FAIL-FIRST against an undecoded 0x19, which lands it in the wrong half silently); 0x19 is not one of the eight register ports, so it does not itself toggle the flip-flop and its read is the write-only port'"'"'s 0xFF.' ;;
    dma_route) echo 'TB_PC9821_DMA_ROUTE: a uPD71071 bus-master byte goes where a CPU byte access at the same address goes, in both directions - the real Xe10 lands the DMA byte exactly where the CPU'"'"'s lands in every window it was measured in (docs/probes/DMAROUTE-XE10.TXT, DMA=CPU in all 11 cases), as NP2kai'"'"'s MEMP_WRITE8/MEMP_READ8 dispatch through the CPU'"'"'s handler table (mem/dmax86.c, i386c/cpumem.c memfn0): with a 0x043F EMS page selected a DMA store to 0xB0040 commits to the EMS page (where the CPU'"'"'s store at that address commits and its read finds it) and a DMA read there returns the page byte, while the unmapped EMS state drops the store and reads 0xFF; in 256-colour mode 0xA8000 and 0xB0000 are the PEGC packed banks (VGARD8'"'"'s {bank, offset[14:0]} splice, bank A at 0xE0004, bank B at 0xE0006), 0xB8000 and the packed windows in plane mode are not connected (store dropped, read 0xFF - memnc), and 0xE0000 is the register file, so a DMA store to 0xE0006 sets bank B and moves the next DMA byte to that bank, and DMA stores of the capture'"'"'s P4 bytes EB 09 00 00 to 0xE0004-0xE0007 read back 2B 00 00 00 to the CPU and the DMA (docs/probes/DMAROUTE-XE10.TXT P4); a bank write landing while a packed DMA byte is held does not move it (one commit, at the bank it was presented under, its payload unmoved); window 0 (0x0461) moves a DMA address in 0x80000-0x9FFFF as it moves a CPU'"'"'s - at 0x0A to the window'"'"'s own RAM page, neither plane B nor the text RAM (the capture'"'"'s W1/W2: the window reads the bytes back while 0xA8100/0xA1000 and the covered RAM keep theirs), and to plain RAM at 0x10, the external snoop naming the moved address; a DMA store into the CG window at 0xA4000-0xA401F reaches the CG byte port at exactly the positions the CPU'"'"'s stores reach, the glyph lines holding the transferred odd bytes and the window reading them back (the capture'"'"'s G1: 0900503120FCF6D600511FC600B902A4 in both views); and with the 15-16 MiB system space on, 0xFA0000-0xFFFFFF aliases the low window (a DMA store to 0xFA8017 reaches plane B) while 0xF80000-0xF9FFFF drops a store and reads 0xFF. The intensity plane at 0xE0000 follows the 16-colour select (port 0x6A FF2 num 0) for a DMA byte as for a CPU access: in the digital mode a DMA store to 0xE0004 is dropped and a DMA or CPU read answers 0xFF (the machine'"'"'s digital-mode slot, docs/probes/DMAROUTE-XE10.TXT P4 view V1; NP2kai memnc there while VOPBIT_ANALOG is clear), in the 16-colour mode the DMA byte lands in the plane, and a digital-mode store never reaches it. The default map is unchanged: RAM, an unmoved 0x80000, a plane and the text RAM. FAIL-FIRST against the physical-address backend arm, which sends every EMS, PEGC, CG and window-0 byte to the SDRAM at its own address.' ;;
    dma_storeq_coherency) echo 'TB_PC9821_DMA_STOREQ_COHERENCY: a CPU store still in the write-through store queue when an external (DMA) write lands in the same line stays coherent on the CPU'"'"'s next read - the queue forwards the youngest store, the external-writer snoop invalidates the written set, and the final memory holds the CPU'"'"'s store (it drains after the external write that overtook it) - while a drained cached line is invalidated by the same snoop, so its next read is the external writer'"'"'s bytes; the pre-T24 whole-L1 walk drained the store queue before invalidating and the per-write snoop does not, so this pins the equivalence that drain used to provide, with expected values from the memory ordering and not from the RTL. It also pins the CR0.NW=1 case: the 486 ignores EADS# while its write-through policy is disabled, so the same snoop is suppressed and the drained cached line survives - a deliberate, reference-backed divergence from the pre-T24 walk, stated as such.' ;;
    dma_tram) echo 'TB_PC9821_DMA_TRAM: a uPD71071 bus-master byte addressed to the text RAM (0xA0000-0xA3FFF) is stored in the text RAM, so a CPU read of the window returns the transferred bytes, and a memory-to-port DMA read answers the text RAM'"'"'s own byte - NP2kai'"'"'s memp_write8 sends an address at or above CPU_MEMWRITEMAX (0xA0000) through the CPU'"'"'s handler table, whose 0xA0000 slot is memtram_wr8 (mem/dmax86.c MEMP_WRITE8, i386c/cpumem.h, mem/memtram.c); MAME pc9801.cpp dma_write_byte writes the program space - with memtram_wr8'"'"'s attribute rule (an odd byte at or above 0xA2000 is dropped and reads 0xFF), no write into the SDRAM backend under the window, a DMA byte at 0xA4000 (the CG window just past the text RAM, docs/probes/DMAROUTE-XE10.TXT case G1) reaching neither the text RAM nor the backend, and one to guest RAM still committing to the backend; a floppy IPL that DMAs a sector to A000:1000 and jumps there then executes that sector.' ;;
    dma_vram) echo 'The uPD71071 DMA engine'"'"'s memory-side behaviour against NP2kai'"'"'s reference: a byte transfer reads and writes the right byte, lane and address in both directions on every channel, a programmed count of N moves N+1 bytes, the 0x21-0x27 bank byte reaches address bits 23:16 (so a VRAM target at 0xA8000 and a banked target at 0x0B0000 both land where programmed while the low 64 KiB is untouched), autoinitialize reloads the address and count, and decrement walks down. B1 pins what a bank write ZEROES: address bits 30:24 go to zero on every 0x21-0x27 write (NP2kai dmac_o21, io/dmac.c:242), so a bank reprogram after an increment carried out of bit 23 returns the channel to the low 16 MiB (FAIL-FIRST against the stale upper bits running the block at 0x01xxxxxx). Plus the cases the reference disagrees on or the RTL does not model, each named with its reference: the 0x29 address-mode field that MAME'"'"'s PC-9801 uses to decide whether the counter carries across a 64 KiB page is latched and never applied, so a block crossing 0xFFFF carries into the bank byte where MAME'"'"'s default wraps within the page; single-transfer mode moves the whole block for a held DRQ where the 8237 moves one byte per request; and there is no memory-to-memory mode, so a VRAM-to-VRAM blit cannot be programmed.' ;;
    dmac) echo 'The uPD71071 register file answering the machine'"'"'s own captured values (the address and count registers readable, 0x11/0x1B/0x29 reading 0x00, the write-only registers driving 0xFF), the ITF self-test shape surviving a real low/high byte flip-flop, and the engine moving bytes in both directions: a programmed count of N+1, the address walking by the mode'"'"'s direction, terminal count reported on the last byte, the channel masked at terminal count, and the status bits cleared when the status register is read. S7 pins the clear WIDTH: the 0x11 read clears only the terminal-count nibble stat[3:0] and leaves the request-state nibble stat[7:4] standing, exactly NP2kai'"'"'s dmac_i11 `dmac.stat &= 0xf0` (io/dmac.c:290-294) (FAIL-FIRST against a full-byte clear that eats the nibble).' ;;
    dmac_autoinit) echo 'The uPD71071'"'"'s autoinitialize reload returns each channel to the values the guest PROGRAMMED, whatever the transfer has walked since: for each of the four channels a block is programmed with a distinct address (low byte, high byte and the 0x21-0x27 bank byte, whose port-to-channel map is NP2kai'"'"'s ((port >> 1) + 1) & 3) and a count, the block is run through its terminal count and the next block starts again at exactly the programmed 24-bit address with the programmed length (checked on the bus addresses the engine presents, not on a register readback), a channel'"'"'s reload is independent of the other three channels'"'"' programming, and re-programming only ONE byte lane of a channel afterwards (the address low byte, the count low byte, the bank byte) changes only that lane of its reload - the other lanes keep what was programmed before.' ;;
    dmac_engine) echo 'The uPD71071'"'"'s transfer engine against a backend with the shape the memory subsystem actually presents - an accept pulse and a SEPARATE completion pulse several cycles later, the returned word valid only at the completion, and the backend'"'"'s produced-word register starting at a sentinel so a read taken a cycle early returns a value this bench can name. A read that sampled its byte at the accept instead of the completion fails here and passes the module'"'"'s earlier engine bench, because that bench'"'"'s memory stub drove a COMBINATIONAL read data path that was already valid in the request cycle. Plus both directions of transfer, the N+1 count at its boundary, the decrement bit, terminal count with the mask and the autoinitialize reload, and a write finishing at the accept with no completion pulse at all. The E7/D2 cases pin the FDC-DMA fixes: terminal count must coincide with the final dack/strobe (8237 EOP-during-final-transfer qualification; FAIL-FIRST against the old late registered pulse), and a CPU register write landing in the engine'"'"'s S_FIN cycle must LAND - its byte lane of the count/address wins over the engine advance or reload (merged readback), the fresh terminal count survives the count write'"'"'s own TC clear (set-wins), and R_CLEARMSK beats the TC mask so a held DRQ runs the block again (FAIL-FIRST against the old source-order resolution). E7d pins the fold'"'"'s bank path: a 0x21-0x27 bank write landing in the S_FIN whose increment carries out of bit 23 must zero adrs.b[31:24] as NP2kai'"'"'s dmac_o21 does on every bank write (io/dmac.c:242), so the next block runs in the low 16 MiB (FAIL-FIRST against the fold keeping the carried upper bits).' ;;
    dmac_reset_mask) echo 'TB_PC9821_DMAC_RESET_MASK: after a hardware reset every DMA channel is MASKED, so a device that raises its request on a channel the guest has programmed (mode, address, count) but not yet unmasked is never granted - no DACK, no memory access for 200 clocks - and the same request is served as soon as the guest clears the masks (register 0x1D), which proves the request path itself was live and only the reset mask held it off.' ;;
    dpram) echo 'The portable RAM'"'"'s four contracts, any of which silently corrupts a consumer elsewhere: port B'"'"'s read enable is a HOLD rather than a free-running read (the module'"'"'s own reason to have an enable at all -- the GDC scanout and the 86-PCM drain both depend on the data phase following only the address that was presented), a write returns the written byte on that port'"'"'s output in the same cycle, both ports share one array, and INIT_EN lands INIT_LEN bytes at INIT_OFFSET and nothing else, and INIT2_EN does the same for a second disjoint run (the battery-backed configuration record firmware refuses to boot without).' ;;
    e_plane_mode) echo 'the intensity (E) plane at 0xE0000-0xE7FFF is a 16-colour-mode window, switched by the guest with its own OUT 0x6A through pc9821_system: in the digital (8-colour) mode the machine resets to, a dword stored at 0xE0004 reads back 0xFFFFFFFF and is not kept, exactly as the real Xe10 answers (docs/probes/DMAROUTE-XE10.TXT case P4 view V1, `ORIG=FFFFFFFF PRE=FFFFFFFF POST=FFFFFFFF` under `9A0_04/07/08/0A=00 00 00 00`; docs/probes/VRAM2.TXT `E000wrote= 00 FF 55 AA` / `got= FF FF FF FF`; NP2kai i386c/cpumem.c installs memnc over the slot while VOPBIT_ANALOG is clear); after OUT 0x6A,0x01 (FF2 num 0, the 16-colour select) the same store round-trips through the plane; OUT 0x6A,0x00 hides the plane again (0xFF, the store dropped) and OUT 0x6A,0x01 shows it with the byte the analog-mode store left, untouched by the digital-mode store. FAIL-FIRST: the core mapped the E plane in every mode, so the digital-mode store read back.' ;;
    egc) echo 'The EGC engine (pc9821_egc.sv) on the sequences the target software actually issues, driven through pc9821_memory_subsystem against a behavioural GVRAM: TH01'"'"'s 0x3100 four-plane page copy where the read latches the pattern register from the source address and the following write stores it, the main BIOS'"'"'s own 0x1400 colour fill (BANK7.BIN@0x35A1) and its 0x28F0 row shift at sft = 0 (BANK7.BIN@0x356B), TH05'"'"'s 0x29F0 rect copy for an aligned source and destination, and the plane-enable and mask registers. Section 1b pins the two read kinds'"'"' pattern-register captures, which is the whole data path of the games'"'"' own copy: a compare read (OPE bit 13, set by the 0x3100 egc_setup_copy the games use) captures ALL FOUR planes from ONE read (EGCREAD.CSV RD17_P_*, `compare-plus-pload-captured-source`), so TH01'"'"'s egc_copy_rect_1_to_0_16 (rec98/th01/hardware/egcrect.cpp:23 (tmp = egc_chunk(vo))) and TH04/TH05'"'"'s tiles_render_all (rec98/th04/main/tile/render_a.asm:21 (ax, es:[si])) - one read, one WS_PATREG write per word, the write'"'"'s datum coming from the pattern register - land the source on all four planes, while a normal read (bit 13 clear) still updates the SELECTED PAT plane only (RD02_PAT_S0..S3, the README.TXT rule that separates a normal PLOAD_R from Compare+PLOAD_R). The backend is a real store, so the resulting four planes and the CPU read-back are the assertions. Section 8 adds sft bit 12, the descending direction master.lib'"'"'s egc_shift_down sets: at the same 0x28F0 mode and the same 2-bit sub-byte shift, dir=0 and dir=1 must produce DIFFERENT streams, which is the relation NP2kai'"'"'s egcshift() fixes by inverting the shift arithmetic and starting its pointers at the end of the unit. Section 9 pins the shifter re-arm: NP2kai'"'"'s 0x4AC/0x4AD/0x4AE/0x4AF handlers each run egcshift() and then set srcmask to 0xFFFF (io/egc.c cases 0x0c-0x0f), and memegc.c'"'"'s egc_opew ANDs srcmask into the write mask in all three of its mode cases including the non-producing default, so the first write after a re-arm must be masked with 0xFFFF and not with the partial value the previous shift sequence left behind. Section 10 is the SHAKE'"'"'s own shape - dstbit 0 with a non-zero srcbit, master.lib'"'"'s DI = SI - 2 row shift - asserted BY VALUE in both directions against a port of NP2kai'"'"'s lagging per-plane byte queue (egc_readword + egcsftb_{upl,dnl}_sub), which carries the cross-unit carry-in byte the shifter used to read as 0 and is exactly what section 8'"'"'s relation check cannot see. Section 11 drives the same word feed with sft[3:0] in 8..15, where NP2kai'"'"'s shiftinput_incw adds 16 - srcbit and CLEARS srcbit instead of taking the byte feed'"'"'s `subtract 8, add nothing` branch - the suspected defect this bench settles - and asserts BOTH the stack, srcbit, dstbit and whether the produce ran after every unit AND the destination row by value against that same reference queue, which also models the word feed'"'"'s `if (srcbit >= 8) outptr++`: a persistent one-byte source-window offset (an open defect, modelled by the egc.buf pointer window). Section 23 sweeps the ROP table: all 256 values of the OPE low byte, with the shifter loaded from a source read and the pattern operand taken from the pattern register, the source itself (OPE 0x0100), the foreground colour and the background colour (FGBG 14:13), each compared byte by byte against a transcription of NP2kai'"'"'s opefn[] dispatch (mem/memegc.c:709-997), plus the same codes through the byte ROP path and through the RL_MEMWRITE (OPE 0x0200) and SHIFT_CPU (OPE 0x0400) operand updates.' ;;
    egc_cache_fuzz) echo 'TB_PC9821_EGC_CACHE_FUZZ: a seeded, reproducible fuzz of the GVRAM write-combining buffer (8 word slots), the posted EGC write and the GVRAM'"'"'s per-word fallback walk. It drives many randomized sequences of EGC, GRCG and plain reads and writes over random page-0/page-1 addresses with random byte enables, widths, posted-write-then-read-own-word shapes, dirty evictions and flush interleavings, checks every read against a byte-exact golden image as it returns, and after EVERY sequence flushes the WCB and compares the WHOLE 256 KiB SDRAM image against that golden, so a lost write-back or a stale read cannot hide behind a buffer hit.' ;;
    egc_compare) echo 'TB_PC9821_EGC_COMPARE: the EGC'"'"'s COMPARE / colour-key path replayed against REAL PC-9821 (V166) hardware - the PC98_EGC_CONFORMANCE suite v1.3 capture in docs/probes/egc-conformance/ - through the REAL pc9821_egc inside the REAL pc9821_gvram client: 230 word/byte compare vectors, 4 selected-PLOAD_R rows and 21 colour-key WRITE rows (CK00_*_DST / CK01_*_DST, each one the software'"'"'s own read-mask -> 0x04A8 -> masked-write sequence), each stimulus RECONSTRUCTED from the hardware'"'"'s own answers by tools/gen_egc_compare_vectors.py and every expectation taken from the Xe10'"'"'s own aB column, with the reconstructed ramps cross-checked against the suite'"'"'s own read-back of the plane it compares (CK00_*_SRC) and the shared pattern solved against the FG colour.' ;;
    egc_conformance) echo 'TB_PC9821_EGC_CONFORMANCE: a DIFFERENTIAL conformance suite for the EGC. The REAL pc9821_egc (through the REAL pc9821_gvram client and the REAL src/memory/sdram.v controller against the chip model) and the behavioural REFERENCE model tests/pc9821/pc9821_egc_ref.sv - a function-for-function transcription of NP2kai'"'"'s mem/memegc.c and io/egc.c - are driven through the same matrix of registers and CPU accesses and must agree BYTE FOR BYTE on the resulting GVRAM and on every read datum. Each case gets its own 16-byte window on both pages. The matrix covers the three write modes (WS_CPU 0x0000, WS_ROP 0x0800/0x0B00/0x0CF0, WS_PATREG 0x1000/0x1400), all 256 ROP codes, the read modes (COMPAREREAD 0x2000, SHIFT_CPU 0x0400, RL_MEMREAD 0x0100), the colour-quad selects, the plane-enable register, the mask register, byte/word/dword and odd-word accesses over every byte-enable set, the shift register (both directions, moved and byte-aligned sub-byte shifts, srcbit >= 8), and the two shapes already bit by the optimizations: a 32-bit put with shift 0 over a WCB-dirty destination (the m_mode fallback) and a posted write followed by a same-word read. It does NOT measure timing or column counts; those are tb_pc9821_egc.sv'"'"'s sections 12-15.' ;;
    egc_mask_pattern) echo 'The EGC'"'"'s mask register and its fgbg colour-quad / pattern selection, driven directly at the pc9821_egc D1 port, against NP2kai'"'"'s egc_writebyte / egc_writeword: the mask register'"'"'s byte-lane placement on the addressed lane (full, partial and zero masks merged into the destination byte-by-byte), the fgbg&0x6000 fgc/bgc quads expanded per plane from the fg/bg low nibble, the fgbg-selected pattern register as a colour source, and the srcmask NP2kai'"'"'s EGCOPE_SHIFTW2 / EGCOPE_SHIFTB apply in each of the four fgbg selections and every ope mode.' ;;
    egc_post_walk) echo 'TB_PC9821_EGC_POST_WALK: the EGC'"'"'s POSTED two-word write burst and the GVRAM'"'"'s per-word fallback walk (the m_mode burst fallback) must not corrupt each other, against the REAL src/memory/sdram.v controller and the REAL pc9821_gvram. The post advances the EGC on the D1 ACCEPT, so a 32-bit access presents its second 16-bit unit while the GVRAM still walks the first unit'"'"'s two words; the fallback is entered when the destination word is DIRTY in the write-combining buffer, and its address used to be the LIVE cc_addr the posted EGC moves. The bench dirties the destination word, runs a 32-bit EGC 0x3100 put, and asserts every word of the destination dword takes the source value - it fails (words 0x1001/0x1002 never written) before the walk latches its base word.' ;;
    egc_regs) echo 'The EGC'"'"'s register file and its GRCG back-compat gate, checked against NP2kai: the eight registers 0x04A0-0x04AE (access, fgbg, ope, fg, mask, bg, sft, leng) are write-only with 0xFF read-back, hold egc_reset()'"'"'s power-up values, drop every write while the EGC enable is clear, hold the mask register while 0x04A2'"'"'s bits 13-14 are set, and re-arm the shifter on 0x04AC/0x04AE; then the GRCG-compat dispatch, where with the EGC gate clear the 0x7C/0x7E tile registers still own a plane-window store and with it set the EGC engine owns it instead, and where NP2kai'"'"'s real gate (the EGC mode AND the GRCG CG-mode bit 7) keeps a store whose GRCG bit 7 is clear on the plain back-compat path - ASSERTED through the bench'"'"'s own model of that AND (egc_disp_gate = egc_gate && grcg_cg, the same post-AND gate pc9821_system'"'"'s gdc_egc_mem_gate forms). Section C is FAIL-FIRST: a configuration write (0x4A6 fg) issued while an EGC op is still in flight must NOT change that op - the op uses the register image it was ACCEPTED with, exactly as NP2kai'"'"'s atomic op does - and must still take effect on the NEXT op (latched at the accept, not dropped); it fails on the pre-change pc9821_egc, where the produce read the registers live and the in-flight op took the new fg.' ;;
    egc_resp_owner) echo 'TB_PC9821_EGC_RESP_OWNER: a GUARD for pc9821_gvram_arb'"'"'s strobe-routed CPU response (the TB timing cut): an EGC-owned CPU word read, with the CPU holding its request through the ready edge as z486 does (src/z486/bus_unit.sv clears ext_valid_r at the accept edge) and a GDC draw visit already waiting so that pc9821_gvram_share grants it at the earliest legal cycle, is answered on the SAME cycle and with the SAME datum as the same read with no visit behind it - for a plain-store, a GRCG-routed-store and a plain-read visit - and every visit completes; in the EGC strobe'"'"'s cycle the share still presents the CPU, which is the handshake fact that makes the cut cycle-identical to the old owner mux. Not fail-first: the old mux answers these reads too.' ;;
    egc_ring_reset) echo 'The EGC'"'"'s M10K ring is scrubbed on reset, so its contents after a reset are the ones a fresh engine has (zero, exactly the register form and the NP2kai reference'"'"'s rst() give) and not whatever the run before the reset left. A descending byte-unit produce serves the byte its OWN feed just wrote (NP2kai egcsftb_dnn_sub reads outptr[0] before the decrement, mem/memegc.c:174-178), and the bench pins that against a POISONED ring: it fills all 64 ring entries with 0xA5 through ascending word feeds, patches the next read'"'"'s source byte to 0x5A, arms a descending byte unit and asserts the read serves 0x5A (the fresh feed) and never the 0xA5 survivor - the fail-first regression'"'"'s within-unit produce-slot fix, where the old slot read one entry BELOW the write pointer and served the survivor. The same shape after a MID-RUN reset serves a third fresh byte 0x5C: no pre-reset ring content may leak from any slot; the scrub keeps the never-fed entries at the register form zero for parity with the reference'"'"'s rst().' ;;
    egc_serial_equiv) echo 'TB_PC9821_EGC_SERIAL_EQUIV: the PLANE-SERIAL pc9821_egc is indistinguishable from the PLANE-PARALLEL engine it replaced (kept verbatim as the bench-only tests/pc9821/pc9821_egc_par.sv) - two complete GVRAM stacks (pc9821_gvram_share + pc9821_gvram_arb + pc9821_gvram_mem + the engine), seeded with the same random GVRAM, run the same randomized register programs and CPU access streams (every OPE bit pattern, random ROPs, plane enables, masks, FG/BG, both shift directions with random source/destination bit addresses and lengths, every byte-enable set, both pages, 0-5 idle cycles between accesses so posted writes and back-to-back reads collide) plus forced COMPAREREAD and shifted-read/shifted-copy phases, and must agree on EVERY CPU read datum, on the ordered stream of GVRAM port-A writes (address, enables, enabled bytes) and on the final GVRAM; while the serial stack runs, its scanout port B samples a GVRAM word every cycle and every sample must be a WHOLE word some write of the reference committed (never some planes old and some new); and, with expected values from independent arithmetic, a CPU read issued the cycle after a posted WS_CPU write is accepted returns the written word on each of the four planes and in the compare mask. FAIL-FIRST: a planted serial-path bug (stage B dropping plane 3 from the compare AND) fails the read-datum comparison.' ;;
    egc_shift) echo 'drives the real pc9821_egc shifter directly, through a four-plane D1 model, and asserts every emitted word byte-by-byte against a port of NP2kai mem/memegc.c'"'"'s shifter (egcshift, shiftinput_incw/decw, egcsftb_*/egcsftw_*): the 0x28F0 shake form, where the source read feeds the shifter and the write stores the ROP src, and the 0x0CF0 write-feed form, where NP2kai'"'"'s EGCOPE_SHIFTW feeds from the CPU word. It sweeps the sft src_bit/dst_bit field, the bit-length register (0x4AE) at 1, 8, 16, 17 and 640 dots, the descending direction (sft bit 12), and master.lib'"'"'s egc_shift_left_all and egc_shift_left register values. It is FAIL-FIRST: the deviations recorded against NP2kai - the re-arm'"'"'s un-cleared stack, the whole-byte src_bit/dst_bit difference sft_moved ignores, and the missing descending dnr window - make it fail, one named MISMATCH line per unit.' ;;
    egc_width) echo 'TB_PC9821_EGC_WIDTH: the EGC'"'"'s access-width handling (pc9821_egc.sv'"'"'s word_unit/unit_lane/acc_be) against NP2kai'"'"'s memegc_rd8/wr8, memegc_rd16/wr16 and memegc_rd32/wr32 (mem/memegc.c:1367-1423): an 8-bit access at each of the four byte lanes, a 16-bit unit at an even and at an odd lane start, the 32-bit access split into its low and high 16-bit units, and every non-zero byte-enable combination -- each driven as z486 presents it (a dword-aligned address with the operand'"'"'s lanes ONLY in cpu_be: z486_pkg.sv:1169 calc_be / :1226 calc_be_first / :1242 calc_be_second) and compared byte for byte against a port of NP2kai'"'"'s own decomposition of the same guest access, including its odd-address fallbacks (memegc_wr16/rd16 doing two byte operations; i386c/cpumem.c:1475 memp_write32 turning an odd dword into byte, word, byte). It covers the shifter only as an UNMOVED copy (sft = 0), where NP2kai'"'"'s output is the source bytes, and it does not cover a moved shift, the ROP or the mask registers.' ;;
    egc_xe10_pace) echo 'TB_PC9821_EGC_XE10_PACE: the EGC through the REAL pc9821_gvram_arb, pc9821_gvram_share and pc9821_gvram_mem keeps every egcplane shape at or under the Xe10'"'"'s own NET cost per access (docs/probes/EGCP.TXT, the egcplane probe on the real Xe10 decoded by tools/hwprobe/egcplane/egcplane_decode.py: EGC1400 and EGC0CF0 write rows 0.6-0.8 ns net, so the CPU never waits on an EGC write; EGC3100SP and EGC28F0SP same-page read+write rows 676.9-677.5 ns net) at 80 MHz (base) AND at 50 MHz (the slowest clk_sys the build system offers a base-like build, `--custom --clk-sys-mhz 50`), driven by a CPU model that issues the probe'"'"'s own loop (body_egc: one word read and/or one word write per row, 80-byte row stride, page 0) with the core'"'"'s own measured loop-shell time between accesses, and the per-row time is IDENTICAL for 1, 2, 3 and 4 planes enabled through 0x4A0 (FFFE/FFFC/FFF8/FFF0) on every shape, as it is on the machine. It reports each shape'"'"'s margin against the machine. FAIL-FIRST: an engine that spends 20 extra clk_sys per unit fails the write rows (the CPU starts waiting on posted writes).' ;;
    ems) echo 'Every non-0x2x/non-0xEx write to 0x043F leaves the EMS window state alone: after 0x22 enables the 64 KiB page, the BIOS'"'"'s pre-mov-cr0 cache-flush write 0xA0 - and 0x80/0xC4 - still serve the EMS page at 0xB0000 instead of resetting the window to the VRAM planes, exactly as qemu'"'"'s mem_bankctl_write acts only on 0x20 (EMS) and 0xE0 (bank) (pc98-mem.c:684-701). FAIL-FIRST: before this fix every other nibble took the else arm, reset ems_state to the identity, and 0xB0000 fell back to VRAM - an EMM386-style driver that enables 0x22 and then toggles paging with the BIOS'"'"'s 0xA0 flush lost its page. And 0x24 written from the identity keeps the VRAM planes (docs/probes/DMAROUTE-XE10.TXT cases E1/E2: the 0x20 and 0x24 views are one storage) while written from the page it unmaps the window (docs/probes/EMS.TXT `B0000 under 20/22/24: 1111 2222 FFFF`) - FAIL-FIRST, the identity case read unmapped.' ;;
    ems_itf) echo 'The ITF'"'"'s own EMS self-test (BANK4@0xF8290-0xF8329, POST stage 0x1D) and its 64 KiB pass-path pattern test, driven end to end through the REAL pc9821_iobus (whose decode owns ems_state) + pc9821_memory_map (whose route decides EMS page vs VRAM plane vs open bus): writing 0xAA55 to 0xB0000 under 0x43F=0x22 then switching to 0x43F=0x20 must NOT return 0xAA55 (the two 0x2x values select distinct 64 KiB stores), exactly as the machine'"'"'s own capture passes it (docs/probes/EMS.TXT: '"'"'22 wrote AA55 at B0000, 20 read: 0000'"'"'; '"'"'B0000 under 20/22/24: 1111 2222 FFFF'"'"'). FAIL-FIRST: on the pre-window tree (which decoded only the 0xEx nibble of 0x043F) both states were the GVRAM R plane, the 0xAA55 survived the 0x22->0x20 switch, and the ITF reported EMS ERROR.' ;;
    fdc) echo 'The FDC model dispatches its command on the LOW FIVE BITS of the opcode byte, as the uPD765 and NP2kai both do (FDC_Ope[fdc.cmd & 0x1f]), so READ DATA with MF=1 (0x46) completes exactly like READ DATA with MF=0 (0x06) -- eight parameters, the running state, and an interrupt at the end -- and READ ID with MF=1 (0x4A) completes like 0x0A. An opcode the model does not implement still answers its one-byte '"'"'invalid'"'"' result WITH NO INTERRUPT. Decoding the WHOLE opcode byte instead sent 0x46 to that invalid arm, raised no interrupt, and left the BIOS'"'"'s FDC interrupt wait (BANK7 0x7D0A, polling DS:0x55E for a bit only the FDC ISR writes) spinning forever -- which is the reported Rusty freeze. This bench pins both halves, so a whole-byte decode fails it. It also pins the 0x04BE 1.44M/RPM register against NP2kai io/fdc.c:1117-1134: the write stores reg144 and, with bit 4 set, one RPM bit of the drive reg144[6:5] names, and the read answers rpm[reg144[6:5]] | 0xFE.' ;;
    fdc_2dd_bank) echo 'One uPD765 sits behind TWO port windows: the 0x00BE strap (bit 0) selects whether 0x90/0x92/0x94 or 0xC8/0xCA/0xCC answers, and the same bit routes the controller'"'"'s completion to IRQ11 (slave_irq[3]) or IRQ10 (slave_irq[2]) and its DMA handshake to channel 2 or channel 3; the unselected window answers the open bus 0xFF and drops writes (NP2kai'"'"'s gate), the register index is the same in both banks, the 2DD window'"'"'s control port answers the Xe10'"'"'s 640K-mode 0x64 (docs/probes/FDMODE-XE10-FDMF0.TXT, -FDMF1.TXT) away from a command and 0x74 (bit 4, the selected drive'"'"'s RDY) straight after a SENSE DRIVE STATUS of a drive with media, as the BIOS'"'"'s 640K read path reads it (BANK7@0x7E67-0x7E7E), where the 2HD one answers the machine'"'"'s 0x44, 0x04BE reads EE with the strap at 0 and FE with it at 1 (bit 4, 1.44 MB access, clear in the 640K I/F mode, the same captures and FDM0), and a floppy READ issued on the 2DD window moves the sector over DMA channel 3 while the same command on the 2HD window still uses channel 2. The Floppy mode field'"'"'s fixed/auto bit reads back as 0x00BE bit 2 - 1M FF, Auto (1M) FB (FA after writing 0x02), 0x00BF reading FF as the Xe10'"'"'s does (docs/probes/FDMODE-XE10-FDMF3.TXT) - and the read follows the field only across a reset (POST samples it).' ;;
    fdc_conform) echo 'The FDC model is driven through its own three ports against NP2kai io/fdc.c with a PC-9821'"'"'s two drives equipped and no diskette inserted, and each command'"'"'s dispatch (which opcodes take parameters and how many), its status (ST0/ST1/ST3), its interrupt, the SENSE INTERRUPT STATUS drain and the DOR read-back are compared byte for byte. The real register bugs the model had are ASSERTED and fixed: the drive/unit is parameter 0 (not the last byte), RECALIBRATE/SEEK completion is the PER-STATE class -- an equipped drive with no diskette answers SE|NR = 0x28 to RECALIBRATE, which the BIOS'"'"'s drive-present probe counts as PRESENT, and a unit whose READY is never asserted answers SE|IC0|NR = 0x68 and is not counted, with the guest'"'"'s forced-ready strap adding EC (SE|IC0|EC = 0x70|unit for an unequipped unit, SE = 0x20 for an equipped one), while SEEK has NP2kai FDC_Seek'"'"'s single not-ready class SE|IC0|NR = 0x68|unit even under forced ready -- a data command on a drive with no diskette answers the seven-byte result with its interrupt and ST0 = IC0|NR (0x48|u, READ ID included, where NP2kai'"'"'s READ ID answers ND) after an MSR of CB alone with no drive-busy bit - the real Xe10'"'"'s NRID/NRRD rows (docs/probes/XE10T-XE10-FDC2C.TXT) - and SENSE INTERRUPT STATUS then answers 80, the SENSE DRIVE STATUS ST3 bits are TS/T0/HD for an equipped drive and the fault bit 0x80 for a unit the PC-9821 does not equip (drives 2/3), its WP bit is the MOUNTED MEDIUM'"'"'s protect state -- the D88 header'"'"'s bit 4 OR the mount'"'"'s read-only flag, never "media is present" -- the parameter-phase MSR is RQM|CB, the DOR read is NP2kai'"'"'s computed 0x44, and the 0x92 idle byte is the MACHINE'"'"'s 0x80 (R10.TXT/PORTS.TXT), not NP2kai'"'"'s zeroed lastdata. The reference deviations this model KEEPS by design are printed as WAIVED, not asserted: the seven eight-parameter commands no target game issues (0x02/0x05/0x09/0x0C and the three SCANs 0x11/0x19/0x1D), its explicit ST_EXEC MSR for FORMAT, and the absent media/data phase. The MSR read-back is pinned bit for bit against fdc.status (fdc_i90 returns it verbatim): the low nibble carries one drive-busy bit per unit from the command'"'"'s claim to the result drain (set fdc.c:562-563/300-302/111-113, cleared fdc.c:907-909) and bit 5 NDM tracks the SPECIFY ND latch (fdc.c:265) over a data command'"'"'s transfer window (fdc.c:450-454) -- FAIL-FIRST: the model answered 0x90/0xD0/0x80 only and 7 of the 12 new cases mismatched.' ;;
    fdc_dbg) echo 'The FDC'"'"'s own diagnostics reach the stall record'"'"'s storage fields end to end: FST packs {IRQ, DRQ, st[2:0], x_st[2:0]} and reads 0x00 after reset, 0x08 mid-parameter, 0x01 waiting on the media'"'"'s answer, 0x42 with a sector byte waiting on the DMA engine and 0x98 in the result phase with the interrupt raised; FCM carries the command byte'"'"'s {MT, opcode}; FRS carries {res0, res1, res2, exec_st0} and reads the missing-sector error7 answer 40040000 (ST0 IC0 with ST1 ND, NP2kai readsector fdc.c:417-427); and FCH names the sector in flight {C, H, R, N} from the command'"'"'s parameters 1..4, so a capture says where a floppy transfer stopped and why it stopped.' ;;
    fdc_int_line) echo 'TB_PC9821_FDC_INT_LINE: the floppy controller'"'"'s interrupt line is the uPD765A'"'"'s INT flag, not the level of its status queue: every command or result byte the guest moves through the data port lowers it and a reset written to the 0x94 DOR lowers it, while the statuses already queued stay for SENSE INTERRUPT STATUS (one per command, down to the one-byte 0x80); each later completion and each later ready change raises it again, so a status the guest left undrained never swallows the next interrupt - the Windows 95 floppy driver'"'"'s reset (SENSE DRIVE STATUS, DOR 0x98, DOR 0x18, then a sleep on the interrupt) still gets its drive-poll interrupt as a rising edge, a WRITE DATA refused on a protected medium and a RECALIBRATE still interrupt; a ready change that arrives while a command is being written, executed or read back is presented when the controller is idle in the command phase again, never inside the command, and one that coincides with a data-port read at idle is not lost. FAIL-FIRST: the base controller drove the line from the queue (any pending status held it high), so after an undrained ready change the reset'"'"'s poll and the refused write raised no edge (the hang after a direct disk swap), a SENSE INTERRUPT STATUS left the line high while another drive'"'"'s status was queued, and a change raised the line in the middle of a command.' ;;
    fdc_log) echo 'TB_PC9821_FDC_LOG: The floppy-controller log (pc9821_fdc_log, the --dbg-fdc debug instrument) puts out the seven-byte record format tools/decode_dbglog.py reads: an ALIVE record (0x5F, a = 0x5A) at power-up; a 0x50 record for every FDC data-port write (0x92 and 0xCA) and a 0x51 for every data-port read, each with the byte and the controller state; an MSR read (0x90/0xC8) only when its value differs from the previous MSR read, carrying the number of unlogged reads of the previous value (a 40-read poll of one value is one record); an interrupt-line edge as a 0x53 record with the new level, also when it coincides with a port access (queued, logged after the access); control-register writes and reads (0x94, 0xCC, 0xBE, 0x4BE, 0xBF) as 0x54 with the register code and a read flag; DMA writes to 0x09/0x0B/0x11-0x1F odd/0x23/0x29/0x0439 as 0x55 and reads of 0x09/0x0B/0x11 as 0x56, while other ports (0x60, 0x05, 0x21) are not logged; a 10 us time that advances; and, with the UART stalled until the ring fills, a LOST record (0x5E) carrying the exact count of dropped records once it drains.' ;;
    fdc_mf) echo 'TB_PC9821_FDC_MF: the FDC carries the command'"'"'s MF bit (MFM vs FM) to the medium and the medium honours it as NP2kai does - a raw image is MFM only, so an FM READ DATA or FM READ ID on it answers IC0 with ST1 ND while the MFM form reads (diskimage/fddfile.c fdd_seeksector_common, fdd_readid_common), and a D88 READ DATA whose header matches in the other recording mode (mfm_flg 0x40 = FM, 0x00 = MFM) finds no sector (fdd_d88.c searchsector_d88), and a D88 READ ID of a header in the other mode answers ND too (an FM READ ID cannot read MFM address marks; NP2kai fdd_readid_d88 does not test MF, a recorded divergence) - so the BIOS'"'"'s FM boot probe of an MFM disk fails and its MFM retry loads the whole boot sector, instead of a phantom FM read moving half of it.' ;;
    fdc_nd_search) echo 'A READ DATA whose sector is not on the track, and a READ ID that finds no header, end at the controller'"'"'s SECOND index pulse, as the uPD765A/uPD72065 searches the IDs (MAME src/devices/machine/upd765.cpp index_callback, SCAN_ID: `if(fi.counter == 2)`): from every starting angle of the spindle there is no interrupt for at least one revolution and the IC0/ND answer {0x40, 0x04, 0x00, C, H, R, N} comes with the interrupt by the end of the second - 360 rpm (1/6 s) on the 1 MB interface, 300 rpm (1/5 s) once port 0x04BE selects it for the unit - while the MSR shows the command busy (CB alone, 10: the real Xe10'"'"'s READ DATA sets no drive-busy bit, docs/probes/XE10T-XE10-FDC2A.TXT DMA_MSR); so a guest that marks its command pending after the last parameter byte (Star Cruiser'"'"'s IRQ0-stepped loader, 0060:1011 then `mov [0x100],3`) cannot lose the interrupt to an answer that comes before that store.' ;;
    fdc_read) echo 'pc9821_fdc'"'"'s Phase-1 data path against NP2kai io/fdc.c: with media mounted, READ DATA (0x02/0x06/0x0C and their MF variants) takes its eight parameters, walks R..EOT, and is ended either by the DMA engine'"'"'s TERMINAL COUNT, whose result carries the uPD765'"'"'s end-of-sector step of the last sector (the real Xe10 answers C=01 H=00 R=01 for a C0 READ DATA whose count ends on R = EOT, both R1 with EOT=1 and R1..R8 with EOT=8, docs/probes/XE10T-XE10-FDC.TXT READ1/READ8, where NP2kai forces R=1 with C unchanged; a count ending before EOT answers R+1, and with MT a count ending at side 0'"'"'s EOT answers H flipped with C unchanged and at side 1'"'"'s EOT H flipped with C+1, the datasheet'"'"'s table and MAME upd765.cpp), or at EOT with no terminal count, which the real Xe10 ends ABNORMALLY - ST0 IC=01 with the last sector'"'"'s head, ST1 EN, C/H/R the last sector'"'"'s (docs/probes/XE10T-XE10-FDC2A.TXT EOT_NT/EOT_MT), where NP2kai advances C - always answering the seven-byte result {ST0,ST1,ST2,C,H,R,N}; with MT the head flips and the other side of the SAME cylinder is read; a missing sector answers ST0 IC0 with ST1 ND; a write against this read-only medium answers IC0 with ST1 NW; READ ID reports the crcn-th header of the track with C from the track register and R/N from the header; in DMA mode the MSR reads CB alone (10: RQM low, no drive-busy bit) through the transfer and D0 in the result phase, the Xe10'"'"'s DMA_MSR row where NP2kai shows D1, and returns to idle at the result drain; and a data command on a drive with NO media answers the seven-byte result with its interrupt, ST0 = IC0|NR|hd|us (0x48|u, READ ID too, where NP2kai'"'"'s READ ID has no NR), C/H/R/N the command'"'"'s (READ ID: what the previous command left), SENSE INTERRUPT STATUS then 80, inside the Xe10'"'"'s timing windows (its NRRD/NRID/NRID_U2 rows), and that answer is its ONE command'"'"'s: a later SEEK or RECALIBRATE of the ready unit still ends through SENSE INTERRUPT STATUS with SE|us and its PCN, no result phase (the Windows 95 floppy driver'"'"'s RECALIBRATE after the BIOS'"'"'s READ ID of an empty drive had replayed the stale not-ready result: device not ready on B:). The DMA model strobes one byte per DRQ with TC coincident with the final strobe, exactly pc9821_dmac'"'"'s S_DEV. A non-DMA (ND) READ DATA read by polling MSR delivers exactly the medium'"'"'s bytes even when each sector'"'"'s bytes arrive after a fetch delay: RQM is shown only while a byte is there (NP2kai fdc_dataread drops RQM between sectors). While RQM is dropped between a PIO read'"'"'s bytes the MSR keeps CB beside DIO (0x50, never DIO alone: NP2kai fdc_dataread clears only RQM and NDM).' ;;
    fdc_readid_wait) echo 'A READ ID that finds its header answers only once the ID field has passed the head, as the uPD765A reads it off the spinning medium (MAME src/devices/machine/upd765.cpp read_id_continue, the result only at COMMAND_DONE): back-to-back READ IDs complete one sector period of the track apart - REV / sectors at 360 rpm on the 1 MB interface and at 300 rpm once port 0x04BE selects it, within the Xe10'"'"'s measured 0.92 of the nominal period (docs/probes/XE10T-XE10-FDC.TXT `c2c_art=5908/5917/9842` on an 8-sector track) and the full period - no answer ever comes within the 160 us an MFM ID field takes to read, the MSR shows CB alone (no RQM, no DIO) while the field is searched, and the seven-byte answer {hd<<2|us, 0, 0, C, H, R, N} still carries the track register and the header'"'"'s R/N, so a driver that sets its "interrupt expected" bit after the last command byte (Windows 95 HSFLOP.PDR'"'"'s media check, obj3 0x3015E8 `mov al,0x4a` .. 0x3015FD `or byte [0x100980],0x2`) cannot lose the interrupt to an instant answer.' ;;
    fdc_ready_change) echo 'TB_PC9821_FDC_READY_CHANGE: a floppy media change raises NP2kai'"'"'s ready-change interrupt (fdd/diskdrv.c): every mount event of an equipped drive - an eject, an insert, an eject of an empty drive - leaves ST0 = AI|NR|drv (0xC8|u) for SENSE INTERRUPT STATUS and raises the interrupt at once, ungated; an insert keeps the drive NOT READY (ST3 RDY clear, a READ DATA answering the IC0|NR class in its own seven-byte result with the interrupt, SENSE INTERRUPT STATUS then empty, the real Xe10'"'"'s not-ready shape) for DISK_DELAY = 20 guest frames and then, only when the 0xBE register'"'"'s bit 2 is clear or the DOR'"'"'s motor bit 0x08 is set, leaves ST0 = AI|drv (0xC0|u) and raises the interrupt again, with the drive READY, while a ready held back by that gate is announced (AI|drv) when the DOR'"'"'s motor bit later rises with 0xBE bit 2 set (NP2kai io/fdc.c fdc_o94); a medium that parses after the delay is announced when it parses; and a mount event while the controller is held in reset raises nothing (fdc_reset zeroes fdc.stat). FAIL-FIRST: the base controller had no ready-change interrupt at all (no media_chg/frame_tick seam).' ;;
    fdc_reset_int) echo 'TB_PC9821_FDC_RESET_INT: a controller reset through the 0x94 DOR, RELEASED (bit 7 falling) by a write with or without the motor bit 0x08 (the real Xe10 interrupts 1.07 ms after both, docs/probes/XE10T-XE10-FDC2A.TXT RST_M and RST_NM), is followed by the uPD765A'"'"'s drive-poll interrupt at the first poll, within 1.024 ms (8192 clocks of the 8 MHz FDC) of the release and never before 100 us nor later than one more poll - not NP2kai'"'"'s 6-frame (100 ms) workaround - with ST0 = AI|unit (0xC0|u) pending for each READY drive, drained one per SENSE INTERRUPT STATUS down to the one-byte 0x80, one interrupt per reset; a status pending for a drive that is not ready survives the reset and follows the AI; no interrupt while bit 7 is held set or for a motor-on write with no reset; and a poll that falls inside a command is deferred to the next one; a later control write with bit 7 clear (a repeated 0x18, 0x1C, 0x08) neither cancels nor restarts the poll, which still interrupts at the first poll after the release (MAME upd765.cpp reset_w acts only on a change of the RESET level, NP2kai never cancels int_timer), while a write re-asserting bit 7 stops it until the next release. The stimulus is the Windows 95 floppy driver'"'"'s own reset (HSFLOP.PDR 4.00.950, NEC build): SENSE DRIVE STATUS reads RDY, DOR 0x98, ten 0x5F waits, DOR 0x18, then it sleeps until the interrupt. FAIL-FIRST: the base controller raised no interrupt after a reset (the Windows 95 Explorer hang), the 6-frame model raised none within the datasheet'"'"'s window, and NP2kai'"'"'s motor-bit condition raised none for the 0x90/0x10 reset.' ;;
    fdc_seek_track) echo 'TB_PC9821_FDC_SEEK_TRACK: a READ DATA reads the sector on the track the head is on - the drive'"'"'s track register as the last SEEK left it, with the command'"'"'s HD bit for the side - and matches the command'"'"'s C and H against the sector header, so a D88 whose sector IDs do not name their own cylinder or side (a copy-protected layout: side 1 holding H=0 IDs, cylinder N holding C=N-1 IDs) is read where the head is, READ ID reports the SEEKed cylinder, and a READ DATA replaces the track register with its C only when the register differs from the last SEEK'"'"'s (NP2kai io/fdc.c FDC_ReadData fdc_lasttreg, diskimage/fd/fdd_d88.c fdd_read_d88 trkseek((treg << 1) + hd) and searchsector_d88).' ;;
    fdc_vfo) echo 'TB_PC9821_FDC_VFO: the floppy interface'"'"'s VFO timer (0x94 bit 0 TTRG, bit 2 TMSK; undoc98 io_fdd.txt) raises the controller'"'"'s interrupt line once, 90.6 ms after a write with TTRG set, as the real Xe10 does for the BIOS'"'"'s hand-back 0x94 <- 09, 0C (docs/probes/XE10T-XE10-FDC2A.TXT and -FDC2C.TXT VFO rows: one rise at 28018 and 27649 ARTIC ticks, 91.2 and 90.0 ms, with SENSE INTERRUPT STATUS answering 80 - the controller has no status of its own), never before 89 ms nor after 91.5 ms; the line then STAYS high (irr_end=18, and the machine'"'"'s later READ DATA completions raise no new edge) until a write with TMSK clear (the RST_M reset'"'"'s 0x98, after which its drive-poll interrupt rises again), and a re-trigger (the BIOS IRQ11 handler'"'"'s 0D, 0C, BANK7@0x7AC4-0x7ACA, whose motor-off countdown the captures'"'"' 0x0485 bytes show running) drops it and raises it again one period later; a trigger with TMSK clear raises nothing while TMSK stays clear, but its expiry is held: kit 2'"'"'s FDVFO row (docs/probes/KIT2-XE10-FD0.TXT:4) - 0x94 <- 09, 08, 200 ms, then 0C - raises the line within 3 ARTIC ticks of the 0C, and SENSE INTERRUPT STATUS answers 80 and leaves it high, until a trigger drops it. FAIL-FIRST: the controller had no VFO timer, and the core'"'"'s capture of the same row reads `t_irq_art=- rises=0` (docs/probes/core/XE10T-FDC2-CORE-A.TXT).' ;;
    fdd_media_change) echo 'TB_PC9821_FDD_MEDIA_CHANGE: a floppy is mounted, swapped and ejected while the core runs, without a reset, and the guest sees NP2kai'"'"'s media change: every mount event of a drive leaves SENSE INTERRUPT STATUS = AI|NR|drv with the interrupt, an insert keeps the drive not ready for the 20-frame insert delay and then reports AI|drv with the interrupt, and the drive'"'"'s READY (ST3 RDY) follows; an eject or swap UNDER a READ DATA is undefined for the guest, and what is pinned there is only that NOTHING HANGS - mid-DMA on channel 2 at 1, 511, 512, 700 and 1000 bytes, before the first byte and while the request is being issued, the controller recovers through the guest'"'"'s own DOR reset to an idle MSR, the bytes moved before the change are the old image'"'"'s, the other drive reads byte-exact across it, and a re-inserted or swapped-in image then reads byte-exact; a mount on the OTHER drive while a read is in flight lets the read finish with its own image'"'"'s bytes; back-to-back mounts leave the LAST image mounted; a remount of the same image and an insert into an empty drive both read correctly; a mount level held for many cycles is one media change; and a request accepted while the OTHER drive'"'"'s read-ahead of the same block index is still outstanding is served its own image'"'"'s block, never the other drive'"'"'s (the stale-block guard). FAIL-FIRST: the base tree finished the ejected read with success out of stale blocks and left READY standing until the deferred walk; the osd-mount tree raised no ready-change interrupt; with the stale-block guard removed, M7 reads drive 1'"'"'s bytes on drive 0.' ;;
    fdd_media_d88) echo 'pc9821_fdd_media reads a D88 and a raw sector image through the HPS block protocol and serves them over the byte seam pc9821_fdc drives: the D88 header is parsed where d88head.h puts its fields (fd_type at 0x1B, fd_size at 0x1C, trackp[164] at 0x20 as little-endian 32-bit offsets), a sector'"'"'s file offset is found by walking the track'"'"'s [16-byte _D88SEC][data] run and testing (c,h,r,n) as NP2kai'"'"'s searchsector_d88 does, so a header that matches C/H/R with a different N is not the sector, a sector whose stored size is shorter than 128<<N still delivers the full 128<<N bytes with a ZERO tail, READ ID answers the requested ordinal with the track'"'"'s own sector count, a raw image maps CHRN straight onto its geometry row'"'"'s file offset and refuses an N that is not the row'"'"'s N, and a missing sector, a missing track, an ordinal past the track'"'"'s headers and an unknown image size are each reported as not available. The host side is a model of the MiSTer block reader: sd_ack held for the transfer, one 16-bit word per clk_sys at sd_buff_addr with (sd_blk_cnt+1) sectors per handshake. A RE-MOUNT MOVES THE MEDIUM, which is what lets the platform stop resetting the machine on a floppy change: mounting a second image of the same geometry answers the SECOND image'"'"'s sector bytes (so neither a cached block nor a parsed track table may survive the swap), an eject - a mount of a sub-sector image - clears present and answers no byte out of the ejected medium, and remounting the first image serves it byte-exact again. THE RAW SIZE GUESSER IS NP2kai'"'"'s fdd_xdf.c supportxdf rows (headersize 0) AND THE GEOMETRY IS PER DRIVE: the 512-byte rows (2DD 9x512 and 8x512, 2HD 15x512/18x512/21x512), the 256-byte 26x256 row and the 9x1024 row mount and each answers READ ID with ITS OWN N and sector count (fdd_readid_common, diskimage/fddfile.c:319-345) while refusing a request whose N is not the row'"'"'s (fdd_read_xdf'"'"'s 0xC0, fdd_xdf.c:341-343); the double-claimed 655,360-byte body answers the DD6 row'"'"'s 8x512 geometry, fdd_set_xdf'"'"'s first size match in table order (:41 before the same-sized DDB row :47, whose .ddb extension tiebreak :129-133 does not exist at this seam), and a D88 whose fd_type major is 2D or 2DD (0 or 1) mounts beside the 2HD classes and answers its geometry from the walked per-track headers (fdd_d88.c:92-118 takes majors 0/1/2). Each medium answers only in its own media mode (the controller'"'"'s 0x00BE bit 1 and the unit'"'"'s 0x04BE rpm bit): a 2DD class read in 2HD mode, a 2HD class in 2DD mode or a minor-0 2HD at 360 rpm misses (NP2kai fdd_d88.c d88trk_read, fddfile.c fdd_seeksector_common). A 2HD D88 whose fd_type minor is non-zero matches each header'"'"'s rpm_flg (byte 13) against the unit'"'"'s rpm bit as NP2kai'"'"'s rpmcheck does (fdd_d88.c `if (sec->rpm_flg != rpm) return(FAILURE);`, applied in searchsector_d88 and fdd_readid_d88): of two headers with the same C/H/R/N the one recorded at the drive'"'"'s rpm answers, a sector recorded at the other rpm misses, a READ ID ordinal whose header fails the test has no ID, and a minor-0 2HD never reads the flag.' ;;
    fdd_media_equiv) echo 'The floppy medium'"'"'s accept-time raw offset (FD-a: the raw image'"'"'s ((C*2+H)*sects + R-1) << (7+N) formed from the request'"'"'s live fields and the accepting drive'"'"'s geometry and latched on the accept edge, instead of formed from the request latches inside ST_SEEK) is cycle-for-cycle identical to the pre-change module (pc9821_fdd_media_ref.sv, the module as of ad9ed84c): over random raw images of every NP2kai size row and a D88 image on both drives, random READ DATA / READ ID / WRITE / FORMAT requests with in-range and out-of-range C/H/R/N, requests for an empty drive, remounts and ejects landing mid-request, aborts, read-only mounts, failed and slow block fetches and core resets, every output (the block request, LBA, slot and write flag, the write-back read data, present/writeprotect/chg, ready/rdy/hdrv/err, dout and the READ ID answer) matches on every clk cycle.' ;;
    fdd_media_fdi) echo 'TB_PC9821_FDD_MEDIA_FDI: pc9821_fdd_media mounts an Anex86 FDI floppy image directly with NP2kai'"'"'s fdd_set_fdi acceptance (diskimage/fd/fdd_xdf.c): a header of eight little-endian 32-bit fields (dummy, fddtype, headersize, fddsize, sectorsize, sectors, surfaces, cylinders) whose sectorsize is a power of two, sectors 1..255, surfaces 2, cylinders 1..127, fddtype'"'"'s high nibble a 2HD, 2DD or 1.44 MB type and whose file size is exactly headersize + sectorsize * sectors * 2 * cylinders mounts with the header'"'"'s own geometry, and every sector is served from headersize + fdd_read_xdf'"'"'s raw offset ((C*2+H) * sectors + R-1) << (7+N): a 2HD 77-cylinder 8x1024 FDI and the library'"'"'s 81-cylinder 9x1024 geometry (cylinder 80 included) read byte-exact at their first, middle and last sectors behind a 4096-byte header, a 512-byte header moves the body to block 1 and a 66,048-byte one to block 129; READ ID answers R = ordinal + 1, the header'"'"'s N and its sector count on several tracks; a cylinder, R or READ ID ordinal past the header'"'"'s geometry and an N that is not its N are refused; a 2DD FDI answers only in 2DD mode and a 1.44 MB FDI only at rpm 1; a file one block longer or shorter than the header says, surfaces 1, sectors 0, a sectorsize of 768, cylinders 128 and an unknown fddtype do not mount, nor (the core'"'"'s own restriction, left to tools/fdconv.py) a 2D FDI that NP2kai mounts but can never read or a header that is not a whole number of 512-byte blocks; and a headerless raw image of the 1,261,568-byte size row - plain, or with an FDI header whose size equation the file does not satisfy - still mounts as that row and reads from offset 0, while a raw size of no row is still refused.' ;;
    fdd_media_width) echo 'The floppy medium decides what it serves from sizes, and every size test gives the same answer at the TOP of its range as at the bottom, against the bench'"'"'s own arithmetic and a synthetic image whose bytes are a function of their file offset: a D88 whose declared extent is exactly D88_MAX_EXTENT (2,000,000) mounts and reads a sector that ends 472 bytes before it back byte-exact, while an extent one byte over, 2^21, 2^21+688, 2^24+2,000,000, 2^31 and 0xFFFFFFFF each refuse to mount (no format matches); an extent beyond the file (5,000 against a 4,999-byte file) is refused and the same extent against a 5,000-byte and a 3,000,000-byte file is served; a track pointer at or past the extent, one byte short of it, 2^21+688, 2^24+688 and 0xFFFFFFFF each answer sector-not-found, and a first-track pointer at or past the extent or of 2^21 and up refuses the mount; and a raw body mounts exactly when its size is one of NP2kai'"'"'s size rows - the 1,228,800 (15x512), 1,720,320 (21x512, the widest), 1,025,024 (26x256) and 1,261,568 (8x1024) rows - and not at the row size less one, plus one, plus 2^21 or plus 2^32, each served row returning its last sector (the farthest file offset of the row) byte-exact.' ;;
    fdd_mount_hold) echo 'pc9821_fdd_media LATCHES a mount that arrives while the module is held in reset and applies it, with the image size that came with it, when the module is released - so the HPS boot mount, whose single-cycle img_mounted pulse lands before the firmware pack download finishes, is no longer discarded. The module'"'"'s reset term is pc9821_system'"'"'s `reset || !cpu_run` and cpu_run is low for the whole pack download, so the pre-change tree took that pulse into the reset branch and never mounted: the guest booted with no floppy. FAIL-FIRST: the same mount with the module released (the control) is present, and with cpu_run held low across the pulse the pre-change tree answered present=0. The bench covers the ordering that matters - the image is listed before the pack finishes - and the second hazard in the same window: hps_io'"'"'s img_size is valid only while img_mounted is asserted, so the size is latched with the request and a foreign disk-sized value arriving after the pulse cannot change the medium that mounts (that arm mounts a RAW image while held, overwrites img_size with 8000000, releases, and requires present=1 and the raw image'"'"'s sector bytes; the pre-latch tree parsed with the foreign size and answered present=0).' ;;
    fdd_mount_reset) echo 'TB_PC9821_FDD_MOUNT_RESET: a MOUNT SURVIVES THE CORE RESET - the flow (mount an image in the OSD while the machine runs, then select Reset in the OSD) leaves the image mounted: present is still asserted and the medium'"'"'s sector bytes still come out through the real shared block adapter after the reset releases, because the parsed container/geometry and the slots'"'"' metadata live in a power-on-only domain while only the transfer/FSM state resets; a reset that lands IN the header walk re-arms and re-runs that walk instead of losing the mount (its img_mounted pulse already fired and the framework never re-pulses it), and the HDD slot'"'"'s latched metadata (mounted/readonly/capacity) survives the same reset; the medium'"'"'s WRITE-PROTECT survives with it (NP2kai sets fdd->protect once at the mount, diskimage/fd/fdd_d88.c:306), from a read-only file and from a D88 header protect bit (0x1A bit 4) on a writable file, across both an OSD Reset and a Load BIOS ROM (cpu_run low), with an unprotected writable D88 reading 0 as the control. FAIL-FIRST: the pre-change tree reset present and st with the walk'"'"'s mount already consumed, so the post-reset request never answered and the guest boots to BASIC.' ;;
    fdd_two_units) echo 'pc9821_fdd_media serves TWO drives with per-drive state behind one shared engine, and the real pc9821_fdc reaches both through it: with a different D88 mounted in each of the two hps_io slots, a READ DATA command on unit 1 returns FDD1'"'"'s own sector bytes and a command on unit 0 still returns FDD0'"'"'s, each drive'"'"'s D88 track table and stored sector sizes are its own (a sector at (C,H) answers the requesting drive'"'"'s image, so seeking one drive leaves the other'"'"'s position alone and a one-header track answers an ordinal the other drive'"'"'s image does not have), a drive remounted with a RAW image answers from that image while the other keeps serving its D88, and an unmounted or ejected drive 1 keeps the controller'"'"'s existing no-media answer (the deferred completion: ST0 IC0|NR|unit 1 through SENSE INTERRUPT STATUS, no result phase, no byte moved).' ;;
    fdd_write) echo 'TB_PC9821_FDD_WRITE: WRITE DATA reaches the floppy image end to end through the real pc9821_fdc, pc9821_fdd_media, pc9821_block_share and pc9821_block_io: a raw-image sector written over DMA lands at its raw offset and reads back through READ DATA, a multi-sector write walks R to EOT and answers NP2kai'"'"'s FDC_WriteData result (R=1, C+1), a D88 sector that straddles three host blocks or starts on an odd byte rewrites only its own bytes (the read-modify-write keeps every neighbouring header and sector), a D88 sector whose stored size is short stores only its stored bytes, a missing sector takes its bytes and then answers IC0|ND, a protected medium (D88 header bit 4 or a read-only mount) answers IC0|NW with no byte moved and reports ST3 WP, a terminal count inside a sector still completes and answers, a write whose image is swapped before the host takes it is withdrawn (from the engine or from the shared queue) so no byte of it reaches the new image, an ejected drive refuses a write with no host write at all, a PIO write that polls RQM loses no byte while a block is in flight, a raw write whose EOT is past the track takes its bytes and answers IC0|ND, a READ ended by a terminal count inside a sector and a DOR reset in the middle of a read or a write leave the drive serving the next command (a reset write leaves its sector unchanged), a raw FORMAT fills the head'"'"'s track with D and answers at once with no ID byte taken, and a D88 FORMAT answers IC0|NW with the image untouched.' ;;
    fdd_write_hps) echo 'TB_PC9821_FDD_WRITE_HPS: a floppy WRITE DATA programmed the way the BIOS programs it - the real uPD71071 (pc9821_dmac) reading the buffer from memory channel 2 memory-to-port, the real pc9821_fdc, pc9821_fdd_media, pc9821_block_share and pc9821_block_io - reaches the image through the REAL framework module sys/hps_io.sv driven over its HPS_BUS by a model of the ARM'"'"'s own command sequence (the '"'"'h1d/'"'"'h1c image mount of slot 2 with a bitmask, the '"'"'h16 status poll, a '"'"'h17 block read and a '"'"'h18 block write reading sd_buff_din at hps_io'"'"'s own sd_buff_addr post-increment): the 1024-byte sector written to drive 1 lands in slot 2'"'"'s image byte for byte, every other byte of the image is unchanged, the controller answers NP2kai'"'"'s success7, and a READ DATA of the same sector brings the written bytes back into memory over the same channel.' ;;
    fill_beat_uniform) echo 'every cache-line fill is FOUR WORD BEATS PRODUCED BY THE ROUTE, IN ORDER, AND A WALKING ROUTE STREAMS THEM THROUGH ITS WALK - for TRAM, the CG window, a GVRAM plane window, the PEGC register file and the absent C0000-D7FFF open bus the four beats at the subsystem'"'"'s cpu_dout/cpu_resp_valid are diffed word-for-word against the bytes each route is known to hold, a walking route'"'"'s line read must be accepted within three cycles of the request and its last beat must land inside the walk'"'"'s own 48 cycles (the pre-P3 assemble-then-split form accepts after the walk and lands at 53, so it fails this while its bytes were identical), the map route is the control (four word beats out of the backend and no line form at all), and the captured fill replayed through the real l1_icache'"'"'s line arm and beat arm installs byte-identical lines, so the L1 sees the same fill data either way, and the beat arm of the L1 replay is fed the four beats with 1-3-cycle GAPS (a window walk'"'"'s cadence), so the L1'"'"'s fill is proved not to need back-to-back beats.' ;;
    floppy_mode) echo 'The OSD'"'"'s Floppy mode field seeds bank-A 0x861E with the byte the Xe10 holds after SETUP stored each floppy disk mode - F7 at auto (1M) (the default, list position 0), 76 at 1M, 75 at auto (640K), F4 at 640K (docs/probes/XE10CFG-XE10-CFG0/CFGF3/CFGF1/CFGF0.TXT SDIPA0 byte 2) - and carries it to the CG record and mirror in setup_cfg[23:16]; port 0x00BE bit 3 reads 0x861E bit 1 and bit 2 reads NOT 0x861E bit 0, so after the ITF'"'"'s `out 0xBE,(0xBE&1)|4|(0x861E&2)` (BANK4@0x1B55-0x1B65) and the BIOS'"'"'s `in al,0xbe / test al,8` interface choice (BANK7@0x5982-0x59AB) the port reads the machine'"'"'s FF / FB / F0 / F4 (docs/probes/FDMODE-XE10-FDM0/FDMF3/FDMF1/FDMF0.TXT, FF the 1M reading after its SENSE) and 0x00BF reads FF in every mode; the two 640K modes leave the strap at 0, so the controller answers at 0xC8 (MSR 0x80) while 0x90 reads FF and its DMA channel is 3, and the 1M modes the other way round with channel 2; the field applies at the next machine reset, and a program'"'"'s 0x861E write (SETUP storing a mode) is re-seeded to the OSD'"'"'s byte by the guest'"'"'s 0x534 reboot. Fail-first by mutation (tests/pc9821/mutations.json): with the seed byte constant, bit 3 constant (the earlier `out 0xBE,03` from the BIOS), bit 2 constant clear (the retired FDD mode field'"'"'s Auto default), or 0x00BF aliased to 0x00BE, the steps fail.' ;;
    font_fetch) echo 'The text GDC'"'"'s JIS glyph fetch is ONE 8-dword SDRAM burst, not eight serialized single-word reads: driving the real pc9821_gdc_text and pc9821_memory_subsystem font path over a backend whose accept-to-first-dword latency is the machine'"'"'s own (~9 clk_sys), a whole 32-byte glyph block is assembled from ONE request in a fraction of the eight round trips the old one-word-at-a-time fetch spent, and the assembled block'"'"'s 32 bytes are still exactly the FONT.ROM glyph - read back through the CPU'"'"'s own port 0xA9 (the block is a block RAM since area-video, so it has no byte array to peek and 0xA5'"'"'s half+row walk is the honest view). The serialized cost is measured through the SAME backend with a reference one-word requester, so the two cycle counts differ only in the request shape. The block RAM'"'"'s READ side is held to the contract a burst write creates: on a fresh burst the port-0xA9 hold (cg_rd_hold, what the I/O bus waits on) must stay asserted on the cycle the last beat is written - its registered read still holds the pre-write word there - and drop exactly one cycle later, presenting the glyph'"'"'s own byte, for all 32 positions on their own bursts.' ;;
    gameport) echo 'The game port at 0x1480-0x1487 answers through the real I/O bus as the card'"'"'s register does: a write triggers all four axis one-shots, each clears after the delay its position asks for (measured in clk cycles against the IBM timed formula, and counted in reads for the count-on-read forms), the button lines are active low with stick 2'"'"'s pair moving to stick 1 in 4-button mode, a disabled stick reads as absent (0xFF), and the same connector'"'"'s six switch lines, which the sound board'"'"'s OPNA register 0x0E answers, follow the D-pad.' ;;
    gameport_np2) echo 'TB_PC9821_GAMEPORT_NP2: an INDEPENDENT differential of the game port against NP2kai cbus/board118.c, the PRIMARY reference, and NOT against the module'"'"'s own bench: the -118'"'"'s eight addresses 0x1480-0x1487 and no neighbour, the byte'"'"'s bits 4..7 = A/B/X/Y active low and bits 0/1 the two axis one-shots, and the whole byte again against a direct port of ao486'"'"'s joystick.v read expression (with this core'"'"'s one-term disable-mask correction), which is where the module'"'"'s fields come from. In every single-stick configuration NP2kai can model, the byte is BIT-FOR-BIT its board118.c:341 transform of the pad, at power-up, the instant after a trigger, and after the axes have run out. It records, one named DIVERGENCE line each, the four remaining places the core and NP2kai disagree - the axis one-shot length (IBM 22..1022 ticks against NP2kai'"'"'s own clkmax/1430 ~= 0.7 ms), the live second-stick bits 2/3 and 6/7 the OSD default leaves enabled, the no-pad 0xF0 where NP2kai answers 0xFF, and the pad'"'"'s 3rd/4th buttons not merged onto OPNA register 0x0E'"'"'s two switch lines - and none of them is asserted, because none is an RTL defect. The fifth divergence, a disabled stick 1'"'"'s buttons 3/4 leaking in 4-button mode, WAS a latent RTL defect: it is FIXED in pc9821_gameport and case 5d now asserts the disabled stick reads absent.' ;;
    gamma) echo 'The three gamma-correction curves are one 256x8 M10K per channel behind the MiSTer gamma_bus: a write to lane R, G or B at an address lands in that channel'"'"'s table, and a pixel presented on RGB_in comes out of RGB_out through that table (or straight through when gamma_en is low) exactly two ce_pix edges later, value- and cycle-identical to the register-array module it replaced across a fully seeded table, every one of the 256 indices on all three channels, and ce_pix gaps.' ;;
    gdc2_equiv) echo 'The graphics GDC'"'"'s area rewrite (the draw engine'"'"'s unit count held as leng-1 in 16 bits, the cursor advance summed in one adder) is cycle-for-cycle identical to the pre-rewrite module (pc9821_gdc2_ref.sv, the module as of 3b134fe4, with the two changes the figure engine makes to what the WRITE slice shows mirrored into it: CSRW'"'"'s third byte loads the mask with its dAD one-hot, and 0xA0 status bit 3 reads DRAWING): over several hundred thousand clk_sys cycles of random well-formed and cut-short command streams on the 0xA0/0xA2 ports (SCROLL/TEXTW, SYNC, CSRW, CSRFORM, VECTW in all eight directions, MASK, every WRITE width and ROP, START/STOP, CSRR/LPEN read-back, and random command bytes except the figure engine'"'"'s ZOOM/GCHRD/FIGD, which the pre-rewrite module never had) against a randomly-stalling GVRAM visit port, with a raster that wraps and snapshots, and one 65,536-unit draw, on streams that leave each WRITE job to finish before their next byte (the shipped module queues a byte written during a walk in its command FIFO, which the pre-rewrite module did not have; tb_pc9821_gdc2_write CHECK 8 pins that order), every output (io_dout, the scanout address and enables, the draw engine'"'"'s visit port) matches on every cycle.' ;;
    gdc2_figure) echo 'The graphics GDC'"'"'s figure engine against a reference model transcribed into this bench (MAME upd7220.cpp'"'"'s draw_line/draw_rectangle/draw_char/next_pixel - the step rules the Xe10 capture confirms row for row in tb_pc9821_gdc2_figure_xe10 - NP2kai'"'"'s gdcsub_vectp single dot and gdcpset pattern rotation, and for the arc NP2kai gdcsub_vectc'"'"'s circle evaluated exactly, s = round(r - sqrt(r^2 - i^2)) per step with r = D + 1, the datasheet'"'"'s D = r - 1): arcs in all eight octants at radii 1..100 with and without a DM start and a rotating pattern; random lines in all eight octants (datasheet D/D1/D2), rectangles in all eight directions, characters with random PRAM rows, widths, zoom 1..4 and the slant, and single dots, under all four ROPs on random old GVRAM, compared word for word over all four planes of page 0 with the cursor and mask; a figure walking past the end of a plane carries into the next plane'"'"'s words (and from 0xFFFF to 0x0000); the GRCG routes paint each dot as NP2kai'"'"'s withtdw (the tile word to every plane) and withrmw (the tile bit at the dot) do; FIGD clears the FIGS block back to NP2kai'"'"'s gdc_vectreset defaults, so a WRITE after it paints one unit; GCHRD without GC draws its FIGS figure with RA15 doubled as the pattern (NP2kai textdraw); CSRW'"'"'s dAD reloads the mask after a MASK (the Xe10'"'"'s MASK_THEN_CURS rows). The 16-entry command FIFO keeps order: a second figure'"'"'s program written while the first draws (status bit 2 FIFO-empty clear, bit 1 FULL at 16 queued bytes, bit 3 DRAWING set) lands after it exactly as the model applies the two in turn. Every figure step takes 14 dot clocks, measured in pixel_ce enables at two different pixel_ce/clk_sys ratios with the same count, which is faster than both the Xe10'"'"'s 2.5 MHz rate (GDCTIME: 3444 milli-ticks of the 2.4576 MHz PIT per dot, 29.5 dot clocks) and NP2kai'"'"'s calc_gdcslavewait charge (0.72 us, 15.2 dot clocks).' ;;
    gdc2_figure_xe10) echo 'The graphics GDC'"'"'s figure commands draw what the Xe10 drew: 1,450 rows of the real machine'"'"'s conformance capture (docs/probes/gdc-conformance/xe10-2.5MHz GDCDRAW, GDCROP and GDCGCHR, re-encoded by tools/gen_gdc_draw_vectors.py) are replayed through pc9821_gdc2 and the real GVRAM (share, arbiter, byte-lane memory) over the 0xA0/0xA2 ports - ROP select, TEXTW pattern, ZOOM, CSRW, FIGS, then FIGD or GCHRD - and every row'"'"'s GVRAM outcome and cursor are the machine'"'"'s own: the FIGD dot at all 16 dADs and all 8 directions, 288 canonical and 12 raw-DDA lines (the D/D1/D2 decision walk the machine runs, which refutes NP2kai gdcsub_vectl'"'"'s division), the A53C line pattern, the rectangles in all 8 directions including the zero-length sides that sweep 16,384 dots and carry into the next plane, the 192 FIGD_ROP and 324 FIGD_PATTERN rows (REPLACE/COMPLEMENT/CLEAR/SET of the pattern'"'"'s bit through the one-hot cursor mask), and GCHRD'"'"'s 512 one-hot PRAM-bit rows in all 8 directions, solid area fills from 1x1 to 16x16 and 17x5, ZOOM 1..16, the slanted character in all 8 directions and the PRAM start bytes 0x78-0x7F. A row whose change list is complete must match the whole of GVRAM page 0 word for word (nothing else written, in any plane); a row the suite truncated must match its listed words and its total count of changed R-plane words. The five bytes the CURD read-back answers - {EAD[7:0], EAD[15:8], 0xF0|EAD[17:16], MASK[7:0], MASK[15:8]} - are the machine'"'"'s own raw_curd after every row, and the 0xA0 status shows DRAWING (bit 3) while the figure runs and clears it when the draw is done.' ;;
    gdc2_pace) echo 'The graphics GDC'"'"'s compile-time draw pacing (pc9821_gdc2 WRITE_PACE_NS / FIG_PACE_NS, build_profile.py --gdc-write-pace / --gdc-fig-pace, 0 = unpaced in every preset) holds the engine to the Xe10'"'"'s own GDCTIME timing (docs/probes/gdc-conformance/xe10-2.5MHz/GDCTIME.CSV, 2.5 MHz GDC, mean milli-ticks of the 2.4576 MHz PIT): with the pace set, a WDAT of N words (VECTW dir 2, DC = N - 1, MASK FFFF, complement - the WDAT_N rows'"'"' own stimulus) keeps 0xA0 status bit 3 DRAWING set so that DRAWING(N) - DRAWING(1) equals the capture'"'"'s WDAT_N(N) - WDAT_N(1) to within one pace period, and a horizontal FIGD line of N dots (the LINE_N rows) does the same against LINE_N, measured at two clk_sys rates (50 MHz with a 1400 ns pace over the rows up to N = 513, where 1400 ns is within a period of the capture, and the shipped 90 MHz with 1401 ns over every row, WDAT_N0001..N1025 and LINE_N0001..N2049, the least-squares per-unit time of both row sets being 1401.4-1401.5 ns), so the busy time is real time, not a clk_sys count, and the per-unit slope over the whole row set is the capture'"'"'s to within 2 ns; and bytes written behind a paced 64-word WRITE at one byte per microsecond wait in the 16-entry command FIFO, so status bit 1 FIFO FULL is reached while DRAWING is still set (the chip'"'"'s FIFO, GDCFIFO DRAW_BURST'"'"'s status 0x0C through a draw) and the 16 queued bytes replay in order after the draw (the last ZOOM'"'"'s value lands). FAIL-FIRST: with the pacing compiled out (W_PACED / F_PACED forced to 0, the default build'"'"'s engine) every DRAWING-time check and the FIFO FULL check fail; the default build'"'"'s own behaviour is held by tb_pc9821_gdc2_write, tb_pc9821_gdc2_figure (14 dot clocks a step) and tb_pc9821_gdc2_equiv, which instantiate the module with its defaults and are unchanged.' ;;
    gdc2_scroll) echo 'The GDC2 command path at 0xA2/0xA0 for SCROLL: TH01'"'"'s and master.lib'"'"'s eight-parameter transaction lands in the graphics GDC'"'"'s two display partitions, the firmware'"'"'s own four-parameter transaction (BANK6@0x788F-0x78C5) leaves partition 2 alone as the machine does, the resulting display start address is the games'"'"' own (line*40 words, with partition 2 wrapping the top line in at address 0), the firmware'"'"'s own CSRFORM write (0xA2 <- 0x4B then one 0xA0 byte, BANK6@0x787e-0x788c) is captured and the address counter advances one 640-dot line per raster line when its line repeat is 1 (640x400) and holds each GDC line for two raster lines when it is 2 (640x200) - the step that fills a 400-line raster from a 200-GDC-line program, keyed on the slave CSRFORM and not on mode1[4] (port 0x68 bit 4), which master.lib'"'"'s own 200-line routine clears after the mode set so the two registers disagree in both directions (Thexder and Touhou shapes benched) - with the armed VSYNC IRQ still firing once per frame in the 200-line mode and the address wrapping inside the 32 KiB plane, the 0xA4 display page is bit 15 of it, and the status protocol the games and the ITF wait on (bit 2 empty, bit 1 full) reads true before, during and after the transaction, with a status read in mid-stream disturbing nothing; and the partition ARITHMETIC (datapaths.md F3/F4/F5): the partition lengths count RASTER lines with the repeat phase restarting per partition (NP2kai makegrph.c:143-167, MAME upd7220.cpp:1857-1903 - fail-first against the old GDC-line boundary at raster 2*lines1), a zero-length SECOND partition takes over at the boundary and runs to the screen end (MAME'"'"'s len==0 -> 0x400, real-HW-verified), and the CSRFORM line repeat covers lr = 1..32 rather than just {1,2}; and the SCROLL length word'"'"'s im bit - bit 14 of EACH partition'"'"'s LENGTH word - selects that partition'"'"'s address step (NP2kai vram/makegrph.c:146-148 reads it per partition from para[GDC_SCROLL + gpos + 2] and doubles the pitch at 0, MAME upd7220.cpp:611/624 takes BIT(m_ra[index * 4 + 3], 6) and pc9821.cpp:103-106 shifts by im ^ 1), which is 2 x PITCH in the machine'"'"'s own im = 0 state and PITCH at the 5 MHz im = 1 a game builds from BIOS work-area 0000:054D bit 2 (rec98/th01/hardware/scrollup.cpp:22-27) - pinned in both directions, one partition'"'"'s bit each way, so a hard-wired step or a swap of the two partitions'"'"' bits cannot pass. It does NOT cover the graphics scanout that consumes the address, the START/STOP/SYNC/CSRW/TEXTW/VECTW commands the firmware issues on 0xA2, or a real FIFO depth. Section 8 pins a same-cycle race (race-idiom #34): a configuration write whose strobe covers the frame_end snapshot edge is DEFERRED to the next frame end and never dropped, swept across the four clk around the snapshot edge with the bus idle in between. Section 13 pins the PACKED page flip: the SCROLL start word'"'"'s bit 14 is the walk'"'"'s bit 15 (gfx_walk_page - NP2kai'"'"'s packed arm walks LOW16(vad << 1) over the full 512 KiB with the page in that bit, vram/makegrex.c:199; MAME'"'"'s flashb note names the flip register - double buffering at SAD $38fc and $fffc - pc9821.cpp:108-113) and it takes effect at the frame boundary with the rest of the SCROLL block - the uPD7220 reads the SAD at the start of the display scanning process, the machine'"'"'s full-frames swap - while the planar view ({display page, walk[14:0]}) stays bit-identical across the flip. FAIL-FIRST: the old LOW15 start decode dropped the bit, so two SADs differing only in it decoded to the same walk and gfx_walk_page never set. Sections 9/10b pin NP2kai'"'"'s four-partition walk (partitions 3 and 4 are the TEXTW parameter-RAM bytes 8..15, and makegrph walks 1-2-3-4-1 until the screen is full), and section 14 pins the slave SYNC'"'"'s vertical graphics window (NP2kai vram/dispsync.c dispsync_renewalvertical: the walk starts at grph_vbp = slave VBP - master VBP and the graphics end at grphymax = AL + grph_vbp). Section 16 pins the PLANAR walk'"'"'s step: it is the guest'"'"'s slave PITCH command (0x47) byte, doubled when the partition'"'"'s im bit is clear and masked to an even byte (NP2kai vram/makegrph.c:147-151) - PITCH 80 with im = 1, the 5 MHz state NP2kai'"'"'s BIOS mode set writes (bios/bios18.c:483-487), steps 80 bytes, PITCH 80 with im = 0 steps 160, and PITCH 40 is the reset 40/80 again - FAIL-FIRST against the constant 40-word pitch, which stepped 40 bytes at im = 1 whatever PITCH said and showed each line'"'"'s two halves side by side.' ;;
    gdc2_write) echo 'The graphics GDC'"'"'s WRITE command executes against the ITF'"'"'s own GVRAM pattern test: the exact BANK4@0xFA70F stream (GRCG TDW tiles 0x33/0x55, VECTW then CSRW 00 40 08, WRITE FF FF) reads back the tile words 0x3333 at 0xA8000 and 0x5555 at 0xB0000 exactly as the Xe10 does - the test that SETS BIOS work-area 0000:054D bit 6, the FLAMEZAP boot check - and with the GRCG never armed those tile words are ABSENT, so the bit becomes correct because the test genuinely passes and never because it is forced. The write'"'"'s data path is the machine'"'"'s own WDAT_WORD/GDCWG rows: the uPD7220'"'"'s internal bit order lands per-byte bit-reversed in CPU VRAM (GDCCURS'"'"'s MASK_0001 reads expected-cpu=0080), CURS.WG - bit 3 of CSRW'"'"'s third byte - selects the WDAT word directly or the 7220'"'"'s bit-0 replication of it, and the four ROPs paint the mask bits with the pattern exactly as the STRICT capture rows say (W_0_FFFF_0000_0001 lands FF7F, W_2_FFFF_0001_A55A lands 5AA5, which refutes NP2kai gdcsub_write'"'"'s clear formula; the capture wins) - fifteen capture vectors replayed byte for byte. Multi-unit draws walk ONE DOT along the VECTW direction per unit and advance the cursor, exactly WDAT_STEP'"'"'s observed pixels (dir 0 paints word 0x0800 and leaves the cursor at 0x0828 = +40 words, dir 1 paints (0x0800,D7) and (0x0828,D8), dir 2 paints D7 then D8 inside word 0x0800, dir 3 paints (0x0800,D7) and (0x07D8,D8); directions 4-7 and the three-unit draws replay the same capture'"'"'s STEP_DC01_D4..D7 and STEP_DC02_D1..D7 rows), with the cursor write-back readable over CSRR'"'"'s first two bytes (the raw_curd EAD the machine reports); a second draw queued while the first walks lands its own capture outcome and nothing is dropped, and three WRITEs chained with no CSRW between them while the first still walks run in order on the cursor each one leaves (one 48-row dir-0 column from word 0x0800, every word 0x0001 as WDAT_STEP'"'"'s rows paint it) with a CURD written behind them answering the advanced cursor 0x8F80, as the uPD7220'"'"'s FIFO and NP2kai gdc_work'"'"'s in-order execution (io/gdc.c:347-349) do. The GRCG-RMW and EGC routes carry each unit as ONE store visit on the GVRAM port share - the route latched per draw exactly as NP2kai'"'"'s gdcpset_prepare latches its pset functions (io/gdc_pset.c:117-137) - and are checked DIFFERENTIALLY against the CPU path'"'"'s own store of the same word, so no expected pixel is ever derived from this repo'"'"'s RTL. It does not cover the FIFO/drawing-busy timing (the conformance capture'"'"'s FIFO_TURN/STATE rows), the CURD read-back'"'"'s five-byte shape, or the FIGD/line/rect/arc command space.' ;;
    gdc_blink) echo 'The text plane'"'"'s character blink (TXTATR_BL) runs at the CSRFORM-derived RATE, measured in FRAMES at the dot level against NP2kai'"'"'s own numbers. NP2kai advances ONE blink count for both the character blink and the cursor, once per `timing` drawscreen() calls, where `timing = ((LOADINTELWORD(gdc.m.para + GDC_CSRFORM + 1)) >> 5) & 0x3e; if (!timing) timing = 0x40` - i.e. the five-bit CSRFORM field {cs[2][2:0], cs[1][7:6]} DOUBLED, 64 when that field is zero (pccore.c:1476-1484) - and blanks a blinkable glyph on the one count in four whose low bits are zero (`blinkdisp = (count & 3) ? 1 : 0`, vram/maketext.c:114 applied at :309-312). The DUT'"'"'s power-on CSRFORM (cs[0] = 0x0F disabled, cs[1] = 0xC0, cs[2] = 0x7B; NP2kai io/gdc.c:1084) makes the field 15, so the measured blink is a 30-frame dark phase, a 90-frame lit phase and a 120-frame period (0.53 s dark / 1.6 s lit at the machine'"'"'s 56.42 Hz raster); a second CSRFORM (field 1, cs[1] = 0x40, cs[2] = 0x78) must make it a 2-frame dark phase and an 8-frame period, which is what shows the FIELD - not a fixed divider - drives the blink. Every window is a change-to-change frame count of the rendered cell sampled once per frame, so it is exact wherever the forced divider phase lands. FAIL-FIRST: the pre-fix RTL advanced its blink count every frame, so the blink was a 16-frame dark phase in a 64-frame cycle whatever the CSRFORM said (the reference'"'"'s numbers are 30 in 120).' ;;
    gdc_cg_dummy) echo 'The CG window'"'"'s unused low page answers the machine'"'"'s measured dummy byte 0x8F in the writable user-CG class rather than zero, pinned by the capture that also fixes the class (its odd page holds the zeroed store and its even writes are dropped, so 0x8F cannot be memory) and without disturbing the odd page'"'"'s write round-trip; and the two GDC status bytes are shown not to be the same byte, since a 2M-sample histogram has 0xA0'"'"'s bit 0 never moving where 0x60'"'"'s toggles.' ;;
    gdc_egc_gate) echo 'TB_PC9821_GDC_EGC_GATE: the EGC memory-dispatch gate `mode2_egc && GRCG 0x7C bit 7` is REGISTERED FROM BOTH SOURCES'"'"' NEXT STATE (pc9821_egc_mem_gate, opt-video VID-1) and is therefore bit-identical, every cycle after reset, to the combinational gate it replaces: the registered gate equals the AND of the two live source registers in EVERY cycle, and a GVRAM request presented in the first cycle after either source moves (the cycle a lagged register would serve stale) is dispatched by the NEW value - the EGC owns the store when the gate is on and does not when it is off. FAIL-FIRST: replacing the next-state form with the plain lagged `gate_q <= egc_gate & grcg_expand` makes the registered gate one cycle behind on every source move, so the same-cycle request check and the transparency check both FAIL.' ;;
    gdc_graphics) echo 'The GDC graphics mode against NP2kai io/gdc.c and vram/makegrph.c (PRIMARY) and QEMU pc98-vga.c: the fixed 24 kHz 848x440 / 640x400 raster in graphics mode (the 15 kHz and 31 kHz selects on 0x6A/0x6E are UNIMPLEMENTED), the CRTC registers 0x70-0x7A (the machine probe reads their reset values back, a reference disagreement the core follows the machine on), the SCROLL display-start word with the partition-2 wrap, the slave CSRFORM line repeat, and the graphics GDC'"'"'s START/STOP compositor gate. Both Round-3 mismatches are fixed and asserted: the scanout fetch is enabled by mode1[7] (the DISP) and a burst that crosses the 32 KiB plane end clamps per burst instead of reading the next plane.' ;;
    gdc_log) echo 'The display-programming log (pc9821_gdc_log, the --dbg-gdc debug instrument) puts out the record format tools/decode_dbglog.py reads: an ALIVE record (type 0x1F) at power-up and after a reset; a seven-byte 0xA5/type/ms/raster-line/value record for every GDC display command and each of its parameter bytes on both GDCs (SCROLL 0x70 and TEXTW 0x78 with their bytes, SYNC, CSRFORM, PITCH, START/STOP) while the drawing traffic (CSRW, VECTW, WRITE, MASK) and its parameters are dropped; the five register ports 0x68/0x6A/0xA4/0xA6/0x09A8 only when the value changes; a millisecond time that advances and the raster line each write landed on; with the UART stalled until the ring fills, a LOST record (type 0x1E) carrying the exact count of dropped writes once it drains; and the text-attribute run records - a fill of consecutive cells with one value is ONE nine-byte record, and a value change or a non-consecutive cell starts a new one carrying the previous run'"'"'s last cell.' ;;
    gdc_raster) echo 'The text GDC'"'"'s raster is the machine'"'"'s 24 kHz mode, measured in dots off the module'"'"'s own sync outputs: 848 dots per line of which 640 are active, 440 lines per frame of which 400 are active, which at the machine'"'"'s 21.0526 MHz dot clock is a 24.83 kHz horizontal rate and a 56.42 Hz vertical rate, so a raster of the wrong size fails while a different but still correct blanking arrangement does not.' ;;
    gdc_sync_raster) echo 'The GDC'"'"'s SYNC command OWNS the raster: the eight-parameter sync block a guest programs through 0x62/0x60 decides the line length, the active width, every porch and the frame length exactly as NP2kai'"'"'s gdc_updateclock() derives them (io/gdc.c:370-444, the only reference that derives a raster - qemu-pc98 latches the same bytes and then syncs on a fixed 18 ms), and the machine'"'"'s power-on block still gives the measured default. The expectations are NP2kai'"'"'s, computed in the bench from the block bytes by a transcription of gdc_updateclock(), never read out of the DUT; the DUT is measured only through its own sync outputs and raster counters (dots per line, lines per frame, active dots per line, active lines, the dot the hsync pulse starts on, the hsync width, the vline the vsync pulse rises on and its width in lines). Five cases: the default block {10 4e 07 25 07 07 90 65} -> 848x440 total with 640x400 active, front porch 80, hsync 64 and back porch 64 dots (the uPD7220'"'"'s field map, MAME upd7220.cpp:1243-1246 - NP2kai only sums those fields into the line), VFP 7, an 8-line VSYNC pulse rising on vline 407, VBP 25, 56.42 Hz; defsyncm15 written on a 24 kHz machine -> x = 112 characters = 896 dots and the raw 260-line frame CLAMPED to the machine'"'"'s 400-line minimum; a hand-built 550-line block (lf 480, vs 10, vfp 20, vbp 40) whose pulse rises on vline 500 at 45.1 Hz; and TXTMODE /D'"'"'s AW 64 and AW 78 blocks (C/R 0x3E and 0x4C), whose lines run 720 and 832 dots (66.45 Hz / 29.24 kHz and 57.51 Hz / 25.30 kHz) as on the real Xe10 (docs/probes/KIT2-XE10-TXTMODE.TXT:36 `ticks=000120F1` and :27 `ticks=00014DE4` against the default'"'"'s :9 `ticks=0001544F` = 848 dots), the 24 kHz x floor being the machine'"'"'s 90, not NP2kai'"'"'s gdcclk[1] minx 100. The default'"'"'s pulse width is the block'"'"'s vs field because the machine'"'"'s own 2M-sample status histogram measures bit 5 high for 36 385 of 2 097 152 samples = 1.735 % = 8 of 440 lines once its loop'"'"'s x1.048 duty bias is divided out (the factor its bit 6 carries against the 208/848 horizontal blank), where the '"'"'2.8 % = 12 lines'"'"' the tree used to carry divided the same count by 1 310 720 instead of the capture'"'"'s own 2^21. FAIL-FIRST: before the kit-2 change case 4'"'"'s line is clamped to 800 dots; on the pre-change tree cases 2 and 3 cannot move the raster at all, case 1'"'"'s pulse is 12 lines wide and case 2'"'"'s line never leaves 848; and on the tree before 2026-10-15 the default'"'"'s hsync started at dot 768 (HFP 128) and was 56 dots wide, a 24-dot back porch; and the VSYNC pin moved 128 dots after the HSYNC leading edge (at the first active dot), where the uPD7220 moves it WITH the HSYNC leading edge (MAME upd7220.cpp update_vsync_timer) - every VSYNC-pin edge is now checked to land on an HSYNC-pin leading edge.' ;;
    gdc_text) echo 'The text GDC'"'"'s character-generator paths: user CG RAM and the ANK fetch through the CG window including the row bit, the code register packing, and the tile/colour registers as readback. It also renders JIS kanji from the pack'"'"'s FONT.ROM copy as a two-cell pair (left half then right half) at the dump'"'"'s own address 0x1800 + (ku-0x21)*0xC00 + (ten-0x20)*0x20, with a spread of glyphs from the symbol, kana, level-1 and level-2 rows checked byte for byte against the dump, the pair'"'"'s second cell drawing its half with no code of its own, and a pair never crossing a line. The GRCG'"'"'s own mode/tile registers are no longer latched here (pc9821_grcg owns them and their four-plane write path, asserted by tb_pc9821_grcg.sv); what this bench still pins is the port-level fact that 0x7C/0x7E read 0xFF. No assertion here can see a colour swap in the pixel path. It also pins the port 0xA9 read answering the glyph byte of the code the guest just wrote rather than the previous code'"'"'s asynchronous SDRAM block (the stale-glyph bug a driver'"'"'s glyph loop hit). It also pins the CODE plane'"'"'s 12-bit word index: with a display start (PRAM screen 0) of 0x1000 words - further than any word the boot configuration'"'"'s 80x25 screen reaches - a plane holding one 2-byte code in every word and 0xE1 in every attribute byte must still fetch the code and draw the glyph, where a 13-bit code index reads the ATTRIBUTE plane as the code and the whole screen draws blank. It also pins the port-0xA4 display-page flip as an IMMEDIATE page-select: a write reaches the scan page on the next clk_sys wherever in the frame it lands - early on the last line, in the last dots before frame_end, and mid active region - because 0xA4 is a page-select mux outside the GDC (MAME/NP2kai/QEMU all apply it at the write, and the real-machine probe shows the tear), reversing an earlier frame-boundary latch. The machine-reset seed is pinned on this same record: pc9821_system re-seeds it from the iobus'"'"'s reset-seed one-shot (one event for both configuration stores), and because pc9821_cg'"'"'s own reset is clamped for the whole machine-reset window the walk must NOT be reset-held - a strobe landing INSIDE that window restores all 38 configuration bytes (the 14-byte record first, then the mirror'"'"'s 24) within 40 clk_sys, so the record is whole before the CPU can leave its own reset and read the ITF'"'"'s 0xFC681 marker, and then clears the ITF'"'"'s saved-table length (store 0x400/0x401), quiet within 42 (FAIL-FIRST: with the walk under `!rst_n_sys` the strobe is swallowed and the corrupt record survives the reset). It also pins the DIP PORT MIRROR at CG store 0x40..0x5B (code 0x0156), the 24 bytes BANK4@0xFC42D copies into the software-DIP ports during POST: bank A must sit at 0x50..0x5B and bank B at 0x40..0x4B - the swap the BANK3@0x2322 accessor helper encodes - each byte the machine DIP image of its row (bank A docs/probes/PORTS.TXT:258 (841E 7C 851E 02 861E 76 871E 3E) except the 0x851E cell, which holds port 0x31'"'"'s image E3 as the firmware'"'"'s own writers compose it (BANK4@0x4839-0x4849), bank B the BANK4@0x4818 factory table), and every byte the ITF'"'"'s odd-parity verify examines must satisfy it (FAIL-FIRST: the unseeded mirror is 24 even-parity zeroes, which that verify rejects at once); the same one restore walk re-seeds the mirror too, record first and whole within 40 clk_sys. With MODE FF1 bit 0 set, an ANK cell whose attribute has bit 4 draws its code as NP2kai'"'"'s simple-graphics 2x4 block pattern (maketext.c'"'"'s TXTATR_BG arm, font.c font_setchargraph, the NEC 0xF2 patch included) instead of the ANK glyph, and with bit 0 clear the same attribute bit is the vertical line again. A two-byte code outside FONT.ROM'"'"'s grid draws BLANK, as both references'"'"' zero slot does (NP2kai font/fontv98.c and QEMU pc98-vga.c kanji_copy load only columns ten 0x20-0x7F of rows ku 0x21-0x7C): every cell 0x0120 (ten 0x01, the code three Zain Soft floppy games clear the text plane with under attribute 0xE1) and every cell 0x0421 draws nothing on all 16 rasters where the dump-offset arithmetic wrapped them onto the glyphs of ku 0x42 ten 0x41 and ku 0x43 ten 0x44, and such a code still consumes its right cell as a blank pair (NP2kai maketext.c kanji2nd), so an '"'"'A'"'"' there is not drawn and a kanji after two blank pairs still draws both halves (FAIL-FIRST: the pre-fix scanout drew the wrapped glyph in every cell).' ;;
    gdc_text_regs) echo 'Drives the text GDC'"'"'s I/O registers (0x60/0x62/0x68/0x6A plus the CSRW/CSRFORM/PITCH/SCROLL commands) and reads the SCANOUT'"'"'s tile, attribute and cursor state back at the dot level, checking each case against NP2kai'"'"'s io/gdc.c and vram/maketext.c. It is FAIL-FIRST: the register-semantics deviations recorded against NP2kai - the missing 0x68 high-nibble write gate, the PITCH bit-0 mask, the solid-block cursor instead of NP2kai'"'"'s inverse-video cursor, the cursor not spanning a double-width cell, the cursor'"'"'s unmasked 16-bit CSRW compare, the ignored attribute bit 4 vertical line, and the underline gated on the visible bit - each make it fail, one named MISMATCH line per case. Further cases pin three more, each of which the earlier cases could NOT see because a single sample is blind to them: the cursor'"'"'s non-blink bit is cs[1]&0x20 SET (the cursor is sampled at BOTH blink phases, so the inverted polarity shows), the vertical line sits at the references'"'"' pattern bit 3 / pixel 4 (the mask, not just lit/not, so dot 7 fails), and the character blink duty is the references'"'"' 3:1 (the blink phase is forced, so 1:1 fails). The 255-cursor-fix additions pin the PARAMETER MODEL itself: the authentic BIOS sends only TWO CSRW bytes and ONE CSRFORM byte (BANK6@0x49cf, BANK7@0x6a45) and both references apply each parameter byte as it arrives, so an apply-at-completion model never latches the cursor address or enable and the text cursor never appears. The 303-cursor-blink additions pin the cursor'"'"'s blink RATE in FRAMES at the dot level: the machine'"'"'s boot CSRFORM (cs[1] = 0x00, cs[2] = 0x7B) makes the five-bit field {cs[2][2:0], cs[1][7:6]} 12 and so NP2kai'"'"'s 24-frame toggle, a 48-frame 1.175 Hz blink at the 56.42 Hz raster; a cs[1] = 0x40 / cs[2] = 0x79 split makes it 5, i.e. 10 frames toggle and 20 frames cycle, so the field'"'"'s high bits cannot be dropped and its two halves cannot be swapped; and a field of zero is NP2kai'"'"'s 64, not the fastest rate. Case 19 pins the 0x7C read as the machine'"'"'s OPEN BUS 0xFF (probe2 /RDBACK wrote 0x5A and read FF anyway, docs/probes/RDBACK.TXT:152) - NP2kai'"'"'s crtc_i7c returns the live grcg.modereg and is the weaker source, and the input is now driven only to prove the read is NOT the register - and the six CRTC render registers store FIVE bits (NP2kai crtc_o70 dat &= 0x1f) so 0xFF written reads back 0x1F (FAIL-FIRST on both: the old row asserted the register). Its wait for the scanout'"'"'s configuration snapshot is on FRAME_END, not on a frame_cnt change: frame_cnt is now the character blink'"'"'s COUNT, advanced once per CSRFORM `timing` frames exactly as the cursor'"'"'s phase is (both consumers share NP2kai'"'"'s one `tramflag.count`, pccore.c:1476-1484), and the blink'"'"'s rate in FRAMES is tb_pc9821_gdc_blink'"'"'s, which waits the real rate out rather than forcing the phase. The 0x68 read is the machine'"'"'s, not NP2kai'"'"'s: bits 6:0 read back the flip-flops and bit 7 (DISP) reads 0 while it is set (the Xe10'"'"'s 0x18 at the DOS prompt and 18/58/18 around an FF6 write, docs/probes/XE10CFG-XE10-P6INIT.TXT).' ;;
    gfx_engine_hold) echo 'TB_PC9821_GFX_ENGINE_HOLD: the GRCG'"'"'s and the EGC'"'"'s per-OPERATION cost measured where the CPU feels it - the number of clk_sys the M10K array'"'"'s port A was ENABLED and the number of clk_sys the CPU held its request unaccepted (the same condition pc9821_perf_msg'"'"'s MW counts) - for the shapes TH05 issues through the real pc9821_gvram with the real GRCG and EGC inside it. It asserts the FLOOR rather than a model of it: a GRCG RMW store MUST issue one array access per enabled lane and no more, and MUST NOT hold the CPU for a presentation state the array does not need (the GRCG captured the access in G_IDLE and presented it in G_ST) nor for a tail state whose only job is a later ready (a write has no response strobe, so its ready IS its completion). The current path removes both and presents the second pair of a two-pair TDW burst in place: the GRCG hold falls 2 clk_sys on the RMW/walk shapes and 3 on the TDW / 0xFF-datum burst shapes while every access count and every byte readback is unchanged. The EGC shapes are FLOOR GUARDS, not wins: its hold is its produce pipeline plus the array accesses, with no dispatch bubble at the ARRAY to remove, so the design leaves it and registers the number the decoupling lane must move. FAIL-FIRST: the access count comes from the array'"'"'s own port-A enable and the GRCG shapes hold the CPU 2-3 clk_sys longer on the pre-change tree, so the same file (which connects no new port) fails there.' ;;
    gfx_msg) echo 'The graphics telemetry record reports what its eight inputs mean and is one line: the four GVRAM path counters (the GRCG RMW walk, the GRCG TDW walk, the EGC, and the plain plane-window write) print the values pc9821_gvram computed, the DEVICE wait counts exactly the cycles an I/O request was outstanding and unaccepted and NOT a memory request, the frame and interrupt counters advance one per pulse, and the JIS row-cache walker'"'"'s skipped-walk counter is printed as the value pc9821_gdc_text produced (it is a passthrough here, like the GVRAM counters) - all driven cycle by cycle and read back out of the record, so it cannot claim a split the handshake does not support. The record is also a SNAPSHOT: its digits are the fields'"'"' values at the report'"'"'s accept (snap), held while the record is read, so moving every input after the snapshot and re-reading reprints the same bytes and a paced emission can never splice two counter values into one field (a telemetry tear); a NEW snapshot takes the new values.' ;;
    gfx_palette) echo 'The graphics palette reads the way the MACHINE'"'"'s palette does, in both modes, for the DIGITAL table entirely (its eight colours, the 8-colour mode'"'"'s use of only the low three index bits, and the intensity bit not changing the answer) and for the ANALOGUE table: the power-on entries are the ITF ROM'"'"'s own (itf.rom 0x042F, entry 2 red / entry 4 green), a CPU write through the 0xA8-index/0xAA-G/0xAC-R/0xAE-B port reaches the displayed table at the next clock with no frame-boundary commit - the dot read in the write'"'"'s own cycle is the old entry and the next dot the new one, which is what a raster palette split needs - a channel write moves only its own nibble of its own entry, the whole 4-bit index selects an entry (the intensity bit is part of it, so entry 8 differs from entry 0), and the read stays combinational so no dot of the pixel path moves. The PEGC 256-colour table is a SECOND store beside them: full bytes per channel at the full 8-bit index (NP2kai stores and renders the written byte verbatim), a registered dot-path read one clk after the index (the async form refuses every block type), and the 16-entry path unchanged by any of it.' ;;
    gfx_scene) echo 'TB_PC9821_GFX_SCENE: models a Touhou-style BUSY sprite frame - the per-sprite work TH05 spends under many objects (the GRCG footprint erase, the plane-window sprite draw, and the EGC background restore) - against the REAL src/memory/sdram.v controller and the REAL pc9821_gvram (which OWNS the plain path, the WCB, the GRCG and the EGC), and reports WHERE the cycles go: the per-sprite cost of each shape, the backend transactions it issues, and the split between the client that serializes the four lanes and the SDRAM backend the coalescer already amortised. It also runs the whole scene with the SCANOUT fetching every raster line and asserts no line loses its 2014-clk_sys deadline under that load.' ;;
    gfx_throughput) echo 'TB_PC9821_GFX_THROUGHPUT: the GVRAM store path measured where the frame budget feels it - the WALL CLOCK of a BACK-TO-BACK store stream, not a single store'"'"'s CPU hold (no longer the cost). The stream presents the next CPU store the cycle its predecessor'"'"'s ready is seen, which is z486'"'"'s own shape, so what is measured is the array'"'"'s throughput plus whatever the engine adds around it: `clk_sys per store` printed beside the array'"'"'s own port-A access count for the same stream. A store that is one clk_sys shorter must not have dropped a lane, so every shape'"'"'s access count is a FLOOR GUARD and every shape'"'"'s result is read back byte-exact (the GRCG merge, the TDW tile, the active-low plane mask). The six shapes are TH05'"'"'s: its per-frame sprite traffic is GRCG RMW (the capture'"'"'s GT - TDW - reads zero in gameplay), with the 0xFF-datum pair-burst read-skip and the two-column TDW burst as the other arms. FAIL-FIRST: the bounds are this bench'"'"'s measurement on the pre-change tree plus nothing - a store used to spend one clk_sys being PRESENTED before the walk'"'"'s first lane, so every shape is 1 clk_sys/store slower there and every bound below fails.' ;;
    gfx_walk_hold) echo 'TB_PC9821_GFX_WALK_HOLD: the GVRAM'"'"'s ONE-VISIT lane walk measured where the CPU feels it - the number of ARRAY ACCESSES the M10K'"'"'s port A was enabled for and the number of clk_sys the CPU held its request unaccepted (the same condition pc9821_perf_msg'"'"'s MW counts) - for the eight store shapes TH05 issues through the real pc9821_gvram with the real GRCG inside it. It asserts the floor rather than a model of it: a plain 32-bit plane-window store MUST be 4 accesses (four byte-interleaved words, one write port) and 4 clk_sys, a mixed-datum GRCG RMW MUST be 8 and 10, a single-plane RMW put with a 0xFF datum must NOT pay the read the old whole-mask test charged it (8 accesses before, 4 now), and an RMW store whose datum is 0x00000000 changes nothing so it must issue NO access at all (it cost 8 accesses and an 11-clk_sys hold before the walk was shortened). The access count comes from the array'"'"'s own port-A enable, and every pattern is checked byte-exact on readback so the count cannot be met by dropping a lane. FAIL-FIRST: it does not connect the walk telemetry, so the same file builds against the pre-change pc9821_gvram and every bound above fails there.' ;;
    gfx_window_gate) echo 'TB_PC9821_GFX_WINDOW_GATE: the GRCG/EGC engines'"'"' cpu_valid is gated by PLANE-WINDOW MEMBERSHIP, not only by the mode/direction owner decode. With GRCG mode bit 7 armed (0x7C = 0x80), an ordinary CPU access OUTSIDE the plane windows (a 0x0000_1000 RAM store and a 0x0000_1000 RAM read) must NOT be captured as a GRCG op: no dbg_grcg_store pulse, no grcg_cl_valid, no GVRAM array write (the phantom TDW/RMW walk into (addr & 0x7FFF)), and the read must return the RAM backend'"'"'s own data (a pattern from a behavioural backend answering 20 clk_sys after the accept) with NO early cpu_resp_valid carrying TCR garbage and exactly ONE response strobe (the pre-fix steal also left a spurious second strobe when the map'"'"'s real response landed). The same pair runs with the EGC gate on (egc_enable), whose owner decode is a pure direction term: no dbg_egc_txn / egc_cl_valid / busy cycles for a RAM access. The plane-window case is the positive control and must be unchanged: an armed store to 0xA8000 still captures exactly once and lands the tile bytes in the array, and a TCR read of 0xA8000 still answers 0x0000_00FF (the tile-compare match byte) on the engine'"'"'s own strobe with no backend traffic. FAIL-FIRST: on the pre-fix pc9821_memory_subsystem (the engines'"'"' cpu_valid gated by gvram_own_{grcg,egc} alone) sections 1/2/5/6 fail by name; the fix ANDs gvram_select into those two gates and every section passes.' ;;
    grcg) echo 'The GRCG VRAM write path in the shapes the five games issue, driven through pc9821_memory_map into pc9821_gvram so the guest window address is translated rather than assumed: a TDW zero-fill through the B window (master.lib graph_clear), an RMW fill that needs the old value (TH01'"'"'s clear) and one that does not (all-ones data), the active-low plane mask (TH04'"'"'s single-plane put), and the low-15-bit address fold that makes a store through ANY of the four plane windows reach all four planes. It also pins that a store with the mode bit clear stays a plain single-plane write, that a GRCG store is exactly four COLUMN writes each enabling all four lanes (no partial-byte-enable write, so no sdram.v read-modify-write), that a masked store writes only the enabled lanes, and that a store outside the aperture never reaches the GVRAM datapath at all.' ;;
    grcg_cpu) echo 'TB_PC9821_GRCG_CPU: the graphics-charger fill END TO END, driven by the REAL z486 executing the REAL guest clear sequence (FSMAIN.EXE @0xB1C: `out 0x7C,0x80`, four `out 0x7E,0`, then `rep stosw` through ES=0xA800 over 0x4000 words) through the REAL pc9821_memory_map plane-window decode into the REAL pc9821_gvram client and its GRCG, so the shape the CPU actually presents on the client port - the operand'"'"'s byte enables, the DWORD-ALIGNED address, the datum and the store order - is what the GRCG'"'"'s TDW burst classification keys on. The leaf benches (tb_pc9821_grcg / grcg_ops) drive that port by hand and prove a whole-pair TDW zero-fill expands to all four planes; what they cannot see is whether z486 PRESENTS those whole pairs for `rep stosw`, which is the seam this bench closes. Every window store the CPU issues is recorded as {addr, be, data}; the shapes are asserted to be whole column pairs (0011/1100/1111), the precondition the burst needs; and the guest-visible outcome is asserted through the CPU'"'"'s own read path: the cleared page reads back zero in ALL FOUR planes while the OTHER access page, filled with a different pattern and not addressed by the clear, keeps it byte for byte - so a one-plane (blue-only) fill, a wrong-page fill or a dead read path cannot pass.' ;;
    grcg_ops) echo 'TB_PC9821_GRCG_OPS: a DIFFERENTIAL conformance suite for the GRCG. The REAL pc9821_grcg (through the REAL pc9821_gvram client against a byte-enable block-RAM backend) and the behavioural REFERENCE model tests/pc9821/pc9821_grcg_ref.sv - a function-for-function transcription of NP2kai'"'"'s io/crtc.c register file, vram/vram.h'"'"'s operating-mode decode, i386c/cpumem.c'"'"'s vacctbl dispatch and mem/memvram.c'"'"'s VRAM/RMW/TDW/TCR memory functions - are driven through the same matrix of mode/tile registers and CPU accesses and must agree BYTE FOR BYTE on every resulting plane byte and on every read datum. The matrix covers the plain plane-window read/write (all four windows, both access pages, every byte-enable set), the TDW tile write (all 16 active-low plane-enable masks, all 16 colours, the CPU datum ignored), the RMW merge (old & ~data) | (tile & data) over a seeded destination with data in {00, FF, mixed} so the read-skip and partial-byte paths are both taken, the four tile bytes in B,R,G,E order with the pointer'"'"'s wrap and the 0x7C re-arm, the 0xA6 access page'"'"'s separation, the low-15-bit address fold through every window, an exhaustive byte-enable x plane-mask x datum sweep, and a randomised 2400-case sweep. The WRITE path agrees with NP2kai on every one of those cases. The READ path now agrees too when the GRCG is armed for TDW: NP2kai'"'"'s vacctbl sends a read with 0x7C = 0x80 to memtcr (the tile-compare read, window-independent, ~(OR of the enabled planes'"'"' (byte ^ tile))), and TH01 issues exactly that shape (grcg_setcolor_tcr -> mode 0x80, then grcg_chunk = VRAM_CHUNK(B,...) reads in th01/op_01.cpp:47 and th01/main/boss/b20m.cpp:1210); section 9 runs 80 TCR byte/word/DWORD/window cases through run_rd_tcr and asserts them BYTE-EXACT against the reference'"'"'s tcr_read. It also records that the 0x7C register read-back is not this module'"'"'s: the video device answers open bus 0xFF (probe2 /RDBACK wrote 0x5A and read FF anyway, docs/probes/RDBACK.TXT:152; pinned in tb_pc9821_readback_gdc and tb_pc9821_gdc_text_regs), where NP2kai'"'"'s crtc_i7c returns the live modereg.' ;;
    grcg_rmw) echo 'TB_PC9821_GRCG_RMW: the GRCG'"'"'s stores and tile-compare reads against the REAL src/memory/sdram.v controller and the REAL pc9821_gvram, timed per store/read for the four shapes an earlier measurement priced - the RMW footprint erase, the TDW tile clear, the 32-bit forms of both, and the TCR read - and checked BYTE-EXACT so the collapse to one client visit per CPU word cannot pass by writing the wrong bytes: the TDW datum is ignored, the RMW merge is per PLANE BIT ((old & ~d) | (tile & d)) with data 0xF0, 0xFF (the read-skip) and 0x00 (a no-op), a two-column 16-bit and a four-column 32-bit store, the active-low plane mask, a partial-lane byte store, and the TCR read'"'"'s per-byte ~(OR of (plane ^ tile)).' ;;
    grcg_tile_order) echo 'TB_PC9821_GRCG_TILE_ORDER: a GRCG store uses the tile registers in effect when the CPU issued it, never a tile written by a later OUT 0x7E. The GVRAM accepts a decoupled GRCG store only after the previous store'"'"'s period (GVRAM_GR_HOLD, ns-derived, so more clk_sys at a faster clk_sys), and the guest'"'"'s next instructions - four OUT 0x7E tile loads in Viper CTR'"'"'s column RLE (SGS386 0040:0298) - run inside that wait; the walk latched the LIVE tile at the GVRAM accept and wrote the NEXT tile. Expected values: the 486 bus is in order (the OUT follows the store) and NP2kai memtdw/memrmw write grcg.tile at the store. Cases at the 100 MHz bench clock: a TDW byte store, a TDW 16-bit (pair-burst) store and a 16-bit RMW store, each issued right behind another GRCG store and followed at once by a full tile reload, then read back plane by plane; FAILS on the live-tile walk, PASSES with the tile captured with the store.' ;;
    gvram) echo 'The one four-plane GVRAM datapath: a CPU plane-window store becomes one D1 column access per enabled byte and lands in that plane'"'"'s byte lane of the byte-interleaved word at gvram_base + page*0x20000 + 4*column, the per-plane byte enables gate writes, the GRCG and the EGC BOTH reach all four planes through the same port (a TDW fill, an RMW merge with and without the old value, the active-low plane mask, the four-window fold, and an EGC 0x1400 WS_PATREG fill), and the scanout fetches 80 columns of a line into a ping-pong buffer one line ahead at no more than one request per 8 clk_sys and advances that buffer EXACTLY ONCE per line tick, however wide the producer'"'"'s pulse is -- a multi-clk_sys LEVEL (which pc9821_system used to send, and which is why games bounced) must not advance it twice. It does NOT cover the pixel pipeline, the palette or the compositor, which are a later lane'"'"'s.' ;;
    gvram_arb_equiv) echo 'The GVRAM arbiter'"'"'s WORD path is cycle-for-cycle identical to the pre-rewrite module (pc9821_gvram_arb_ref.sv, the module as of 3b134fe4): over several hundred thousand cycles of random single reads, byte-enable walks and stores on random planes, pages and offsets against a synchronous GVRAM model, every arbiter output (cpu_ready, cpu_resp_valid, cpu_dout, the GVRAM port as pc9821_gvram_mem reads it (en and we, the address under en, the datum and byte enables under en && we), the client dones and the telemetry strobes) matches on every cycle. LINE READS ARE DELIBERATELY OUT OF THIS STIMULUS: P3 moved a line read'"'"'s accept to the walk'"'"'s start so its beats stream through the walk (a shorter fill, not an equal one), so a cycle-for-cycle line comparison no longer exists; the line path'"'"'s bytes are pinned against the GVRAM model by tb_pc9821_gvram_coherence and its four-beat form and streaming cadence by tb_pc9821_fill_beat_uniform.' ;;
    gvram_bandwidth) echo 'TB_PC9821_GVRAM_BANDWIDTH: measures, against the REAL src/memory/sdram.v controller and the REAL pc9821_gvram, the two bandwidths the flicker and the intro slowdown are made of - the CPU plane-window WRITE path (a stream of DWORD stores, one byte-lane read-modify-write per enabled lane on the DQM-less MiSTer module) and the scanout'"'"'s 80-column line fetch, both alone and with the write stream saturating the shared controller - and reports each as an effective MB/s plus the worst-case line-fetch time against the 2014 clk_sys raster-line budget. It also ASSERTS the two defects are closed: the scanout issues bursts wider than one dword and no line misses its deadline under a saturating CPU write stream, and the ping-pong display swap only ever follows a COMPLETE fetch (a window too short to fetch the line holds the previous one instead of blending it).' ;;
    gvram_coherence) echo 'TB_PC9821_GVRAM_COHERENCE: the write-combining buffer (8 word slots) holds every coherence the GVRAM'"'"'s single datapath promises, against the REAL src/memory/sdram.v storage shape and a golden byte image: a read of a just-written word returns the buffer'"'"'s copy and not stale SDRAM, a store to a word updates every write path (the plain lane store, the full-word GRCG/EGC store, the partial-store fill and the EGC two-word burst), every dirty word is written back before the scanout'"'"'s SDRAM fetch reads it (flush/drain, including a display-page change), a dirty victim is written back and a clean one dropped (eviction), a one-lane store preserves the other three planes (partial-byte RMW), and a read fill never overwrites a word the buffer still holds dirty (the over-fetched beat). It drives interleaved reads and writes to the same and adjacent words, both pages, and the posted-write-then-read-own-word shape, then compares the WHOLE SDRAM image to the golden model, so a lost write-back cannot hide behind a buffer hit.' ;;
    gvram_combine) echo 'TB_PC9821_GVRAM_COMBINE: measures, against the REAL src/memory/sdram.v controller and the REAL pc9821_gvram, what the word-keyed write-combining buffer does to the plane-write path - the FOUR-PLANE column write in the two shapes the sprite put/restore makes (four single-lane stores to one column, and four 16-bit stores to one column, both of which the buffer merges into ONE word read and ONE full-word write-back) against the single-plane DWORD-store fill (four fresh words per store, which the buffer cannot merge) - and it ASSERTS the coalesced shapes issue no more than three backend transactions per four plane bytes where a read-modify-write needed four, and that the coalesced bytes reach the SCANOUT rather than only the buffer. It also pins the read-after-write forwarding: a plane-window read of a column whose four planes were coalesced returns all four bytes. Phase E is TH01'"'"'s CROSS-PAGE restore shape - four plane lanes per column re-written over the same words with the access page (0xA6) toggled per column-visit - and asserts that neither the restored bytes nor the scanout'"'"'s view of either page changes while the write-backs do: the access-page change is not a fetch boundary, so it must not flush the buffer the coalescer exists for.' ;;
    gvram_contention) echo 'TB_PC9821_GVRAM_CONTENTION: a CPU plane-window store completes while the scanout is fetching, because the two clients reach the planes on SEPARATE SDRAM requesters and the store must never wait on the scanout'"'"'s line fetch -- the ITF'"'"'s own boot code fills a whole 32 KiB plane with a rep stosd while the display fetches every line.' ;;
    gvram_decouple) echo 'TB_PC9821_GVRAM_DECOUPLE: the ACCEPT-AND-DECOUPLE of a plane-window STORE. A store is ACCEPTED (its cpu_ready pulsed) in the cycle its access is captured and the array work completes BEHIND the CPU, so the clk_sys the CPU held the request unaccepted (pc9821_perf_msg'"'"'s MW condition) is 0-1 for every store shape TH05 issues - the plain dword/byte store, the GRCG'"'"'s mixed-datum RMW at 16 and 32 bits, the all-planes TDW burst, and the EGC'"'"'s 16- and 32-bit fill - against 4-12 on the pre-change tree. It is FAIL-FIRST: the file connects NO new port, so it runs on the pre-change pc9821_gvram (git stash push -- src/) and the store holds there fail every bound. The CORRECTNESS BAR is read-after-write coherency: a store immediately followed, with NO drain wait, by a read of the SAME column must return the store'"'"'s byte, for the plain and the GRCG RMW arms, and a 16-store stream must read back byte-exact at every column; a read is NOT decoupled (its data is not ready until the array captures it), so its hold is a guard. Every store shape'"'"'s array-access count is a floor guard and every pattern is checked byte-exact so a shorter hold cannot be met by dropping a lane.' ;;
    gvram_fill) echo 'A 32 KiB fill of the GVRAM plane window completes against a backend that answers the way src/memory/sdram.v documents - a one-cycle accept pulse, several cycles to the first read beat, one response per beat, and a controller that serves one request at a time - and the read-back returns the fill value, so the ITF'"'"'s stage-0x1B graphics VRAM test is not wedging in the route; and a cache-line read to normal RAM completes as FOUR WORD BEATS with no line producer anywhere, the beat-uniform fill (tb_pc9821_fill_beat_uniform diffs every beat against the bytes its route holds and pins the streaming cadence) - the old 128-bit line path is deleted end to end now, not merely dead.' ;;
    gvram_latency) echo 'TB_PC9821_GVRAM_LATENCY: the GVRAM'"'"'s per-OPERATION cost is BOUNDED BY the real Xe10'"'"'s, as a TIME floor, at MORE THAN ONE clk_sys. The real pc9821_gvram is instantiated TWICE in one simulation - 50 MHz and 100 MHz - and each of the machine'"'"'s own benchmark shapes (docs/probes/GFX2.TXT gfxbenc2 and docs/probes/GFXB.TXT gfxbench, decoded by tools/hwprobe/gfxbenc2/gfxbenc2_decode.py) is driven as the capture drove it: the next CPU access presented the cycle the previous one'"'"'s ready is seen, z486'"'"'s own shape, so what is measured is the device'"'"'s per-operation cost in NANOSECONDS, and the contract is a FLOOR: no operation may be SLOWER than the capture by more than the allowance, while being FASTER is legal, so the two clocks need NOT read the same and a divergence is REPORTED rather than failed. The expectations are the captures'"'"': the plain plane-window word write 0.1420 us (GFXB GVRAM0 WRITE=13753 KB/s, the plain GRCG-off fill), the plain word read 0.4784 us (GFX2'"'"'s NET OF CONTROL: PW4RW'"'"'s 300.0 ns/access and PLANE_COPY'"'"'s 320.4 are two measurements of one plane word'"'"'s read PLUS write, mean 310.2, minus GFXB'"'"'s 142 write half), PW4RW 300.0 ns/access, PLANE_COPY 320.4 per access and 640.9 per word, the GRCG'"'"'s own read-merge-write store 103.9 ns (it is FAST - a CPU-driven four-plane RMW is eight plane accesses, and the two shapes are never given one cost), and the busy-scene sprite at 3743 clk_sys (the decoder'"'"'s GRCG_RMW 230.7 + PW4RW 2732.5 + EGC3100SP 780.3, the three rows net of the 0xA6 page switch, which is an I/O cost this seam does not own and which the bench REPORTS as the decoder'"'"'s 5118 figure instead of asserting). Every access COUNT is a floor guard asserted exactly at both clocks - the one-visit walk'"'"'s array accesses may not move - and every store shape is read back byte-exact. FAIL-FIRST: with the three periods in pc9821_gvram_arb'"'"'s GVRAM_PERIODS set to zero (bit-identical to the pre-change tree, where a GVRAM access was priced as its walk plus nothing) every ns assertion fails and PRINTS the pre-fix value, while every access count stays exactly what it is here.' ;;
    gvram_line_page) echo 'TB_PC9821_GVRAM_LINE_PAGE: a CPU line read of a plane window reads all sixteen columns from the page the CPU presented at its accept, even when a GDC draw visit on the OTHER page is granted in the middle of the walk: pc9821_gvram_arb accepts a line read at the walk'"'"'s start, z486 then drops its request (src/z486/bus_unit.sv clears ext_valid_r at the accept edge), and pc9821_gvram_share admits the waiting visit, whose own page (pc9821_gdc2 latches it per draw) then drives the share'"'"'s page output while the walk still has columns to read. The sixteen bytes must be the CPU page'"'"'s and never the visit'"'"'s page'"'"'s, the visit must be granted while the walk is in flight (so the case is not vacuous), and the visit'"'"'s store must still land on its own page once the walk ends. FAIL-FIRST: with the column walk addressing the live access page (`c_cl_addr <= {access_page, c_col}`) the columns after the grant are read from the visit'"'"'s page.' ;;
    gvram_pagecopy) echo 'TB_PC9821_GVRAM_PAGECOPY: Farland Story'"'"'s page-to-page map copy END TO END on the REAL z486 running the guest'"'"'s own instruction bytes (FSMAIN.EXE @0x5C7) through the REAL pc9821_memory_map window decode into the REAL pc9821_gvram client, so the 0xA6 access-page write reaching the window decode is tested on the operand forms the CPU really presents: both pages and all four planes are pre-filled with a pattern that is a pure function of (byte offset, plane, page), the destination page must then hold the SOURCE page'"'"'s pattern at the offsets the routine'"'"'s own arithmetic computes (the first 200 words from dst, then 160 more from dst + 5 rows) and the source page must stay byte-identical to its own pattern - so a wrong page on either leg, a wrong plane window (the routine walks 0xA800/0xB000/0xB800/0xE000 and one failing iteration leaves one plane stale), a dropped store or a byte-enable fault each changes the bytes that must match.' ;;
    gvram_porta_equiv) echo 'TB_PC9821_GVRAM_PORTA_EQUIV: a CYCLE-EXACT EQUIVALENCE (characterization, not a hardware claim) for the CPU-request -> GVRAM write-enable cone - an 80 MHz fit'"'"'s worst same-clock family, ext_addr_r -> u_gmem'"'"'s altsyncram port-A write captures. The array'"'"'s port-A pins as the array reads them ({en, we, addr} on EVERY capturing clk_sys edge, {be, din} on every capturing write edge) must be bit-identical (and en/we idle-identical every cycle) to a golden computed from the documented semantics in independent arithmetic: the device-alias/window decode transcribed from pc9821_memory_subsystem/pc9821_memory_map, the one-visit walk and G_IDLE/G_RDC/G_RMWC tails of pc9821_gvram_arb, the GRCG capture-cycle presentation of pc9821_grcg (TDW pair bursts incl the both-pairs form, RMW mixed / 0xFF read-skip / 0x00 dead lane / holed datums, TCR read runs) and the EGC unit walk and column write issue of pc9821_egc (WS_CPU word-unit bursts and byte-unit singles, COMPAREREAD runs), with the RMW merge bytes from the golden'"'"'s own array image. The same stream through the device aliases (the 15-16 MiB mirror, window-0 bias, the PEGC high alias and the EMS-shaded window, whose silence is pinned), posted back-to-back stores with the accept stall, read-after-write same/adjacent words, scanout line ticks during a walk, mid-walk 0x7C/0x7E writes, win0_sel/sys16m_en/ems_state changes between requests, a mid-walk reset and a cpu_run stall (the array is not reset: captures up to the reset edge are kept and pinned, later ones must vanish). The altsyncram has no input-register stage (OUTDATA_REG_A=UNREGISTERED, no INDATA/WRCONTROL/ADDRESS regs), so the block'"'"'s edge capture IS the port contract this pins.' ;;
    gvram_rw) echo 'TB_PC9821_GVRAM_RW: The GVRAM storage carries a CPU store'"'"'s bytes to the plane lanes the byte enables name and returns them unchanged on a later read, at BOTH access pages and ALL FOUR plane windows, at byte, word and DWORD widths, at the start and the END of each plane, and a one-plane store preserves the other three lanes -- all of it THROUGH the SDRAM backend, whose byte-enable write is the read-modify-write a DQM-less module needs.' ;;
    gvram_rw_check) echo 'TB_PC9821_GVRAM_RW_CHECK: the GVRAM array'"'"'s CROSS-PORT read-during-write is deterministic. Port A is the CPU store, port B the scanout fetch, and they share no arbiter, so a port-B read of the exact word a port-A write commits in the SAME clk_sys is REACHABLE; the array keeps `ramstyle = "M10K, no_rw_check"` (dropping it doubles every lane at synthesis), so the module terminates that same-cycle mixed-port read with a cross-port write-forward. The bench instantiates pc9821_gvram_mem directly and drives the worst pattern - the SAME address on both ports, a write on A and a read on B in the one clk_sys - asserting the fetched word is always the whole committed word (the store'"'"'s byte on every lane the store ENABLES, the array'"'"'s byte elsewhere), over all 15 partial byte-enable patterns, a full-word store, a no-op store and a non-colliding read. It fails if the forward is removed and on any byte-tear across the four independent lane blocks.' ;;
    gvram_store_order) echo 'TB_PC9821_GVRAM_STORE_ORDER: every decoupled GVRAM store (plain, GRCG, EGC) is executed with the device state in effect when the CPU issued it, and a port write issued after its ready changes only the stores after it. The plain path, the GRCG and the EGC each pulse the store'"'"'s ready at their CAPTURE while the array takes it later (the ns-derived GVRAM period behind the previous store, or the EGC'"'"'s produce), so the guest'"'"'s next OUT lands inside that wait; the store must not read the access page (0xA6), the GRCG mode (0x7C: plane enables, TDW/RMW, the GRCG gate), the EGC gate (0x6A) or the EGC register file (0x4A0-0x4AF) at the later accept. Expected values: the 486 bus is in order (the OUT follows the store) and NP2kai performs each store atomically with the registers of its moment (mem/memvram.c, memtdw/memrmw, mem/memegc.c egc_writebyte). Cases at the 100 MHz bench clock, each store issued right behind another so it waits, and the state changed in the cycle after its ready (and checked to land BEFORE the array accepted it): a plain store vs a 0xA6 page flip; GRCG stores vs a 0xA6 page flip, a plane-enable change, an RMW-to-TDW change and the GRCG turned off; a plain store vs the GRCG and the EGC turned on; an EGC store vs a new register file, a shifter re-arm and the EGC turned off, with the next EGC store taking the new registers.' ;;
    gvram_w2b_wrap) echo 'The GVRAM array-access FSM'"'"'s two-word write tail (G_W2B) wraps its second word at the 15-bit plane offset inside its own page (aliases.md A8): the array key is {page, offset[14:0]}, so the plain b_addr_q + 16'"'"'d1 carried into the page bit at {page, 0x7FFF} and wrote the OTHER page'"'"'s offset 0, where every GVRAM path in NP2kai wraps at 15 bits (LOW15, mem/memvram.c:42,114,136, mem/memegc.c:1131,1327) and this module'"'"'s own c_col column walk already wraps the same way. Driven at the arbiter'"'"'s EGC client port with the two-word burst shape burst_fits names. FAIL-FIRST: the second word lands at the other page'"'"'s offset 0 on both pages; PASS: it lands at the same page'"'"'s offset 0 and the off-boundary pair is unchanged.' ;;
    gvram_xe10_store) echo 'TB_PC9821_GVRAM_XE10_STORE: with OSD I/O timing = Xe10 (pc9821_gvram_arb'"'"'s timing_xe10) a back-to-back GVRAM store stream is accepted at the real Xe10'"'"'s sustained store period per class, and with Fast (timing_xe10 = 0) every class keeps the period it had before the field existed. Each shape is a stream in z486'"'"'s own shape (the next store presented the cycle the previous one'"'"'s ready is seen), timed accept to accept at 50 MHz and at 100 MHz, and must sit within one clk_sys of the capture: the GRCG RMW byte and word store on a graphics line 299.5 ns (docs/probes/RWIPE-XE10.TXT, the logo wipe'"'"'s 4000-word fill, 368 ARTIC ticks = 1197.9 us) and outside the graphics lines 255.3 ns (docs/probes/XE10T-XE10.TXT:141 RMWSTOSW), the RMW dword 497.5 ns (XE10T :135 RMW32) and on a graphics line 583 ns (that row scaled by the word'"'"'s 299.5/255.5), the TDW word 168.3 ns (:145 TDW16) and dword 210.6 ns (:157 TDW32), the plain dword store 280.9 ns (:68 W32) and the plain word 142 ns (docs/probes/GFXB.TXT, GVRAM_WR_HOLD). In Fast the GRCG RMW and TDW byte and word stores, on any line, read the GRCG period 103.9 ns (docs/probes/GFX2.TXT GRCG_RMW - CTRLGC), the GRCG dword stores (walk-bound) are never under it, and both plain stores read the plain-write period 142 ns. Every shape'"'"'s array-access count is asserted equal in the two tables (the walk is untouched, only its spacing moves) and a stored word is read back in both. FAIL-FIRST: on the tree before the Xe10 table (timing_xe10 not read), every Xe10 row reads its Fast value (the RMW word 100 ns against 299.5) and fails, while every Fast row passes.' ;;
    hdi_img) echo 'TB_PC9821_HDI_IMG: An HDI (Anex86/FDI, 4096-byte front header: reserved 0, HeaderSize 4096, DataSize 34816, 512 bytes/sector, 17/2/2 geometry) and a fixed VHD (Microsoft Virtual PC: the same 68 payload sectors at offset 0 plus a 512-byte big-endian '"'"'conectix'"'"' footer whose current size is 34816) both mount on the real pc9821_ata -> pc9821_hdd_image -> pc9821_block_share stack as drives whose IDENTIFY pages and every guest READ SECTORS sector are IDENTICAL to the same 68 sectors mounted raw, with capacity cylinders x heads x sectors for HDI (NP2kai fdd/sxsihdd.c totals; the file is 76 sectors), every guest LBA translated by HeaderSize/512 for reads AND writes (a write of guest LBA 5 lands at file LBA 13 and the header bytes 0..4095 are never read or written as guest data), capacity current size/512 for VHD (the file is 69 sectors), offset 0 there (a write of guest LBA 5 lands at file LBA 5, and the footer'"'"'s sector 68 is refused by the drive and never exposed), the drive held not-ready until the mount probes have read sector 0 and, for VHD, the last sector through the block port with the read-ahead window tag low, a queued guest request served after the probes with its offset, a remount during a probe discarding that probe and re-reading, an eject clearing the offset, a raw PC-98 image (first DWORD the IPL, not 0) NOT detected as either, and every header NP2kai'"'"'s reader refuses (heads 0, heads 256) or this ATA cannot serve (bytes/sector 256), or that content detection cannot tell from a raw disk (reserved != 0, HeaderSize < 32, HeaderSize not a multiple of 512), and every corrupt VHD footer (bad cookie, bad checksum, disk type 3 with a cxsparse header at offset 512, current size with no room for the footer, data offset not all-ones) falling back to the raw image byte for byte; while the headers NP2kai mounts although the file is not exactly header + DataSize (a DataSize that disagrees, a geometry past the file or short of DataSize, trailing padding) mount as HDI with the header'"'"'s offset and NP2kai'"'"'s capacity, cylinders x heads x sectors clamped to the sectors the file holds after the header.' ;;
    hps_slot_integrity) echo 'Every sector the hard disk'"'"'s block path (pc9821_block_share -> pc9821_block_io) delivers is the image'"'"'s own sector while the CD-ROM (pc9821_atapi, hps_io slot 4) streams CD-DA through the SAME word bus, however the disk'"'"'s request lands against the CD'"'"'s transfer, and the CD-DA samples are the audio sectors'"'"' own samples with none of the disk'"'"'s words in them: the host model is hps_io-shaped (ONE slot served at a time, ONE shared sd_buff_wr/addr/dout bus, a per-slot sd_ack that is high only for the slot being served), the disk requests are issued 2..700 clk after the CD'"'"'s transfer is acknowledged so they wait in the engine while the CD'"'"'s words go by, and every expected byte is the host model'"'"'s own image (a function of sector and byte index), never the RTL'"'"'s.' ;;
    hps_slot_stress) echo 'With hard-disk reads and writes, both floppies'"'"' reads and writes, CD data reads and CD-DA streaming issued concurrently at random times against ONE hps_io-shaped host (one slot served at a time, one shared sd_buff_wr/addr/dout bus, a per-slot sd_ack high only for the slot being served), every sector the disk and floppy engine delivers is the host image'"'"'s own sector, every byte it writes back is the byte its drive buffer presented, every CD data sector and every CD-DA sample is the CD image'"'"'s own, and a second copy of the whole cluster fed through pc9821_hps_slot_demux (the shared bus'"'"'s single owner) is cycle-for-cycle identical on every output to the copy wired straight to the shared bus; every expected byte comes from the host model'"'"'s image functions, never the RTL'"'"'s.' ;;
    icache_flush_scope) echo 'The I$ half of the cache-flush migration is what the rewritten leaf still owns: its NATIVE whole-L1 flush clears EVERY line whatever the tag - a line whose tag matches nothing in the swept range, and the 0xFFFF8000 fw_high alias of the banked ROM window, are the cases a set-local clear leaves behind - because the rewritten l1_icache'"'"'s EXTERNAL invalidate is TAG-MATCHED ONLY (its own comment: there is no set-wide external invalidate) and the PC-98 platform stopped sweeping when it moved to this native port; the D$->I$ STORE PATCH path stays TAG-MATCHED - a same-set neighbour keeps its line - and the patched line'"'"'s refill carries the patched bytes out of the leaf'"'"'s own patch queue; and the lookup-vs-patch one-cycle race (src/z486/docs/coherency-audit.md C9) cannot answer a stale hit: a fetch accepted on the snoop'"'"'s registered cycle lands its tag capture on the clear'"'"'s commit edge, and the response must refetch (or already carry the patched word) rather than return the pre-clear line. Refills are measured at the backend'"'"'s accept count, not read out of the FSM. FAIL-FIRST by mutant: (a)/(d) fail against a set-local clear and (c) against a tree without the race guard.' ;;
    ide_bank1_hdd) echo 'TB_PC9821_IDE_BANK1_HDD: In the CD build of pc9821_system with hard-disk images in hps_io slot 0 and slot 3, the OSD'"'"'s IDE bank 1 field decides bank 1'"'"'s master and applies only at a platform reset: at its default (CD-ROM) bank 1 answers the ATAPI signature 0x14/0xEB, port 0xF0 reads 0x89 and slot 3 is never read; set to HDD2 (after the reset) bank 1 answers the ATA signature (STATUS 0x50, ERROR 0x01, cylinder 0x00/0x00, 0x0433 = 0x00 with nothing pending), the CD drive is absent (no ATAPI signature, its interrupt cut), IDENTIFY at bank 1 reports slot 3'"'"'s capacity in words 60/61 and at bank 0 slot 0'"'"'s, READ SECTORS at bank 1 returns slot 3'"'"'s sectors and at bank 0 slot 0'"'"'s (each fetched from its own slot only), WRITE SECTORS at bank 1 reaches slot 3 alone, each bank keeps its own task file and nIEN, a completion on bank 1 holds the IDE interrupt and 0x0433 bit 1 while bank 0 is selected until bank 1'"'"'s STATUS is read (and at the default a pending CD interrupt is 0x0433 bit 1 at either bank selection), a bank-1 read while bank 0'"'"'s transfer holds DRQ shows bank 1'"'"'s own registers (no DRQ), and an eject of slot 3 sets port 0xF0 bit 6 and leaves bank 1 the measured empty connector (0x0E/0x0F); and back at the default after a reset the CD is bank 1'"'"'s master again.' ;;
    ide_layout) echo 'TB_PC9821_IDE_LAYOUT: At the OSD IDE bank 1 field'"'"'s default (the CD-ROM; tb_pc9821_ide_bank1_hdd covers the second hard disk) the built-in IDE layout is fixed in pc9821_system whatever the build and the media: in the build WITHOUT the CD (CDROM_EN 0) and in the build WITH it (CDROM_EN 1), the one hard disk (hps_io slot 0) answers as bank 0'"'"'s master - 0x0432 = 0x00, 0x064C = 0xA0: STATUS 0x50, ERROR 0x01, cylinder 0x00/0x00 (an ATA, not a packet, signature) and 0x0433 = 0x00 with nothing pending - and the CD-ROM, when built, as bank 1'"'"'s master - STATUS 0x00, 0x0642 = 0x01 and the 0x14/0xEB ATAPI signature the Xe10 IDE ROM'"'"'s presence test reads - with bank 1 an empty connector without it (the measured empty-bank float: 0x064E = 0x064C = 0x0E, 0x074E = 0x0F); port 0xF0 reports exactly that, bit 5 = the hard disk (clear once it is mounted) and bit 6 = the CD drive (clear only in the CD build), so the Xe10 strap reads 0xE9 then 0xC9 without the CD and 0xA9 then 0x89 with it, the stock CD-equipped Xe10'"'"'s 0x89; and in the CD build a disc mount and an eject move none of it. And a SOFTWARE REBOOT resets the drives in both builds: after INITIALIZE DEVICE PARAMETERS has left ERROR at 0x00, the SHUT0/SHUT1 BSR bytes and the BIOS reset entry'"'"'s 0x534 toggle (BANK7@0xFD81F) bring back bank 0'"'"'s post-reset signature (STATUS 0x50, ERROR 0x01) that the Xe10 IDE ROM'"'"'s presence check requires, as NP2kai'"'"'s ideio_basereset on its CPU reset (devinit `drv->error = IDEERR_AMNF`) and QEMU'"'"'s armed-0x534 machine reset do; FAIL-FIRST, because the task file kept ERROR 0x00 and the re-POST after a Windows 95 restart found no disk.' ;;
    inta_bridge) echo 'The five-state INTA bridge driven through the exact protocol of its own header comments - the first INTA answers with one dummy ready and din=0, the second pulses pic_interrupt_done for exactly one clk and the PIC'"'"'s acknowledge-latched vector is read back a cycle later before ready drops and the FSM returns to idle - and the five corners that hide a protocol bug pinned: a strobe held high answers the first INTA once, an INTA with no pending request passes the PIC'"'"'s spurious vector through unchanged, back-to-back interrupts re-arm because inta_responded clears between them, the vector handed over is the one on pic_interrupt_vector at the transfer (the acknowledge latch is the PIC'"'"'s, not the bridge'"'"'s - recorded rather than asserted), and reset_n in each of the five states returns to idle without a stray acknowledge.' ;;
    interleave_200) echo 'The 200-line graphics/text interleave as NP2kai renders it, driven as one scene through the REAL pc9821_gdc_text_dut (raster, MODE FF1, text render, GDC2 line origin) + the REAL pc9821_gvram line fetch + the REAL pc9821_video_mixer, wired exactly as pc9821_system wires them: with MODE FF1 bit 4 set, graphics appear on EVEN display lines only and ODD lines carry text only over BLACK - NP2kai fetches a display line only `if ((!(mg.liney & 1)) || (!(gdc.mode1 & 0x10)))` (vram/makegrph.c:155) and its p_2i draw paints the odd line of each pair as `(q[x] >> 4) + NP2PAL_TEXT` with the graphics plane absent (vram/sdraw.mcr:199-243, the interleave row selected on `gdc.mode1 & 0x10`, vram/scrndraw.c:227). The scene is line-uniform so every active dot has one expected colour: a bench-loaded full-block glyph (attr 0xE1 = colour 7 = white, pal_makeskiptable degpal1) on the even text rows, a bench-loaded space on the odd ones, and GVRAM filled so every dot folds to palette index 2 = the power-on table'"'"'s red (itf.rom 0x042F / defanapal) - expected values from those references, never from our RTL. It also pins the FETCH side (makegrph.c:155): the gvram port-B beat count between line ticks is 0 for the interleave odd line and 80 otherwise (the tick at the end of raster line L feeds display line L+2 - tb_pc9821_video_frame'"'"'s +2 pipeline - and +2 preserves parity), and pc9821_crtc'"'"'s gfx_odd_line must equal MODE-FF1-bit-4 && vline[0] at every tick. Case 1 is the 400-line control (bit 4 clear): every line composite, so a fix that hid graphics unconditionally cannot pass. FAIL-FIRST: the space rows'"'"' odd lines must read black and the odd ticks must count zero fetches; the pre-fix scanout composites and fetches both raster lines of a doubled GDC line. The file drives the fix'"'"'s new seam (crtc gfx_odd_line -> gvram/mixer), so it cannot BUILD on the pre-change tree; the fail-first run is this exact file against the fix'"'"'s interface with the two behaviour terms at their pre-fix form (bit-identical behaviour to the pre-change tree). Not covered: the text plane'"'"'s own 200-line programming (glyphs are bench-loaded) and the one-frame-pair residual when MODE FF1 bit 4 flips in the ticks that arm rows 0/1.' ;;
    interleave_odd_vtotal) echo 'The 200-line graphics/text interleave on an ODD VTOTAL (441: defsyncm24 with one added back-porch line, y = lf + vs + vfp + vbp per NP2kai io/gdc.c:370-444). NP2kai keys both the fetch skip and the odd-line draw on the DISPLAY LINE parity (vram/makegrph.c:155, vram/sdraw.mcr:199-243 p_2i), but our scanout'"'"'s tick at the end of raster line L feeds display line (L+2) mod VTOTAL (the +2 pipeline) and the wrap SUBTRACTS the odd VTOTAL, flipping parity at the two end-of-frame ticks: the fetch gate must key on the FEED line'"'"'s parity or display line 0 loses its fetch and shows the stale ping-pong buffer at the top of every frame. Byte-exact frame over a scene where the loss is unmistakable (all-transparent bench-loaded space text; GVRAM row 0 = ITF blue itf_entry(1), every other row = ITF red itf_entry(2), itf.rom 0x042F = NP2kai defanapal; SCROLL start 0 / lines 0 never expires - makegrex.c:35-51 - with CSRFORM lr-1 = 0 so display line L shows GDC row L (deliberate: the lr = 2 pairing MASKS the loss behind its pair partner'"'"'s identical row - observed on the pre-fix tree): line 0 must be blue, even lines 2..398 red, odd lines black, the port-B beat count 80 or 0 per tick by the FEED line'"'"'s parity, and pc9821_crtc'"'"'s gfx_odd_line must carry the feed parity AT THE TICK. FAIL-FIRST on the pre-fix tree (this file builds and runs unchanged there): the pixel check at vline 0, the wrap-tick beat counts at vline 439/440 and the seam value at those ticks all fire. Expected values from NP2kai and the ITF ROM only; the SYNC arithmetic is tb_pc9821_gdc_sync_raster'"'"'s np2_sync_geometry, the +2 pipeline tb_pc9821_video_frame'"'"'s, the even-VTOTAL interleave tb_pc9821_interleave_200'"'"'s.' ;;
    interrupts) echo 'The interrupt path from the device pins to the CPU-facing ports: master ICW4 programming, slave IRQ9 reaching the vector, the cascade acknowledge, the special fully nested mode the authentic disk ISR relies on, and the atomic acknowledge (a higher-priority request arriving between the vector being published and the acknowledge edge must not leave the master'"'"'s ISR bit naming a different IRQ than the vector). No CPU is instantiated, so '"'"'end to end'"'"' means to this module'"'"'s ports. The interrupts.md F1 addition pins the SPURIOUS-vector selection: after a prior cascade ack, a master-level spurious INTA (the request withdrawn by an IMR write before the handshake) must answer the MASTER'"'"'s icw2_vec|7 = 0x0F, never the slave'"'"'s parked 0x17 - the ack event decides the source, not the stale int_acked_irq (FAIL-FIRST: the old mux answers 0x17).' ;;
    io_5f) echo 'TB_PC9821_IO_5F: port 0x5F through the real pc9821_iobus at the shipped 90 MHz clk_sys, against the real Xe10 (docs/probes/XE10T-XE10.TXT /IO): an `in al,5Fh` costs no more than the machine'"'"'s 788.4 ns (its R8_005F row, the default read class), an `out 5Fh,al` releases the CPU no later than 1404.1 ns after it starts (the machine'"'"'s write period 1744.2 ns less the 340.1 ns of nops its W8_005F k=32 row overlaps with it: the write is posted), and back-to-back writes with the machine'"'"'s own between-write work (its W8_005F control times 19.1, 41.3, 57.2, 98.5, 178.0 and 340.1 ns for k = 0..32 nops) recur every 1744.2 ns within one clk_sys at every k (the row'"'"'s flat PIT period, 1744.0-1744.4 ns), where a blocking write grows with the work; an I/O access to another port after a 0x5F write starts no earlier than that period after it, so a firmware delay pair (`out 0x02 / out 0x5F`) is never shorter than on the machine.' ;;
    io_class) echo 'The min-profile I/O class table, measured end to end through the real pc9821_iobus: a full sweep of the 16-bit port space recovers exactly two values - an 8253 WRITE 0x0071/0x0073/0x0075/0x0077/0x3FD9-0x3FDF = 21 (one 2.4576 MHz PIT tick, the write-side floor the ITF'"'"'s POST timer self-test needs), and EVERY other access = 4, the FSM floor - in place of the machine'"'"'s measured ladder (WAIT 30 > 8253 21 > default 15 = IDE task file 15 > graphics GDC 13 > text GDC 8 = IDE data 8 > EGC 7). An 8253 READ is the FSM floor and not 21, because that floor is a WRITE-side requirement and the capture reads the 8253 at the default class (its PITD0/PITD1 rows in docs/probes/IO.TXT): measured here in both directions. The IDE data ports 0x0640/0x0641, the IDE task-file/interface registers 0x0642-0x064E/0x074C/0x074E/0x0430/0x0432/0x1E8E, the text GDC 0x0060/0x0062, the graphics GDC 0x00A0/0x00A2, the EGC ports 0x04A0-0x04AF/0x006A/0x007C/0x007E/0x00A6 (including the GRCG pair) and the default are all at the FSM floor, and so is 0x005F (its read is the machine'"'"'s default read class, XE10T R8_005F 788.4 ns; its write is posted, which tb_pc9821_io_5f measures); the 46 named ports keep their arms so the inventory stays exact, and the bench prints the floor and the survivor. And a MULTI-BYTE access is priced LANE BY LANE: on the machine each byte of an access is its own bus cycle and therefore carries its own class, so a two-lane read of 0x5E/0x5F costs 4 + 4 = 8 clk_sys (that ARTIC word read is the one the firmware does 20 times across BANK3/BANK6/BANK7/BIOS.ROM), two same-class lanes cost 4 + 4 = 8 where one class plus one cycle used to be charged, and the terminated high lane of a word read at 0x7C - which the machine never fetches - keeps its one cycle and pays no class.' ;;
    io_latency_profile) echo 'TB_PC9821_IO_LATENCY_PROFILE: The OSD'"'"'s I/O timing field (pc9821_iobus io_timing_xe10), measured end to end through the real pc9821_iobus at 50 MHz: FAST (0, the default) is today'"'"'s table EXACTLY - every class but the 8253 WRITE is the FSM floor IO_MIN_HOLD = 4 (0x005F both ways: its write is posted), the 8253 write the ITF self-test'"'"'s one 2.4576 MHz tick (21 clk), and a word access two floors; XE10 (1) makes each WHOLE I/O instruction - the bus time measured here plus the core CPU'"'"'s own 8 clk_sys around it (docs/probes/core/KIT2-CORE-SPEED.TXT: `in al,dx` 12 clk in Fast around a 4-clk access) - the Xe10'"'"'s measured time within one clk and never short of it, every value the net_ns of an XE10T /IO row (docs/probes/XE10T-XE10.TXT): default read 791.5 / write 823.3 ns, 0x5F read 788.4 (its write posted, the floor), EGC 0x6A read 349.7, the fast write class 314.7-317.9 on 0x6A, 0x7E, 0x64 and 0xA4, text GDC 413.3, graphics GDC 639.0, the 8253 read 791.5 and write at the default write, the EGC file'"'"'s even read 5989.1 and odd read 791.5, 0x09A0/0x09A8 352.9 and 0x0FAA 254.3 - and a 16-bit access costs ONE class (R16_005C 791.5, R16_0188 788.4, W16_04A8 314.7; within two clk, the high lane'"'"'s walk cycle included), and `in al,dx` at 0x02 and 0xA0 sits on kit 2'"'"'s SPEED (docs/probes/KIT2-XE10-SPEED.TXT: 802.30 and 650.77 ns net) within XE10T'"'"'s own disagreement with it plus one clk; no Xe10 access is faster than its Fast one, and a third iobus whose input is flipped at run time, with no reset, measures Fast'"'"'s list before the flip and Xe10'"'"'s after it, access for access. FAIL-FIRST: before the field the bus had no Xe10 table at run time (IO_LATENCY_PROFILE was a compile-time parameter), and the faithful table it replaces charged IO.TXT'"'"'s times on the bus alone, 8 clk (160 ns) over the machine'"'"'s instruction, and two classes for a word.' ;;
    io_trace) echo 'The watchdog message names the most recent I/O access. pc9821_iobus latches the port, the byte and the direction where each access lands - a read in S_BYTE, a device read one cycle later in S_DEVWAIT, a write in S_BYTE - and the message renders that latch as IO/DR/IV, so a guest freeze loop that polls one port can be read straight out of a serial capture rather than inferred.' ;;
    io_wait) echo 'Counts actual clk_sys cycles from request to cpu_ready for one port per class (0x005F wait, the 8253 0x0071 in BOTH directions, 0x0043 chipset, 0x0060 text GDC, 0x00A0 graphics GDC, the GRCG pair 0x007C/0x007E, an unmapped port) after a change lowered every class but the two the firmware measures to the FSM minimum: an 8253 WRITE still carries the one-2.4576-MHz-tick floor (21) while an 8253 READ is the FSM floor, which is the direction the floor is FOR (the ITF'"'"'s own self-test programs a count and reads it straight back, and the capture reads 8253 count registers at the default class), the I/O-wait port 0x005F is the FSM floor on its own access in both directions (a read is the default class; a write is posted) while a write plus the access behind it is still the LONGEST wait (the posted write'"'"'s bus time), and every other class -- the chipset default, both GDC idle polls, the GRCG pair and an unmapped port -- is now the 4-cycle FSM floor, so the machine'"'"'s measured ladder (default 15 > graphics GDC 13 > text GDC 8 > EGC 7, with the GRCG pair at the EGC class) is no longer modelled. Also checks the responder contract on every access: the response must arrive on a later cycle than the accept, never in it.' ;;
    iobus) echo 'The I/O bus: the A20 gate (masked at power-on, and moved only by 0xF0/0xF2/0xF6 -- the system PPI'"'"'s port C is a plain latch and not a second control), the 0x534 soft reset (only bit 0 requests one, and it masks A20), the ARTIC timestamp counter, the flush-pulse log over a full sweep, POST writes, and open-bus behaviour. It also decodes 0x043F'"'"'s 0x2 nibble into the machine'"'"'s three measured EMS-window states: 0x20 the identity (the ordinary VRAM planes, which the BIOS'"'"'s own mode-set stub writes and the ITF writes back after its test), 0x22 the 64 KiB RAM page, and anything else unmapped when written from the page, while written from the identity it keeps the planes with no flush (docs/probes/DMAROUTE-XE10.TXT cases E1/E2). Further cases pin: the FDC 1.44M/RPM register 0x04BE is decoded (rpm[reg144[6:5]] | 0xFE, NP2kai io/fdc.c:1117-1134) and stays out of the unmapped telemetry, and the 0xF2 read tracks the A20 state (0xFE released / 0xFF masked, NP2kai cpuio_if2) instead of the old constant 0xFE. The word-read cases pin: a 16-bit read at 0x7C answers 0xFF:the-byte and never fetches 0x7D (the Xe10'"'"'s `in ax,7Ch`, docs/probes/XE10T-XE10-AUDIT.TXT W007C `w1=FFFF`), while 0x7E keeps the byte split with 0x7F'"'"'s open-bus 0xFF high byte and byte reads at 0x7C/0x7D are untouched -- FAIL-FIRST on the 0x7C word read alone (0x00C3, NP2kai'"'"'s minus_term, where the machine'"'"'s shape answers 0xFFC3). The machine-reset seed: the software-DIP store is walked back to its power-up image by the machine-reset strobe (the same `reset || !cpu_run` this module'"'"'s other configuration latches re-sample), instead of surviving as the previous run left it - otherwise a reset brings the ITF'"'"'s SET THE SOFTWARE DIP-SWITCH message back - and the CPU is held in its reset class for the whole 24-byte walk (`cpu_reset_req`, turned into the CPU'"'"'s reset_n by a real pc9821_cpu_reset_gate instance in this bench), so no read can land on a half-seeded bank. The walk starts at the reset ASSERTION, so a window longer than the walk - every real reset: a reload'"'"'s seconds, an OSD reset'"'"'s SDRAM calibration - finishes inside it and asks for no hold at all, which is what keeps the boot golden exact, and a reset pulse SHORTER than the walk is checked from the other side (the hold is requested and the walk is observable with the store still partly corrupt, so that hold check is not vacuous). FAIL-FIRST: before the change the corrupt byte survives a platform reset. And the memory-switch strap cell 0x79 - the machine'"'"'s stored extended-memory size in MiB - answers the OSD RAM size as a read fallback (the recorded derivation: total MiB minus 2, the inverse of the MEASURED 0x1E at 32 MiB), 0x0E at the 16 MiB default and 0x1E at 32 MiB, until the guest writes its own byte and wins for good; FAIL-FIRST, because the pre-fix threshold answered 0x00 at every guest below 18 MiB. And the ITF'"'"'s cold-start flag 0x439 reads 0x00 out of a machine reset (BANK4@0x40D6-0x40DF takes the cold branch only on 0x00; the measured 0xB4 is the ITF'"'"'s own write, BANK4@0x40EC-0x40F5) and keeps a written 0xB4 through out 0xF0'"'"'s CPU reset; FAIL-FIRST, because the core answered 0xB4 at reset, the ITF took its warm branch and the OSD'"'"'s held HELP never reached SETUP. And a software REBOOT re-enters through the ITF: with the BIOS top mapped (a DOS prompt) and Windows 95 WIN.COM'"'"'s reboot stub replayed byte for byte (port 0x37 0x92/0x07/0x0B/0x0F/0x08, so SHUT0 = SHUT1 = 1), its out 0xF0 requests a CPU reset and leaves the BIOS top mapped (NP2kai io/cpuio.c cpuio_of0 moves no bank), and the BIOS reset entry'"'"'s 0x534 toggle (BANK7@0xFD81F, read 0x7E, write 0x7F) requests a reset AND maps the ITF (top_bank 8), which is where every reference lands next - the same entry'"'"'s own trampoline `out 0x43D,0 / jmp FFFF:0000` (BANK7@0xFD826-0xFD83D) under NP2kai, which decodes no 0x534, and QEMU'"'"'s machine reset (pc98_mem_reset: `s->top_bank = BANK_ITF`); FAIL-FIRST, because a reset that kept the BIOS top re-entered FD80:0000 with the SHUT bits still set and looped on 0x534 forever behind the blanked GDCs - the black screen after `exit` from Windows 95'"'"'s MS-DOS mode. The same 0x534 reboot returns the rest of the memory-map latch set to its machine-reset values, as QEMU'"'"'s pc98_mem_reset does on that reset: the 0xD8000 IDE ROM gate open and its select at the IDE ROM (0xFC), the BIOS RAM gate and the IDE work-RAM gate shut, window 0 at 0x08 and window 1 at 0x0A, the top-window shadow disabled, and ONE soft_reboot pulse (QEMU restores the stored drive-bitmap byte, `s->f8e90_value = s->f8e90_reset`), while out 0xF0 pulses none; FAIL-FIRST, because the latches kept the DOS session'"'"'s values. 0x9892 and 0x9894 read F0 out of reset and each latches bit 3 from a write whose high nibble is 0 (docs/probes/KIT2-XE10-RISKY-989.TXT: `W989 9892 08 -> ... 9892=F8`, while 55, AA, FF and the put-back F0 leave the byte as it was, each port on its own) and a 00 write clears it (the POST'"'"'s bit-3 branch, BANK4@0x4AF5-0x4AFE).' ;;
    iobus_word_term) echo 'TB_PC9821_IOBUS_WORD_TERM: a 16- or 32-bit IN at a byte port answers as the real Xe10 does (docs/probes/XE10T-XE10-AUDIT.TXT, the W/P/D rows of XE10T /AUDIT), through the real pc9821_iobus: at an even port of the terminated set (0x30-0x36/0x40-0x46 even and 0x7C with high byte 0xFF; 0x20, 0x22, 0x26, 0x60-0x7A and 0xA0-0xAC even with high byte 0x00, while 0x24 splits: docs/probes/KIT2-XE10-AUDIT2.TXT W0024 `b0=FF b1=0E w1=0EFF`) port P is read and P+1 is never fetched (`in ax,40h` does not read the keyboard'"'"'s 0x41, `in ax,70h` leaves the PIT'"'"'s 0x71 alone, `in ax,7Ch` answers 0xFF:P, `in ax,20h` answers 0x00:P, `in eax,70h` is two terminated words); every other word read is two byte reads P and P+1 with their side effects (`in ax,01h` and `in ax,00h` each toggle the DMAC byte flip-flop, `in ax,71h` reads 0x71 and 0x72, `in ax,0F6h`, `in ax,0D0h` and `in ax,0ADh` answer their two bytes), the set applies only when port bits 11:10 are clear (`in ax,1070h` is terminated, `in ax,0470h` byte-splits), and byte reads are never terminated.' ;;
    iowait_release) echo 'A back-pressured device write is released within 2 cycles of the device freeing its buffer - the S_IOWAIT re-check runs EVERY CYCLE once the bus-time class hold (io_hold) has expired, and the class hold itself is untouched (the strobe is never early). FAIL-FIRST: on the pre-fix iobus the 11-bit io_hold wraps 0 -> 2047 under a still-high device_wr_wait, so the write re-checks the wait only once per 2048 cycles and a 0xA46C write whose wait outlasts the hold lands ~2047 dead cycles after the device freed up. This bench sweeps the wait length around the wrap point (1/2/3/5 cycles, the expiry crossing, and 64/200 cycles past it) and floods 16 back-to-back writes against a device busy 7 cycles of every 8; both name the miss and fail by thousands of cycles on the pre-fix tree. Warrant: pc9821_iobus.sv'"'"'s own S_DEVWAIT contract (the read wait is re-checked every cycle - the pattern F1 should follow) and NP2kai'"'"'s direction (a write is applied the moment the device can take it); the bounds are the class'"'"'s own FSM shape (IO_MIN_HOLD = 4), not a capture.' ;;
    irq_share) echo 'TB_PC9821_IRQ_SHARE: the PC-9801-86 board'"'"'s INT5 line (IRQ12, the slave 8259'"'"'s IR4) carries the OPNA'"'"'s timer interrupt and the 86-PCM'"'"'s buffer interrupt, and every OPNA timer event is its own request even while the 86-PCM holds the line: with 0xA468 = 0xA0 (the PCM interrupt enabled, the buffer empty - the state Metajo SP'"'"'s PCML leaves after Riql'"'"'s PCM driver) every Timer A overflow reaches the PIC and is answered with vector 0x14, as NP2kai raises each timer event itself (sound/opntimer.c fmport_a: `pic_setirq(opna->s.irq);`) and as the real PC-9821Xe10 behaves, which plays Metajo SP'"'"'s timer-driven intro after Riql (docs/probes/KIT2-XE10-RUNLOG.TXT steps 06 and 07); a PCM event while the OPNA holds its flag is likewise a new request (sound/pcm86c.c pcm86_cb: `pic_setirq(pcm86->irq);`); an event from a low line reaches the PIC in the same cycle as before (no added latency); two events in one cycle are one request and an event hidden by the other source drops the line for exactly one cycle. FAIL-FIRST: with the two lines plainly ORed (the bench built with -DPRE_IRQ_SHARE, the system'"'"'s wiring before the change) no Timer A overflow is delivered while the PCM line is high.' ;;
    irq_withdraw) echo 'IRQ9 is WITHDRAWN when the 0x064E status read lowers the IDE line and IRQ1 likewise on the keyboard line'"'"'s falling edge (the machine measured post-ack IRR bit clear on both, docs/probes/IRQWD.TXT), IRQ15 (the calendar) is withdrawn while masked when its line falls - at a tick'"'"'s short low pulse, latched again at the pulse'"'"'s end, and not at a 0x128 read (docs/probes/KIT2-XE10-AUDIT2.TXT CALIRR) - and delivers one INT 17h per tick with 0x128 never read (docs/probes/XE10T-XE10-AUDIT.TXT, the CAL and CASC rows); the unmask after a withdrawn poll delivers nothing and after a surviving one exactly one INT/INTA. FAIL-FIRST: the old pure edge latch delivered a phantom IRQ9 here. IRQ1 is ALSO withdrawn while UNMASKED when the 0x41 read lowers the line, so a byte the guest polled while the request was latched is not dispatched a second time (the guest would read the stale data register and queue a duplicate key); the positive control still delivers it once while the line is high at the acknowledge. IRQ11 (the 1 MB FDC, the real pc9821_fdc) is WITHDRAWN while masked when its line falls: the real Xe10'"'"'s XE10T /FDC run (IR11 masked throughout; RECALIBRATE, SEEKs and SENSE INTERRUPT STATUS, READ ID, READ DATA, a final SENSE INTERRUPT STATUS drain) takes NO interrupt when the slave IMR is restored (docs/probes/XE10T-XE10-FDC.TXT `I FDC irq11_taken=0`), so a RECALIBRATE completion drained by SENSE INTERRUPT STATUS while masked delivers nothing at the unmask, while one left pending delivers exactly one INT 13h. FAIL-FIRST: the pure edge latch held the drained completion and delivered a phantom INT 13h (the core'"'"'s own capture read irq11_taken=1). And the cascade follows the Xe10'"'"'s XE10T /AUDIT CASC rows: the master'"'"'s IR7 is withdrawn when the slave'"'"'s request goes away (IRQ12 masked at the slave, or its line lowered with IRQ12 open, which withdraws the slave'"'"'s own unmasked IRQ12 request too), and STI then delivers nothing; FAIL-FIRST, the master held IR7 and delivered a spurious interrupt. And the master'"'"'s IR6 (INT2, the MPU-PC98'"'"'s line) is withdrawn while masked when its line falls, as the Xe10 with an MPU-PC98 shows (docs/probes/MPUTEST-XE10.TXT `IRQ imr=757D before=1001 pend=9841 after=9801`: the masked IR6 set by the card'"'"'s ACK clears when the ACK is read), so the unmask delivers nothing while an unread request delivers one INT 0Eh; FAIL-FIRST, the master held the latch and delivered it.' ;;
    kbd) echo 'MiSTer Set-2 keyboard input, the uPD8251 interface, keyboard commands and the FIFO, including NP2kai keyboard_ctrl()'"'"'s control-queue clear: a command sent while an unread command response is pending DISCARDS it (keybrd.ctrls = 0 on the leading FA/FC), so the ITF'"'"'s back-to-back 0x00-then-0x9F identity burst reads FA A0 80 and its stage-0x27 probe sets the 0x0481 bit-6 extended-keyboard flag.' ;;
    kbd_codes) echo 'Every Set-2 key the controller maps reaches the guest as the right make and break byte, the modifier keys (SHIFT/CTRL/GRPH/KANA/NFER/XFER) pass through in the order pressed, a re-sent make of an already-held key emits no second byte, and the 0x9D LED query and 0x43 status report what NP2kai reports. The Round-3 remaps (LAlt->NFER, RAlt->XFER, RCtrl->GRPH) and the newly decoded RO/Yen/PrintScreen/Win/Menu keys are asserted and the 0x96 ID byte is 0x83; both Shift keys reach NEC 0x70; KANA (NEC 0x72) is bound to the Menu/Application key and its toggle is driven alongside CAPS; read in the JIS layout (NP2kai sdlcnv106 is a JIS 106-key map), the JIS ] key (set-2 0x5D) reaches the PC-98 ] key 0x28, and the per-key count (99 agree, 1 wrong value, 9 controller-only, 2 unmapped, 2 mapped by neither) is pinned.' ;;
    kbd_layout) echo 'In the JIS layout the JIS ] key (set-2 0x5D) gives the PC-98 ] key 0x28, @ (0x54) gives 0x1A, [ (0x5B) gives 0x1B and half/full-width (0x0E) gives nothing (NP2kai sdlcnv106); in the US layout [ gives 0x1B, ] gives 0x28, \ gives the YEN key 0x0D and ` gives 0x1A (NP2 winkbd.cpp key106[] by US virtual key); in both, KEY_YEN gives 0x0D, KEY_RO and KEY_102ND give 0x33, muhenkan/henkan give NFER/XFER, katakana/hiragana toggles KANA, F11/F12 give VF1/VF2 and NumLock/ScrollLock give keypad = and keypad ,; and a layout key held across a layout change breaks as the key it made, its re-sent make stays silent, and the next press uses the new layout.' ;;
    kbd_led) echo 'The PC-98 lock state reaches the MiSTer HOST keyboard'"'"'s LEDs in hps_io'"'"'s {scrolllock, numlock, capslock} order, on a CAPS lock toggle and on the host'"'"'s 0x9D+0x7x set command, and agrees with the 0x9D+0x60 query.' ;;
    kbd_pace) echo 'TB_PC9821_KBD_PACE: the keyboard presents one byte per transfer tick (NP2kai io/serial.c keyboard_changeclock: pccore.realclock / 1920 = 2,457,600 / 1920 = 1280 byte-times/s), not one clk after the previous byte is read. The first byte after idle is still immediate; after a delivery the next FIFO byte is presented only after XFER_TICK_CYCLES, so a guest interrupt hook that reads port 0x41 itself BEFORE chaining (a disk image'"'"'s HSB.EXE INT 09h hook at 0x2CE5/0x2C3D, which tracks the 0x70-0x74 modifiers) and then lets the chained BIOS handler read 0x41 again sees the SAME stale latch both times, instead of the peek consuming the next queued make or break and leaving a modifier stuck. A host burst is delivered with consecutive presentations a full tick apart and no byte is lost or reordered;  slow/ordinary typing (an idle longer than a tick) still presents its first byte immediately. The 8251 bit-3 keyboard reset clears the divider (the first byte after it is immediate again) while keeping the held-key dedup epoch, so a held key is never forgotten and its break still arrives.' ;;
    kbd_pipeline) echo 'The keyboard'"'"'s event pipeline depth is a PINNED CONTRACT, not a comment: a ps2_key toggle edge posts its FIFO byte on the THIRD clk_sys after the toggle (clk 1 = the translation ROM'"'"'s registered read, clk 2 = the dedup table'"'"'s clocked read, clk 3 = the event decision'"'"'s enqueue), the deliver follows one clk later, and NOTHING is enqueued or delivered on the first two clk_sys. The event STREAM is unchanged across that pipeline: a burst of makes and breaks - including a duplicate make of a key pressed on the previous clk, a locking key, and a back-to-back make/break pair for one key - reaches the guest as exactly the same ordered byte sequence, so the T6 registers-to-MLAB trade moved the decision'"'"'s cycle and nothing else.' ;;
    kbd_repeat) echo 'A held key typematically auto-repeats: one break then one make after the delay, then a break+make pair every interval, until the key breaks; a released key stops and a KBEX_NONREP key never repeats (the modifiers, locks, F1-F10, RWIN and APP - the keystat.tbl table leaves LWIN repeatable, and this bench pins that it repeats where RWIN does not). The delay and interval are parameters here, set from NP2kai keystat.c defaults.' ;;
    kbd_repoke) echo 'While a delivered byte waits unread the IRQ line holds (RXRDY'"'"'s own level - qemu pc98-kbd.c raises it from KST_RXRDY and tb_pc9821_kbd_reset pins the hold) but re-strokes once per keyboard transfer tick (NP2kai io/serial.c keyboard_callback pokes pic_setirq(1) at every xferclock tick; keyboard_changeclock: realclock/1920 -> 1280 byte-times/s), so an acknowledge that is NOT followed by a 0x41 read is re-requested within one tick by the edge-latched PIC and the re-strokes stop the moment the byte is read; and the receive status has ONE next-state, so a same-clk data read + overflow cannot resurrect RXRDY after the guest'"'"'s read (the old last-wins chain handed the guest its already-read byte again) while the overflow flag still lands.' ;;
    kbd_reset) echo 'The 8251 command register'"'"'s bit 3 resets the keyboard on its 1-to-0 transition: the receive status, the IRQ, the pending command responses and the queued key bytes are all discarded, which is the transition the ITF'"'"'s own POST performs and which NP2kai implements as keyboard_resetsignal().' ;;
    kbd_reset_race) echo 'The 8251 bit-3 keyboard reset is a priority fold over the receive side'"'"'s same-cycle updates (the Single-Cycle Event Discipline): a key enqueued one cycle before the reset write delivers INTO the reset write'"'"'s cycle, and the reset must still win - fifo indices zeroed, no RXRDY/IRQ resurrection with the pre-reset byte, rx_latch untouched - and the next key must still be the next key, while a key held across the reset keeps its dedup state (NP2kai keeps keystat.ref), so a duplicate make stays silent and its break still delivers.' ;;
    kbd_resp_race) echo 'A command response loaded in the exact clk the old response'"'"'s last byte delivers must not be lost (defer-not-lose): the in-flight byte still reaches the guest, the new FA A0 83 of the 0x96 ID command is installed intact (resp_count 3, 0x83 at the shift top) instead of being clobbered by the deliver'"'"'s bookkeeping, and the guest sees the whole ack sequence byte by byte.' ;;
    kbd_single_service) echo 'One delivered byte produces exactly ONE interrupt request and the request dies with its 0x41 read: the re-poke'"'"'s one-clk dip may not arm the edge-latched PIC in a normal service'"'"'s dispatch-to-read window (the BIOS IRQ1 handler at BIOS.ROM 0x16640 reads 0x41 without checking RXRDY - so any request that survives the read re-processes the stale byte as a second keypress) and a read landing on the dip'"'"'s recovery cycle cancels that edge atomically - while a genuinely unread byte still re-fires (tb_pc9821_kbd_repoke pins that side).' ;;
    kbd_stop) echo 'No host stream puts a STOP make (0x60) in the guest'"'"'s key stream unless the user pressed the one key NP2kai maps to STOP (Pause): an F11 tap must reach the guest as VF1 (0x52/0xD2) and a NumLock tap (the non-extended set-2 0x77) as keypad = (0x4D/0xCD), never as STOP, and the framework'"'"'s normalized Pause token (Main_MiSTer sends the E1 14 77 E1 F0 14 F0 77 make sequence at key RELEASE with no break, user_io.cpp "pause does not have a break code"; hps_io normalizes it to one {pressed,extended,0x77} event, sys/hps_io.sv:325) must reach the guest as the NP2kai tap pair 0x60 make + 0xE0 break and then SILENCE - no stuck STOP make and no typematic flood of a key that can never break.' ;;
    kbd_warmreset) echo 'The keyboard'"'"'s warm-reset state: key_down is cleared by the MODULE reset, so the first make after a card reset is delivered instead of swallowed as a re-send of a held key, while the 8251 bit-3 keyboard reset KEEPS it (NP2kai keyboard_resetsignal keeps keystat.ref), so a held key'"'"'s break still reaches the guest and a duplicate make stays silent; the PS/2 event edge detector tracks through reset, so a completed event held on ps2_key with its toggle bit set cannot replay as a fresh key at reset release (the L2 replay); and ctrl_writes and the dbg telemetry are reset state - the first two control writes after a reset land in dbg_ctrl0/dbg_ctrl1 and none of the telemetry is X in 4-state sim (run under iverilog for the fail-first X-lock).' ;;
    l1_alias_fill) echo 'The z486 L1s hold no line for the platform'"'"'s unmapped addresses and high alias windows (aliases.md A1/F6): the tags carry phys[26:0] only, so X and X + k*0x0800_0000 share tag+set and one cached line served both encodings - a cached open-bus fill at 0x0800_1000 poisoned (or was poisoned by) the RAM line at 0x0000_1000, and the I-cache'"'"'s fw_high/peg_high windows at 0xFFFF8000/0xFFF00000 shared lines with 0x07FF8000/0x07F00000. The fix keeps open-bus and the high alias windows OUT of the fill path (ram_cache_top = the end of guest RAM; D served direct, I by a pass-through line fill that installs nothing), so every cached encoding has a unique [26:0] key - the property widened 32-bit tags provide, without growing the tag arrays. FAIL-FIRST: the RAM read-back after an open-bus fill, the twin'"'"'s read after a cached store, and both directions of the fw_high/peg_high pairs fail on the pre-fix behaviour.' ;;
    l1_counters) echo 'The two L1 caches'"'"' MISSES ARE THE FILLS, counted from the FSM'"'"'s OWN STATE REGISTER (the rewritten z486 exports no L1 observation port): against the real src/z486/l1_cache.sv and src/z486/l1_icache.sv at the platform'"'"'s deliberate 2 KiB (SET_BITS=5), a warm loop that fits the cache hits, a stream that misses every line misses, the S_FILL entries equal the fill requests launched on the memory port, and the lookups are the S_LOOKUP entries whose arm actually consults tags - while an uncacheable access (the D-cache'"'"'s bypass arm; the I-cache'"'"'s non-installing line read, fill_uncached_r, since upstream 6613858 removed its S_BYPASS_WAIT, which this bench counts on its own and checks it takes exactly once) and a protection-fault write (the req_protect_write arm) produce NO lookup and NO miss at all, so the counters cannot inflate the miss rate in the region the aperture and MMIO traffic live.' ;;
    line_doubler) echo 'The analog line doubler (pc9821_line_doubler) sends every GDC line twice at twice the GDC'"'"'s line rate, at the guest'"'"'s own frame rate, for whatever raster the guest'"'"'s SYNC block programs: driven by the real pc9821_crtc with the dot enable on every other clock and the doubled enable on every clock, the doubled output'"'"'s line period is HALF the input'"'"'s (HTOTAL clocks against 2 x HTOTAL), its frame is 2 x VTOTAL lines with 2 x VACTIVE active lines of HACTIVE dots, its hsync is HSYNC_W samples and its vsync 2 x VSYNC_W lines, and output active lines 2k and 2k+1 both carry exactly input active line k'"'"'s pixels (a per-line checksum over position and colour) - every figure computed in the bench from the SYNC block bytes by tb_pc9821_gdc_sync_raster'"'"'s transcription of NP2kai'"'"'s gdc_updateclock() (io/gdc.c:370-444), never read out of the DUT. Three blocks: the default defsyncm24 (848x440, 640x400 -> 49.66 kHz, 880 lines, 56.42 Hz), defsyncm15 (an 896-dot line) and a hand-built 550-line block; and with the runtime enable off the output is the input itself (2 x HTOTAL clocks a line, VTOTAL lines). FAIL-FIRST: with the doubler absent (the input passed through) every doubled figure is off by two, and a doubler that replays two different lines fails the content check.' ;;
    memmap_ref) echo 'Every window the PC-9821 memory map decodes is driven against the reference decode and the RTL'"'"'s answer is recorded: the first-megabyte RAM, window 0'"'"'s 0x0461 alias (the identity, the 0x0C absent area and the 0x0E ROM window), the A0000-BFFFF text/CG/plane windows, the 0xD8000 option-ROM window and its 8 KiB byte routing, the 0xC0000-D7FFF absent area and the 0xCC000-0xCFFFF PC-9801-86 SOUND.ROM window carved out of it, the E8000-F7FFF lower BIOS, the F8000-FFFFF banked ROM window (every bank selection plus the 0xFFFF8000 top-of-4G alias), the 0x043F EMS page window, the 0xF00000 PEGC window, the 0x043B system-space split (PEGC VRAM, the F80000-F9FFFF hole, and the FA0000-FFFFFF mirror onto the low routes), the open-bus responder'"'"'s phases, and window 0'"'"'s own 0x0A page (RAM of its own on the machine, docs/probes/DMAROUTE-XE10.TXT W1/W2, not QEMU'"'"'s VRAM overlay), the E plane at 0xE0000 only in the 16-colour mode (the digital-mode slot is open bus, docs/probes/DMAROUTE-XE10.TXT P4 view V1 and WIN.TXT 0461=0E); the references are qemu-pc98 hw/i386/pc98-mem.c (the memory map) and NP2kai i386c/cpumem.c + mem/memvram.c + mem/memems.c, with the machine'"'"'s own probes (docs/probes/WIN.TXT, EMS.TXT) where they disagree. The PEGC 256-colour window arms are driven the same way (pegc-scope-REPORT.md 4 row 2): the packed windows 0xA8000/0xB0000 splice to peg_base + {bank, offset[14:0]} and shadow the EMS overlay entirely, 0xB8000-0xBFFFF is not connected, 0xE0000-0xE7FFF is the register file'"'"'s byte port under the view, plane mode (0xE0100 bit 0) reads open bus because the engine is ladder rung L3 and is not built, the flat aperture is gated by the register file'"'"'s [0xE0102] bit 0 (open bus with it clear - the FAIL-FIRST row for that gate), and with the view down the planar arms are unchanged.' ;;
    memory_map) echo 'The memory map'"'"'s decode: every region routes where it should, including the 32 MiB open-bus boundary and the absent ROM area at 0xC0000-0xD7FFF minus the 0xCC000-0xCFFFF PC-9801-86 SOUND.ROM window (served from the pack; its 0x461 = 0x0C alias included), which this block decodes and leaves unclaimed rather than serving. The local open-bus path answers in one cycle and these assertions record that shape: it is a z486 hazard, unreachable in the built design, and tb_pc9821_open_bus.sv is the bench that pins the correct phases. It also transforms the 0x043F 0x2-nibble EMS window at 0xB0000-0xBFFFF: the identity state leaves the GVRAM planes there alone, the 0x22 state reads and writes a 64 KiB page in the backend, and the unmapped state reaches neither the planes nor the backend -- and the window is a first-MiB aperture, so a guest address above 1 MiB with the same low 20 bits stays ordinary RAM. And it pins the ROM windows'"'"' read-only contract: a store into the 0xE8000-0xF7FFF main BIOS, the 0xD8000-0xD9FFF option-ROM window or the 0xCC000-0xCFFFF SOUND.ROM window is rejected in BOTH 0x053D bit-1 gate states (their only backing is the pack, and NP2kai discards those stores: cpumem.c:135 memnc_wr8), while the 0xF8000-0xFFFFF window'"'"'s store is gated by 0x053D bit 1 and falls into the BIOS shadow. The intensity window 0xE0000-0xE7FFF, and its window-0 0x0E alias at 0x80000, is a plane only in the 16-colour mode (0x6A FF2 num 0): in the digital mode it decodes no plane and is the open-bus slot e_plane_nc, as the machine answers FF there and keeps no store (docs/probes/DMAROUTE-XE10.TXT case P4 view V1, docs/probes/VRAM2.TXT `E000wrote= 00 FF 55 AA` / `got= FF FF FF FF`, docs/probes/WIN.TXT `0461=0E rb=0F b=FFFF a=FFFF`) - FAIL-FIRST, the core decoded the plane in every mode.' ;;
    memory_subsystem) echo 'ROM loading and bus arbitration: the CPU is held until the ROM has loaded, and the load/arbitration handshake grants the bus correctly afterwards. The absent ROM area at 0xC0000-0xD7FFF is a route of its own: a read returns all ones in z486'"'"'s accept-then-respond phases with no backend transaction, an instruction fetch returns four all-ones words, and a store is swallowed. THE 12 KiB HOLE BETWEEN THE TEXT RAM AND THE PLANES, 0xA5000-0xA7FFF, is the same shape, because the machine reads 0xFF there and discards stores (docs/probes/VRAM.TXT:141-142): the direct address must not reach the backend, must answer all ones, and must swallow its stores, while 0xA4FFF stays the CG window'"'"'s and 0xA8000 the B plane'"'"'s, and window 0 at 0x0461 = 0x0A claims none of them, its page being RAM of its own on the machine (docs/probes/DMAROUTE-XE10.TXT W1/W2). It also carries the JIS KU-BANK SWEEP: one JIS code from each bank of the FONT.ROM grid, driven through the real pack download, must land at the font store word the text scanout computes for that code and carry FONT.ROM'"'"'s own bytes there, so a word offset that is a bit too narrow puts a whole ku bank at another bank'"'"'s word and the log names the misplaced bank. THE 0x1E8E-GATED IDE WORK RAM AT 0xDA000-0xDBFFF IS THE MACHINE'"'"'S OWN GATE (docs/probes/SHADOWW.TXT:226-231: the 1E8E read-back runs FE FF FE through the 0x80/0x81/restore writes, the DA000 marker reads back FFFF under 0x80 and 5AA5 under 0x81, and the IDE ROM'"'"'s work bytes reappear under 0x81): with the gate clear the window takes the absent route'"'"'s own shape - all ones, stores swallowed, no backend transaction - and with it set the same address is ordinary RAM that takes the store at 0xDA000 and answers the read from it. THE TAIL ABOVE IT, 0xDC000-0xDFFFF, IS ABSENT IN BOTH GATE STATES (docs/probes/SHADOW.TXT:201-213 reads 0xFF over all 12 x 2 KiB blocks of the DA000..DFFFF region with the gate clear, ffblocks=000C; QEMU models the rest of the 0xD8000 window as rom_empty): all ones and swallowed stores whatever 0x1E8E holds, the gate-ON state measured as well (docs/probes/XE10CFG-XE10-CFGWR.TXT:9-13: with 0x1E8E written 0x81 the sixteen bytes at 0xDC000 read FF as found, gated, after a marker write and restored). THE DOWNLOAD GATE HAS NO LIVE ioctl TERM (docs/timing.md, the download gate): in the cycle the ioctl_download of a reload rises the CPU still runs and the loader does not own the backend, in the next cycle the CPU is held and the loader owns it, cpu_run rises in the second cycle after the download falls, a platform reset with the pack loaded keeps cpu_run high, and an out-of-range word keeps the CPU held after the download.' ;;
    memrange_correspondence) echo 'THE SAFETY INVARIANT OF THE WHOLE ADDRESS MAP: any address the CPU'"'"'s cache classifier calls CACHEABLE is an address the memory map serves as PLAIN GUEST RAM - the identity route, below the guest RAM top, with no device, alias, ROM window or side-effectful storage behind it - swept over every 4 KiB block of the first MiB, every 64 KiB block of the first 16 MiB, every MiB of the // rest of the module, and the boundary byte of each of the platform'"'"'s windows, under both states of the 15-16 MiB system-space window; and, in the other direction, the addresses that are plain RAM yet classified UNCACHED (a deliberately conservative loss, never a correctness one) are PINNED as a list, so widening or narrowing that loss is a visible change and not a silent one.' ;;
    mount_eject) echo 'the OSD'"'"'s per-slot Eject actions (T[8..10]: HDD, FDD1, FDD2, and in a --cdrom build T[11]: CD-ROM, on hps_io slot 4 with slot 3 unused) become the slot'"'"'s own mount event with img_size 0, the sub-sector unmount every image consumer already reads, through pc9821_mount_eject in front of the real pc9821_disk_change_reset and pc9821_block_share: an HDD eject with the pack loaded unmounts the disk'"'"'s latch and raises reset_req for exactly one cycle however long the OSD holds its bit; an FDD eject delivers one size-0 event on its own slot and raises no reset; an eject before the pack is loaded unmounts without a reset; an eject requested while hps_io is mounting another slot is deferred until the channel is quiet, never shares a cycle with a host mount, and leaves the host mount'"'"'s size on its own slot; two ejects requested in one cycle (the disk and a floppy) are issued one slot per cycle and reset once, for the disk; a host mount of a slot after its eject mounts it again (and resets once); and hps_io'"'"'s own events pass through unchanged; with the module spanning all five slots as the --cdrom top instantiates it, a CD-ROM eject delivers one size-0 event on slot 4 and raises no reset, and an HDD eject pressed while hps_io is mounting the CD is deferred past that mount, which keeps its own size. FAIL-FIRST: the base tree has no eject path at all (no module, no OSD entry), so this bench does not build on it; and with -DPRE_CD_EJECT (the pre-change top: the module over slots 0..2 only, the CD'"'"'s slot passed straight through with no eject) the CD-ROM cases fail.' ;;
    mouse) echo 'The PC-98 BUS MOUSE (pc9821_mouse behind pc9821_iobus'"'"'s 0x7FD9-0x7FDF/0xBFDB decode) against NP2kai io/mouseif.c: the power-up read-back composes to 0xEF (buttons 0xE0 with the latch_x = -1 reset nibble, mouseif_reset:325-326), a 0x7FDF MODE WRITE resets the Port C latch to 0 (mouseif_o7fdf'"'"'s setportc(0), :197-216 - unambiguous in NP2kai and identical in qemu-pc98'"'"'s hw/input/pc98-mouse.c), PS/2 packets accumulate into the X/Y delta counters with Y NEGATED (PS/2 up-positive vs the PC-98 down-positive counter, sdl/mousemng.c:229-234) and portc bits 7/6/5 select latch-vs-live, axis and nibble (i7fd9:239-271), the Port C low nibble is 0x08 | three strap bits DERIVED from the live software-DIP store rather than a constant (mouseif.c:296-303 computes bit 2 from dipsw[2] bit 7 and bits 1:0 from ~dipsw[0] bits 5:4; over the measured power-up image 0x841E = 0x7C / 0x8E1E = 0x49 all three are 0, so the power-up 0x7FDD read is 0xF8 - the old hardcoded 0x03 strap answered 0xFB, a nibble no power-up bank yields), the buttons read as active-low L/M/R at bits 7/6/5 (mousemng.h + MAME'"'"'s bit map; middle PRESSED is the one MAME-only behaviour and is NOTE'"'"'d, not silently claimed), the latch edge clamps to [-128,127] and arms the four-read overflow guard (setportc:142-163, i7fd9:253-266: +496 reads nibble F clamped for four reads and then nibble 0 wrapped - software ASSUMES the overflow), and IRQ13 pulses every (SYS_CLOCK_HZ/120) << timing clk with the 0xBFDB timing field (mouseint:131-140 + mouseif_obfdb:309-312; the 120 Hz base qemu and MAME agree on), silenced by INT# and re-armed by its falling edge (setportc:164-173). The R4 packet/latch coincidence merge is swept across the latch write with the same-cycle hit counted and asserted. FAIL-FIRST: the file connects no NEW port (pc9821_iobus by .*, ps2_mouse/mouse_irq joining the existing nets), so the same file builds against the pre-change pc9821_iobus.sv and every section fails there (0xE0 not 0xEF, no Port C reset, the strap nibble 0x0B where the store'"'"'s bytes give 0x08, frozen deltas/buttons, no guard, mouse_irq never pulses). With Port C'"'"'s upper half an input and the lower half an output, the 0x7FDD read is the latch masked to 0x1F (bit 4, INT#, survives).' ;;
    mouse_sign) echo 'TB_PC9821_MOUSE_SIGN: the bus mouse decodes hps_io'"'"'s PS/2 packet deltas as the 9-bit two'"'"'s-complement values Main_MiSTer sends - the X sign in byte 0 bit 4 and the Y sign in bit 5, with the delta byte the low 8 bits, so magnitudes 128-255 arrive in both directions - and a fast move of +200 / -200 counts as +200 / -200 (latched and clamped to 0x7F / 0x80), never as the -56 / +56 an 8-bit sign extension of the delta byte gives, on both axes including Y'"'"'s PS/2-up to PC-98-down negation.' ;;
    mpu) echo 'The MPU-PC98II register model answers the read side the driver hangs on (idle status 0x80, the ACK 0xFE per command with bit 7 clearing while it is queued, the data register'"'"'s last-read latch), raises one IRQ edge per response byte (an enqueue onto a non-empty queue and a read that leaves a byte each re-raise, the drain does not), frames the intelligent-mode transfers it receives (0xD0 + 3 bytes emitted as one complete 0x90 40 7F message, a 0xD0 message sent under running status without its status byte re-emitted with the full status byte as 0x91 3C 00, 0xDF + F0..F7 byte-identically), emits the 0xFF reset'"'"'s 16-channel all-notes-off burst in order, produces no MIDI bytes for a follow-byte command, enters UART mode on 0x3F and leaves it on 0xFF with no ACK (the MPU-401 manual'"'"'s one unacknowledged command: status bit 7 stays set, no request, no byte on the line) while the next intelligent-mode command is ACKed, raises the H.CLK 0xFD after 0x95, and loses no byte from a writer that polls BUSY (status bit 6, the MPU-401'"'"'s DRR) before every write - every byte reaches the line in order across the 0x3F burst, the 32-byte queue filling and a 63-byte 0xDF system exclusive behind the 0xFF burst, and BUSY reads set whenever a byte is parked waiting for room - all at the real 31250-baud line rate, which a two-clock probe measures as the SAME 32 us bit time and 320 us byte time at 50 and 100 MHz.' ;;
    mpu_probe) echo 'The DOS probe MPUTEST'"'"'s /BYTES op script (tools/hwprobe/mputest, generated into mputest_bytes.svh), run through pc9821_system'"'"'s real I/O bus with the probe'"'"'s own access discipline (BUSY bit 6 polled before every command and data write, status bit 7 polled after every command, the ACK read and any further queued byte drained), is ACKed 0xFE for every command the reference ACKs (every intelligent-mode command, not the 0xFF that leaves UART mode, which the Roland MPU-401 manual does not ACK) and puts on the card'"'"'s 31250-baud MIDI line exactly the byte stream NP2kai'"'"'s cbus/mpu98ii.c produces for it - both 0xFF and 0x3F all-notes-off bursts, UART-mode bytes byte-exact including running status and a 140-byte SysEx, 0xFF in UART mode leaving it with no burst and no ACK, 0xD0 running status expanded to a full status byte and 0xDF SysEx passed - with no byte missing, extra or reordered; a blind burst (200 bytes written back to back in UART mode with no BUSY poll, once while the 0x3F burst is still going out and once 100 ms after 0x3F as the probe'"'"'s /RAW does it) is MEASURED and REPORTED, not asserted.' ;;
    mpu_system) echo 'The MPU-PC98II reaches the guest through pc9821_system when the build carries it AND the OSD'"'"'s UART mode is MIDI (mpu_present): with MPU_EN 1 (the base preset) and the card present an I/O read of its status port 0xE0D2 walks the real pc9821_iobus and device select to the card and answers 0x80 (response queue empty, not busy), its data port 0xE0D0 answers the reset latch 0xFE, a command written to 0xE0D2 queues an ACK that clears status bit 7 (0x00) and raises the card'"'"'s IRQ6 into the master PIC, the data read returns that ACK 0xFE, and status returns to 0x80; with MPU_EN 0, and with MPU_EN 1 in any other UART mode, both ports read open bus 0xFF in the same bus time, a command raises no IRQ6 and is not ACKed; and a mode flip mid-session (H.CLK running, then UART mode entered) drops the card at once and brings it back in its power-on state: status 0x80, no H.CLK byte 12 ms later, and a command ACKed in intelligent mode.' ;;
    msw_hold) echo 'The OSD Boot order field reaches the guest only if the ITF'"'"'s memory-switch init leaves the seeded 0xA3FF2 cell alone: with port 0x31 bit 4 SET (the measured Xe10'"'"'s 0xF3, MAME pc9801.cpp SW2:5 "Memory Switch Init" = initialise) the ITF rewrites the cells at every POST (BANK4@0x5B9A-0x5BD7, MSW5 = 0x01), so a non-default Boot order drives the switch to HOLD: after the next machine reset port 0x31 reads 0xE3 (NP2kai'"'"'s default DIP-2 byte, pccore.c:135, bit 4 clear) at 2.5 MHz and 0x63 at 5 MHz, while Auto keeps the measured 0xF3; a mid-run change and the warm CPU-reset class move nothing, and the ITF'"'"'s 0x851E merge stays odd in every state because the merge masks bit 4. Fail-first: on the tree before this change port 0x31 reads 0xF3 with the hold asked for, and the bench fails at step (d). Port 0x31 also follows the store as the Xe10 does (docs/probes/XE10CFG-XE10-CFGWR.TXT): bit 4 follows a bank-A 0x871E write'"'"'s bit 5 (1E reads E3, 3E reads F3), and a bank-A 0x851E write stores only its bit 4 while its other bits become port 0x31'"'"'s (12 reads back 12 on both ports, then 02 reads 02 with 0x31 still 12; the ITF'"'"'s E3 merge reads 02 / F3), until the next machine reset puts the switches back (F3).' ;;
    msw_switch) echo 'The OSD'"'"'s ONE hardware-switch field of the P1 DIP Switch page (Boot order = the memory-switch cell 0xA3FF2'"'"'s high nibble) arrives as a LIST POSITION, is latched inside pc9821_crtc on the same machine-reset strobe the machine-ID and GDC-clock straps use, and is WRITTEN into that text-VRAM cell then, while the 0xA3FEA base-memory cell is HARDCODED to the measured 640 KB (code 4) and stays 0x0C at every reset: position 0 is the MEASURED machine - the 0x00 FDD nibble, which the authentic BIOS'"'"'s boot helper treats as its `jz` class (BANK7@0x598C-0x5999) - and it seeds 0x3FF2 = 0x01 and 0x3FEA = 0x0C, byte for byte the INIT_BYTES literal a config-less card already got, while every legal Boot order encoding round-trips to the nibble the references name: position 1 = the 0x20 the authentic BIOS compares against at BANK7@0x599B, 2 = the 0x40 of BANK7@0x59A0, 3/4/5/6 = NP2kai bios1b.c:883-926'"'"'s boot-device codes 0x60/0xa0/0xb0/0xc0 (MO / SASI 1 / SASI 2 / SCSI), seeding 0x21/0x41/0x61/0xA1/0xB1/0xC1 with the non-field bits preserved (0x3FF2'"'"'s low nibble, which the dormant PCI bank'"'"'s `and byte [es:0x3ff2],0x0F` at BANK0@0x1205 keeps), and 0x3FEA'"'"'s hardcoded 0x0C keeps its bit 3 (the ITF'"'"'s own `or byte es:[0x3FEA],8` marker at BANK4@0x1D15); a MID-RUN change to the port moves neither cell - the FDD cell keeps the latched nibble and the base-memory cell keeps 0x0C across any number of cycles, because the field is latched on the strobe and applied at reset, so the board'"'"'s switch cannot move under the running firmware - and only the next machine reset applies the new selection (position 2 -> 0x41), with a reset back on entry 0 restoring 0x01/0x0C; a position the CONF_STR list does NOT offer (FDD 7) decodes to that measured machine rather than to one nobody captured; and the seed is a reset-time WRITE and not a read-side override, so a guest write still lands when the cells'"'"' own write enable (MODE FF1 bit 6, pc9821_text_mem'"'"'s tram_msw_cell gate) is set. Fail-first: on the fixed-snapshot wiring the cell never leaves the INIT byte and the bench fails at step (b), and on the three-entry decode the round-trip loop at step (f) fails - positions 3-6 seed the measured 0x01 where the boot-device codes belong.' ;;
    nmi_accept_race) echo 'The z486 interrupt_controller'"'"'s NMI latch folds its set and the boundary-accept clear into ONE next-state: an NMI edge landing in the accept cycle while the boundary consumes an OLDER pending NMI is deferred into the latch (set wins, never eaten), while an edge that IS the accepted request is consumed exactly once and not re-latched (a plain set-wins latch would deliver one edge twice through nmi_request_active'"'"'s combinational path). FAIL-FIRST against the old clear-wins fold, which lost the colliding edge entirely.' ;;
    open_bus) echo 'the open-bus responder'"'"'s phases: no combinational response, acceptance separate from the data response, all-ones data, and a line read answered as FOUR all-ones word beats on cpu_resp_valid (the L1'"'"'s only fill form; there is no 128-bit line port since P3).' ;;
    open_ports) echo 'Tests that a port this core does not implement reads 0xFF, which is what the MACHINE answers, at two sites taken from the machine'"'"'s own capture: an address in neither decode list, and the 0x04A0-0x04AF range the core decodes explicitly as 0xFF with a comment calling it accidentally right. docs/probes/PORTS.TXT records 04A0 through 04AF all reading FF, and the low open-bus ports 0012 through 001A likewise, so the expectation is the machine'"'"'s rather than the RTL'"'"'s - which matters here because the explicit 0x04A0 case exists only because the value "was accidentally right", and nothing would notice a cleanup that dropped it or a default that returned 0x00.' ;;
    opna) echo 'The PC-9801-86'"'"'s own port behaviour through the real I/O bus: the games'"'"' SOUND_O/SOUND_I/WAITREADY sequence, SSG register 7 reading back what was written, register 0x0E answering the joystick, the 0xFF chip ID, and 0x188'"'"'s BUSY bit clearing within a bounded number of accesses -- which is the property the games'"'"' WAITREADY loop hangs on. It also pins the 0xA460 extension gate on 0x18C/0x18E. It asserts the chip'"'"'s OWN audio output three ways: a programmed SSG tone moves snd_l, a keyed FM channel SWINGS BOTH WAYS (so a slowly drifting, block-0 channel - a silent FM that still moves the output - is a failure), and a triggered ADPCM-A rhythm instrument plays a supplied ROM image. It keys the FM a second time with the key-on first and a register write immediately behind it, and requires the board'"'"'s timer IRQ line to rise, because a driver clocks the FM from that interrupt. It also keys a channel written through the extended port pair, which is the only path to FM channels 4-6. The FM register bank is written through the vendored core'"'"'s sample-rate operator scan and was silent under the same back-to-back register writes the SSG tolerates. It does not cover ADPCM-B.' ;;
    opna_balance) echo 'The SSG-vs-drums BALANCE at the -86 board'"'"'s sum, the one gain stage the die cannot speak for (the die stops at its pins: the SSG leaves on analog pin 27 as `o_analog = volume_lut[vol_a/b/c]`, fmopna_impl.c:2438-2441, while FM+ADPCM leave as the YM3016 DAC word, so the ratio between the two paths is the BOARD'"'"'s analog trim). The real machine is the authority and the PAIRED SNDTEST captures settle it: the WITHIN-SIDE ratio SSG(max)/FM(max), measured on each segment'"'"'s own steady-state plateau, reads XE10+PMD -8.79 dB, XE10 no-PMD -8.79 dB and this core -3.03 dB (SEG 07 over SEG 01; tools/sndtest_balance.py, output docs/probes/SNDTEST-balance-2026-09-25.txt), and the frequency sweeps'"'"' own pairing in the same files agrees to 0.05 dB. So 5.76 dB of SSG leg was hot and the join became psg * 33/16 = the psg << 2 baseline (PC88MiSTer.vhd:2505-2513 / pc98-opna.c:372) times the measured board trim 33/64 (pc9821_opna.sv'"'"'s SSG_JOIN_NUM/SSG_JOIN_SHIFT). tb_pc9821_opna_sndtest_balance is the RATIO'"'"'s fail-first, replaying exactly those two SEG 07/SEG 01 register programs; THIS bench keeps the same join'"'"'s invariant side against the die'"'"'s drum law: one max channel joins at (1023 * 33) >> 4 = 2109 unipolar, AC-COUPLES zero-mean to ~+-1054.5 with the analytic droop peak ~1075 (C7C1 law declared by tb_pc9821_opna_ssg_level), pinned in the band +-1040..+-1110 (analytic peak +-3%). CHK-4 pins the balance ratio against the die'"'"'s per-drum maximum 15,352 (((2047<<4)*15)>>5 & ~3; fmopna_impl.c:2681/:2745/:5364-5366 == ymfm_adpcm.cpp:223-243): SSG_ch:drum_max amplitude ratio x10000 in 1354..1446 (= -17.38..-16.79 dB, derived from the same peak band as CHK-2). No die-exact value moves: the drum-hit/program-bed balance still matches the machine to 0.0 +/- 0.3 dB paired.' ;;
    opna_busy) echo 'TB_PC9821_OPNA_BUSY: the OPNA status BUSY bit (0x188 bit 7) after every write class the real Xe10 capture times (docs/probes/XE10T-XE10.TXT /OPNA: the address write, FM data 0x30/0x40/0xA0/0xB0, SSG 0x08, rhythm 0x11, ADPCM-B 0x00 through 0x18C/0x18E) reads CLEAR at the first status read after the write, through the real I/O bus and the real board glue: 32 trials per row, each run as the probe'"'"'s opna_trial (wait clear, select, wait clear, write, poll 0x188 and count), give min and median 1 poll on every row and a max no higher than the capture'"'"'s (29 polls on the FM and SSG rows, 1 on the address, rhythm and ADPCM-B rows), where the core used to hold BUSY 25-29 us after FM, SSG and rhythm writes; every write of the run still reaches the chip.' ;;
    opna_extend) echo 'The 0xA460 bit 0 (extension enable) side effects the references model and the board glue was missing: NP2kai extendchannel() (cbus/board86.c:79-94) reconfigures the FM to THREE channels of MONO when the extension is disabled and keys OFF every rhythm drum by writing rhythm register 0x10 = 0xff, then restores six-channel stereo when enabled. The FM side effect is checked at the accumulator'"'"'s pan mux (channels 0-2 forced center, 3-5 muted) and the drum side effect through the chip'"'"'s own rhythm key-on latch, which must end at 0xff after the drop.' ;;
    opna_flagsync) echo 'TB_PC9821_OPNA_FLAGSYNC: a status read of the OPNA (0x188, 0x18C) never reports a timer or ADPCM-B flag that a write queued before it has reset, and IRQ12 has fallen by the time that read completes, through the real I/O bus and the real board glue driven with PMD 4.8o'"'"'s own port sequences (rdychk, opnset44, opnset46, INT_check, FM_Timer_main, PMDB2'"'"'s ADPCM-B key-on and EOS read): T1 a timer A reset (0x27) behind a loaded queue reads clear at the next rdychk read and IRQ12 is low; T7 a timer A overflow after that reset still reads set once the queue has drained; T2 PMD'"'"'s IRQ12 handler over a main-thread write stream services every timer A and B overflow exactly once (no double tick from a stale flag, none lost); T3 ADPCM-B EOS and the BRDY mask read as the key-on'"'"'s 0x110 writes left them; T4 a 400-call rdychk-paced burst reaches the chip complete and in order with the queue never past 64 words plus the one pair in flight; T6 no chip write lands inside the chip'"'"'s own 32-cycle busy after an FM-register data write.' ;;
    opna_junction_range) echo 'The FM + ADPCM-A junction never reaches the die'"'"'s 18-bit wrap: driven at its extremes (all 24 FM operator slots at the 14-bit rails with the 16-bit wrapped six-drum bus at its rails, ADPCM-B unwired), jt08_acc'"'"'s wide accumulator equals the die'"'"'s 18-bit masked sum (YM2608-LLE fmopna_impl.c ac_fm_accm1 & 0x3ffff) -- +131047 and -131072 exactly, the 18-bit range'"'"'s own ends -- and its output equals the die'"'"'s output-shifter rail (+32767 / -32768), so the core'"'"'s wide sum + clamp16 and the die'"'"'s 18-bit wrap + shifter rail are the same function over every reachable input.' ;;
    opna_kit1) echo 'TB_PC9821_OPNA_KIT1: the real Xe10'"'"'s kit-1 OPNA rows hold on the core'"'"'s 86-board glue, driven with the probes'"'"' own port sequences: READMAP, with 0xA460 bit 0 closed every base-bank select 0x00-0x2F and 0xFF read through 0x18A answers the machine'"'"'s byte (0x00-0x0D 00, 0x0E/0x0F FF, 0x10-0x1F FF, 0x20-0x2F the select except 0x22 and 0x29 FF, 0xFF the chip ID 01; docs/probes/KIT1-XE10-YM29.TXT READMAP), RD29, 0x29 reads FF just before and just after each write of 1F, 03, 02, 01, 00 and 1F (`RD29 v29=.. pre=FF post=FF`), so a read-modify-write of 0x29 starts from FF as on the machine; STATUS3, with Timer B running (0x26 = 00, 0x27 = 2A) and its flag set, 0x110 <- 1F clears the flag in 0x188 and 0x18C at once and holds it clear for 100 ms while the timer keeps overflowing, and 0x110 <- 00 brings it back only at the next overflow (docs/probes/KIT1-XE10-OPNA.TXT `S3 A0 ... 018C=0A`, `B0 ... 018C=00`, `B1 0188=00 018C=00`, `C0 0188=00 018C=08`, `C1 0188=0A 018C=0A`; the 0x188 rows compared without bit 3, a +0 status bit the core does not model, item 155). Kit 2'"'"'s rows (docs/probes/KIT2-XE10-OPNA4.TXT, XE10T /OPNA4): RMAP_C, with the extension closed every select 0x30-0xFE echoes except 0xB4-0xB7, which read FF; RMAP_O, with it open every select 0x10-0xFE echoes (0x00-0x0D 00, 0x0E/0x0F FF, 0xFF 01); S3A, the STATUS3 rows with Timer A running (NA = 0, 0x27 = 15): 0x110 <- 1F clears Timer A'"'"'s flag in 0x188 and 0x18C at once and holds it clear for 100 ms, and 0x110 <- 00 brings it back only at the next overflow (`S3A_A0 st188=29 st18C=09`, `B0 st188=88 st18C=80`, `B1 00 00`, `C0 st188=80 st18C=88`, `C1 st188=09 st18C=09`; 0x188 compared without bits 7, 5 and 3, 0x18C without BUSY).' ;;
    opna_port) echo 'The PC-9801-86 board'"'"'s DATA SHADOW through the real I/O bus: after a write to the low select port 0x188 alone, a read of the data port 0x18A answers the byte that write carried, and the extended data port 0x18E answers that shadow for every extended register except 0x08 and 0x0F, which are the only two NP2kai reads from the chip - and 0x0F IS answered from the chip (NP2kai s.reg[0x10F], 0x00 after reset), not the shadow; the 0x08 side (adpcm_readsample) is not asserted. Both expectations come from the board model itself (NP2kai cbus/board86.c opna_o188 assigns s.addrl AND s.data; opna_i18a returns s.data above 0x0F and below 0xFF; opna_o18e reads the chip only for addrh 0x08 and 0x0F), not from this RTL. It does NOT assert the 3ch/6ch stereo switch, which needs a vendored file, and it does not assert the chip'"'"'s own register contents. It also pins the S1 same-cycle case: a queued write'"'"'s drain fire meeting a host status read'"'"'s S_BYTE must never corrupt the read - the fire holds the chip port to the host and retries - across a swept-phase stream of 0x188 reads whose bytes must all show the flags 0x00, never the data-port case.' ;;
    opna_regs) echo 'The PC-9801-86'"'"'s OPNA port model against NP2kai, register by register and flag by flag: the 0x18A shadow'"'"'s per-register bytes and the 0x188 status flags match cbus/board86.c and sound/opntimer.c wherever the two references agree -- the timer overflow flag appears in the status read and the IRQ line follows the enable bits, a 0x27 write with bit 4 or 5 clears the matching flag, and registers above 0x0F read back the last data byte (0x20, 0x23), while the rhythm bank 0x10-0x1F reads the machine'"'"'s FF with the extension closed (KIT1 YM29 READMAP, XE10T /OPNA2 IDC r10=FF). The SSG shadow'"'"'s bytes are ASSERTED: register 7 reads the MACHINE'"'"'s 0x00 (probes/OPNA.TXT CHIPID) and register 0x0f reads 0xff as NP2kai'"'"'s opna_reset holds it, so master.lib'"'"'s js_start read-modify-write (js_start.asm:67-72) lands on 0x80 - every tone/noise source routed, which is what js_start means - where NP2kai'"'"'s 0xbf seed disconnects all six. An earlier reading took the 0x80 outcome as corruption; the capture shows it is the hardware'"'"'s own. The remaining measured divergences are named and printed rather than asserted: the joystick at 0x0E ignores SSG register 0x0F bit 6 (NP2kai gates the stick on it), a timer whose IRQ enable is clear still sets its overflow flag where NP2kai'"'"'s fmport_a/fmport_b set the flag only when the enable bit is set. Register 0x29 does not gate the timer IRQs, as on the Xe10 (docs/probes/YM29-XE10.TXT, YM29B-XE10.TXT) and in NP2kai, which never reads that register: a Timer B started with its 0x27 enable after 0x29 = 00 still raises the line.' ;;
    opna_rhythm_level) echo 'Every rhythm write a BUSY-polling driver makes reaches the chip, at the bus'"'"'s own rate and behind a loaded write queue: MDRV2 3.4F'"'"'s rhythm set-up (Touhou 1'"'"'s title music, 0x11 then the six instrument levels 0x18-0x1D written back to back) leaves each drum at the level and pan written to it, and two rhythm key-on writes (0x10) back to back start both drums, as NP2kai sound/rhythmc.c rhythm_setreg and ymfm adpcm_a_engine::write apply every write; the vendored rhythm unit holds one pending level and one pending key word and takes each only when its slot comes round, so the board'"'"'s queue must not present the next one sooner.' ;;
    opna_shadow_reset) echo 'pc9821_opna'"'"'s SSG read shadow (ssg_reg) restores the board'"'"'s reset image ATOMICALLY across a device reset, and a guest write is never clobbered by the 16-clk seeding sweep that stands in for the block memory'"'"'s missing reset. The reset is atomic at the FIRST access: a 0x18A read of register 0x0F issued about seven clock after the reset lifts answers the reset byte 0xFF - what the flop form installed and what the `initial` image carries - even though the sweep has not reached entry 15, where the pre-change tree answered the PRE-reset 0x80, so this arm is FAIL-FIRST. A guest write is preserved ACROSS CYCLES: a write of 0x5A to register 0x0A four clock into that sweep reads back 0x5A once the sweep is done, where the pre-change tree read back the reset 0x00 because the sweep visited entry 10 later and overwrote it - the code'"'"'s claim that a host write is never clobbered held only for the same-cycle collision, so this arm is FAIL-FIRST too. Both are proven at the module boundary, for a bare master as well as through pc9821_iobus, because the module answers the reset image for an entry that is neither seeded nor guest-written and skips the entries the guest has written. The power-up image and the steady state are asserted with them: register 7 reads the MACHINE'"'"'s 0x00 (probes/OPNA.TXT section CHIPID) and register 0x0F reads NP2kai'"'"'s opna_reset 0xFF, and after the sweep all sixteen shadow entries (register 0x0E excepted - 0x18A answers that one from the joystick) read the reset image.' ;;
    opna_sndtest_balance) echo 'The SSG-vs-FM level BALANCE at the -86 board'"'"'s sum against the PAIRED SNDTEST captures - the fail-first for the measured board trim (the die stops at its pins: the SSG leaves on the analog pin 27 as `o_analog = volume_lut[vol_a/b/c]`, fmopna_impl.c:2438-2441, while FM+ADPCM leave as the YM3016 DAC word, so the two paths'"'"' ratio is the BOARD'"'"'s analog summing network and is in no chip model). The WITHIN-SIDE ratio SSG(max)/FM(max) is immune to the two recorders'"'"' unrelated gains; measured on each segment'"'"'s own STEADY-STATE plateau (tools/sndtest_balance.py, output docs/probes/SNDTEST-balance-2026-09-25.txt; an earlier transcript-timed aligner'"'"'s windows are SUPERSEDED: they were off by one segment) it is XE10+PMD -8.79 dB, XE10 no-PMD -8.79 dB, and this core -3.03 dB, with the frequency sweeps'"'"' own SSG/FM pairing in the same files agreeing to 0.05 dB - so 5.76 dB of SSG leg was hot and the JOIN is the psg<<2 baseline times the measured board trim 33/64 (pc9821_opna.sv'"'"'s SSG_JOIN_NUM/SSG_JOIN_SHIFT). The bench replays SEG 01'"'"'s FM register program (one sine at TL 0x00, 440.104 Hz) and SEG 07'"'"'s SSG program (one tone at volume 15, 439.437 Hz) verbatim, measures each leg'"'"'s steady RMS at the board output, and requires the amplitude ratio x10000 to land in 3239..4077 (-9.79..-7.79 dB) around the capture'"'"'s -8.79 dB; it also checks the board is silent unprogrammed, that the FM arm really swings (not a drift), and that the FM release is finished before the SSG arm is measured so the residual cannot inflate the ratio. The machine'"'"'s own SSG sweep is flat within 0.35 dB from 100 Hz to 4 kHz and its FM sweep within 0.1 dB to 2 kHz, its SSG square arrives with its 3rd harmonic at -9.5 dB (the ideal -9.54), and the FM at 440 Hz is a sine below 1 kHz, so the analog capture chain cannot have materially distorted the ratio.' ;;
    opna_ssg_level) echo 'The SSG joins the -86 card'"'"'s mix AC-COUPLED (zero-mean), not DC-coupled: the real board takes the YM2608'"'"'s SSG off pin 27 (a YM2149-style unipolar output carrying a ~2 V DC offset, which jt49 models), passes it through the series coupling capacitor C7C1, and only then sums it equally into the YM3016 FM/DAC L/R (the PC-9801-86 board design). A series cap is a 1-pole high-pass y[n]=x[n]-x[n-1]+a*y[n-1], a=1-2^-14, clocked at the SSG sample rate (499.2 kHz = cen/16). This bench is the fail-first for that DC block: BEFORE the DC block the unipolar term has mean ~= +1054 (one full-scale channel is a 0..2109 square at the join scale) and never goes negative; AFTER the DC block it is zero-mean and peaks near +-2109/2 = +-1054.5. The join scale is psg * 33/16 = (psg << 2) * 33/64 - the upstream PC-88 board mix BASELINE (PC88MiSTer.vhd:2505-2513) times the -86 board'"'"'s SSG:FM summing ratio, MEASURED from the paired SNDTEST captures on each segment'"'"'s own steady-state plateau (the within-side SSG(max)/FM(max) is XE10+PMD -8.79 dB, XE10 no-PMD -8.79 dB and this core -3.03 dB before the trim; tb_pc9821_opna_sndtest_balance is that ratio'"'"'s fail-first, and pc9821_opna.sv'"'"'s SSG_JOIN_NUM/SSG_JOIN_SHIFT carry the derivation) - and the per-channel unit is jt49_exp.v'"'"'s LUT peak 1023 (upstream-identical file); the coupling cap'"'"'s analytic droop (2109/(1+a^640) ~= 1075, period register 0x140 -> half period 640 SSG samples) lands in the checks'"'"' band +-1040..+-1110 (analytic peak +-3%). ymfm'"'"'s s_amplitudes and NP2kai'"'"'s vol_ssg absolutes are deliberately unused (their FM operator units do not close; NP2kai'"'"'s absolute SSG path is demoted below the captures).' ;;
    opna_ssg_powon) echo 'The jt49 SSG register file powers up at NP2kai'"'"'s opna_reset state (sound/opna.c:137-139: reg7 = 0xBF, reg15 = 0xFF, the rest 0). This is the CHIP-SIDE seed alone: the board read shadow follows the machine'"'"'s 0x00 (probes/OPNA.TXT CHIPID), so the two no longer agree and the guest-visible read is the shadow'"'"'s - what this bench pins is the never-written power-on mixer, silent where the reference is silent: register 7 reads back 0xBF before any write and the never-written power-on mixer produces the exact same output stream as a twin chip explicitly programmed 0xBF at the fastest tone period and maximum amplitude (NP2kai psggeng.c:25 early-outs to exactly 0 at that mixer state, and a 0x00 power-on routes ALL tone and noise sources in).' ;;
    opna_ssg_strobe) echo 'The OPNA SSG write strobe (psg_wr_n) deasserts on the host clock, not the FM clk_en: with cen held inactive so clk_en never fires, an SSG-data-write-then-FM-select sequence leaves SSG register 1 at its power-on reset 0x00 instead of the stale data byte the ~37-host-clock strobe wrote into the newly-selected register, matching the die'"'"'s level-gated SSG write (fmopna_impl.c:301-302, 2144-2190) that the level-sensitive jt49 (write = !wr_n && !cs_n) applies every host clock the strobe is low.' ;;
    opna_timers) echo 'The PC-9801-86'"'"'s two hardware timers actually fire, at the rate the machine'"'"'s master clock implies, and reach the guest both ways a driver can use them: the overflow flag appears in the 0x188 status read, and the board'"'"'s IRQ line asserts; the periods are measured in clk cycles against the YM2608'"'"'s own formula at this board'"'"'s 7.9872 MHz master clock, and two ratio checks assume no clock at all. It also proves where that IRQ LANDS: the board'"'"'s factory-default INT5 is IRQ12 (the slave 8259'"'"'s IR4, vector 0x14), so the timer line is wired to the slave - not the master IRQ5 - and an INTA cycle answers vector 0x14. Register 0x29 does not gate the Timer A interrupt: with 0x29 = 00 or 02 Timer A still interrupts at its own period, as the Xe10 does (docs/probes/YM29-XE10.TXT).' ;;
    opna_wrq) echo 'The PC-9801-86 command buffer (the WRQ in pc9821_opna) delivers its queued select/data writes to the vendored JT12 chip in guest order with every data write seeing its select latched first, captures a single worst-phase 1-clk fire into every operator register class (the pending latches persist and the sample-rate scan commits them, so no bus hold is needed), still lets jt12_kon sample a 0x28 key-on with the next write queued right behind it, reads 0x188 bit 7 clear at the first poll after a write (the real Xe10'"'"'s XE10T /OPNA rows) and set only while the queue holds 64 words or more (half full), clearing once a word drains below that, gates 0x18C/0x18E writes away from the chip while the 0xA460 extension bit is clear, loses zero of 48 back-to-back no-poll writes across all 24 operator slots in both banks, answers a 0x18E read after a 0x18C select write with the select byte just written (NP2kai'"'"'s opna_o18c s.data shadow) rather than the previous data byte, and pins the drop boundary at the reduced write cadence: a 160-write no-poll burst at 5 clk_sys per access overflows the 128-deep queue and loses writes, while the same burst behind the guest'"'"'s WAITREADY poll loses none.' ;;
    opna_xe10_rows) echo 'TB_PC9821_OPNA_XE10_ROWS: the real Xe10'"'"'s XE10T /OPNA2 rows hold on the core'"'"'s 86-board glue and 8259 pair, driven with the probe'"'"'s own port sequences (tools/hwprobe/xe10t/sec_opna2.inc): FR_TMRA and FR_TMRB, a timer A (NA 0x3E8) or timer B (NB 0xFE) flag raised with IRQ12 masked in the slave IMR (0x75) shows the slave IRR bit 4 set before the 0x27 reset, and after the reset (0x27 = 0x15 or 0x2A, the timer kept running) the flag reads clear within the machine'"'"'s 3 status reads and the first IRR read finds bit 4 CLEAR no later than the machine'"'"'s fastest drop (26 PIT counts, 10.58 us; docs/probes/XE10T-XE10-OPNA2.TXT `irr0=10 irr1=00 irrn=1` in 32/32 trials of each), and restoring the IMR then delivers no interrupt, while a flag left set still delivers exactly one; IDC, the identity reads with 0xA460 bit 0 closed and then open read the machine'"'"'s bytes (`X IDC b=0188 ext=0 ... id=01 id2=01 r0e=FF r0f=FF r10=FF r20=20 w5a=5A wa5=A5 ... s4=FF s6=FF x00=FF xff=FF` and `ext=1 ... r10=10 r20=20 ... s6=00 x00=00 xff=FF`): register 0x10 reads FF while the extension is closed and echoes its select once it is open; TMRA_3FF and TMRB_FF, the probe'"'"'s start sequence (o2_tmr) at 8 phases each reaches the 1-count flag no later than the machine'"'"'s slowest trial (70 PIT counts, 28.48 us, for timer A; 751, 305.58 us, for timer B); TMRQ, the same 1-count starts written behind a polling driver'"'"'s FM stream that has loaded the write queue (64 or more words) still reach the flag no later than those bounds (the machine has no queue; the core trailed by about 0.86 ms before the timer registers acted at the write), a load written while timer A runs takes effect at its overflow and does not restart the count (ymfm update_timer, NP2kai opna_settimer), and 0x27'"'"'s CH3-mode bit 6 reaches the chip after every FM write queued before it and not at the guest'"'"'s write.' ;;
    osd) echo 'The OSD'"'"'s two stored selections, decoded: all eight RAM Size list positions select the guest size their label names, the list rotated so position 0 - what a card with no config file passes - is 16 MB instead of the 1 MB that cannot boot the firmware, with every size in the set still reachable exactly once; Floppy mode passes its list position through unchanged (0 = Auto (1M), the default, to 3 = 640K; pc9821_iobus decodes it). Keyboard position 0 is the US host layout and position 1 JIS. The two SETUP items: Text lines position 0 is 25 lines and position 1 20 (port 0x31 bit 3), Memory switch position 0 Initialise and position 1 Keep, each position 0 the measured Xe10 (0x31 = 0xF3).' ;;
    osd_audio_levels) echo 'The OSD Audio page'"'"'s PC-9801-86 and Beeper fields, decoded: each of the eight list positions of each field (0 dB, -2 dB, -4 dB, -6 dB, -8 dB, -10 dB, -12 dB, Off) selects the pc9821_audio_volume gain code its label names (31,30,29,28,27,26,25,0: code 31 is 0 dB, each step down is -2 dB, code 0 is exact mute), so position 0 - what a card with no config file passes - is 0 dB, the unity level both sources had before the page existed; each field drives only its own code (moving one field leaves the other two codes where they were, the CD audio code included), and both fields decode the same way in a build without the CD drive'"'"'s audio player (CD_AUDIO_EN 0), where only the CD code is held at 0.' ;;
    osd_cd_volume) echo 'The OSD'"'"'s CD audio volume field, decoded: each of the eight list positions (0 dB, -2 dB, -4 dB, -6 dB, -8 dB, -10 dB, -12 dB, Off) selects the pc9821_audio_volume gain code its label names (31,30,29,28,27,26,25,0: code 31 is 0 dB, each step down is -2 dB, code 0 is exact mute), so position 0 - what a card with no config file passes - is 0 dB, the unity level the CD-DA channel had before the field existed (the owner'"'"'s choice), with NP2kai'"'"'s default CD-DA level (DAVOLUME 128 of 255 = -6 dB, sdl/ini.c:704, applied as davolume/255 in cbus/ideio.c:1738-1743) one of the steps; and a build without the CD drive'"'"'s audio player (CD_AUDIO_EN 0) gives code 0 at every position.' ;;
    pack_boundary) echo 'The ROM loader'"'"'s reserve boundary follows its OWN PACK_BYTES parameter rather than a hardcoded 1 MiB, and BOTH sides of that boundary are asserted: the last legal word of the reserve is accepted and written to SDRAM, and the first address at or above the reserve raises load_error with no write and never publishes rom_ready. The bench drives the DUT with an explicit small reserve, so a loader that ignored its parameter and hardcoded the shipped 1 MiB fails here, and the accept side of the boundary - the last word, which the existing rom_loader bench never sends - is checked rather than assumed.' ;;
    pcm86) echo 'The PC-9801-86'"'"'s 86-PCM block through the real I/O bus: 0xA460 answering the board sound ID 0x40 with the extension latch in bit 0 and the ID nibble read-only, 0xA468 and 0xA46A reading back what was written with 0xA46A multiplexed between the IRQ threshold and the DAC control and the reset values 0x00 and 0x32, 0xA466 full/empty/phase status whose full bit only a real 32 KiB buffer can set, the drain stopping while playback is disabled and then consuming one sample frame per sample period at the selected rate, the 16-bit big-endian (mono and the reset format'"'"'s stereo, left word first) and 8-bit left-justified sample decode as an exact audio level, and the buffer-low interrupt at 0xA468 bit 4 as a LEVEL that is gated by the IRQ enable (bit 5), that re-asserts on a bit-4-clear write while the buffer is still below threshold, and that clears only when the level is no longer true. It does NOT cover DMA, which neither reference models, and it does not assert the analogue output.' ;;
    pcm86_buf) echo 'The SDRAM-backed sample buffer is byte-addressed and lossless: a byte written at any of the 32768 addresses reads back from that same address, whatever byte lane of the 32-bit SDRAM word it occupies, and back-to-back writes are queued rather than dropped, so a 0xA46C stream from the guest survives the move out of block RAM.' ;;
    pcm86_bufrst) echo 'The 86-PCM'"'"'s 0xA468 bit-3 buffer reset is AUTHORITATIVE while the buffer is RUNNING: for every one of the sample period'"'"'s phases, a reset issued while the drain engine is mid-frame still leaves the byte count at zero rather than at 0xFFFE, because the engine'"'"'s own rdptr/count update sits later in the same always block and otherwise subtracts a frame from a count the reset has just zeroed. And the full+write+D_DONE collision (datapaths.md F7 / buffers.md F6): a guest byte written on the EXACT cycle a draining frame completes while the ring is FULL survives as the newest byte and keeps count == wrptr - rdptr (mod 0x8000) - in NP2kai'"'"'s serialized order (the drain runs at sound_sync before the port write, cbus/pcm86io.c pcm86_oa46c), so the frame leaves first, the ring is no longer full and no slip is owed: count = 0x8000 - nbyte + 1 - swept over nbyte 1, 2 and 4 and compared byte-for-byte against that golden stream.' ;;
    pcm86_cdc) echo 'With the -86 card'"'"'s sample bus crossing from clk_sys (50 MHz) to CLK_AUDIO (24.576 MHz) through the held cdc_vector_handshake_continuous that z486_mister.sv now instantiates, exactly as the CD path already does, every 86-PCM sample pair driven on the bus arrives at the destination intact and in order - including the full-scale 0x7FFFF and 0x80000 OF THE 20-BIT BUS and a walk of every bit - and the destination never holds a value whose L and R halves differ, which is the old/new bit-mix a per-bit or unsynchronised crossing produces; the crossing'"'"'s latency is bounded and printed. The L/R-halves check can only see a LOGIC-level mix (a half registered on another strobe); a 2-state zero-delay simulation cannot produce a metastable capture, so the unsynchronised-crossing case it names is argued from the structure, not observed.' ;;
    pcm86_equiv) echo 'The 86-PCM block'"'"'s area rewrite (the IRQ threshold held as an 8-bit count plus a ceiling flag, the DAC volume scale as a nibble repeat instead of a multiply, the SDRAM write FIFO'"'"'s entries as unreset memory, the buffer address concatenated under the 32 KiB-aligned base instead of added, and the read request'"'"'s don'"'"'t-care write data left alone) is cycle-for-cycle identical to the pre-rewrite block (pc9821_pcm86_ref.sv, the two modules as of 3b134fe4): over a random mix of 0xA466/0xA468/0xA46A/0xA46C writes (buffer resets, every rate and format, thresholds including 0xFF, byte bursts that fill the write FIFO) against a random-latency SDRAM, every read port, the write wait, the interrupt, the DAC outputs, the logical count and the SDRAM request (valid, write, byte enable, burst count, and the address and write data whenever they are meaningful) match on every clock.' ;;
    pcm86_fifo) echo 'The PC-9801-86'"'"'s 86-PCM block against NP2kai, FIFO level rather than waveform: the reset values 0xA468 = 0x00 and 0xA46A = 0x32, 0xA466'"'"'s full and empty bits at NP2kai'"'"'s own thresholds (virbuf >= 0x8000 for full, virbuf <= stepmask for empty, so the empty boundary moves with the 0xA46A format field), the logical count saturating at 0x8000 and the oldest byte being dropped past it, the 0xA468 read substituting the interrupt for bit 4, and the buffer-low interrupt rising at the 0xA46A threshold and re-asserting on a bit-4-clear write while the buffer is still below threshold. The four spare ports NP2kai leaves as a dummy read (0xA462, 0xA464, 0xA46C, 0xA46E) are ASSERTED to answer the Xe10'"'"'s bytes FF, FF, EE, FF (docs/probes/XE10T-XE10-AUDIT.TXT), not pcm86_inpdummy'"'"'s 0x00. And the 0xA46C write FIFO back-pressures its writer when full -- twelve pushes at the strobe cadence with the SDRAM port withheld are all held and land byte-exact, never dropped -- which is what io_wr_wait and the bus'"'"'s S_IOWAIT hold now guarantee. The remaining measured divergences are named and printed rather than asserted: a 0xA468 bit-7 rising edge does not restart the sample clock where pcm86_oa468 resets lastclock, and an arming write with the count already at or below the threshold raises the interrupt on the next cycle where NP2kai waits datawriteirqwait.' ;;
    pcm86_irq_rearm) echo 'TB_PC9821_PCM86_IRQ_REARM: an 86-PCM buffer-low interrupt that is acknowledged (0xA468 written with bit 4 clear) while the buffer is STILL at or below the 0xA46A threshold - so the flag re-latches, as NP2kai'"'"'s pcm86_oa468 forces it - and then refilled, raises a NEW interrupt edge when the buffer drains back to the threshold: every armed threshold event is a fresh request, as NP2kai delivers it (sound/pcm86c.c pcm86_cb: `pcm86->irqflag = 1; ... pic_setirq(pcm86->irq);` once per write-armed reqirq), instead of the line staying high from the re-latch so the edge-triggered PIC never sees the next one and playback stalls; a first event from a low line still gives exactly one edge.' ;;
    pcm86_irqmask) echo 'The 86-PCM block'"'"'s irq WIRE is NP2kai'"'"'s interrupt QUERY, not the raw latched flag: with the IRQ enable (0xA468 bit 5) clear the wire is LOW even while the pending flag is latched, because pcm86gen_intrq returns FALSE the moment !(fifo & 0x20) (sound/pcm86c.c:472-475) and 0xA468 bit 4'"'"'s read is that same query (cbus/pcm86io.c:299-308). The reported defect sequence is asserted exactly: a pending interrupt, then a 0xA468 write of 0x90 (bit 5 clear, bit 4 SET so the latch survives, cbus/pcm86io.c:114-121), drops the wire low for the whole masked window while bit 4 reads 0 as before; and the re-enable (0xB0) brings the query TRUE again off the still-latched flag (sound/pcm86c.c:476-479), so the wire produces ONE FRESH RISING EDGE - which is what the core'"'"'s edge-latched PIC needs (ir_set = ir_in & ~ir_prev, pc9821_pic.sv:168) and what a wire driven by the latched flag alone cannot do (it stays high through the mask and eats the re-enable'"'"'s edge). The enable-gate AND (latched flag OR live fifo level) shape is NP2kai'"'"'s query exactly (pcm86gen_intrq, sound/pcm86c.c:465-490). FAIL-FIRST: IRQMASK_WIRE_FOLLOWS_QUERY_WHILE_MASKED and the IRQMASK_REENABLE_* checks fail against the pre-fix wire. When the query is genuinely FALSE on both sides (flag cleared above the threshold, cbus/pcm86io.c:114-126) a mask/unmask pair must produce NO edge (IRQMASK_NO_EDGE_WHEN_QUERY_FALSE). And the live-level half must raise the wire with NO arming write: with irq_arm consumed by one fire and the flag cleared, a threshold raise (not a data write) must raise the wire EXACTLY once off the live level alone (IRQMASK_NOARM_LIVE_LEVEL_*; FAIL-FIRST: the pre-fix flag-only wire has no source for that edge and produces zero), while the latched flag alone holds the wire when the level goes false again (sound/pcm86c.c:476-479).' ;;
    pcm86_overflow_align) echo 'TB_PC9821_PCM86_OVERFLOW_ALIGN: overfilling the 86-PCM'"'"'s 32 KiB buffer by a byte count that is not a whole frame keeps the drain FRAME-ALIGNED: the read pointer slips in NP2kai'"'"'s 4-byte groups (cbus/pcm86io.c pcm86_oa46c: `g_pcm86.realbuf -= 4; g_pcm86.readpos = (g_pcm86.readpos + 4) & PCM86_BUFMSK;`), a multiple of every format'"'"'s frame size, so after a 16-bit-stereo buffer is overfilled by 2 bytes every played frame still takes its left sample from a frame'"'"'s first two bytes and its right sample from its last two - never the 1-byte slip that swaps channels and splits samples for the rest of the session.' ;;
    pcm86_underrun) echo 'The 86-PCM DAC on a STARVED buffer outputs SILENCE, not the last frame: with playback enabled (0xA468 bit 7) and the count below the 0xA46A format'"'"'s frame_bytes - the references'"'"' own '"'"'effectively empty'"'"' level, pcm86_ia466'"'"'s virbuf <= stepmask / qemu'"'"'s count < frame_bytes - every sample period is exactly 0 on both channels. NP2kai zeroes smp/lastsmp on all four *_bufempty paths (sound/pcm86g.c:105-109,188-194,259-263,341-347) and qemu stores l = r = 0 as the DAC state (opna_pcm_consume, hw/audio/pc98-opna.c:289-325), so neither repeats the previous frame. FAIL-FIRST: it FIRST requires a frame to be observed at the output, so the starved window is measured against a live non-zero hold - the pre-fix RTL held that frame forever and fails by name. The frame-size boundary is covered too (one byte of a 2-byte 16-bit frame is silent even though count != 0), and the drain recovers when the frame completes.' ;;
    pcm_phase) echo 'The 86-PCM'"'"'s 0xA466 phase bit free-runs at the selected sample rate and keeps toggling while playback is DISABLED, because a driver calibrates the card by watching that bit before it ever starts playback and would hang on a frozen one.' ;;
    pegc) echo 'TB_PC9821_PEGC: the PEGC register file at 0xE0000 answers as NP2kai'"'"'s memvgaio byte store does, except where the machine'"'"'s read-back differs - bank A at 0xE0004 and bank B at 0xE0006 keep bits 5-0 of the written byte (bits 7-6 read 0) and 0xE0005/0xE0007 read 0x00 whatever is written, as the real Xe10 answers (docs/probes/DMAROUTE-XE10.TXT case P4: B1 53 5A 5A reads back 31 00 1A 00 and EB 09 00 00 reads back 2B 00 00 00; NP2kai round-trips the whole byte), while the exported bank value is the low nibble, 0xE0100 keeps bit 0 only (the packed/plane mode select) while 0xE0102 keeps the whole written byte with bit 0 exported as the flat-aperture VRAM enable, the plane engine'"'"'s control registers 0xE0104-0xE011F round-trip as plain bytes with no engine consuming them, every other offset in the 32 KiB window reads 0x00 with its stores dropped, a dword read composes the four byte answers in little-endian lane order, and the responder never accepts and answers in one cycle. FAIL-FIRST: the full-byte round-trip read 0xB1/0x53 and 0xEB/0x09 back where the machine reads 0x31/0x00 and 0x2B/0x00.' ;;
    pegc_480) echo 'TB_PC9821_PEGC_480: the PC-9821 256-colour 640x480 mode the Windows 95 PC-9821 display driver sets displays a packed 640x480x8 frame correctly on the 31 kHz raster: with clk_sys at 90 MHz (the shipped clock), the real pc9821_dot_ce (31 kHz dot clock), pc9821_crtc, pc9821_gdc2, pc9821_pegc_scan behind the real sdram_sync_bridge and src/memory/sdram.v (port 3) and pc9821_video_mixer wired as pc9821_system wires them, programmed through the GDC and mode ports with the firmware'"'"'s 640x480 rows (INT 18h AH=30h BH=0x31: master SYNC 10 4E 4B 0C 03 0B DB 95 = 800 x 525 with AL 475, slave SYNC 02 4E 4B 0C 83 06 E0 95 = AL 480, slave PITCH 80, SCROLL 00 00 F0 7F, the 5 MHz clock 0x6A 0x83/0x85 - Xe10 BANK3@0x1E25-0x1E4D and 0x1F8E-0x1FA7, the same bytes as NP2kai bios/bios18.c gdcmastersync[4]/gdcslavesync[1]) and then the driver'"'"'s own sequence (PEGCV8.DRV: 0x6A 07/21/69, slave PITCH 0x80, START), every one of the 307,200 active dots of a whole frame - all 480 lines - is the 256-colour palette entry of the packed byte at line*1024 + dot of the store (NP2kai vram/makegrex.c: the stride is PITCH words of 8 bytes at 5 MHz, `if (!(gdc.clock & 0x80)) mg.pitch <<= 1; mg.pitch &= 0xfe`, and the driver'"'"'s own 0x0400-byte pitch), the displayed area is 480 lines a frame although the master'"'"'s block has 475 (NP2kai dispsync_renewalvertical `scrnymax = MAX(grphymax, textymax)`) with the VSYNC still at the master'"'"'s line 486 and 525 lines a frame, the frame holds while a saturating CPU cache-line stream competes for the SDRAM, and every packed line fetch completes inside the 31 kHz line (800 dots, 2860 clk_sys at 90 MHz), with every port-3 request held, payload unchanged, until the controller takes it (the bridge'"'"'s contract, which a line tick during the mode set broke at 90 MHz before pc9821_pegc_scan deferred its restart); the read-backs of the state touched answer: the 0x9A0 query bits of the FF2 clock/256/256E latches (pc9821_crtc'"'"'s gdc_ff2_state) and the slave'"'"'s 0xA2 after PITCH (0xFF: PITCH queues no read-back, NP2kai gdc_ia2). FAIL-FIRST (each a recorded mutant of the shipped change): with the packed stride taken from the SCROLL im bit over a constant 40-word PITCH (the pre-change walk) 304,832 of the 307,200 dots are wrong, and so with the PITCH byte dropped; with the 5 MHz clock ignored (PITCH 0x80 doubled and masked to a stride of 0) 305,920; with the walker stepping on the line parity alone it misses the wrap of this ODD 525-line frame and shows every line below row 1 one raster line late (305,920 wrong - the 31 kHz 449-line block has the same odd wrap); and with the displayed lines limited to the master'"'"'s window the frame shows 475 lines (304,000 dots).' ;;
    pegc_equiv) echo 'The PEGC register file'"'"'s area rewrite (mio2'"'"'s 32 read-back bytes held in one block RAM behind per-dword dirty flops, with plane_mode and vram_en in their own flops) is cycle-for-cycle identical to the pre-rewrite module (pc9821_pegc_ref.sv, the module as of d8f166a6): over random dword-aligned reads, line reads and byte-enabled writes across the mio1 and mio2 windows and their neighbours, with random gaps between requests and random resets mid-run (so the lazy zero of a clean dword is exercised after dirty data), cpu_dout, cpu_ready, the word response pulse, bank_a, bank_b, plane_mode and vram_en match on every cycle, and a line read'"'"'s four word beats (the post-P3 form - the ref answers one 128-bit line) are the ref'"'"'s four line words, in order, four times the dword.' ;;
    pegc_scan_dot) echo 'TB_PC9821_PEGC_SCAN_DOT: the packed 256-colour dot path, sampled the way the FRAMEWORK samples it, must put the byte of line offset x on dot x - one packed byte per dot, byte 0 leftmost, NP2kai'"'"'s own mapping (`vramex + vc*8 + 0..7` lands at the surface row'"'"'s bytes 0..7 in order, vram/makegrex.c) and the identity this core'"'"'s planar path already has (its fold takes bit 7-dot of the column'"'"'s plane byte). The REAL pc9821_pegc_scan and the REAL pc9821_video_mixer run over a machine raster (848-dot lines, the 21.0526 MHz dot enable, h_active = the first 640 dots), the line store'"'"'s byte k is k & 0xFF so every fetched byte names its own line offset, the palette is loaded with entry v -> {v,v,v} so the sampled red byte IS the index the dot carried, and the sampler latches at sys/scanlines.v'"'"'s rule (at the pixel enable, the value present during the cycle the enable ends); the run asserts 640 of 640 active dots correct and cannot pass silently (a window that sampled fewer than 600 FAILS). FAIL-FIRST: the 256-colour palette read is REGISTERED (the async form is refused by every block type, measured at 2,264 ALMs), so the pixel'"'"'s index must be on the palette'"'"'s input one clk before the sample - and because a dword covers four dots, the dword address must be presented one clk EARLY at every 4-dot group boundary or the group'"'"'s first cycle still holds the previous group'"'"'s dword: every dot that OPENS a group then displayed the byte FOUR DOTS to the left, measured at 160 of a line'"'"'s 640 dots (dots 0, 4, 8, ...: 0->byte 4, 4->byte 0, 8->byte 4) before the fix and 0 after, with the blank->display boundary (the line'"'"'s first active dot) its second instance.' ;;
    pegc_scanout) echo 'TB_PC9821_PEGC_SCANOUT: the packed scanout fetches one 640-byte display line per raster line as SDRAM bursts on the fourth requester - 160 dwords from {display page, GDC line base[14:0], 3'"'"'b000} at peg_base (MAME pc9821.cpp:104-113'"'"'s `(...) << 3` line addressing over the GDC'"'"'s area partition, qemu pc98-vga.c:4426-4437'"'"'s 640-byte pitch) - and serves the mixer'"'"'s column walk byte-for-byte in dot order (one dword for its four dots, the byte picked by dot[1:0]), with the display page forced to 0 while the FF2 dat-0x68 VRAM-boundary f/f is set (MAME pc9821.cpp:98) and reachable at +0x40000 without it, ping-pong buffers so the displayed line survives while the next is fetched, and NO 200-line interleave (the references copy packed rows directly: qemu 4422-4438, MAME 100-123). The walk is repeated at TEN line-base alignments that put the line start at every position around a 512-dword row end - including the start where the 10-dword burst exactly fits the row - so the controller'"'"'s mid-line row SPLIT and the packed page/base arithmetic are walked, not just one aligned case: a second segment read from the wrong row, an off-by-one column or a wrong-page read is a byte the walk cannot match. The packed page is TWO terms (pc9821_pegc_scan'"'"'s line_base_new): the SCROLL walk'"'"'s own page bit - the GDC2 start word'"'"'s bit 14, gfx_walk_page - XOR the 0xA4 term MAME adds (pc9821.cpp:98,112), with the FF2 dat-0x68 f/f zeroing the 0xA4 TERM only (NP2kai'"'"'s packed arm drops the 0xA4 term and its non-packed arm drops the walk bit - the recorded divergence); the page-flip legs pin that composition in all four term combinations. FAIL-FIRST: with the walk bit dropped (the pre-fix page term) the walk-1 fetch reads page 0 and every flip leg mismatches its tag. FAIL-FIRST: before pc9821_pegc_scan existed the mixer seam had no packed_idx at all. The run goes through the REAL sdram_sync_bridge (the controller on clk_sys) and the REAL src/memory/sdram.v on PORT 3, so the row-6 fourth-requester wiring is in the path. A line tick that lands while a port-3 request is presented and not yet taken defers the restart until that request is taken and its burst drains (the sdram_sync_bridge contract: valid and payload held to ready), and the next line is then fetched whole - FAIL-FIRST: the restart moved the address under the held request and the seam showed the wrong line.' ;;
    pegc_store_rate) echo 'TB_PC9821_PEGC_STORE_RATE: the PEGC posted store buffer sustains the packed-store rates the DOOM path needs (trade-candidates T21 / T23): a back-to-back byte store stream (rep movsb'"'"'s shape: one byte lane per dword address, walking the lanes) and a dword store stream, driven through the real pc9821_pegc_storebuf onto the REAL sdram_sync_bridge (the controller on clk_sys at the shipping 90 MHz) at the deployed settings (ADQM=1, FAST_GRADE=0) and the REAL src/memory/sdram.v against sdram_chip_model on port 0 while the packed scanout line fetch runs on port 3 the whole time, sustain at least 25 MB/s of byte stores and at least the 14.44 MB/s frame need in dword stores into SDRAM (the 55 MB/s dword budget, set for the 50/100 split-clock build, is REPORTED since the SDRAM moved onto clk_sys - trade T36), the byte stream'"'"'s four neighbouring bytes of a dword collapse into ONE backend write (write transactions <= bytes/4 + 4), the dword stream drains through port 0'"'"'s TWO-WORD write (din0_hi real data, read back byte-exact), a read issued right behind a store sees the buffered bytes (drain-before-read), and dirty dwords never linger: the buffer reports empty within 2 us of the last store'"'"'s accept and a scanout line fetched 4 us after stores that fill its first 64 bytes shows the new bytes (P2-6, the horizontal-stripe class). THE COLUMN STREAM IS ASSERTED NOW, not raised: one byte lane per step at stride 640 (the shape a packed-mode game draws its frame with) must sustain at least the RATE MODEL'"'"'s own floor for the shape - the GD gather-session cost 7+s clk_sdram per session of s byte stores (the G3 7+2s less one per store for the read the mask eliminates) over the stream'"'"'s run distribution 4,3,3,3,3 stores per 2 KiB SDRAM row, its effective band minus the model allowance, at the CONTROLLER'"'"'s clock (21.30 MB/s at this bench'"'"'s 90 MHz same-clock controller and GATHER_K=4, measured 29.92; 11.83 at the 50 MHz clock this bench ran before, 23.66 at the old 100 MHz clk_sdram) and never below the 14.44 MB/s a 640x400 frame at 56.42 Hz needs, because same-row stores now drain as GATHER sessions (up to GATHER_K entries per request: one ACT, NO half reads, one masked half write per touched half with the byte mask on A[12:11], one precharge) instead of one controller read-modify-write per byte; FAIL-FIRST: this leg measured 5.8-6.7 MB/s before G3 and 5.87 MB/s on HEAD'"'"'s un-posted path. THE SCANOUT'"'"'S OWN CAPACITY IS MEASURED THROUGHOUT: with either stream running the packed line fetch still completes inside its raster line (worst 594-604 of the 3627-clk_sys line) and the fourth requester keeps feeding the line buffer at 149-173 response beats per line against 160 owed (at least 128 asserted), so the store stream does NOT starve the packed scanout (an earlier form that gated each burst on the buffer being empty measured 2 beats per line - a held line on the display - and is NOT shipped). RESIDENCY BOUND (FAIL-FIRST): a SKIPPED-TO-SAME-DWORD byte stream driven at one store per clk_sys holds one PARTIAL entry for the whole stream under a window that ages only on idle cycles, so the backend received 0 writes in six hold windows and the fetched line read the replaced dword; every entry'"'"'s window now ages from its OWN allocation (the 1aacf864 fix generalised) and the same run measures 5 writes in 540 clk_sys, six windows (>= 4 = the bound at HOLD_CYCLES 90, 1 us). FROZEN PRESENTATION (the pair-drain payload freeze): with the backend'"'"'s accept held off while a pair drain is presented, a store to the pair'"'"'s SECOND dword posts as its own entry - the presented payload mem_din_hi included does not move, and the store'"'"'s bytes reach the backend in a later request (pre-fix the merge landed in the presented word and vanished at the drain'"'"'s pop). FULL-BUFFER COMBINE (FAIL-FIRST): in_ready is count < DEPTH alone, so a store that could only combine into a FULL buffer waits for one drain and then merges (8 backend writes, not 9); the pre-change buffer accepted it at once.' ;;
    pegc_storebuf_equiv) echo 'The PEGC posted store buffer'"'"'s area rewrite (per-entry per-lane merge writes with a one-hot match instead of a read-back merge, 25-bit entry addresses, age counters saturating at HOLD_CYCLES) is cycle-for-cycle identical to the pre-rewrite buffer (pc9821_pegc_storebuf_ref.sv, the module as of ac2538dc): over several hundred thousand cycles of random posted stores into a small pool of dwords and rows (so combines, adjacent pairs, same-row gathers, row changes, age-outs, a full buffer and drain-before-read all occur) with random backend accepts and response-queue back-pressure, in_ready, mem_valid, mem_burstcount/write/gath and busy match on every cycle, and the payload the controller reads - mem_addr/din/be, din_hi of a two-dword pair and the carried entries 1..burstcount-1 of a gather - match whenever a request is presented (the arbiter picks only a valid port, and an idle buffer'"'"'s free slot at rp is written on every post since the C5-free cut), in four different 512 KiB windows (the entries hold only the window offset, byte address [18:2], and one shared copy of bits [26:19]).' ;;
    pegvram) echo 'With the 15-16 MiB system-space hole on (port 0x043B bit 2 cleared, as the ITF'"'"'s phase-2 test requests with `out 0x43B,0`) the PEGC'"'"'s 512 KiB extended VRAM exists at physical 0xF00000-0xF7FFFF: a 512 KiB fill of 0xFFFFFFFF completes and reads back, then a fill of 0 reads back, at real sdram.v timing, through the ordinary mapper route; and the same window with the hole OFF is ordinary RAM that leaves no trace in the PEGC store. The aperture is gated by the register file'"'"'s [0xE0102] bit 0 (NP2kai memvgaf_rd8/wr8; MAME pegc_vram_view): with it clear a read answers 0xFF and a store leaves no trace (FAIL-FIRST row: before the gate landed the store reached the backend), and the machine'"'"'s own enable flow - open the PEGC view, write [0xE0102]=1 through the register file - turns it on. The two packed windows (0xA8000 with bank 0xE0004, 0xB0000 with bank 0xE0006) reach the SAME store at {bank, offset[14:0]} (NP2kai memvga.c VGARD8/VGAWR8, qemu (bank & 0x0f) * 0x8000) and round-trip against the flat aperture, up to the store'"'"'s top - banks 14 and 15, where the 640x480 frame at the Win95 driver'"'"'s 1024-byte pitch ends (line 479 at 0x77C00) - and against the 0xFFF00000 alias (NP2kai i386c/cpumem.c memp_read8 -> memvgaf) in both directions. The ITF'"'"'s own phase-2 block fills and verifies exactly this range at 0xF00000 after clearing 0x043B bit 2 and setting [0xE0102]=1, and its failure prints the warn-only "EXTENDED GVRAM ERROR". The size and the address are qemu-pc98'"'"'s (PEGC_POST_SIZE = 0x80000 decoded at 0xf00000, hw/display/pc98-vga.c pegc_post_readb/writeb) and NP2kai'"'"'s (memvgaf over vramex[0x80000], mem/memvga.c), both 512 KiB flat RAMs.' ;;
    perf_msg) echo 'The per-interval performance record reports what its TWENTY-ONE fields mean and is one line: a cycle with a CPU memory request outstanding and unaccepted is a memory-wait cycle and one with the same request accepted is not, a transaction on the main SDRAM port counts once when it is ACCEPTED, split read from write, the other three SDRAM requester ports (font/PCM/scanout) count their own accepts (S1/S2/S3) and the scanout port'"'"'s present-but-unaccepted cycles are counted separately (W3), the GVRAM walk'"'"'s two pulses count walks started and walk cycles and neither counter sees the other one, the EGC transaction and unit pulses and the GRCG store pulse each count their own events and nothing else (so EG/ET and EG/EU split many operations from long operations), the GRCG'"'"'s and the EGC'"'"'s OWNERSHIP pulses count into GV/EV only while the raster is on a visible line (so a visible sprite-store cycle - one the beam can catch - is distinguishable from one in blanking), PD is a GAUGE that restarts at the vblank edge and tracks the last GRCG store'"'"'s position in the frame (the sprite pass'"'"'s wall-clock), PC counts a GRCG store IN FLIGHT while the access page EQUALS the page the scanout'"'"'s line fetch is latched on (the page-coincidence leak: correct double-buffering keeps the two pages OPPOSITE, so a nonzero PC is a store the beam can catch) with SP and AP carrying the two page bits themselves, and CY advances every cycle - all driven cycle by cycle and read back out of the record, so it cannot claim a rate the handshake does not support. The record is also a SNAPSHOT: its digits are the counters'"'"' values at the report'"'"'s accept (snap), held while the record is read, so moving the stimulus after the snapshot and re-reading reprints the same bytes and a paced emission can never splice two counter values into one field (a telemetry tear); a NEW snapshot takes the new values.' ;;
    pic) echo 'The 8259: reset masking, ICW1/ICW2, edge-triggered latching including no re-latch after INTA and a falling edge not withdrawing a request, INT with vector = base + IRQ, the IRR to ISR transfer on INTA, non-specific EOI, masking, no spurious INTA, and the same-cycle edge-set vs ack-clear collision (a new IR edge landing on the acknowledge edge of the same level must SURVIVE set-wins, race-idiom S2; FAIL-FIRST against the old clear-wins fold).' ;;
    pic_cmd) echo 'The 8259A'"'"'s COMMAND and REQUEST-LATCH semantics, which the init-sequence bench does not reach: an ICW1 clears the mask and the latched requests but deliberately keeps the in-service register (NP2kai io/pic.c:326-337 leaves the isr clear disabled inside #if 0; FAIL-FIRST against the old clear); OCW3 selects whether a command-port read returns the IRR or the ISR and only when RR is set; a masked request is latched but not forwarded; a non-specific EOI clears the highest-priority in-service level and only that one; a specific EOI clears exactly the level it names; an unacknowledged request answers the ICW2|7 spurious vector; and after an acknowledge a still-held line does not re-request while a fresh rising edge does, which is the edge-triggered behaviour the authentic ITF selects.' ;;
    pic_equiv) echo 'The 8259'"'"'s priority resolver rewrite (the rotate-and-scan of the request, the in-service block and OCW2'"'"'s level replaced by a mask-and-encode form with no barrel rotators) is cycle-for-cycle identical to the pre-rewrite module (pc9821_pic_ref.sv, the module as of 6b457f7f): over several hundred thousand cycles of random ICW1-ICW4 programming (with and without ICW3/ICW4, AEOI, SFNM, buffered), OCW1 masks, OCW2 commands of every form (non-specific and specific EOI, rotate on/off, set priority, rotate on AEOI), OCW3 (read IRR/ISR, special mask mode), random edge requests on all eight lines and random interrupt acknowledges, for a master-shaped and a cascade-shaped instance with and without the falling-edge withdraw lines, every output (dout, int_out, int_vec, int_irq, int_acked_irq, int_acked_valid and the three debug views) matches on every clk cycle.' ;;
    pic_ref) echo 'Drives the real pc9821_pic with every 8259 register the machine can reach - ICW1-4, OCW1/OCW2/OCW3 - and checks each result against NP2kai io/pic.c, one named case per register semantics. OCW3 special mask mode (ESMM/SMM) and OCW2 priority rotation (R/SL/EOI) are implemented and asserted, so those cases pass. ICW1 keeping the in-service register is asserted against NP2kai'"'"'s own contract (io/pic.c:326-337 leaves the isr clear disabled inside #if 0; FAIL-FIRST against the old clear). The four cases where NP2kai'"'"'s model itself diverges from the datasheet (its RIS latches without RR, its ICW3 count ignores SNGL, its ICW4 ignores AEOI, its reset presets the IMR) print a WAIVED line instead of asserting NP2kai: three of them assert the RTL against QEMU hw/intc/i8259.c instead (RIS gated on RR, SNGL ending the sequence after ICW2, AEOI setting no ISR bit), and the reset IMR (RTL 0xFF; QEMU and MAME 0x00) is not asserted because the ITF programs the IMR before enabling interrupts. The vector takes ICW2 bits 7:3 only (pic.c `(icw[1] & 0xf8) | num`), asserted both tracked and at the acknowledge edge. AEOI is implemented in full against that datasheet/QEMU/ao486 answer: no ISR bit is set in AEOI mode and an OCW2 with R=1, SL=0, EOI=0 arms rotate-on-auto-EOI, so each acknowledge advances the priority pointer and the second of two pending levels is serviced (asserted). The in-service block is asserted to follow that rotation base too: with pry=3 an IRQ4 already in service blocks IRQ0 and permits IRQ3.' ;;
    pit) echo 'The PIT'"'"'s tick rate and zero-divisor behaviour, instantiated at the clock frequency the system ships (50 MHz) with the divider derived from the verified 2.4576 MHz input strap, so the counts here are the ones the hardware produces. The read side is pinned against the real 8253/8254 counting element too: a mode-3 read spans 0..N - the full divisor, down by 2 per source clock, QEMU hw/timer/i8254-pc98.c:100-103 `counter = count - ((2*d) % count)` - never the 0..N/2 half-count model; read-back STATUS bit 6 is the LIVE NULL COUNT (set by a control word, cleared by the count byte that COMPLETES the load, 8254 / NP2kai io/pit.c:244-254,307); and mode 0 keeps counting past terminal count, wrapping to 0xFFFF, instead of freezing at 0.' ;;
    pit_driver_rate) echo 'The PIT'"'"'s channel-0 tick rate is the DEVICE'"'"'s, not a clk_sys count: programmed exactly as the Rusty intro'"'"'s sound drivers program it (control word 0x36 to 0x77 = channel 0, LSB-then-MSB, mode 3, binary; divisor 0x00F6 = 246 to 0x71 twice, the port-0x42 bit-5 strap reading 0), the OUT0 interval is 246 / 2.4576 MHz = 100.0977 us -- 9990.24 Hz -- in simulated REAL TIME at 50 MHz, so the intro'"'"'s INT 08h-driven music/PCM tick is 9990.24 Hz and not a cycle count that would move with the build profile. Mode 2 (rate generator) at the same divisor produces the same interrupt period with a one-tick-wide low pulse, mode 3 a 50 % square at that period (so a mode 2/3 mix-up is visible as the duty, not hidden as a rate), LSB-only and MSB-only access program the same period as LSB+MSB, a mid-flight reprogram to divisor 200 (0x00C8, the drivers'"'"' other strap arm) takes effect without stopping the channel, and the source-tick generator itself runs at 2457600/50000000 of clk_sys (49152 ticks per 1000000 cycles) - the constant that scales every tick.' ;;
    pit_irq_clear) echo 'A masked IRQ0 request does NOT stay latched: the master IRR bit 0 follows the PIT channel-0 OUT pin and clears on the falling edge (pc9821_pic'"'"'s WD_MASK), so the STEELGUN master-IRR falling-edge poll terminates. Expected from the real Xe10 (docs/probes/IRQWD.TXT: with ch0 forced to mode 3, '"'"'IRQ0 masked poll ... falls 2826 rises 2826'"'"') and NP2kai'"'"'s masked-timer clear (io/pic.c:217-225, :240-252). FAIL-FIRST: the pre-change pure edge latch never fell. The positive control delivers exactly one IRQ0 (vector 0x08) when unmasked, and an unmasked request is not lost.' ;;
    pit_irq_reprogram) echo 'An UNMASKED IRQ0 request that the PIT withdraws before it is acknowledged is not delivered: reprogramming channel 0 to mode 0 (OUT falls) clears master IRR bit 0 whether IRQ0 is masked or not, so a timer handler that re-arms mode 3 and then mode 0 inside its own tick (Might and Magic III'"'"'s NEC26K/Roland driver over the BIOS INT 1Ch interval timer) does not loop on a phantom IRQ0. Expected from NP2kai'"'"'s channel-0 write clearing master IRR0 (io/pit.c:378 after a complete count, :470-471 on a mode-set control word) and from the real Xe10'"'"'s IRR following the OUT pin (docs/probes/IRQWD.TXT; the 8259'"'"'s IRR sits upstream of the IMR, so the mask does not change it). FAIL-FIRST: the masked-only withdraw left the request latched and delivered it 1 clk after the EOI. The positive control delivers exactly one IRQ0 (vector 0x08) at the new count'"'"'s terminal count.' ;;
    pit_itf) echo 'The ITF'"'"'s own PIT self-test sequence, from the firmware dump: for each of its three (control, data, latch) triples the channel must still be counting when the latch is read, so the data port answers with a live count rather than the 0xFF the test just wrote into it. A PIT whose data port echoes the last written byte halts the authentic POST with TIMER ERROR before the option ROM, and a PIT whose latch is never consumed freezes the channel for the rest of the session.' ;;
    pit_latch_pair) echo 'TB_PC9821_PIT_LATCH_PAIR: a counter-latch command on an LSB+MSB channel freezes the WHOLE 16-bit count, not just the byte read first: with the channel counting down through 0x0102 and the latch issued there, the low byte read ~600 clk_sys later still comes from the latched value (0x00..0x05, not the live ~0xDD) and the high byte read another ~600 clk_sys later is the latched 0x01 although the live counter'"'"'s high byte has fallen to 0x00 by then, so a second byte served from the running counter (a high byte that disagrees with the low byte it pairs with) fails here.' ;;
    pit_oneshot) echo 'TB_PC9821_PIT_ONESHOT: PIT modes 1, 4 and 5 are ONE-SHOTS that raise exactly one OUT rising edge (one IRQ0) per count write and never oscillate: after the control word OUT is HIGH in modes 1/4/5 (only mode 0 starts low), a mode-4/5 count makes one OUT low strobe of one PIT clock about N clocks after the write and then OUT stays high while the counter wraps, and a mode-1 count (software-triggered here, as NP2kai counts it) takes OUT low and back high once at terminal count; the read-back status byte returns the control word'"'"'s bits 5:0 as written (mode 6 stays 6, the BCD bit is kept), not a normalised mode. The read-back status byte'"'"'s RW field (bits 5:4) is the written control word'"'"'s own for the single-byte formats too (RL=1 reads 0x14 for control 0x14, RL=2 reads 0x28 for control 0x68), not a fixed LSB+MSB (NP2kai pit_setflag).' ;;
    pit_ref) echo 'Runs the real pc9821_interrupts PIT (channels 0/1/2) and the interrupt it feeds - the same block the machine instantiates, with the master 8259 programmed as the ITF programs it - and checks every channel'"'"'s mode 0/2/3 output period and the interrupt-on-terminal-count against NP2kai io/pit.c: mode 3 and 2 re-arm every period, mode 0 fires once, channel 1 and 2 are independent of channel 0, and IRQ0 lands on the master PIC'"'"'s IRQ0. The uPD71054 read-back command is implemented and asserted (0xC2 on ch0 returns the status byte 0x36 in bits 6:0 after a completed count load - NULL COUNT is live: set by a control word, cleared when the load completes, the first byte of an LSB+MSB pair not counting - with bit 7 the channel'"'"'s live OUT pin, asserted separately). NP2kai'"'"'s channel-0 write that clears master IRR0 is waived: the machine'"'"'s own IRQWD run shows the bit set before and after a latch command (docs/probes/IRQWD.TXT; confounded by the as-found mode-0 static OUT, so the falling-edge model in pit_irq_clear supersedes it). Two fail-first cases pin the word-read byte phase against NP2kai'"'"'s PIT_FLAG_R (io/pit.c:224-239,248-249,348-358) and the Xe10 capture (docs/probes/PIT-fixed.TXT:146-147): a counter latch does NOT reset the phase, so a latched pair is consumed high-then-low after one word read, and a mode-set control word DOES reset it, so the next word read is low-first. An MSB-only (RL=2) count read answers the count'"'"'s HIGH byte (NP2kai io/pit.c:346-347, PIT_RL_H).' ;;
    pit_reload_edge) echo 'A count write to a RUNNING mode-2/3 PIT channel never drives OUT, so it can never make an IRQ0 edge: master.lib'"'"'s BGM timer (bgm_timer.asm _BGM_TIMER_INIT: control 0x36 to 0x77, count to 0x71; bgm_timerhook.asm _BGM_TIMERHOOK: the INT 08h handler rewrites the ch0 count on EVERY tick) is replayed with handler latencies that land in both halves of the square wave, and every OUT0 rising edge must be a counted-down half-period end, never the cycle after a count write. NP2kai'"'"'s ch0 write (io/pit.c pit_o71) restarts the count as an absolute event and raises no interrupt; the 8254 loads a mode-3 count at the next half-cycle and leaves OUT alone. Only the CONTROL WORD drives OUT high in modes 2/3 (8254 datasheet), and that edge is kept and asserted here too.' ;;
    pit_tick) echo 'TB_PC9821_PIT_TICK: The 8253 arm of the I/O class table is DERIVED from the profile clock, so the ITF'"'"'s POST self-test floor holds at every clock the tree can be built for and not only at the 50 MHz base profile: with the iobus parameterised at 85 MHz (the `production` clock) an 8253 WRITE still outlasts one 2.4576 MHz PIT tick, where a fixed 21-cycle arm delivered 22 cycles = 259 ns and fell 148 ns short. The floor is asserted on the WRITE because that is the direction it exists for -- the ITF'"'"'s self-test programs a count into the counter and reads it back, while the machine READS 8253 count registers at the default class (its PITD0/PITD1 rows in docs/probes/IO.TXT), so the read carries no floor at all. The bench asserts the property -- the delivered write, converted at SYS_CLOCK_HZ, covers one tick -- on the real port 0x0071 and its 0x3FDB alias, and that the arm still fits the bus'"'"'s 12-bit hold field.' ;;
    plain_write) echo 'TB_PC9821_PLAIN_WRITE: profiles the PLAIN plane-window WRITE path - the shape the GFX counters show dominates TH05'"'"'s MUSIC ROOM - against the REAL pc9821_gvram, whose plane store now lives in on-chip M10K block RAM, and asserts the three shapes'"'"' stored bytes are BYTE-EXACT when read back through the same plane windows: the four plane windows of one column (the sprite/music-room column write), a single-plane consecutive 16-bit run (the graphics library'"'"'s rep movsw fill), and the GRCG RMW 16-bit store whose merge needs the old word (data 0xF0). Each shape is timed over a fixed window (clk_sys/store and the client'"'"'s own cs split) as the measurement, and the plain WRITE'"'"'s accept cadence is asserted: a store completes on ready and never enters the response strobe C_RESP2, which only a READ needs.' ;;
    plane_fold) echo 'The four-plane column folded to a palette index the way the machine orders it, exhaustively: all 16 plane-bit combinations at all 8 dot positions. GVRAM is four planes (0 = blue at 0xA8000, 1 = red at 0xB0000, 2 = green at 0xB8000, 3 = intensity at 0xE0000), the index is {E,G,R,B} in bits 3..0, the column is one byte per plane MSB first so dot 0 is bit 7. Also checks NP2kai'"'"'s relation (B = i&1, R = (i>>1)&1, G = (i>>2)&1) over every index the fold can produce, so a red/green swap cannot pass, and names the blue and intensity lanes individually.' ;;
    planewin_draw) echo 'TB_PC9821_PLANEWIN_DRAW: a FULL-SCREEN multi-plane picture drawn through the GVRAM plane windows (0xA8000-0xBFFFF) is asserted BY VALUE at every plane byte, so a store that lands the WRONG WORD in a few columns shows up as a horizontal stripe from elsewhere in the picture. Every shape the games issue is driven: plane-major and row-major multi-plane draws, the raster'"'"'s flush interleaved per row, all four planes written to the SAME columns, a second pass over the picture, the `or es:[di],ax` read-modify-write composite, a 32-bit plane store (the four-byte width), and the graphics library'"'"'s own `rep movsw` whole-plane stream at consecutive offsets. The check is a software model compared after a raster flush.' ;;
    pll_cfg_lockstep) echo 'The framework'"'"'s write-only pll_cfg_hdmi and the full pll_cfg, each driven by its own copy of the real pc9821_vpll_ctl with identical stimulus (the N counter at address 3, the M counter at address 4, the 32-bit fraction K at address 7 and a start at address 2 at power-up, on the 24 kHz-to-31 kHz change and on the 31 kHz-to-24 kHz change), accept the same sequence of management writes and produce the same sequence of DPRIO writes (address and data, including the read-modify-write of the bypass-enable and odd-division registers at 0x15/0x17 and the fraction'"'"'s two words at 0x18/0x19) and byte-identical 64-word PLL reconfiguration register files after every step, so swapping one core for the other cannot change the dot clock the guest'"'"'s raster runs on; waitrequest pacing is allowed to differ and is not compared, the PLL model answers reads with the value last written two clocks after the address (where the vendor core'"'"'s readLatency=3 capture point samples it), and the final registers are pinned to the words the stimulus wrote (N 0x10000 bypass at 3, M 0x0808 24 kHz / 0x20A09 31 kHz at 4, K 0 / 0x20B00000 at 7, the bypass/odd-division read-modify-write at 0x15/0x17, and every other register zero) rather than to either core'"'"'s internal state. FAIL-FIRST: a pll_cfg_hdmi mutant that puts the M counter at the N counter'"'"'s DPRIO address, or that never recognises the start register, makes the sequences or the register files differ, and both are compared; the bench also fails if the model'"'"'s reads are not returned, because the bypass write-back would then not preserve the preloaded bits.' ;;
    pram) echo 'The PC-9821'"'"'s battery-backed BIOS settings store -- the two-bank software-DIP switch at 0x841E-0x8F1E with its 0x8F1F address/control latch -- against NP2kai'"'"'s io/dipsw.c and qemu-pc98, with the machine'"'"'s own captures deciding where they disagree (docs/probes/PORTS.TXT'"'"'s read-only sweep and docs/probes/RDBACK.TXT:162, the one write-then-read round): the power-up image is the measured bank A, the latch selects bank A (the twelve data ports 0x841E-0x8F1E) or bank B (12..23, off by +12 and not +16), a write stores the guest byte verbatim except 0x84'"'"'s bit 1 (the one unmeasured strap) - the bench used to MASK each register'"'"'s PARITY bit too and was inverted here on the machine'"'"'s evidence, because the firmware writes those bits (BANK3@0xFA219'"'"'s parity idiom; the ITF'"'"'s `or al,0x80 / out 0x8E1E`) and the ITF'"'"'s repair installs its factory tables BANK4@0x480C/0x4818 through these ports, so a frozen parity bit composed its 0x7F over the machine image 0xBF into an EVEN 0xFF - the SET THE SOFTWARE DIP-SWITCH loop (the Xa7C9W capture holds the factory 0x7F at that cell; NP2kai'"'"'s write mask is OVERRULED by the machine, same adjudication as the read side; the falsifier is probe2 /RDBACK at 0x8B1E/0x881E on the machine), a READ RETURNS THE STORED BYTE VERBATIM (the machine reads back the EVEN-parity 0x0C it wrote at 0x871E, `port=871E before=3E wrote=0C after=0C back=3E`, and qemu-pc98'"'"'s pc98_sdip_read/pc98_sdip_write store and return it verbatim too, so the capture and qemu agree against NP2kai'"'"'s odd-parity recompute on read), the authentic ITF'"'"'s own read-back verifier passes, the MACHINE-RESET strobe RE-SEEDS the store to the power-up image (without a reset path a platform reset leaves whatever the previous run had written, which brings the software-DIP message back; a guest SETUP change therefore does not survive a platform reset any more, where the machine'"'"'s own battery-backed store would keep it - the deliberate divergence item 94 records), and the ports outside 0x841E-0x8F1E answer open bus. NP2kai'"'"'s FOUR divergent behaviours on this store -- it treats 0x871E/0x8E1E as PC-9801 control ports, it recomputes an odd-parity bit on every read, it refuses a write whose byte is not already odd-parity, and it masks each register'"'"'s parity bit on write -- are printed as WAIVED lines rather than asserted, because the machine'"'"'s own capture reads 0x871E=0x3E and 0x8E1E=0x49 as stored bytes and returns an even-parity write verbatim, which resolves all four for this RTL. And the ITF'"'"'s own REPAIR is replayed byte for byte (BANK4@0xFC7B2-0xFC8DE: the factory tables out the ports, 0x8B'"'"'s bit 6 saved and ORed back) and must leave an image the ITF'"'"'s own predicate accepts and that a second repair cannot move - FAIL-FIRST, because with the old frozen parity bits the install composed EVEN 0x5C/0xFF at 0x881E/0x8B1E over the machine image and the message loop formed.' ;;
    raster_palette) echo 'TB_PC9821_RASTER_PALETTE: an analogue palette write reaches the raster lines scanned after it, not the next frame: the raster split a game makes (its VSYNC handler writes palette A in the vertical blank, a PIT one-shot writes palette B in the horizontal blank of raster line 105 and palette C in that of line 346, all sixteen entries each time through 0xA8/0xAA/0xAC/0xAE), run on the REAL pc9821_gdc_text, pc9821_gvram line buffer and pc9821_video_mixer for three frames, shows palette A on raster lines 0-104, B on lines 107-345 and C on lines 348-399 at entry 0 and entry 9 in the last frame, as NP2kai draws it (io/gdc.c gdc_setanalogpal logs each write with its frame clock and vram/scrndraw.c rasterdraw applies it from the next line pair) and as MAME does (src/mame/nec/pc9801.cpp pc9801rs_a0_w: update_partial at the write'"'"'s vpos); the two lines after each write, where the references'"'"' line quantisation differs, are not asserted. And an 8-colour DIGITAL palette write reaches the dots after it inside its own line, as the real Xe10 shows it (XE10T /RASTER'"'"'s DTOGGLE phase, docs/probes/XE10T-XE10-RASTER.TXT:28 `P RASTER DTOGGLE frames=283`, filmed as staggered blue/black dashes about one `out` long), not at the next frame as both references draw it: with 0x6A = 00 and 0xAE written 0x14 at dot 100 and 0x04 at dot 400 of raster line 200 in each of three frames, the last frame'"'"'s line 200 shows digital entry 0 black at dot 60, blue at dot 300 and black at dot 600.' ;;
    readback_ata) echo 'The IDE interface'"'"'s own registers read back as the Xe10 reads them (docs/probes/KIT1-XE10-IDE1.TXT, XE10CFG /IDE): 0x0432 written 00, 01, 02, 03, 2A and 80 reads back 00, 01, 00, 01, 22 and 00 - bit 0 and bit 5 read back, bit 3 never, bit 1 only beside bit 5, and a bit-7 write is a no-op that leaves the bank as it was (KIT1-XE10-IDEPROBE.TXT `0432<-80 rb=01`) - while the bank select itself still follows bit 0 of the written byte; 0x0433 reads 00 after every one of those bank writes, and is the interrupt-pending port: a CHECK POWER MODE completion on bank 0 reads 01 (twice, and an alternate-status read does not clear it) until the 0x064E status read acknowledges it, then 00, where NP2kai'"'"'s ideio_i433 connect flag answered 02 in every row; bit 1 is bank 1'"'"'s interrupt (qemu-pc98 irq_levels), set by a completion on a second disk on bank 1 whichever bank is selected, and a completion under nIEN sets neither bit; and 0x074E reads 0F with the slave selected (`IDES dh=A3 da=0F`) and 00 with the master (`IDEB w=00 ... da=00`). Kit 2'"'"'s sweep (docs/probes/KIT2-XE10-IDE2.TXT, XE10CFG /IDE2): 0x0432 written 04, 06, 07, 10, 20, 22, 23 and 40 reads back 00, 00, 01, 10, 20, 22, 23 and FF; with bit 2 set (04, 06, 07) every task-file register 0x0642-0x064E, 0x074C and 0x074E reads that read-back byte and 0x0433 reads 00, and with 10, 20, 22 and 40 the task file is bank 0'"'"'s (status and alternate status 50, 0x074E 00).' ;;
    readback_gdc) echo 'The GDC block'"'"'s parameter read-back and mode read-back against NP2kai (and the palette row): 0x62 pops the text GDC'"'"'s parameter queue (CSRR 0xE0 answers {CSRW0, CSRW1, CSRW2&3, 0, 0} and LPEN 0xC0 the three never-written LPEN slot bytes 00 00 00, one byte per read, 0xFF when empty), 0xA2 does the same for the graphics GDC, whose CSRR answer is the Xe10'"'"'s {EAD[7:0], EAD[15:8], 0xF0|EAD[17:16], MASK[7:0], MASK[15:8]} (docs/probes/gdc-conformance/xe10-2.5MHz/GDCCURS.CSV raw_curd; GDCWG'"'"'s WG row reads F8 in byte 2) instead of returning a second status byte (the machine'"'"'s own `GDC status 60 A0 A2: 05 04 ff` pins the empty answer at 0xFF) and 0xA0 status bit 0 flags that queue nonempty (read-back data available), 0x6A reads 00 whatever MODE FF2 holds (the Xe10, docs/probes/KIT1-XE10-PEGCRD.TXT `MODE 6A=00` in the 16- and 256-colour states, where NP2kai gdc_i6a returns its mode2), and the 16-colour analogue palette data ports answer the LOW NIBBLE of the byte the guest wrote with the upper four bits 0 (the probed Xe10, docs/probes/PEGCRD-XE10.TXT: wrote R=FF G=00 B=7F read 0F 00 0F; wrote 12 34 56 read 02 04 06; entries 5A and A5 index the 16-entry table as 0A and 05), the 0xA8 index register round-trips the FULL byte (`out 0xA8,0xAB / in 0xA8` reads 0xAB, not 0x0B: NP2kai io/gdc.c:841 stores `gdc.palnum = dat` unmasked and :913-919 returns it verbatim), the 16-colour palette read mirror is 16 entries indexed palnum & 15 (the Xe10'"'"'s 16-colour palette), and the PEGC 256-colour mirror is 256 entries at the UNMASKED 8-bit index with full-byte channels read back verbatim (NP2kai anareg[16*3 + palnum*4 + c], io/gdc.c:852-953; MAME returns 0 in 256-colour mode - the recorded divergence, capture-pending), with the 16-entry mirror unchanged after the mode round trip. FAIL-FIRST: on the pre-fix tree the index reads 0x0B, the 0x15 write aliases onto entry 5 (masked), and the 16-colour channels read the full byte instead of the capture'"'"'s nibble. Plus the CG block'"'"'s three register ports: 0xA1/0xA3/0xA5 READ BACK their latches (docs/probes/PORTS.TXT reads 00A1=2F 00A3=57 00A5=0F with the open ports 0xA7/0xAB/0xAD/0xAF beside them all FF, and six more read-only captures repeat those bytes), where NP2kai'"'"'s cgromia1 (a read handler only at index 4) and the old open-0xFF arm are both wrong. The slave'"'"'s 0xA0 status bit 2 (FIFO empty) is the queue'"'"'s complement - 0x04 empty, 0x01 once a read-back is armed (Xe10 capture docs/probes/RDBACK.TXT:172-173) - and the six CRTC render registers 0x70..0x7A read back as found (00 0F 10 00 00 00, RDBACK.TXT:82-87) and store five bits each (NP2kai crtc_o70 `dat &= 0x1f`). The graphics display/access page latches 0xA4/0xA6 read back their own bit (as found 0, RDBACK.TXT:100-101).' ;;
    readback_sysports) echo 'The system-port read-backs, against NP2kai and the machine captures: 0x9A0 is the extended-video INDEX/DATA QUERY (write the index, read the indexed bit; un-written it answers the machine'"'"'s 0x01), 0x043B round-trips the guest'"'"'s own bit-2 request on the machine'"'"'s base byte, the bus mouse'"'"'s uPD8255 PPI answers its live latches through NP2kai'"'"'s mode-masked read model (mouseif.c), the printer trio echoes lastData and the strobe latch and carries the one-shot SELECT-BUSY read side effect, 0x11 returns the DMA status register and clears its low nibble on the read, and 0x00BE/0x00BF decode the FDD channel-group media strap instead of floating. 0xA460 reads the board ID 0x40 with the extension-enable bit 0 following the guest'"'"'s write (0x40 as found, 0x41 once enabled; docs/probes/OPNA.TXT:6). 0x006C is a latch of bits 7-4 that reads 00 as found, 70 after a write of 70, 50 after 5A and 00 again after 00 (docs/probes/KIT1-XE10-CFGWR.TXT:8-9 `RT 006C was=00 wrote=70 got=70`, `wrote=5A got=50`), where the core read open bus FF and NP2kai attaches nothing.' ;;
    rhythm_guard) echo 'The rhythm loader'"'"'s region bounds, which is where its own arithmetic can alias: waddr is offset[12:1], so a write one word past the region'"'"'s end computes a word index that truncates to 0 -- it would overwrite ROM word 0, the first byte of the first percussion sample, on every pack download, using data from a region the loader does not own. The pack download walks the whole 1 MiB pack during the same stream, so the addresses on both sides of the region really do pass this module. The bench drives writes below the region, at its first and last words, one word past its end, and far past it, and requires that only the in-region words land; the source of the layout is tools/build_pack.py'"'"'s RHYTHM_OFFSET/RHYTHM_SIZE and its region tuple, which places the slot at 0x49000, 8192 bytes, between the extended banks ending at 0x49000 and the option-ROM slot at 0x70000.' ;;
    rhythm_rom) echo 'The rhythm ROM'"'"'s pack-loader path: a synthetic pack stream written through pc9821_rhythm_loader fills RHYTHM_ROM at pack offset 0x49000 with the exact bytes the stream carried (byte order and offsets included), a stream whose region is zero leaves the ROM reading zero (which is NOT silence -- the decoder ramps zero codes; this bench only pins the LOADER, not the audio), and a download under another ioctl index writes nothing. With +RHYTHM_REGION=<file> it additionally drives the region from the 4096 words build_pack.py wrote there in a real pack, so the loader is proved against the tooling'"'"'s own output and not only a fixture. It does NOT use or assert any percussion sample data, and it does not claim the channel is audible without a user-supplied image.' ;;
    rom_loader) echo 'The ROM loader: it holds the first word, accepts the write sequence, and signals completion; its registered cpu_run equals rom_ready && !load_error in every cycle.' ;;
    rom_windows) echo 'every firmware ROM window answers the dump bytes at the address and bank the machine puts them at - the 0xD8000 8 KiB option-ROM window reads BANK3.BIN from offset 0 (EIP = file offset, a verified site), 0xE8000-0xF7FFF reads the fixed lower BIOS (BANK5/BANK6), and 0xF8000-0xFFFFF reads each of the ten selections'"'"' own 32 KiB bank (the states 0x043F = 0xE0|bank<<1 and 0x043D install - the port decode itself is tb_pc9821_iobus'"'"'s) from its first byte to the window'"'"'s end, and the PC-9801-86'"'"'s 16 KiB SOUND.ROM window at 0xCC000-0xCFFFF answers the machine-captured dump at all eight of SHADOW.TXT'"'"'s sampled blocks (FAIL-FIRST: before the window this core answered 0xFF there), bounded above and below by the absent area'"'"'s 0xFF and swallowing its stores like every other ROM window. The expected bytes are transcribed from bios/xe10/BANK*.BIN and SOUND.ROM - each BANK'"'"'s first and last dwords, chosen because those pairs are unique across the eight banks - and cross-checked against docs/probes/SHADOW.TXT:150-199, never derived from the RTL. The 0xD8000 gate-off shape (0x053D bit 4 clear) is a recorded DIVERGE row, not a failure: QEMU non-PCI says 0xFF, QEMU'"'"'s Xa7 class says a DRAM shadow, NP2kai has no gate at all, and the machine has never been read there (the owed probe: read 0xD8000 under 0x53D=0x02).' ;;
    route_bounds) echo 'TB_PC9821_ROUTE_BOUNDS: text RAM and the character-generator window answer correctly at the EDGES of their regions -- the last DWORD, the last cache line and the last byte of the 16 KiB text RAM, and the last DWORD of the 4 KiB CG window -- because the existing benches drive only interior offsets (0x0123, 0x0121, 0x0037, 0x07, 0x27, 0x47, 0x11) and a base that let a transfer run off the end would pass every one of them.' ;;
    rt_queue) echo 'The mapped-read response-route queue (rt_beats/rt_left in pc9821_memory_subsystem) under a flood of outstanding reads: with the response side withheld, three reads are queued and their rt_beats slots read back as the ACCEPTED mem_burstcount sequence, the FOURTH read is REFUSED (map back-pressure at three outstanding) and the fifth with it, and when responses flow again each held read is accepted within 2 cycles of the head'"'"'s RETIRE beat (back-pressure releases at rt_rd'"'"'s advance, never at a counter expiry). Every owed beat reaches cpu_resp_valid exactly once, with its own tagged data, in accept order, and the queue drains to empty. FAIL-FIRST: on the pre-fix tree the 4th accept lands and the queue'"'"'s 4th push fires the sim-only overflow guard (and 8 outstanding reads alias rt_wr == rt_rd so the queue reads EMPTY with 8 owed -- the delivered responses are gated off and the CPU deadlocks). The warrant is the responder contract in pc9821_memory_subsystem.sv itself plus r2-buffers F2'"'"'s confirm-test shape; no magnitude comes from a capture.' ;;
    scanout_frame_base) echo 'The scanout'"'"'s frame-boundary line bases: a SCROLL write mid-vblank (the machine'"'"'s own shape - the games write SCROLL under a VSYNC wait at vline 407-419) must move the DISPLAYED raster rows 0, 1 AND 2 of the coming frame together, byte-exact, through the REAL pc9821_gdc_text_dut line origin + the REAL pc9821_gvram ping-pong fetch + the REAL pc9821_video_mixer read port (every GVRAM word carries its own plane offset, so a displayed word names the row it was fetched from). The frame-boundary configuration snapshot (cfg_snap) copies the SCROLL block at the last dot of line 439, but the +2 fetch pipeline latches row 0'"'"'s base at the line-438 tick and row 1'"'"'s at the line-439 tick - a full line and 1-2 clk_sys BEFORE that copy - so a decode reading the DISPLAY registers shows the PREVIOUS frame'"'"'s scroll on exactly those two rows, every frame while the game scrolls: a two-pixel band at the top of the tile background displaced by the per-frame scroll delta. tb_pc9821_gdc2_scroll cannot see it: it samples the decode a frame after the write, when the copy has already landed. Case 2 is the 640x200 shape - the slave CSRFORM line repeat and a SCROLL in the same vblank - where rows 0-1 are one GDC line doubled and must use BOTH the new line repeat and the new start.' ;;
    scanout_pipeline) echo 'The 640x400 16-colour scanout against the machine'"'"'s own power-on palette: the REAL pc9821_gdc_text'"'"'s line origin and frame-coherent palette snapshot, the REAL pc9821_gvram ping-pong line buffer and the REAL pc9821_video_mixer, sampled at the framework'"'"'s own pixel enable through five cases - the four-plane fold to the 4-bit index and its RGB out, the ITF ROM'"'"'s power-on analogue table (itf.rom 0x042F, where entry 2 is red and entry 4 is green), the 0xA4 display page, the CSRFORM 640x200 line repeat, and the HBLANK/VBLANK border - so every active dot'"'"'s 8-bit R/G/B must be the reference table entry for that dot'"'"'s index and a red/green swap in the table the core powers up with shows as a wrong colour rather than a plausible one.' ;;
    scanout_write_race) echo 'The scanout'"'"'s one-line-ahead ping-pong prefetch against a concurrent GVRAM write, on the REAL pc9821_gvram fetch and line buffer read through the REAL pc9821_video_mixer'"'"'s read port, byte-exact: a write to the line currently on screen is not picked up until that line is fetched again (the displayed BRAM snapshot is byte-identical before and after the write, no mid-line tear); a write to the line already prefetched shows the OLD value for the whole line on the next raster (consistent, no half-written line); the WCB flush the line tick sets makes a dirty word reach SDRAM before the fetch reads it (the partial store is still only in the buffer at the tick, and the fetched line carries the merged word); and a full-word GRCG write racing the fetch never presents a corrupted column word (every fetched column is a whole old word or a whole new word) even when the fetch catches the group mid-write and tears the line at its own burst boundary.' ;;
    sdram) echo 'The SDRAM controller honours its own stated timing and its consumers'"'"' handshake: a request is accepted with a one-cycle ready pulse, a read'"'"'s data arrives several cycles later and never in the accept cycle, the response count equals the burst count, a partial-byte write preserves the other bytes through the read-modify-write path the DQM-less MiSTer module requires, and the 32/64/128 MB geometry decode moves the bank bits as documented. THE TWO-WORD WRITE BELONGS TO PORT 0: a burst_cnt == 2 write on requester 1 stores exactly one dword and leaves the word at addr+4 untouched, because only port 0 has an upper payload word (din0_hi).' ;;
    sdram_act) echo 'The SDRAM address pins carry the request the arbiter GRANTED, not whatever the request mux says while the row is being activated: with all four ports changing their addresses on the accept edge itself, every BankActivate still puts the accepted request'"'"'s bank and row on SDRAM_BA/SDRAM_A for the geometry in force, that row/bank then holds on the pins from the ACT until the first READ or WRITE of the same transaction, and the ACT lands exactly one cycle after the ready pulse, so the +1 clk_sdram cycle the registered ACT stage costs is accounted for instead of drifting.' ;;
    sdram_adqm) echo 'TB_PC9821_SDRAM_ADQM: with ADQM=1 (the MiSTer 128MB XSDS wiring: the chips'"'"' DQML/DQMH are tied to A11/A12) a partial store is ONE masked WRITE that changes exactly the enabled bytes and leaves the disabled bytes at their stored value for EVERY byte-enable pattern - no read-modify-write - and the mask on A[12:11] does not alias a row whose own A11/A12 differ, so masked stores and the DQM-less RMW path produce byte-identical memory. EVERY CYCLE the ADQM DUT'"'"'s SDRAM_DQM pins equal its SDRAM_A[12:11], the wiring they had before the DQM twin register (sdram.v dqm_a, dtm-cure), so the twin is pin-identical to it.' ;;
    sdram_bridge) echo 'TB_PC9821_SDRAM_BRIDGE: split_sdram_backend - the module z486_mister.sv instantiates - carries every requester port'"'"'s whole request into the controller at the deployed settings (90 MHz, ADQM=1, FAST_GRADE=0, a 128 MB two-chip module) against two sdram_chip_model instances (chip 0 on nCS low, chip 1 on nCS high, the -7 grade'"'"'s AC checks on): a port-1, port-2 and port-3 read of a chip-1 address (bit 26 set) returns the word port 0 wrote THERE and not the different word at the same offset in chip 0, and a port-1 write to chip 1 lands there; a byte-masked store on ports 1, 2 and 3 changes exactly its enabled byte and leaves the dword'"'"'s other three bytes as port 0 wrote them; and refresh_allowed reaches the controller - while it is low for three tREFI intervals no AUTO REFRESH reaches the pins, and within 100 clocks of its return both chips are refreshed.' ;;
    sdram_bw) echo 'TB_PC9821_SDRAM_BW: the four-stream SDRAM demand fits inside the machine'"'"'s own deadline through the REAL sdram_sync_bridge at the deployed settings (the controller on clk_sys, FREQ = clk_sys = 90 MHz by default, the shipping build, ADQM=1, FAST_GRADE=0, GATHER_K=4) against sdram_chip_model with its AUTO REFRESH gap held to tREFI under this load - the PEGC packed scanout'"'"'s 160-dword line completes inside the SHORTER of the machine'"'"'s two raster lines - the 31 kHz line (800 dots at the Xe10'"'"'s 25.168 MHz 31 kHz dot clock, pc9821_vclk_pkg / docs/probes/CRT31K.TXT: 31.79 us = 2860 clk_sys at 90 MHz, the 640x480 256-colour mode'"'"'s line), not only the 24 kHz 40.28 us one (3625) and the CPU is served under a saturating cache-line stream, its MB/s REPORTED against the old 40 MB/s requirement (tb_pc9821_sdram_tdm'"'"'s, a 50/100 split-clock number the same-clock build does not meet, the accepted T36 trade), while the single-port engine ceiling (15-dword read and single-word write MB/s) and the all-four-saturating adversarial aggregate are REPORTED, not asserted, so the instrument decides whether the serial transaction engine limits the current demand.' ;;
    sdram_chain) echo 'TB_PC9821_SDRAM_CHAIN: the SDRAM controller'"'"'s write path runs at the module'"'"'s own timing floor without breaking it - at 90 MHz CL2 (base), 120 MHz CL3 (heavyoc) and 50 MHz CL2 (the clock of the integrated boot tier, run_boot_real.sh, where tWR is one clock and tDAL three), through split_sdram_backend at the deployed settings (ADQM=1, FAST_GRADE=0, a 64 MB module) against sdram_chip_model with the -7 grade'"'"'s AC checks on (AS4C32M16SB datasheet Rev 1.4 Table 16: tRCD, tRP, tRAS for explicit and auto precharge, tRC, tRRD, tWR as the precharge'"'"'s start, tRFC, tMRD) and the AUTO REFRESH gap held to tREFI: single-dword stores that alternate between two rows of one bank (no row can stay open) are accepted no further apart than the supported set'"'"'s write floor - an ACT, the two halves'"'"' WRITEs tRCD (21 ns) after it, then tWR (15 ns) and tRP (21 ns) to the next ACT, or tRC (68 ns) if longer, each in whole clocks: 7 at 90 MHz, 9 at 120 MHz, 6 at 50 MHz - and port 0'"'"'s two-word writes alternating the same way no further apart than the same floor with their four halves (9, 11 and 8), a gap that holds an AUTO REFRESH on the pins being reported and not judged; every such store'"'"'s next ACT, exactly tDAL after its auto-precharged WRITE, is legal by the datasheet'"'"'s own rule (tWR + tRP in nanoseconds from the WRITE), which the chip model judges at each clock; a randomized soak of single and byte-masked stores in a few hot rows with an L1-like re-presentation (the next store 0-2 clocks after the accept), 4-dword line reads, reads pipelined behind stores, sequential line streams and background reads on ports 1 and 3 reads every word back as the scoreboard holds it; and ready pulses exactly once per request (no request is accepted twice while its requester still presents it in the ready cycle).' ;;
    sdram_col16) echo 'Column-address cone (S4a) is value-identical in its shipped combinational form: on the pins every length-1 READ'"'"'s (bank,row,half-column) follows the request'"'"'s cells in order -- a same-row 4-dword read visits halves col0*2..col0*2+7 in that row, a read crossing a row boundary finishes the first row'"'"'s tail then restarts at half 0 of the next row, and the last row of a bank carries into the next bank'"'"'s row 0 -- so {rd_col_base,1'"'"'b0}+rd_issued in segment 1 and rd_issued-rd_seg_halfs after the split reproduce the old {rd_col_base + dw, rd_issued[0]} exactly.' ;;
    sdram_gather) echo 'TB_PC9821_SDRAM_GATHER: one accepted G3 gather-RMW request carries up to GATHER_K same-row partial PEGC stores and the REAL src/memory/sdram.v services it as ONE session - one BankActivate, one length-1 half READ per half any entry'"'"'s be touches with some but not all of its bytes, one merged half WRITE per touched half with auto-precharge on the LAST write alone (one precharge for the whole session) - where the same stores as single requests cost one ACT-plus-re-ACT read-modify-write each: K byte stores in one row issue exactly 1 ACT and K READ and K WRITE commands (counted on the SDRAM pins), a mixed group writes full halves directly (a be=1111 entry and a be=1100 entry issue NO reads and take 3 WRITEs between them) and merges only the partial one, every stored byte lands byte-exact with the dword'"'"'s other lanes preserved (the read-merge writes the untouched bytes back), a 1-entry session keeps the shape at K=1 (1 ACT, 1 READ, 1 WRITE - the single-RMW path'"'"'s intermediate re-ACT is gone), and a read issued behind a gather sees every entry of it (accept order is visibility order). The session runs through the REAL sdram_sync_bridge (the controller on clk_sys) at the deployed settings (FAST_GRADE=0) against sdram_chip_model, whose tRP/tWR discipline checks hold the auto-precharge sequencing. FAIL-FIRST: before G3 this request shape did not exist - a stride-640 packed column store was one full controller read-modify-write transaction per byte (5.83 MB/s measured in tb_pc9821_pegc_store_rate) and there was no gath0 port to ask for a session with.' ;;
    sdram_gather_map) echo 'TB_PC9821_SDRAM_GATHER_MAP: the G3 gather-RMW session'"'"'s slot maps are a pure function of the accepted byte-enable masks, so REGISTERING them at the accept (the Class-2 stage that takes the entry-count -> gk_wmap -> dq_out cone off the clk_sdram critical path) is value-identical: across 1..4-entry sessions with byte, half and full-dword enables, the merged half-WRITE data stream on the pins matches an independent software model of the pre-change behaviour in the same slot order, the auto-precharge rides the last write alone, and a read issued behind the session sees every stored byte (only the internal cycle count may change, never a value).' ;;
    sdram_gd) echo 'TB_PC9821_SDRAM_GD: with ADQM=1 a G3 gather request is serviced as a WRITE-ONLY session - one BankActivate, NO half READs, one masked half WRITE per touched half (the byte mask on A[12:11]) with auto-precharge on the last write alone - so K same-row byte stores cost 1 ACT and K WRITEs where the RMW session costs K extra READs, and every stored byte lands byte-exact with the dword'"'"'s other lanes preserved by the mask. A session that touches fewer than two halves is padded with fully masked WRITEs (DQM = 11 on A[12:11]) to two, so its auto-precharge comes tWR after a write at tRCD+1 and the precharge begins after tRAS (AS4C32M16SB datasheet Rev 1.4 Table 16 and its WRITE-with-auto-precharge rule; the -7 chip model checks it), and a session whose every byte enable is 0 still issues its two masked WRITEs and closes its row, leaving memory unchanged (an open bank under the next ACT is a protocol violation the chip model fails on).' ;;
    sdram_phase) echo 'TB_PC9821_SDRAM_PHASE: the run-time SDRAM_CLK phase instrument (pc9821_sdram_phase_ctl on clk_sys + pc9821_sdram_dps on the PLL'"'"'s 50 MHz scan clock), driven through its two device-bus ports against a model of the Cyclone V PLL'"'"'s dynamic-phase-shift ports, moves the SDRAM pin clock by exactly the number of PLL steps the guest asks for and in the direction it asks: a requested offset written to 0x5FF2 (+5, -7) is walked one step at a time and 0x5FF2 then reads back the applied offset the PLL model has counted; command 01/02/03 at 0x5FF0 step later, step earlier and return to nominal; every step obeys the handbook sequence the model enforces (CNTSEL = 1, the SDRAM pin clock'"'"'s logical output; PHASEEN held at least two scan clocks and until PHASEDONE falls; never re-raised before PHASEDONE is back high; exactly one shift per PHASEEN pulse); 0x5FF0 reads 0xA in its high nibble with busy set while a walk is in flight; when the guest stops writing for WDOG_MS the request returns to 0, the PLL is walked back to its generated phase and the sticky `reverted` bit is set until command 04 clears it, while keepalive writes every 1 ms hold a shifted phase indefinitely; a write in the very cycle the watchdog would expire wins (no revert) and a core reset requests nominal; and a PLL that never answers sets `fault`, stops further stepping and does not wedge the controller. POWER-UP AS QUARTUS BUILDS IT: both fault registers start at 1 (a set-only flag under ALLOW_POWER_UP_DONT_CARE was tied to VCC in every fitted build), and the instrument still reads 0xA0 after the power-up core reset and steps; `fault` reaches clk_sys settled at least two scan clocks before the acknowledge that reports it, is held until a core reset, and a reset in the very cycle of a failing acknowledge still reports it (set wins); after a reset a recovered PLL steps again, one PLL step per request. The expected step counts are the guest'"'"'s requests, never read out of the controller.' ;;
    sdram_preact) echo 'The controller'"'"'s speculative pre-ACT really moves the row activate off the accept'"'"'s serial path: with port 0 running a 4-dword read and port 1 holding a read, a BankActivate carrying PORT 1'"'"'s row/bank lands on the pins between port 0'"'"'s first and last response, the reserved request then reads back the exact dword written to its address, the ready for it is still one per transaction and still after port 0'"'"'s last response (so the response-order contract the TDM bench asserts is untouched), the same port-0 read with port 1 silent puts no ACT inside the data phase, and every ACT/REFRESH is still T_RC apart with every READ T_RCD after its ACT.' ;;
    sdram_strict) echo 'The platform'"'"'s SDRAM controller, at the platform'"'"'s OWN settings (ADQM=1 so the byte mask rides A11/A12, FAST_GRADE=0, a 128 MB module and GATHER_K=4), at EVERY preset clock - 90 MHz CL2 (base, the shipping build), 100 MHz CL3 (mediumoc) and 120 MHz CL3 (heavyoc) - and at 50 MHz CL2 (the integrated boot tier'"'"'s clock, where tWR is one clock and a store'"'"'s tDAL three), one rig per clock running the same program, the 90 MHz one with the module size arriving from the HPS only after the controller'"'"'s power-up init has finished (so the second chip must have been initialised without it) - is driven against the MiSTer community'"'"'s STRICT XSDS module model set to the -7 speed grade (the slowest part of the supported set, AS4C32M16SB datasheet Rev 1.4 Table 16: tRCD/tRP 21, tRC/tRFC 63, tRRD/tMRD/tWR 14, tRAS 42 ns), with CKE tied to VCC and DQML/DQMH strapped to A11/A12, and the MODULE ITSELF is the judge: every command it sees is decoded against the CONTROLLER_GUIDE'"'"'s protocol and AC-timing rules plus the model'"'"'s local checks (tMRD to any command, tRAS for both auto-precharge forms with no tRAS lockout, tRP before AUTO REFRESH), every AUTO REFRESH is recorded per chip, and a single violation anywhere in the run fails this bench through the model'"'"'s own error count rather than through anything this bench believes. The traffic is a real one: the power-up hold the model requires, a dword write/read-back walk across BOTH chips and across rows and banks, byte-masked writes (the mask-on-address path the XSDS board forces), a burst read spanning a row boundary, a port-0 gather session, a store and a gather session whose byte enables are all 0 (each must write nothing and still close its row before the next ACT to that bank), and bursts across the 64 MB chip boundary (a 4-dword and a 15-dword read and a two-word write that start in chip 0'"'"'s last row and continue in chip 1'"'"'s first, every word checked against single-dword writes and against distinct data at chip 0'"'"'s first row, so a second segment on the wrong chip select reads or writes the wrong word). The module is clocked the MiSTer way - by the forwarded SDRAM_CLK, lagging the controller clock by the pin-clock lead the configuration'"'"'s STA I/O model implies, with read data returning through the FPGA'"'"'s I/O path - so the controller'"'"'s DQ capture register samples the module where the fit'"'"'s STA places that capture, about 1 ns before the module'"'"'s CAS-latency edge. Each chip'"'"'s AUTO REFRESH commands, read off the module'"'"'s own pins (RAS#/CAS#/WE# = 0/0/1, the chip by Cs1_n), arrive at least twice in the run and never more than 64 ms / 8192 = 7812.5 ns apart (nor that long before the run ends; on the late-size rig chip 1 is counted from the moment the size names it, since nothing can address it before), since the model only warns on tREFI.' ;;
    sdram_sync) echo 'TB_PC9821_SDRAM_SYNC: the SDRAM backend - split_sdram_backend over sdram_sync_bridge, the controller on clk_sys at FREQ = clk_sys, the only build since the split-clock configuration was removed - keeps its requester-side contract at EVERY preset clock, 90 MHz (base, the shipping build), 95 MHz (the fastest CL2 clock), 100 MHz (mediumoc), 120 MHz (heavyoc, at the SDRAM part'"'"'s limit) and 50 MHz (the clock the other bridge benches run), each driven by the SAME requester program against sdram_chip_model at the deployed module settings (ADQM=1, FAST_GRADE=0, GATHER_K=4) with its protocol checks and the -7 grade'"'"'s AC checks (AS4C32M16SB datasheet Rev 1.4 Table 16) on and the AUTO REFRESH gap held to tREFI = 64 ms / 8192: every ready is one clk_sys cycle and answers a presented request, every read answers exactly one resp_valid per dword on the port that asked and nowhere else, a write answers none, every word read back is the word written (single dwords, 4-dword line fills, byte-masked stores, 8/10-dword bursts), port 0'"'"'s dout holds its last word between its own pulses while ports 1-3 answer in between (the hold the controller does not keep and the bridge must), and all four ports are served while they contend. The single-dword read/write round trip, the 4-dword line fill, the posted-store and line-fill stream rates and the byte-store cost are MEASURED at each clock in clk_sys cycles and ns and REPORTED, not asserted. Every MODE REGISTER SET the controller issues programs the CAS latency the hardware requires - CL2 below 100 MHz (90, 95, 50), CL3 at 100 MHz and up (100, 120), the cutover two DE10-Nano boards set on 2026-10-13 - and the device model returns read data at that latency, so a controller reading at the CL2 edge at 100 MHz also fails the data checks. FAIL-FIRST: a bare pass-through without port 0'"'"'s hold fails the dout0 hold check. FAIL-FIRST: with CL2 kept at 100 MHz (the old FREQ <= 100 MHz rule) the 100 MHz harness fails its MRS and read-data checks.' ;;
    sdram_tdm) echo 'The time-sliced SDRAM schedule holds at 100 MHz: with a saturating z486 cache-line stream on requester 0 (4-dword read bursts and single-dword writes), the font/ROM line on requester 1, the 86-PCM on requester 2, and the GVRAM scanout'"'"'s 80-dword line fetch on requester 3, the worst scanout line completes inside the 2014 clk_sys budget, the CPU sustains at least its measured 40 MB/s, and the round-robin arbiter gives the scanout a bounded slot (no more than three other accepts between its own) where the fixed priority that stood there could not.' ;;
    sdram_wburst) echo 'Port 0'"'"'s opt-in TWO-WORD SDRAM write is real and honest: one accepted burst_cnt=2 request writes two consecutive dwords -- word 0 on din0, word 1 on din0_hi -- and both read back IN COLUMN ORDER, while the same pair written as two single-word requests issues two BankActivates and the burst issues ONE; a partial-byte write with burst_cnt=2 still takes the read-modify-write path and preserves the other three bytes, and the single-word contract every other caller uses is unchanged.' ;;
    serial) echo 'The PC-9821 serial port is NOT modelled: at NP2kai io/serial.c'"'"'s RS-232C register addresses -- the uPD8251A data port 0x30, its status/control port 0x32, and the FIFO extension'"'"'s 0x130/0x132/0x134/0x136/0x138/0x13A -- this core answers open bus 0xFF (0x30 and the FIFO ports are neither device ports nor system ports, so the I/O bus never strobes a device and the open bus reaches the CPU) or an unrelated fixed system strap (0x32 reads 0x45, 0x134 reads 0x0F), where NP2kai'"'"'s 8251 answers its own reset register state (0x30 = 0xFF data register, 0x32 = 0x85 status) and the FIFO registers their reset values (0x132 = 0x83, 0x134 = 0xF0, 0x136 = 0x41, 0x138 = 0x00, 0x13A = 0x00). Every NP2kai case is recorded by value and printed as a WAIVED line: the RTL facts the core does answer are asserted, the absent serial registers are documented, and the bench stands as the conformance target for whenever the port is implemented. It is a GAP, not a correctness bug -- pc9821_system.sv:1182 already says "no serial port is modelled" and leaves the PIT channel-2 baud clock unconnected.' ;;
    setup_osd) echo 'The OSD owns the SETUP configuration and every reset re-seeds it: port 0x31 bit 3 is the Text lines field (0xF3 at 25 lines, the measured byte; 0xFB at 20, the bit the BIOS'"'"'s POST CRT-mode call reads at BANK7@0x595D and NP2kai bios_screeninit adds as 20 lines), and the software-DIP store'"'"'s reset walk writes the OSD'"'"'s Memory switch into bank A - 0x871E = 0x3E at initialise (the measured byte, docs/probes/PORTS.TXT:258) and 0x9E at keep (bit 5 the switch, NP2kai io/dipsw.c'"'"'s 0x871E bit 5 = port 0x31 bit 4; parity bit 7 set so the byte stays odd for the ITF'"'"'s jpo verify at BANK4@0x46E2), with port 0x31 bit 4 clear (0xE3) and the ITF'"'"'s 0x851E merge odd in every state; setup_cfg carries the same two bytes to the CG record and mirror; a field changed while the reset is still held (a core load'"'"'s config arriving after the assertion) re-runs the walk with the new value; a program'"'"'s own write to the store survives the warm CPU reset that resumes it (out 0xF0, a triple fault) and is re-seeded to the OSD value by the guest'"'"'s 0x534 reboot (SETUP'"'"'s exit, BANK4@0x2574), with the CPU held in its reset class for the whole walk; and a program'"'"'s 0x31 write (the ITF'"'"'s own out 0x31, BANK4@0x46AB) does not move the GDC clock strap, which answers the OSD until the next reset; bank-A 0x881E is the measured 0x7C at every reset (bit 5 set: SETUP'"'"'s Sound item not used, the owner'"'"'s Xe10 as found, docs/probes/XE10CFG-XE10-CFG0.TXT), and a program'"'"'s 0xDC (the byte the item '"'"'used'"'"' leaves, docs/probes/XE10CFG-XE10-CFGSND.TXT) reads back until the 0x534 reboot re-seeds 0x7C. Fail-first by mutation (tests/pc9821/mutations.json): without the reboot re-seed the program'"'"'s 0x871E survives the reboot, without the in-reset restart the store keeps the default written at the assertion, and with bit 3 constant or the seed byte constant the 20-line and keep steps fail.' ;;
    setup_record) echo 'The configuration record and the software-DIP mirror in the CG store carry the OSD'"'"'s SETUP items: record row 2 (code 0x2056), which SETUP stores as the inverted DIP switch 2 and the ITF writes back inverted into port 0x31, is ~port 0x31: 0x0C at the defaults (GDC 2.5 MHz, 25 lines, initialise) with bit 3 cleared for 20 lines (0x04), bit 4 set for keep (0x1C) and both for both (0x14), and bit 7 set at GDC 5 MHz (the factory 0x8C, BANK3@0x09F0), because SETUP'"'"'s GDC clock getter reads row 2 bit 7 (BANK3@0x37A3 `call 0x20db / shr al,0x7`, value 1 = 2.5 MHz) and the owner'"'"'s Xe10, photographed in SETUP, shows 2.5 MHz (docs/bios-setup.md, The Xe10 as found), row 3, DIP switch 3 stored inverted, is 0x80 at the default floppy disk mode auto (1M), the factory 0x81 at 1M and 0x82 / 0x83 at auto (640K) / 640K (bits 1:0 the complement of SETUP'"'"'s value 3/2/1/0), row 4 is 0xCF in every state (the factory 0xCD with bit 1 set: SETUP'"'"'s Sound item stored 1 = not used, the owner'"'"'s Xe10 as found, docs/bios-setup.md, The Xe10 as found, whose 0x881E reads 0x7C in docs/probes/XE10CFG-XE10-CFG0.TXT; the ITF'"'"'s sound POST reads the bit at BANK4@0x2967), and row A is recomputed so the ITF'"'"'s sum check over rows 1..0xA stays 0 with the AA55 marker in place (BANK4@0x4677-0x46B5) in every state, with 0xE6 at the defaults (row 2 0x0C, row 3 0x80), 0xE5 / 0xE4 / 0xE3 at 1M / auto (640K) / 640K, 0x66 at GDC 5 MHz and 0x65 at GDC 5 MHz with 1M (the factory 0x67 less row 4'"'"'s bit 1); a program'"'"'s write to a record row reads back until the CPU-reset echo clear, then the OSD row again; the bank-A mirror cell the ITF copies into port 0x871E at POST (code 0x0156 row 3, BANK4@0x4439-0x4465) is re-written from setup_cfg by every restore pulse, so a program'"'"'s write there lasts until the next reset (the machine reset or the guest'"'"'s 0x534 reboot, pc9821_iobus'"'"'s sdip_seed_pulse) and the OSD'"'"'s 0x871E byte is what POST copies, the other mirror cells keeping the measured bytes (0x881E = 0x7C, Sound not used, the capture'"'"'s byte); the mirror'"'"'s 0x861E cell (code 0x0156 row 2) is the OSD'"'"'s floppy-mode byte after every restore, the bytes the Xe10 holds after SETUP stored each mode (F7 / 76 / 75 / F4 at auto (1M) / 1M / auto (640K) / 640K, docs/probes/XE10CFG-XE10-CFG0/CFGF3/CFGF1/CFGF0.TXT). The mirror'"'"'s 0x851E cell (code 0x0156 row 1) is the port-0x31 image the firmware'"'"'s own writers store there, not the port'"'"'s read-back 02: the ITF'"'"'s re-sync (BANK4@0x4839-0x4849) and SETUP'"'"'s accessor (BANK3@0x220B-0x221D) take port 0x31 with bit 4 made the odd-parity bit, so after every restore it is E3 at the defaults (port 0x31 F3, docs/probes/XE10CFG-XE10-CFG0.TXT), FB at 20 lines, E3 at keep, FB at both and 73 at GDC 5 MHz (the Xa7C9W capture'"'"'s own cell, qemu-pc98 roms/pc98bios/memsw.inc `.byte 0x01, 0x01, 0x73`), bit 5 (the internal hard disk, use) set in every state, because the ITF'"'"'s POST copy writes it to 0x851E, port 0x31 follows that write, and the IDE ROM detaches the hard disks when port 0x31 bit 5 reads 0 (BANK3@0x2F5-0x324). After a restore at the defaults every one of the 24 mirror cells is the byte the ITF'"'"'s re-sync (BANK4@0x4824-0x486F) stores from the ports the core seeds, which its POST copy (BANK4@0x4439-0x4482) writes back: bank A 7C E3 F7 3E 7C 7F FF BF 7F 7F 49 98 (docs/probes/XE10CFG-XE10-CFG0.TXT SDIPA0 with 0x851E'"'"'s cell port 0x31'"'"'s image and 0x861E auto (1M)), bank B 8F then eleven 80 (SDIPB). Fail-first by mutation (tests/pc9821/mutations.json): with the record row or the mirror cell constant, or the checksum not recomputed, the non-default steps fail, with row 2 bit 7 the constant factory 1 (the core before it followed the GDC clock) the defaults fail, with row 4 the factory 0xCD (Sound used) the defaults fail, and with the 0x851E cell the port'"'"'s read-back 02 (the core before this cell was composed) every row-1 step fails. The ITF'"'"'s saved-table length (code 0x1056 line 0x20 rows 0/1, the length word of the port table the ITF saves from code 0x5610 up, BANK4@0x2136-0x2192, and does not rewrite while it is non-zero, BANK4@0x1D34-0x1D64) reads 0000 after every restore, the length a core load starts from: the next POST'"'"'s KANJI CG-RAM test (BANK4@0x0076-0x00F4) zeroes the table'"'"'s part past code 0x5620 (the Xa7C9W capture'"'"'s length 0x15A crosses it too, qemu-pc98 roms/pc98bios/memsw.inc), so a kept length made the boot after fail the table check (BANK4@0x4741-0x47AD) and install the factory DIP image (BANK4@0x47B2) - FAIL-FIRST, the replayed length 0183 survived the restore.' ;;
    shadow_romsum) echo 'an OSD reset no longer fails the ITF'"'"'s own ROM sum. The window at 0xF8000-0xFFFFF has TWO layers -- the selected pack bank and a writable shadow in the SDRAM reserve above the pack -- and the gated STORE the ITF makes at 0xF8E90 goes to the shadow, never the pack, so the pack the sum reads stays pristine. The READ serves the shadow only while the shadow is enabled (0x043F'"'"'s 0x8 nibble, reset-cleared) AND has been written since reset, so a boot that has not stored yet reads the pack. And a SOFT REBOOT - the 0x534 soft reset, with RAM and the shadow'"'"'s stored 0x04 intact and no platform reset - clears the dirty flag through pc9821_iobus'"'"'s soft_reboot strobe, as QEMU'"'"'s armed-0x534 machine reset restores the stored byte (pc98_mem_reset: `s->f8e90_value = s->f8e90_reset`), so the re-POST'"'"'s second sum pass reads the pack'"'"'s 0x00 and a new store is read back; FAIL-FIRST, because the dirty flag survived the reboot and the pass read the stale 0x04 - ROM SUM ERROR after a software reboot.' ;;
    shadowmask) echo 'The shadowmask'"'"'s 256x11 mask_lut is one mixed-clock M10K, not 2,816 flip-flops: a word written with a 3'"'"'b011 command on clk_sys is read back through the module'"'"'s registered clk read (lut <= mask_lut[mask_idx]) at every one of the 256 indices the counters sweep, with the clk read latency and the mask_enable gating unchanged. The oracle is the pre-M10K register-array module run on the same stimulus, and the bench requires the two to agree on dout and the sync outputs at every clk edge while the oracle'"'"'s own registered read equals the pattern written to each index.' ;;
    sound_sum) echo 'TB_PC9821_SOUND_SUM: the loud-chord clip census. It drives every sound source the PC-9801-86 board has and reports the running peak and 16-bit-rail count at EVERY summing stage of the audio path, and pins the F1 contract: the FM+SSG join delivers the WIDE 20-bit sum UNCLAMPED (snd_l === mix_l, fail-first against the pre-fix intermediate sat16) with the AC-coupled (zero-mean) SSG added after the junction clamp, the single saturation of the chain living at the final DAC (ymfm'"'"'s clamp16 / NP2kai'"'"'s _saturation_s16)' ;;
    sprite) echo 'TH01'"'"'s sprite ERASE / BACKGROUND-RESTORE makes the machine'"'"'s own bus shape, and the EGC'"'"'s byte LANE comes from the CPU'"'"'s BYTE ENABLES, not from the address: z486'"'"'s address output is 30 bits ("Physical address [31:2]", z486.sv:78) and pc9821_system forms the guest address as {z_addr, 2'"'"'b00} (pc9821_system.sv:647,1061), so every access arrives DWORD-ALIGNED with the operand'"'"'s byte lane carried only in cpu_be (z486_pkg.sv:1169 calc_be). Deriving the EGC'"'"'s lane from the address'"'"'s low bits yields 0 for every access, so a 16-bit unit on lanes 2:3 (be = 1100) became a BYTE unit on lane 0: one byte written, the two intended bytes untouched. The two sequences asserted here are the two observed symptoms, driven exactly as th01 uses them (op_01.cpp:257 whiteline_put, th01/hardware/egcrect.cpp:3 egc_copy_rect_1_to_0_16, th01/formats/ptn_main.cpp ptn_put_quarter_8 / ptn_sloppy_unput_quarter_16): the whiteline row is drawn with the GRCG RMW and restored from page 1 by the EGC (the row must come back as the background, not stay white), and a 16x16 sprite is erased, drawn and restored (the footprint must come back as the background, not stay black). Every restoring chunk is a 16-bit access at an even plane offset, so HALF of them (offset & 3 == 2) are on lanes 2:3; the bench checks all 80 bytes of the row in all four planes, and both lane-0:1 and lane-2:3 sprite positions.' ;;
    sprite_scene) echo 'TB_PC9821_SPRITE_SCENE: models a busy Touhou-frame graphics scene - many EGC 0x3100 sprite put units per frame plus the plain plane-window restores - against the REAL src/memory/sdram.v controller and the REAL pc9821_gvram, and profiles the write path end to end: the EGC units per clk_sys, the GVRAM plane-byte rate in MB/s, the backend transaction mix (the partial-byte RMW the DQM-less module would need, the full-word write-backs, the fill reads), the write-combining buffer'"'"'s hit rate, and the scanout line fetch'"'"'s worst latency against the 2014 clk_sys raster budget while the scene saturates the client. It ASSERTS the scene'"'"'s writes land by value (the EGC 0x3100 read-then-write copies the source plane bytes to the destination, a plain restore reads back), that NO partial-byte write reaches the controller (the buffer'"'"'s whole point), and that the scanout still meets its deadline under the load.' ;;
    stripe_repro) echo 'TB_PC9821_STRIPE_REPRO: the observed horizontal stripe under the CONTENDED case the earlier value benches never drove. tb_pc9821_planewin_draw draws a full-screen picture with a raster flush per row and read every byte back right, but it drove the scanout and the CPU one after the other; this bench drives them TOGETHER through the DEPLOYED route - the real pc9821_memory_subsystem (so the response-route queue is in the path), the real pc9821_gvram, the real sdram_sync_bridge (the controller on clk_sys, here 50 MHz) and the real src/memory/sdram.v controller - with the scanout'"'"'s line fetch ticking every raster line on its own port while the CPU runs a plane-window row stream (the graphics library'"'"'s rep movsw / rep stosw shape: consecutive 16-bit stores down a row, the LAST one a partial byte), and compares every plane byte of the row against a software model.' ;;
    subsystem_stalls) echo 'TB_PC9821_SUBSYSTEM_STALLS: the INTER-SUBSYSTEM handshakes complete under adversarial interleavings, or a scenario that genuinely wedges FAILS BY NAME instead of hanging the runner. The whole pc9821_memory_subsystem (the route queue, the mapper, the real GVRAM with its WCB + M10K cache, the font requester, the scanout line fetch) runs over the REAL SDRAM bridge and the REAL src/memory/sdram.v controller (on clk_sys, as every build runs it) against the chip model.' ;;
    sys16m) echo 'With the 15-16 MiB system-space window ON (port 0x043B bit 2 cleared, which is the guest'"'"'s request: reset leaves the window OFF so 15-16 MiB is RAM) the machine puts no SYSTEM RAM at 0xF00000-0xFFFFFF, and the subsystem splits it three ways: 0xFA0000-0xFFFFFF reaches the same route and the same address as the low 0xA0000-0xFFFFF it aliases, 0xF00000-0xF7FFFF is the PEGC'"'"'s 512 KiB extended VRAM served by the mapper as one ordinary backend access at peg_base = pack_base + PACK_BYTES + 0x80000 - but ONLY with the register file'"'"'s [0xE0102] bit 0 set (NP2kai memvgaf_rd8/wr8'"'"'s VRAM-enable gate; the ITF sets it inside its own 0x6A 0x21 bracket before filling), and open bus with it clear - and only 0xF80000-0xF9FFFF reaches NO memory at all; with the window OFF the same addresses are ordinary identity RAM. Both references put the alias base at 0xFA0000 and not at 0xF00000 - qemu-pc98 aliases lowmem[0xa0000..0x100000] at 0xfa0000 (hw/i386/pc98-mem.c sys16m_mirror) and decodes its 512 KiB PEGC POST store (PEGC_POST_SIZE) at 0xf00000, and NP2kai reduces the address by 0xF00000 but selects that path only for address bits 19:17 = 5..7 (i386c/cpumem.c memsys_* vs memvgaf/memnc), its own 512 KiB memvgaf extended VRAM (vramex[0x80000]) covering the rest - so 0xF80000-0xF9FFFF is the part of the hole the ITF subtracts as the 15-16 MiB system-space hole (pc98-mem.c mem_sync_sys16m_workarea, ram[0x401] -= 8).' ;;
    sysclk_invariance) echo 'Every guest-visible TIMEBASE and device-pacing TIME this core owns measures the same WALL-CLOCK rate at two clk_sys frequencies (the base profile'"'"'s 50 MHz and mediumoc'"'"'s 100 MHz): each device is instantiated twice in ONE simulation, on its own clock, and read at the same simulated nanosecond, so an equal count is an equal real time rather than an equal cycle count - a model counting clk_sys instead of a real time reads 2x at 100 MHz. THE VIDEO ENABLE IS THE PRODUCTION ONE, not a copy: the real pc9821_system runs at both clocks (its CPU is tests/pc9821/z486_stub.sv) and its exported video_ce - the production PIXEL_CE_INC = round(PIXEL_HZ * 2^24 / SYS_FREQ) accumulator - drives the standalone CRTC probes and is itself counted, so a divider that counted clk_sys fails here. Covered, each with the time it is derived from named in the bench: PIT channel 0 at BOTH programs a guest uses - the BIOS'"'"'s 100 Hz (divisor 0x6000 at 2.4576 MHz) and the sound drivers'"'"' 9990.24 Hz (divisor 0x00F6, the Rusty intro'"'"'s INT 08h music/PCM tick) - plus channel 1 (the beeper tone, the ITF'"'"'s own 2000 Hz program: control 0x76, divisor 0x04CD) and channel 2 (the RS-232C baud clock), all three on the 8253'"'"'s own 2.4576 MHz input clock; the uPD4993A'"'"'s four guest-writable tick rates (1/64, 1/32, 1/16 s and off) and its 1 Hz divider; the keyboard'"'"'s typematic delay and interval (NP2kai'"'"'s 500 ms / 50 ms); the calendar'"'"'s CDAT serial read, whose bit clock is the GUEST'"'"'s (both clocks are driven with one wall-clock command schedule and the recovered 48-bit payload is compared); the bus mouse'"'"'s IRQ13 at its default 120 Hz and at its guest-programmed 60 Hz and 30 Hz forms; the game port'"'"'s 1.1 us tick and the axis one-shot it times (the right end stop'"'"'s 1022 ticks); the ARTIC 307200 Hz counter the ITF'"'"'s delay() spins on (qemu pc98-sys.c:64 TIMESTAMP_HZ); the two I/O-wait floors that are real times and therefore must be paid at both clocks (0x005F = a posted write'"'"'s 1744 ns bus time, the machine'"'"'s write period (XE10T-XE10.TXT /IO W8_005F), paid by the access behind it, an 8253 port >= one 2.4576 MHz tick); the GVRAM arbiter'"'"'s captured per-access holds (478 ns read, 142 ns write, 104 ns GRCG store - the capture'"'"'s times, converted per profile and asserted back as times); the FDC'"'"'s 20 us recalibrate delay; the OPNA'"'"'s 7.9872 MHz chip enable, the SSG'"'"'s cen/16 sample enable and its guest-programmed Timer A/B IRQ period (144 and 2304 chip enables per count); PCM86'"'"'s sample strobe at a guest-selected rate of NP2kai'"'"'s pcm86rate8 table; and the Xe10 I/O class table (the OSD'"'"'s I/O timing field at Xe10, pc9821_iobus io_timing_xe10), whose per-class times all come from docs/probes/XE10T-XE10.TXT /IO and whose WHOLE I/O instruction - the bus time plus the core CPU'"'"'s own 8 clk_sys (docs/probes/core/KIT2-CORE-SPEED.TXT) - must be the same REAL TIME at 50 and 100 MHz and within one clk_sys of the capture: the default class (792 ns read / 823 ns write), the EGC named ports 0x006A/0x00A6 (350 read / 318 write), the text GDC 0x0060/0x0062 (414), the graphics GDC 0x00A0/0x00A2 (639), the GRCG tile write 0x007E (318), the 8253 write 0x0077 (823, the default write), the 0x005F read (792) and posted write (1744, the XE10T capture'"'"'s write period) and the EGC register file 0x04A1 odd / 0x04A0 even (792 / 5990). The iobus'"'"'s per-access class floor (IO_MIN_HOLD = 4 clk_sys, a deliberate stall reduction against the measured 840 ns machine class) is PRINTED at both clocks and asserted by nothing, because locking in either number would decide a stall-budget question this bench does not own. FAIL-FIRST history: the 0x60 status byte'"'"'s data-ready bit 0 was a bare `stat_dr <= ~stat_dr` on every clk_sys, so its edge count read 2x at 100 MHz before the fix. The data-ready bit'"'"'s RATE IS NOT A MACHINE FACT and is not claimed: the machine'"'"'s 2^21-sample 0x60 histogram (docs/probes/GDC.TXT) measures only its ~50 % duty and no reference gives its period, so the bench asserts the DERIVATION (one toggle per two 21.0526 MHz dots) and the two clocks'"'"' agreement. The MPU-PC98II'"'"'s MIDI-out serialiser is measured here too: its start bit is the same 32 us at both clocks, which is MIDI'"'"'s own 31250 baud. The 31 kHz mode (0x09A8 bit 0 set) is covered at both clocks too: the production dot enable with its 31 kHz select high drives a CRTC programmed with the BIOS 31 kHz master SYNC block, and its frame and line periods must be the Xe10'"'"'s measured 70.0669 Hz / 31.460 kHz (docs/probes/CRT31K.TXT, calibrated) within 3 ppm at 50 and 100 MHz alike.' ;;
    system_integration) echo 'The I/O port map the fabric actually answers is compared, port by port, against a real PC-9821Xe10'"'"'s capture, so a decoded byte the machine does not drive and a machine byte the fabric answers open bus are both named; the device-select decode is cross-checked against the ports the fabric routes to the device bus, so the two decoders cannot drift apart silently; and the CPU reset release is checked to still wait for the SDRAM to leave its initialization.' ;;
    system_reset_seed) echo 'pc9821_system'"'"'s unreset telemetry: the INTA pulse counter inta_count/z_inta_d counts exactly the CPU'"'"'s INTA pulses PER RESET EPOCH (it X-locked and never armed in 4-state sim and ran across warm resets). The CPU is tests/pc9821/z486_stub.sv - the real z486 tree cannot elaborate in the 4-state simulator these checks are FOR - issuing exactly two INTA pulses per epoch, so count == 2 is the whole F6 contract.' ;;
    text_colour) echo 'The text plane'"'"'s colour contract, from the attribute byte to the RGB wires, and the byte the machine'"'"'s own BIOS reads to choose that colour: every attribute colour index renders through the fixed 8-colour text table (R=idx1, G=idx2, B=idx0) and does NOT change when the graphics palette ports 0xA8/0xAA/0xAC/0xAE are programmed, and the eight text-VRAM memory-switch bytes survive the guest'"'"'s attribute-plane fill because MODE FF1 bit 6 is their write enable -- which is what keeps the BIOS'"'"'s console attribute white (0xE1) instead of green (0x81).' ;;
    text_modes) echo 'TB_PC9821_TEXT_MODES: the text plane'"'"'s ROW GEOMETRY follows the master GDC and the CRTC the way NP2kai'"'"'s maketext() does (vram/maketext.c:162-435, transcribed in this bench as a whole-frame loop over ints): for every active raster line of a frame, the TRAM word the scanout fetches for column 1 is that line'"'"'s character-row address plus one, the pixel drawn there is the glyph line only while 0 <= nowline < min(CL,16), the underline lights the row'"'"'s last line (nowline + 1 == lines) and the cursor the lines nowline in [CSRFORM top, bottom], and on each row'"'"'s first line the kanji row-cache walk asks for exactly the row that is drawn next (its address and a bank that differs from the one on screen and is the one that row is then drawn from) - over the 25-line default, NP2kai'"'"'s BIOS 400-20 setting (LR 0x13, PL 0x1E, BL 0x11, CL 0x10: 20 rows of 20 lines, two blank lines above and below each 16-line glyph), a 30-row 480-line SYNC block, each CRTC register on its own (PL below 16 starting the row inside the glyph, BL, CL, the GDC LR against a different CRTC row length), smooth scrolling with SUR = 0 (SSL lines eaten at the top, the SDR+1-row region cut) and with an unscrolled SUR band above the region, and 2-, 3- and 4-partition split screens from the full 16-byte PRAM (a partition switch in mid-row, the 0x74 PRAM command'"'"'s offset, and the wrap from the fourth partition back to the first), rows short enough that the row index passes 63 (LR 5 / BL 5: 67 rows, LR 1 / BL 1: 200 rows on 400 lines, NP2kai'"'"'s row address accumulating with no row limit, maketext.c:321), and the row-0 re-walk after a mid-frame start-word change reading all 80 cells of row 0 at the new start word although the sequencer'"'"'s next row has moved to partition 1 by the walk'"'"'s second line; and the real Xe10'"'"'s text pitch and row start, from its filmed run of TXTMODE /D (docs/probes/KIT2-XE10-TXTMODE.TXT, the probe'"'"'s own SYNC blocks): a SYNC block'"'"'s C/R byte loads the pitch, for 0x0F and the display-off 0x0E alike, and a later PITCH overrides it (P1 C/R 0x4E, P3 0x4C and P4 0x3E with no PITCH run pitch 78, 76 and 62; P5 SYNC 0x0E then START runs 78; P6 PITCH 80 then SYNC runs the SYNC'"'"'s 78), and every line'"'"'s column 0 draws its row'"'"'s own first word at AW 78 and AW 64 too (the cell the render registers latched at the line before'"'"'s last cell), with or without PITCH 80. FAIL-FIRST: the pre-change scanout decoded every line as a fixed 16-line row of one partition (row = vline >> 4) and ignored the CRTC registers and the PRAM lengths, so every case but the 25-line default and the 30-row block (whose 16-line rows the old decode already drew; its walk request did not follow) mismatches there; and before the kit-2 change a SYNC block left the pitch at 80, so P1-P6 mismatch on every row after the first, and a block narrower than 80 cells drew the cell past the line'"'"'s end in every column 0, so P3, P4 and AW 64 + PITCH 80 mismatch on every line.' ;;
    trace) echo 'The instruction trace records one ring entry per EIP transition carrying the cycles spent at that PC, commits the PC it is stuck on with a saturated dwell so the stall itself is in the ring, wraps without losing the newest transitions, and dumps it oldest-first as ASCII hex framed by a header, a labelled stall-context line, and a footer; and it arms its stall detector only after real transitions, so a reset is not mistaken for a wedge. The context line carries the address of the transaction the CPU was waiting on when the detector fired, with its write bit and the valid/ready/response flags beside it, and the font port'"'"'s own request valid - the one thing a ring of past PCs cannot say, and the field a raw capture needs labelled because nothing else tells an operator what a bare hex run means.' ;;
    tram_addr_equiv) echo 'TB_PC9821_TRAM_ADDR_EQUIV: the CYCLE-EXACT EQUIVALENCE contract for the text fetch address - the REFACTOR pin for the pc9821_crtc timing seam (an 80 MHz fit'"'"'s worst path, v_sync[2][5] -> the text-RAM port-B address register), a CHARACTERIZATION against the documented semantics and not a hardware claim (the hardware behaviour stays in the reference-derived benches): tram_vid_addr is bit-identical EVERY clk_sys cycle to a golden address computed in the bench from the SYNC field map/clamp, the documented fetch schedule and the cfg_snap/cfg_pending snapshot semantics, every mux arm included (the walker arm is combinational and keeps its priority, so a registered walker address cannot hide behind an equal value), over frames with mid-frame register writes, writes straddling the frame_end snapshot edge (the epoch-defer window), frame-boundary SYNC swaps, 40/80-column swaps and irregular pixel_ce gaps; the arm selects and f_wordidx at its consuming dots are bit-identical, and the render captures (r_wordidx, r_raster) never move. It also pins the odd-sync-parity premises by name (geom-shared-bit-identity: HTOTAL/VTOTAL/HACTIVE/VACTIVE and the o_htotal/o_vtotal/o_vline outputs are the documented field map at every cycle - the single shared source the fetch walker, pc9821_gdc2'"'"'s wrapped display-line parity and the feed_odd/at_line_tick expression must all read together, never a mix of live and registered; linetick-dot-outside-active: HACTIVE <= HTOTAL-8 so the line-tick dot stays outside the mixer'"'"'s active window).' ;;
    tram_bridge) echo 'The TRAM bridge: byte-enable writes land in the right lanes and a DWORD read returns the response in the expected cycle. The attribute half (0xA2000-0xA3FFF) is pinned to the machine: odd attribute bytes take NO store (NP2kai mem/memtram.c:82-99/130-161 gate on `!(address & 1)`) and read back as 0xFF whatever the plane holds (the Xe10 read-back row (`A2000` | `00 FF 55 AA` | `00 FF 55 FF`), the row behind both paths), on the DWORD read path and on the cache-line fill; the code half odd bytes still take. The store half is checked against a store-port control, never against the bench array. FAIL-FIRST: the pre-fix bridge returns the plane bytes, 83558100 instead of FF55FF00.' ;;
    unk18f) echo 'The 0x18F0/0x18F2 pair returns what the firmware wrote to it. The ITF'"'"'s routine at BANK4@0x5460 writes four words to 0x18F2 after selecting 0x18F0<-0x0015, then RE-SELECTS 0x18F0 and reads four words back FROM THE BEGINNING, copying each byte into the CG store under code 0x2A56 - so a select write must reset the window pointer, the data port must return the written bytes, and 0x18F3 must be the upper half of a word access. Before this the family was decoded nowhere, the reads returned open bus 0xFF, and the CG image built from them was not the one the machine builds.' ;;
    video_cleaner_equiv) echo 'pc9821_video_cleaner is output-identical to the framework'"'"'s video_cleaner as z486_mister.sv wires it (HBlank/VBlank/interlace/f1 tied 0): VGA_R/G/B, VGA_HS, VGA_VS and DE_out match on every clock for sync waveforms of either polarity whose active pulse is shorter than the saturation bound (CLOCK_RATE_HZ/16 clocks), with arbitrarily long inactive phases, stuck syncs and ce_pix gaps, and a planted waveform whose two phases both exceed the bound does separate them.' ;;
    video_clk) echo 'The analog video leaves the core on an exact dot-locked video clock with a CONSTANT pixel period and a CONSTANT line and frame period in every analog mode, at every clk_sys: with clk_sys at 80, 100 and 120 MHz (three lanes in one simulation) and the core'"'"'s own dot enable (pc9821_dot_ce) driving a 848 x 440 raster (640 x 400 active, the default SYNC block'"'"'s geometry) into pc9821_video_out, the CLK_VIDEO it hands the framework runs at 2 x 400/19 MHz (period 23 750 ps) and over three whole frames after the output has settled every CE_PIXEL period, every hsync period and every vsync period is one and the same number of video clocks: native 2 clocks a pixel and 1696 a line (848 x 47.5 ns = 40.28 us, 24.83 kHz), doubled 1 clock a pixel and 848 a line (49.66 kHz), each frame 440 x 1696 = 746 240 native clocks (56.42 Hz) whatever the mode; and in every mode every active pixel is the raster'"'"'s own dot in order (none dropped, repeated or reordered across the crossing); and over a LONG doubled run (100 frames, at each clk_sys) there is no FIFO re-centre or underflow, every frame is exactly 880 lines x 848 clocks with a 3-LINE VSYNC (the doubled picture'"'"'s compatibility pulse: the replayed 16-line one lost sync on a VGA monitor under composite sync; FAIL-FIRST on the 16-line output), every VSYNC edge lands on an HSYNC leading edge, every VS-delimited frame holds exactly 800 DE lines (what the framework'"'"'s scaler frames on, sys/ascal.vhd), and DE is never high inside HSYNC or VSYNC. The figures are the dot clock'"'"'s (400/19 MHz = 50 MHz x 8/19, docs/pc9821-port-status.md "Pixel clock and the video pipeline") and the raster'"'"'s, never read out of the DUT. FAIL-FIRST: on the fractional-enable design (CLK_VIDEO = clk_sys, CE_PIXEL a 24-bit accumulator carry) the video clock is clk_sys and every pixel period takes two values (3 or 4 clocks at 80 MHz), so the clock, pixel and line checks all fail.' ;;
    video_frame) echo 'The frame-start line origin: with the REAL pc9821_gdc_text driving the line origin and the REAL one-pulse-per-line tick (pc9821_system'"'"'s gvram_h_count) into the REAL pc9821_gvram ping-pong, and every plane word painted with its picture-line number, the line displayed at raster line k is picture line k for every one of the 400 active lines -- raster line 0 is picture line 0 and not the vertical-blanking origin the un-compensated origin decode latched two raster lines late. The line-buffer fetch delivers a line two raster lines after the tick that requested it (pc9821_gvram), so an origin decoded from the CURRENT raster line draws the blanking origins of the frame'"'"'s last two lines across the top; this bench is that edge case, and it fails on the tree before the +2 compensation and passes after it (fail-first measured on the pre-fix tree).' ;;
    video_mixer) echo 'The graphics side of the scanout: the four planes fold to the 4-bit index {E,G,R,B} -- one bit per lane per dot, B bit 0, R bit 1, G bit 2, E bit 3, MSB first -- the palette reads that index out of the 16-entry analogue table or, in 8-colour digital mode, out of the pack the machine'"'"'s own power-on bytes imply (0xA8/AA/AC/AE = 37/15/26/04 -> black, blue, red, magenta, green, cyan, yellow, white), a 256-colour index bypasses the fold into the 256-entry FULL-byte table (NP2kai pal_make9821 renders the bytes verbatim) whose registered read delays BOTH arms by one clk_sys in 256-colour mode only - the uniform one-dot delay, with the boundary capture still showing the old TEXT dot - while the 16-colour path moves not one dot (a 24-vector sweep against the closed-form old path), the 256-colour index is the full 8-bit byte (0x16 is not 0x06), and the 200-line odd-line gate is dropped in packed mode and kept in 16-colour mode, and the D4 compositor puts an opaque text dot over the graphics pixel, shows graphics under a transparent one, and changes its answer when the rule select is flipped.' ;;
    video_reprogram) echo 'The analog output recovers within one output frame when the guest reprograms the master GDC mid-frame: with clk_sys 80 MHz, the core'"'"'s dot enable and the real pc9821_crtc feeding pc9821_video_out (native and doubled), a SYNC block written in the middle of a frame - the 550-line block (lf 480), defsyncm15 (an 896-dot line) and back to the default - and a GDC RESET command with the default block leave, from the SECOND output frame after the write on, every line exactly the new length (native 2 x HTOTAL, doubled HTOTAL video clocks - HTOTAL from the block by NP2kai'"'"'s gdc_updateclock() transcription), every VSYNC edge on an HSYNC leading edge, no DE inside HSYNC or VSYNC, and no crossing-FIFO event; what the transition frame carried is printed. FAIL-FIRST: it passes on the current design, so it is proven able to fail by recorded mutants of the crossing FIFO'"'"'s recovery and the doubler'"'"'s line resynchronisation.' ;;
    video_scanout) echo 'A line painted into the plane windows is scanned out as the pixels the raster asks for, at the machine'"'"'s pixel-enable timing: the REAL pc9821_gvram fetch and its REGISTERED line buffer drive the REAL pc9821_video_mixer, so the read'"'"'s one-clk latency and the mixer'"'"'s column sequencer are both in the path, and every active dot'"'"'s colour must be the fold of the column that dot belongs to -- dot d of column c is bit 7-d of that column'"'"'s plane bytes -- for all 80 columns and all 8 dots; the sequencer this pins used to prefetch column c+1 on column c'"'"'s last dot, which served the eighth dot of every column from the NEXT column'"'"'s word, a static error every 8 pixels that no bench with a combinational line buffer can see.' ;;
    vmode_notify) echo 'A guest switch between the 24 kHz and the 31 kHz raster makes the framework re-report the video mode on the native path, where both rasters are 640 x 400 active: with pc9821_video_out (doubler built, the OSD at Native) carrying the BIOS 24 kHz raster (848 x 440, NP2kai bios/bios18.c gdcmastersync[1]) and then the 31 kHz one (800 x 449, gdcmastersync[2]) into the framework'"'"'s own video_calc (sys/hps_io.sv, its new_vmode fed from video_out'"'"'s vmode_31), with the video side flushed for the relock in the vertical blank as pc9821_vpll_ctl does, so that no active line is lost and the active count (hcnt 640 pixels on the first line, vcnt 400 lines) is the same in both modes, every switch (24 to 31 kHz and back) bumps vid_nres by exactly one and re-takes vid_fclks as the new frame'"'"'s length in video clocks (800 x 449 x 2 = 718 400 at 31 kHz, 848 x 440 x 2 = 746 240 at 24 kHz), and 20 steady frames in one mode bump it by none. FAIL-FIRST: with new_vmode tied to 0 (the top level'"'"'s old wiring) the active count is all video_calc compares, so no switch is reported and vid_fclks keeps the 24 kHz frame'"'"'s 746 240 after the switch to 31 kHz.' ;;
    vpll_ctl) echo 'The video PLL controller (pc9821_vpll_ctl) puts the fractional video PLL into the dot clock the guest'"'"'s raster runs on and holds the video side off until the PLL has relocked: on every configuration change it writes the N counter (address 3, bypass), the M counter (address 4) and the 32-bit fraction K (address 7), then a start (address 2), each held until waitrequest drops, exactly once per change and never while nothing changes; decoding those words as Altera'"'"'s Cyclone V PLL reconfiguration map defines them (counter = high + low, bypass = 1; output = 50 MHz x (M + K/2^32) / (N x C0), C0 the generated 19) the video clock is exactly 2 x 400/19 MHz with 0x09A8 bit 0 clear (the 24 kHz dot clock, and the configuration the controller settles on at power-up) and 2 x the Xe10'"'"'s measured 31 kHz dot clock within 3 ppm while 0x09A8 bit 0 is set (docs/probes/CRT31K.TXT: 25 168 031 Hz, calibrated); `vclk_31` names the requested configuration from the first write; `busy` rises with the first write and falls only after lock has been continuously high for SETTLE clocks after the start; and a lock loss while idle raises `busy` again until the PLL has relocked and settled, with no write. FAIL-FIRST: the pre-31 kHz controller has no 0x09A8 input and writes no fraction, so the bench cannot build against it.' ;;
    watchdog) echo 'The stall-watchdog telemetry record is a function of the diagnostic fields and nothing else: all 351 bytes are compared against a golden capture of this formatter, and the 137 bytes that carry fields are shown to move with them. The record is also shown to be ONE line - exactly one LF, as its last byte, and no CR anywhere, because a CR arrives as a second newline through a tty and that is the blank line between records that was removed. The record is also a SNAPSHOT: its bytes are the fields'"'"' values at the report'"'"'s accept (snap), held while the record is read, so setting the fields again without a new snapshot reprints the old bytes and a paced emission can never splice two counter values into one field (a telemetry tear); a NEW snapshot takes the new values.' ;;
    wdg_trigger) echo 'The stall watchdog'"'"'s trigger decision distinguishes the three states it must report, and the one it exists for is reachable: a request held outstanding and never completed for the window reports '"'"'W'"'"', no request at all reports '"'"'S'"'"', and an unbroken stream of completions reports the '"'"'P'"'"' heartbeat, so the wedge the old `!z_valid` condition could never fire on is now the case a test drives.' ;;
    win0_device_class) echo 'The window-0 overlay at 0x80000-0x9FFFF classifies like its TARGET, not like its own address (aliases.md A5): with 0x0461 = 0x0E the overlay IS the 0xF8000-0xFFFFF banked window, whose canonical encoding is served direct/uncached while the overlay matched none of the device terms and stayed cached over the same storage. A writer behind the cache (a canonical uncached store, a bank flip, the GRCG/EGC) then left the overlay'"'"'s line stale where the canonical side read fresh. The z486 memory unit takes the platform'"'"'s win0_unmapped verdict (pc9821_system sets it for EVERY moved window, 0x0461 != 0x08, because a window over plain RAM is also one byte with two names) and the backend models the window-0 bias. FAIL-FIRST: the overlay reads the pre-store bytes after a canonical store, over the banked window and over plain RAM; PASS: both encodings agree, and the verdict-0 identity window still caches.' ;;
    win0_out_of_range) echo 'A window-0 access whose TARGET page is not guest RAM behaves exactly like the direct encoding of that page (aliases.md A6): past guest_ram_bytes both are open bus with stores discarded, and under sys16m with win0 over 0xF00000-0xF7FFFF both reach the PEGC EVRAM at peg_base. out_of_range used to test cpu_addr (0x80000, inside RAM) instead of the biased d_addr, so the fallthrough emitted raw d_addr and the overlay reached ordinary SDRAM behind the map. FAIL-FIRST: the overlay'"'"'s store lands in SDRAM and reads back where the direct encoding reads F'"'"'s, and the two encodings disagree byte for byte over the PEGC window. A window page below 8 is a hole on the real Xe10 (docs/probes/KIT2-XE10-RISKY-461.TXT:5-17, RISKY /461): with 0x0461 = 00, 02, 04 or 06 every window word at 0x80000-0x9FFFC reads FFFFFFFF while the conventional memory the page names keeps its contents, and a store through the window at pages 00 and 02 never reaches that memory, while page 08 is the identity - FAIL-FIRST, the core aliased the conventional memory there.' ;;
    window_coverage) echo 'Every address in window 0 is claimed by exactly one owner, for EVERY window base the 0x0461 register can select (all 256 of them, not the three or four the firmware is known to write, and not the 252 that no bench has ever driven).' ;;
    wrq) echo 'The OPNA register-write queue'"'"'s storage is one 128x12 M10K, not flip-flops with 128 per-word write enables: 128 host writes fill the queue, the drain fires them back in order with their exact address/data/stretch payload and a clear flag-class bit, and the drain'"'"'s cycle-by-cycle behaviour (its fire strobe, state, read and write pointers and count) is identical to the pre-M10K register-array implementation run as an oracle on the same stimulus, so the block RAM'"'"'s registered output replaces the old drain register one-for-one and the DRAIN_GAP/SHORT_GAP pacing is unchanged. Both models carry the S1 fire-retry discipline (a fire meeting a host access holds and retries; the pop moves only when the fire completes), so what this bench proves is the STORAGE equivalence, edge for edge.' ;;
    xe10_absent_ports) echo 'TB_PC9821_XE10_ABSENT_PORTS: on the Xe10 build (pc9821_iobus at its default parameters) three ports answer as the machine and the references do: 0x043B reads 0xFB out of reset with the 15-16 MiB system space enabled (bit 2 clear: NP2kai'"'"'s zeroed necio latch, QEMU'"'"'s power-up sys16m = 1 reading 0x00, on the machine'"'"'s measured 0xFB base byte), the eight PC-9801-118 game-port addresses 0x1480-0x1487 read open bus 0xFF and reach no device in either direction (the Xe10 has no -118; NP2kai attaches the port only with that board), and 0x0EE0 reads 0xFF (PORTS.TXT 0EE0=FF) even after a POST-code write, the debug-status read-back existing only with DBG_STATUS_PORT.' ;;
    xsds_readpipe) echo 'TB_PC9821_XSDS_READPIPE: the strict XSDS SDRAM model PIPELINES its READs the way a real SDR SDRAM does - two back-to-back BL=1 READs return BOTH words, each exactly CAS-latency clocks after its own command (CL=2 and CL=3), a READ issued while an earlier BL=4 burst is outputting truncates that burst only from its own CL slot on, and the bus is Hi-Z outside the data slots - with no protocol or timing violation recorded by the model.' ;;
    z486_reloc_equiv) echo 'TB_PC9821_Z486_RELOC_EQUIV: the CYCLE-EXACT EQUIVALENCE contract for the z486 address_unit relocation cone - the REFACTOR pin for the Class-1 CSA fold (the base-profile limiter'"'"'s serialized 3-operand sums), a CHARACTERIZATION against the documented semantics and not a hardware claim. Every relocation OUTPUT (issue_ea/issue_linear/issue_linear_low) is bit-identical EVERY cycle, and every relocation CAPTURE (the stack push/pop forms with forwarded_esp +/- 2/4 and 16/32-bit SS, moffs, the modrm EA and the split two-cycle EA, plus the exec IND three-term and two-term relocations over all mask16 override classes) lands bit-identically in ind/ind_linear/ind_delta on every capturing edge, to a golden computed in independent arithmetic with the documented 16-bit offset wraps preserved explicitly (zext16 the offset BEFORE the base add - the masked arms are NOT 32-bit three-operand sums). Blind spots stated in the header: not a hardware bench, simultaneous issue+exec ownership, and the ea register overlay.' ;;
    z486_reqaddr_equiv) echo 'TB_PC9821_Z486_REQADDR_EQUIV: the CYCLE-EXACT EQUIVALENCE contract for the z486 paging request-address cone - the REFACTOR pin for the Class-1 cut-2 flatten, a CHARACTERIZATION against the documented semantics and not a hardware claim. paging_tlb'"'"'s registered and VIPT lookup ports return the documented translation EVERY cycle over refill/INVLPG/CR3 sequences and all four ways (the documented PLRU fill order, the tag-matched INVLPG and the sidecar-hit PLRU refresh are transcribed so eviction runs are pinned too; the VIPT sidecar'"'"'s 256-entry direct-mapped table, its epoch validity and reset/wrap scrub, its SUBSET rule - a walker refill that evicts a valid entry also invalidates the victim'"'"'s sidecar slot, one cycle later - and its hazard poison are pinned exactly; the live demand port this contract used to pin was REMOVED by upstream'"'"'s one-port TLB, where walker, demand and prefetch share the registered lookup as on the i486), paging_unit'"'"'s request-owner mux equals the documented priority merge over ALL owner-overlap combinations (the arms are driven with force - the x87_pulse_race idiom - so the full operand space is covered), and memory'"'"'s four A20-masked addresses equal the PC-98 preset'"'"'s A20 mask (bit 20 cleared, the Xe10'"'"'s measured BIT20-ONLY) every cycle with every external capture edge carrying the documented masked phys[31:2] and holding it between captures. Blind spots stated in the header: not a hardware bench, the request classification cones (alias coherency'"'"'s), the walker and store queue quiet - and, measured by the hit counters this bench prints, it NEVER REACHES A HIT on the registered port or the sidecar (0 and 0 over 20 001 checks / 20 006 cycles; the sidecar never leaves its scrub at this stimulus'"'"'s CR3 rate), so its paging_tlb contract above is a MISS-path claim there and a mutant that takes way 0'"'"'s PFN from way 1 PASSES it; the registered port'"'"'s hit paths are pinned by tb_pc9821_z486_tlb_window, not here.' ;;
    z486_sigma_equiv) echo 'TB_PC9821_Z486_SIGMA_EQUIV: the CYCLE-EXACT EQUIVALENCE contract for the z486 data_unit SIGMA next-state arm mux - the REFACTOR pin for the Class-1 cut-3 flatten (the sink of the vipt_load_wb_dst_r -> data_unit|sigma family), a CHARACTERIZATION against the documented semantics and not a hardware claim. sigma captures the documented next-state on EVERY clock edge across the full arm space and its overlaps: the stack-pointer advance (forwarded_esp +/- 2/4, 16/32-bit stack, both directions, wrap cases directed), the gate arm, the two shift arms, the 17-arm aluop->alu_result class, the 8-arm muldiv class gated by muldiv_sigma_write (a documented NO-OP/hold when the gate is low), the unlisted-aluop hold, and the any_fault override over everything. The internal arm VALUES are driven with force (the x87_pulse_race idiom) so the golden is independent arithmetic over chosen values. Blind spots stated: not a hardware bench, the z486.sv forwarded_esp arm mux is pinned by its in-tree FWD-ESP FUSE in every simulation, the ALU/shifter/muldiv engines are forced at their results.' ;;
    z486_tlb_window) echo 'TB_PC9821_Z486_TLB_WINDOW: paging_tlb'"'"'s REGISTERED lookup port - since upstream'"'"'s one-port TLB its ONLY four-way lookup port (walker, demand and prefetch share it as on the i486; the live demand port this bench also checked was REMOVED with it) - never hands back a translation the array does not hold. When the data array was read one cycle early, in the cycle after a walker insert the port held the PRE-insert word while the valid bits were post-insert: when the insert replaced the same VPN'"'"'s entry (a CR3 switch or an INVLPG+PTE edit, where invalidate_all resets PLRU to 000 and way 0 can still carry that VPN'"'"'s old tag) the pair reported a HIT with the old physical frame, the old permission bits and the old VGA class. The bench drives the port contract directly, with a golden transcribed from the module'"'"'s documented update rules (in-place match else PLRU victim; CR3 clears every valid bit and resets PLRU; INVLPG clears only the tag-matched way), over seven directed sequences: the CR3 re-walk of the same VPN, an INVLPG re-walk that changes the permission bits, a re-walk that replaces a DIFFERENT VPN'"'"'s way (the probing lookup must then miss), a four-way fill whose fifth insert evicts by PLRU, and the VGA-class change - all with the window probe in the cycle right after the insert. Every cycle the port must EITHER equal the documented post-edge state field for field (hit/frame/writable/user/dirty/is_vga_mem) OR take the no-hit form, and OUTSIDE the window it must equal the documented state exactly, so the refusal may not be blanket: a probe of another set in the window keeps its hit and an INVLPG cycle needs no refusal at all. FAIL-FIRST: on the pre-window-guard RTL the same bench reports the stale HIT (frame=old, want=new) in each of the three same-set re-walk sequences. Blind spots stated in the header: not a hardware bench, the walker/paging_unit FSM and the VIPT sidecar are outside it.' ;;
    *) echo "" ;;
  esac
}

